    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/247-veterinary-care-assistant-vca-small-animal-wing-pp51363": {
      "title": "24/7 Veterinary Care Assistant (VCA)",
      "description": "24/7 Veterinary Care Assistant (VCA) - Small Animal Wing | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs 24/7 Veterinary Care Assistant (VCA) - Small Animal Wing Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs 24/7 Veterinary Care Assistant (VCA) - Small Animal Wing Salary: £23,934.67 (pro rata for 0.95 FTE) + 15% Shift Allowance = £27,524.87 per annum. This equates to an hourly rate of £13.24 plus a 15% shift allowance. We have an exciting opportunity for someone to join us as a 24/7 Veterinary Care Assistant in our Small Animal Wing on a permanent basis. The referral hospital is a very fast-paced environment working with complex and seriously ill animals. The main objective of the role is to provide animal care to an excellent standard for the Queen's Veterinary School referral Hospital, to meet the needs of the service in the Small Animal Wing. The small animal wing consists of four dog, two cat, critical care, isolation wards housing an average of 15-25 patients during the overnight period and Theatre Suite. You will play an important part in a primary care team working alongside nurses and other veterinary care assistants. Responsibilities will include cleaning and animal care duties in order to assist veterinary nurses in the inpatient area and in theatres. In return, we offer an encouraging and nurturing environment and have a dedicated team of clinicians, nurses and veterinary care assistants who are committed to providing the best care for our patients. Benefits Generous paid annual leave including bank holidays Defined benefit pension scheme Enhanced family friendly policies Access to a dedicated Personal and Professional Development team Wellness programme including Occupational Health team and Staff counselling Staff discount scheme including shopping vouchers Cycle to work scheme Travel to work loans Eye care voucher scheme Discounted gym membership Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this role please contact the QVSH Clinical HR Team by email: qvsh.hr@vet.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PP51363 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. Further particulars for the role and information about the Department are available in the Further Particulars document enclosed with this advert. If you are viewing this advert on an external site, please visit the University's job pages for access. Applications will be monitored regularly, and we may contact candidates prior to the closing date. Therefore, if you are interested, please submit your application as early as possible. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Veterinary Care Assistant (24/7) Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £25,249 pro rata Reference PP51363 Category Assistant staff Date published 9 October 2026 Closing date 29 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/accounts-assistant-cashier-temporary-cover-ag51340": {
      "title": "Accounts Assistant, Cashier [Temporary Cover]",
      "description": "Accounts Assistant, Cashier [Temporary Cover] | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Accounts Assistant, Cashier [Temporary Cover] Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Accounts Assistant, Cashier [Temporary Cover] Applications are invited for the position of Accounts Assistant, Cashier within the Accounting Services Team, based in the Finance Division. The Finance Division manages the financial resources of the University and the support services for the financial functions carried out by the University's departments. This role is part of the Accounting Services section of the Finance Division which performs a range of key strategic and operational accounting and administrative duties. If you are passionate about Finance, keen to develop your skills and qualifications, and looking for an exciting opportunity to deliver a high-quality service, then this is the role for you. The Role The Cashier provides financial / specialist advice to departments and manages all University currency International Payments. The role holder reviews and inputs all international payments, which are made to meet supplier contractual terms, for an average annual payments of £36m covering 11,000 payments.  The role holder carries out half yearly and annual reviews of the accuracy of the University wide Petty cash/ Float control accounts of around £24,000 across 100 departments. The role holder provides cover for the Cashier/Payables Co-ordinator during holiday and sickness periods for any queries and issues related to international payments. The role holder also manages their own regular BACS runs weekly and provides cover for the main BACS payment administrator. The role holder will be responsible for making payments to meet supplier contractual terms for an average annual payment of £650m covering 360k invoices. Payees include Suppliers, Contractors, Students, Employees and Foreign visitors to the University. As such, the role holder will have contact with a wide range of people / organisations, enquiring about the status of payments. Prompt and efficient payments are important to the smooth running of the University and form part of its credibility. Your Skills The successful candidate will be expected to have excellent communication skills and be able to relay information to a wide range of people at all levels. They will also have good organisational skills with a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. Knowledge of standard Microsoft packages, with a particular knowledge of Excel is essential. Experience of working with the Cambridge University Financial System (CUFS) would be highly desirable, however previous experience of working in an accounts environment is essential. Benefits The University offers an excellent benefit package including: 36 days annual leave, inclusive of Bank Holidays Defined benefits pension schemes Flexible working options Family-friendly initiatives Career development opportunities Support for health and mental wellbeing Discounts on shopping Location The University is supportive of hybrid working, where some work is undertaken on University premises and some in a remote working environment. The aim of our approach is to enable as many staff as possible to work in a hybrid way if they wish and where their role allows. This arrangement is subject to change and will not form a part of the contractual terms of the role. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check, a health assessment and a security check. If you would like more information about this role, informal enquiries can be directed by email to Stuart Emmerson on Stuart.Emmerson@admin.cam.ac.uk . If you have any questions the application process, please contact a Recruitment Administrator at Finhr_recruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is 25 October 2026. The interview date for the role is 2 November 2026. Please quote reference AG51340 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Key information Department/location Finance Division Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference AG51340 Category Assistant staff Date published 12 October 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/aicam-programme-manager-xn51107": {
      "title": "AI@Cam Programme Manager",
      "description": "AI@Cam Programme Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs AI@Cam Programme Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs AI@Cam Programme Manager Drive AI innovation that serves science, citizens, and society at Cambridge About this opportunity ai@cam has rapidly established itself as Cambridge's flagship mission for AI innovation with real-world impact. In just two years, we've engaged over 3,000 people across all six Schools of the University and in our local community, launched seven interdisciplinary research programmes spanning 26 departments, trained more than 1,500 researchers in AI methods, and positioned Cambridge as a trusted voice in national AI policy discussions.\nWe're looking for a Programme Manager to join our dynamic team to help drive our next chapter of growth. What you'll be working on Programme delivery and coordination: Support the delivery of our core programmes including the AI-deas incubator, the AI for University Operations and Teaching programme, and our Policy Lab activities that engage with government departments and local authorities. Community building and stakeholder engagement: Help coordinate our vibrant community of researchers, policymakers, and practitioners. Support events, workshops, and engagement activities that bring together diverse perspectives on AI development and deployment. Partnership development: Work with the team to develop and maintain relationships with internal and external partners including local authorities, industry collaborators, and international research networks. Strategic coordination: Contribute to planning and programme management across ai@cam's portfolio of internal and external engagements, helping to position the University at the forefront of responsible AI innovation. Programme management and impact: Develop our infrastructure for programme management and tracking impact. Implement financial management processes across programmes, monitor budgets and expenditure, and ensure compliance with University policies and funder requirements. Support procurement activities and contract management. What we're looking for We're seeking someone who thrives in a dynamic, innovative environment and is excited by the opportunity to shape how AI development can better serve society. You'll have: Programme management experience: Demonstrated ability to coordinate complex, multi-stakeholder programmes, working in conditions of uncertainty. Stakeholder engagement skills: Experience building relationships and facilitating collaboration between diverse groups including researchers, policymakers, industry partners, and communities. Strategic delivery: Ability to see the bigger picture while managing day-to-day delivery. Communication excellence: Strong written and verbal communication skills. Adaptability: Comfort working in a dynamic environment where priorities can shift as opportunities emerge. Project coordination: Experience managing competing priorities and supporting teams to deliver on ambitious timelines. We offer flexible working arrangements, with regular attendance in Cambridge necessary for effective collaboration with our team and partners. This is an excellent opportunity to contribute to AI innovation while developing experience in programme management, policy engagement, and partnership development. Limited funding: The funds for this post are available until 31 July 2028 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Candidates should apply with a cover letter setting out how their experience relates to the requirements of the role (with examples using the STAR method), a CV, and details of two referees. Informal enquiries about the role should be directed to Jessica Montgomery, ai@cam Executive Director, email: jkm40@cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Karin Kuchta, HR Coordinator, email: csihhr@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference XN51107 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information XN51107 - Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cross School Initiative Hub Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference XN51107 Category Academic-related Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/assistant-professor-programme-lead-in-autism-and-early-development-rn51294": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Programme Lead in Autism and Early Development)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Programme Lead in Autism and Early Development) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Assistant Professor (Programme Lead in Autism and Early Development) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Assistant Professor (Programme Lead in Autism and Early Development) The newly created K. Lisa Yang (KLY) Centre for Autism Research at Cambridge seeks to appoint an outstanding Assistant Research Professor (Grade 9) to lead the Autism and Early Development Research Programme within the KLY Centre. This is an exceptional opportunity for an ambitious researcher to establish and develop an internationally recognised programme of research focused on autism and early development, investigating how prenatal, perinatal, genetic, biological and environmental factors contribute to autism and related neurodevelopmental outcomes. Working across large-scale population registers and birth cohorts in the UK, Sweden, Israel, Norway and Denmark, the postholder will help integrate genetic, clinical, epidemiological, biomarker and early developmental data to develop innovative autism likelihood scores that can support earlier identification and intervention. The programme combines cutting-edge population health research, machine learning, developmental neuroscience and translational science, with the ultimate goal of improving the early detection of autism and ensuring that children and families can access assessment, diagnosis and support at the earliest possible stage. The successful candidate will work within a highly collaborative international network and contribute to research with both fundamental scientific importance and clear potential for real-world clinical impact. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to shape the scientific direction of the programme and develop innovative lines of enquiry aligned with the strategic goals of the KLY Centre. The successful candidate will provide scientific leadership for the Autism and Early Development Research Programme and contribute to the strategic development of the KLY Centre. As part of the leadership role, they will supervise postdoctoral researchers, develop collaborations, and lead grant development. Eligible applicants should have (1) a PhD in Psychology, Psychiatry, Neuroscience, Child Development, Public Health, Epidemiology, or a closely related discipline; (2) have a strong track record of internationally recognised research in prenatal endocrinology and perinatal neurodevelopment; (3) an excellent publication record commensurate with career stage; (4) evidence of obtaining competitive external research funding; (5) strong quantitative, methodological and analytical skills. Experience in leading longitudinal, cohort, clinical, developmental or population-based studies is desirable. The K. Lisa Yang Centre for Autism Research at Cambridge is part of the global Yang Tan Collective, alongside sister centres at Harvard and MIT. The Centre is closely linked to the Autism Research Centre (ARC) in Cambridge (see www.autismresearchcentre.com ) and contributes to a collaborative international effort to accelerate discovery and translation in autism research. For further details please read the further particulars. Applicants should submit a CV including a full list of publications, a brief cover letter explaining their suitability for the role, a research vision statement (maximum three pages) outlining their plans for the development of the Autism and Early Development Research Programme over the next five years (which should include details on scientific priorities, funding strategy, collaborations and community engagement translation and impact), and contact details for two academic referees. For more information, email Professor Simon Baron-Cohen, Director of the ARC and the KLY Centre, on sb205@cam.ac.uk Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 5 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. The closing date for applications is: 18th October 2026\nThe interview date for the role is: To be confirmed Please quote reference RN51294 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Psychiatry Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference RN51294 Category Research Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
