      "title": "Assistant Professor in Islamic Philosophy",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Islamic Philosophy | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Assistant Professor in Islamic Philosophy Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Assistant Professor in Islamic Philosophy The Faculty of Divinity wishes to appoint to an Assistant Professorship in Islamic Philosophy from 1 September 2027. The postholder will be based in the Faculty of Divinity, West Road, Cambridge. Applicants should have a doctorate in Islamic Philosophy and either a proven track record of, or demonstrable potential for high quality research and publications in any aspect of Medieval Islamic Philosophy. The successful candidate will be required to teach on the Undergraduate Tripos, Advanced Diploma and the MPhil, as well as to supervise doctoral students and to undertake administrative duties within the Faculty. The person appointed will be expected to deliver around 40-48 lecture-room contact hours of teaching across the three terms of the academic year.  This will involve a substantial contribution to teaching in part II of the 'Tripos' (i.e. the second and third years of the BA Hons programme), including some teaching in Qur'anic Arabic, and at least one module for the MPhil. Together with colleagues in the Religious Studies section of the Faculty, the appointee will share responsibility for designing and delivering the Faculty's teaching in the field of Islamic Studies. The successful applicant will also be expected to undertake College teaching for additional remuneration. Applicants need to upload the following documents on that system: Your Curriculum Vitae (CV) with a list of publications A covering letter A statement of research plans of no more than two sides of A4 An outline of a course of 8 lectures introducing second-year undergraduates to key themes in medieval Islamic philosophy or Philosophical theology (no more than 4 sides A4) A sample of published or completed research (journal article, chapter from book or dissertation) of no more than around 8000 words The online system will ask for the names and contact details of 3 referees. References will be taken up only after shortlisting. Informal enquiries about the post may be made to Professor Richard Rex, Chair of the Faculty Board, rawr1@cam.ac.uk . For questions on the application process, please contact the School's HR team on sahhr@admin.cam.ac.uk The closing date is midnight (BST) on Friday 16 October 2026.  It is expected that interviews will be held in late January or early February 2027. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference GF50963 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and we particularly welcome applications from candidates from a Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic background for this vacancy, as people from these backgrounds are currently underrepresented at this level in our Faculty. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Faculty of Divinity Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference GF50963 Category Academic Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/assistant-professor-in-modern-chinese-history-gu51261": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Modern Chinese History",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Modern Chinese History | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Assistant Professor in Modern Chinese History Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Assistant Professor in Modern Chinese History The Department of East Asian Studies, within the Faculty of Asian and Middle Eastern Studies, wishes to appoint to an Assistant Professorship in Modern Chinese History from 1 October 2027. The successful candidate will hold a PhD (or will do so by the start of the contract) in modern Chinese history and have a proven track record of, or clear potential or high-quality research and publications in any aspect of the history of China during the 20th and 21st Centuries. The post-holder will be required to teach undergraduate and postgraduate courses as well as to supervise doctoral students. They are also expected to offer supervisions (small-scale tutorials). They will engage with all aspects of the academic life, including teaching, research and administration duties within the Department and Faculty. A commitment to teaching and working with Chinese language sources, as well as fluency in Mandarin, is a prerequisite. More information about the role and the person specifications is available in the Further Particulars attached. For your application to be considered, please submit the following: Cover Letter Curriculum vitae, including a list of publications Research Statement (3 pages max.) Teaching Statement (2 pages max.) Shortlisted candidates will be asked for samples of written work in due course. Please also provide the names and contact details of three referees who are familiar with your work. References will be taken up only after shortlisting. The closing date for applications is midnight (GMT) on Tuesday 15 December 2026. We expect to hold campus visits and interviews with shortlisted candidates on 3 and 4 March 2027, subject to change. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For questions about this vacancy please contact Prof. Roel Sterckx ( rs10009@cam.ac.uk ) and for questions about the application process, please contact the Deputy Faculty Manager Deputy@ames.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference GU51261 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Faculty of Asian and Middle Eastern Studies Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference GU51261 Category Academic Date published 12 October 2026 Closing date 15 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/assistant-research-professor-software-engineer-fixed-term-sw51275": {
      "title": "Assistant Research Professor (Software Engineer) (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Assistant Research Professor (Software Engineer) (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Assistant Research Professor (Software Engineer) (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Assistant Research Professor (Software Engineer) (Fixed Term) Join us at the world-leading CRUK Cambridge Institute. We're a unique department of the University of Cambridge, core funded by Cancer Research UK's charitable activities, and we're eagerly searching for an Assistant Research Professor with a background in software engineering to join us in a unique role. Embedded in the Institute's Markowetz research group, the Assistant Research Professor role will lead critical software engineering research projects in support of the Institute's AI research strategy, and as part of the wider cross-Institutional CRUK AI Alliance. The CRUK AI Alliance will link all four CRUK institutes and partners through shared infrastructure, data and expertise. The Alliance will build foundation models spanning genotype, molecular regulatory networks and tumour-organ interactions, factoring in age, sex and environmental exposure. These models will let federated teams study disease processes that can't be observed directly, such as pre-cancerous lesions, dormant residual disease and tumour-organ interactions. The Alliance will pursue questions such as: What drives cancer initiation, and how can it be intercepted to prevent disease? How can we infer patient states that can't currently be measured? How do we deliver precision healthcare? By pairing shared AI infrastructure with question-focused workstreams, the Alliance aims to make the UK cancer research ecosystem more cohesive and powerful, enabling approaches beyond the reach of current methods. Principal responsibilities will include: Evaluate new and existing AI tools and software for relevance and fit to the Institute's research needs. Build, adapt and integrate selected AI tools into the Institute's existing systems and workflows. Test and validate AI tool outputs against the standards required for research use. Communicate the capabilities and limitations of AI tools clearly to non-specialist researchers. Guide the Institute in adopting good practice around AI tool use and software development and provide training on using adopted or built tools effectively. Collaborate with other groups across the Institute and the CRUK AI Alliance to share evaluations and avoid duplicated effort. Liaise with external groups and developers to inform evaluation and adoption decisions. Follow good software engineering practice (version control, testing, documentation) when building or adapting tools. Assess the trade-offs (cost, maintainability, data security) between adopting third-party tools and building bespoke solutions. Report on the progress and outcomes of evaluations and integrations to the Institute and wider CRUK AI Alliance. Maintain and troubleshoot integrated tools, and keep up to date with a fast-moving AI/software landscape. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 years in the first instance. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference SW51133 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Assistant Research Professor Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference SW51275 Category Research Date published 2 October 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/assistantassociate-professor-in-pathogen-genetics-pc51175": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Pathogen Genetics",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Pathogen Genetics | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Assistant/Associate Professor in Pathogen Genetics Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Assistant/Associate Professor in Pathogen Genetics Applications are invited for an Assistant or Associate Professorship in Pathogen Genetics to be held in the Department of Genetics. We are searching for a talented individual with a track record of fundamental or applied research on the genetics of pathogens or host-pathogen interactions and a strong commitment to teaching. The post is available from 1st October 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. We welcome applications from researchers in the broad field of pathogen biology, whose research addresses fundamental questions at any level of biological organisation, including molecular and cellular mechanisms of infection, immunity and resistance, dynamics within hosts and populations, and evolution and population genetics. Research can focus on any pathogen or host, including humans, other animals and plants. We are particularly interested in candidates who use genetic and genomic approaches, whether experimental, computational or theoretical. The successful applicant will have a PhD in a relevant subject area and show evidence of research excellence that is reflected in their published work (including pre-prints), as well as the vision and ability to attract grant funding to support an internationally leading research programme (including a track record commensurate with experience). Applicants will also have an aptitude and enthusiasm for teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels and will be expected to contribute to the teaching and other academic activities in the Department. The successful applicant will be motivated to develop innovative teaching in the School of Biological Sciences, across areas such as genetics, pathogen biology, mathematical biology or evolution. The Department offers an outstanding research and teaching environment with considerable strengths in pathogen biology, genome biology, evolution and population genetics, and computational and mathematical biology. The Department houses the Pathogen Dynamics Unit (PDU) https://www.pdu.gen.cam.ac.uk , a research grouping applying epidemiology, disease ecology, and traditional microbiology to real-world problems in human infectious disease through genomics, laboratory experimentation and mathematical modelling. We particularly welcome applications from individuals with impactful research programmes focused on computational and/or experimental approaches to pathogen biology that synergise with ongoing research in the PDU and the wider infection biology community across the School of Biological Sciences. Across the Department and School of Biological Sciences we will provide laboratory and office space, and access to containment facilities, imaging, genomics and high-performance computing. The starting salary is dependent on the level of appointment being made, as determined by the Selection Committee. Assistant Professorships are in the range of £47,389 - £59,966 per annum; Associate Professorships are in the range of £63,606 - £67,468 per annum. The Department is committed to the Athena SWAN Charter to provide equal opportunities and to advance the representation of women and other under-represented groups in science. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Appointment will be based on merit alone. As a signatory of DORA ( https://sfdora.org ) the Department is committed to assessing research on its own merits not on the basis of the journal in which it is published. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that in the Upload section of the online application you provide: A document containing your Curriculum Vitae (CV), research publications list, and a brief narrative (300 words maximum per output) on each of the three outputs you consider most significant. The Department is committed to the responsible use of bibliometrics and applicants are asked not to include uncontextualized citation counts, Journal Impact Factors or H-index metrics in their applications. A statement of research of no more than two pages, outlining your current and future research plans, with an emphasis on how these will address fundamental questions in pathogen biology and how they will complement and extend research in the Department and across Cambridge. A one-page statement focusing on (i) your teaching experience and interests, (ii) your contributions to and/or vision for open research and improving research culture, equality, diversity and inclusion. Prospective candidates are invited to contact Professor Steve Russell for further information sr120@cam.ac.uk The closing date for applications is Friday 27th November 2026 at midnight. Interviews for shortlisted candidates are planned for January 2027. Please quote reference PC51175 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Genetics Salary £47,389 - £59,966 or £63,606 - £67,468 Reference PC51175 Category Academic Date published 24 September 2026 Closing date 27 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/astrazeneca-non-clinical-phd-studentship-in-development-of-an-integrated-pre-clinical-pipeline-for-rq51135": {
      "title": "AstraZeneca Non-Clinical PhD Studentship in Development of an Integrated Pre-Clinical Pipeline for Safety Assessment of Small Molecule and Peptide-Based Radioconjugates",
      "description": "AstraZeneca Non-Clinical PhD Studentship in Development of an Integrated Pre-Clinical Pipeline for Safety Assessment of Small Molecule and Peptide-Based Radioconjugates | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs AstraZeneca Non-Clinical PhD Studentship in Development of an Integrated Pre-Clinical Pipeline for Safety Assessment of Small Molecule and Peptide-Based Radioconjugates Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs AstraZeneca Non-Clinical PhD Studentship in Development of an Integrated Pre-Clinical Pipeline for Safety Assessment of Small Molecule and Peptide-Based Radioconjugates Applications are invited for an AstraZeneca Non-Clinical PhD Studentship in Development of an Integrated Pre-Clinical Pipeline for Safety Assessment of Small Molecule and Peptide-Based Radioconjugates Using In Vivo PET Imaging and Multimodal Ex Vivo Tissue Analysis. The Department of Radiology at the University of Cambridge, in partnership with AstraZeneca, is offering a funded PhD studentship to develop innovative approaches for the safety assessment of next-generation radiopharmaceuticals. We are seeking a motivated, enthusiastic and inquisitive student with an interest in molecular imaging, radiopharmaceutical sciences, oncology, translational medicine and drug development. The successful candidate will join a highly multidisciplinary team, supervised by Professor Luigi Aloj (Department of Radiology), Dr Flaviu Bulat (Department of Radiology), Dr Nisha Kuzhuppilly Ramakrishnan (Department of Clinical Neurosciences), and Dr Andreas Dannhorn (AstraZeneca), and gain experience working across both academic and industrial research environments. For the full details of this studentship, please see the Student Funding Portal here Project Aims: Targeted radionuclide therapy is an emerging and highly promising approach for cancer treatment. Radioconjugates combine tumour-targeting molecules with therapeutic radioisotopes, enabling the selective delivery of radiation to cancer cells while minimising damage to healthy tissue. Despite recent clinical successes, there remains a significant need for improved methods to evaluate how these compounds distribute throughout the body and to better understand potential toxicities in normal organs. This project aims to establish an integrated preclinical framework for the assessment of small molecule and peptide-based radioconjugates using advanced molecular imaging and tissue analysis techniques. Additional details about the project can be found on the Student Funding Portal here - https://www.student-funding.cam.ac.uk/fund/development-of-an-integrated-pre-clinical-pipeline-for-safety-assessment-of-small-molecule-and-peptide-based-radioconjugates-using-in-vivo-pet-imaging-and-multimodal-ex-vivo-tissue-analysis-2026 Candidate: We are seeking a highly motivated and enthusiastic individual capable of thinking and working independently within a multidisciplinary research environment. Applicants should have or shortly expect to obtain a first or high 2:1 bachelor's degree from a UK university, or an equivalent standard from an overseas university, in a relevant subject such as imaging, biology, biochemistry, chemistry, biomedical sciences, pharmaceutical sciences, data analysis, or medicine. Funding: The funding for this post is available for 4 years. Funding will cover the student's stipend and tuition fees at the Home rate. This studentship is also open to overseas students who meet the UK residency requirements (home fees) or who can cover the extra costs associated with international student fees through scholarships or funding schemes. Students may not supplement fees via self funding. How to Apply: The deadline for applications is Friday 30th October 2026. Applications should be made via the University Application portal. When asked to select a course, please apply for the PhD in Radiology. You should then put the title of this studentship as your proposed project title. You are not required to write your own research proposal. When asked for a research proposal in the applicant portal, instead please upload one side of A4 with your motivation for applying for the studentship, your relevant skills, what you hope to achieve if appointed, and long-term career aspirations. You do not need to secure a supervisor before applying for this studentship. Informal enquiries about the project are welcome and should be directed to Professor Luigi Aloj: la398@medschl.cam.ac.uk For application process queries, please contact clusterPG@medschl.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference RQ51135 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Radiology Salary Reference RQ51135 Category Studentships Date published 30 September 2026 Closing date 30 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/astrazeneca-non-clinical-phd-studentship-in-next-generation-tpd-for-oncology-harnessing-ubiquitin-pl51373": {
      "title": "AstraZeneca Non-Clinical PhD Studentship in Next-generation TPD for oncology: Harnessing ubiquitin-independent pathways",
      "description": "AstraZeneca Non-Clinical PhD Studentship in Next-generation TPD for oncology: Harnessing ubiquitin-independent pathways | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs AstraZeneca Non-Clinical PhD Studentship in Next-generation TPD for oncology: Harnessing ubiquitin-independent pathways Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs AstraZeneca Non-Clinical PhD Studentship in Next-generation TPD for oncology: Harnessing ubiquitin-independent pathways The Department of Pharmacology at the University of Cambridge, in partnership with AstraZeneca, is offering a funded PhD studentship to study proteasome-targeting tools and use them to design novel approaches for Targeted Protein Degradation (TPD). We are seeking a motivated and curiosity-driven student with a strong interest in cell biology and biochemistry, as well as an enthusiasm for TPD approaches. The successful candidate will join the lab of Dr Catherine Lindon in the Department of Pharmacology, with a supervisory team that includes Dr Gustavo Gutierrez (Discovery Sciences, AstraZeneca), Professor Laura Itzhaki (Department of Pharmacology), Dr Sharan Bagal (Oncology Chemistry, AstraZeneca) and Dr David McGarry (Oncology Bioscience, AstraZeneca). Project Aims: The project aims to establish a novel TPD platform by co-opting Midnolin, a protein capable of delivering substrates directly to the proteasome, for ubiquitin-independent degradation. The research programme will include an exploration of Midnolin biology, to understand its endogenous regulation across cellular time and space, and the development of novel tools to direct Midnolin activity towards desirable targets linked to therapeutic outcomes. This part of the project will include a campaign to discover binders that can harness the cell's endogenous Midnolin pool, and engineering of custom exogenous Midnolin-based degrader tools, for directed degradation of oncology targets. We are seeking a highly motivated individual able to work independently, and with good knowledge and/or experience in both biochemistry and cell biology. Applicants should have or shortly expect to obtain a first or high 2:1 bachelor's degree from a UK university, or an equivalent standard from an overseas university, in a relevant subject. Funding: The funding for this post is available for 4 years. Funding will cover the student's stipend and tuition fees at the Home rate. This studentship is also open to overseas students who meet the UK residency requirements (home fees) or who can cover the extra costs associated with international student fees through scholarships or funding schemes. Students may not supplement fees via self-funding. How to Apply: The deadline for applications is Monday 16th November 2026. Applications should be made via the University Application portal. When asked to select a course, please apply for the PhD in Pharmacology. You should then put the title of this studentship as your proposed project title. You are not required to write your own research proposal. When asked for a research proposal in the applicant portal, instead please upload one side of A4 with your motivation for applying for the studentship, your relevant skills, what you hope to achieve if appointed, and long-term career aspirations. Informal enquiries about the project are welcome and should be directed to Dr Catherine Lindon: acl34@cam.ac.uk For application process queries, please contact the Postgraduate Admissions Team https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/contact-us Please quote reference PL51373 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Pharmacology Salary Reference PL51373 Category Studentships Date published 12 October 2026 Closing date 16 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/az-funded-non-clinical-phd-studentship-fixed-term-in-selective-nanoparticle-targeting-of-senescent-sa51375": {
      "title": "AZ funded Non-clinical PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) in selective nanoparticle targeting of senescent alveolar epithelium using an alveolar organoid model",
      "description": "AZ funded Non-clinical PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) in selective nanoparticle targeting of senescent alveolar epithelium using an alveolar organoid model | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs AZ funded Non-clinical PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) in selective nanoparticle targeting of senescent alveolar epithelium using an alveolar organoid model Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs AZ funded Non-clinical PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) in selective nanoparticle targeting of senescent alveolar epithelium using an alveolar organoid model AZ funded Non-clinical PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) in selective nanoparticle targeting of senescent alveolar epithelium using an alveolar organoid model Applications are invited for 4-year PhD studentship based in Cambridge Institute of Medical Research (CIMR), University of Cambridge and the new AstraZeneca Discovery Centre at Cambridge. The student will be working on a collaborative project jointly supervised by Dr Jennifer Dickens in CIMR and Dr James Garnett and Dr Catherine Overed-Sayer at AstraZeneca and will work have opportunity to work across the two sites. Project Overview Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) is a progressive, fatal interstitial lung disease (ILD) in which alveolar type 2 (AT2) cell epithelial dysfunction plays a key initiating role. Epithelial senescence is increasingly recognised as playing a role in triggering and perpetuating disease. This project aims to develop a platform in which to assess and optimise the delivery of nanoparticle approaches to senomodulatory therapeutics in the lung. Senescent-cell targeting nanoparticles will be engineered and loaded with exemplar senomodulatory drugs in collaboration with Prof. Ljiljana Fruk (Department of Chemical Engineering and Biotechnology) and delivered to AT2 cells, using human alveolar organoids as the primary in vitro model. Working with the AstraZeneca team, drug selection will be optimised and an automated screening platform developed to undertake proof-of-concept studies to assess the efficacy and functional effects of nanoparticle-mediated senolytic delivery to AT2 cells. Candidate We are looking for a highly motivated and enthusiastic individual capable of thinking and working independently. Applicants should have or shortly expect to obtain a first or upper second-class degree from a UK university, or an equivalent standard from an overseas university, in a relevant subject which will equip them for cell biology research. These positions are open to UK citizens or overseas students who meet the UK residency requirements (home fees) or are able to augment the funds to cover the extra costs associated with international student fees through scholarships or funding schemes. Students will not be allowed to supplement fees via self-funding. Full details of the University's entrance requirements and scholarships are specified on the following link: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/ Funding Full funding covering Maintenance fees at £21,500 per annum and the University Composition Fee is provided for the studentship, with effect from 1 October 2027. Student Support and Training As a graduate student at Cambridge, you will have access to a wide range of training opportunities and benefit from close supervision provided by a primary and secondary PhD supervisor as well as a personal mentor. All students are expected to attend all internal and external seminars held within CIMR. Students will also be encouraged to attend and present at the annual AstraZeneca students symposium. Students are encouraged to attend the wide variety of lectures and training courses available to them across the Institute and wider University. This includes a centrally run Statistics course and the University Core Skills Training Programme, which includes sessions on Time Management, Presentation and Performance and Scientific Writing. Students at the Institute will be members of the University's Postgraduate School of Life Sciences (PSLS) who offer a wide variety of core skills and professional development training. Visit the Researcher Development page on the PSLS website for more information. In addition, the student can also take advantage of training courses and seminars in therapeutic sciences offered by Cambridge Academy of Therapeutic Sciences. Application Process Applications should be made through the University Application Portal https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvimpdmsc/apply This includes details of the required documents for the application. Informal enquiries are encouraged before applications are made and should be directed to Dr Dickens ( jac72@cam.ac.uk ). Deadline The deadline for applications is 8th December 2026 . Interviews will take place in December 2026 or January 2027, to be confirmed. Please quote reference SA51375 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. Diversity and Inclusion The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. We place major emphasis on the importance of teamwork and an enjoyable work environment as a foundation for performing internationally leading research. This will allow the student to acquire cutting edge research methodologies in a supportive environment, where they can focus on undertaking the best possible scientific programme The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location C.I.M.R. Administration Salary Reference SA51375 Category Studentships Date published 12 October 2026 Closing date 8 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/bhf-multidisciplinary-doctoral-training-programme-fixed-term-rc51350": {
      "title": "BHF Multidisciplinary Doctoral Training Programme (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "BHF Multidisciplinary Doctoral Training Programme (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs BHF Multidisciplinary Doctoral Training Programme (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs BHF Multidisciplinary Doctoral Training Programme (Fixed Term) Secondary Prevention of Vascular Dysfunction and Disease University of Cambridge, in collaboration with the University of Oxford Start date: October 2027 Fully funded studentships Applications are invited for the second intake of the new British Heart Foundation (BHF) 4-Year Multidisciplinary Doctoral Training Programme that brings together researchers from the Universities of Oxford and Cambridge, with a focus on Secondary Prevention of Vascular Dysfunction and Disease. This flagship programme will train the next generation of cardiovascular scientists to develop innovative, cross-disciplinary approaches to halt, stabilise and reverse vascular disease in high-risk patients. Students will be admitted to either the University of Oxford (up to 8 studentships) or the University of Cambridge (2 studentships) and will benefit from co-supervision, training, collaboration and access to world-class research infrastructure across partner institutions. Instructions for applying to Cambridge are given below. Vascular diseases, including coronary artery disease, stroke and aortic aneurysm, remain leading causes of death and disability worldwide. While acute care has advanced significantly, therapeutic innovation aimed at preventing disease progression and reversing established pathology has stagnated. We will identify new targets for secondary prevention and disease regression, ultimately reducing cardiovascular risk in vulnerable populations. Recent transformative technological advances, including multi-omics and AI-enhanced imaging, provide unprecedented new opportunities to gain novel mechanistic insights and develop urgently needed treatments. In a truly multidisciplinary programme , students will work across four interconnected research themes: Defining disease - Deep phenotyping, imaging and patient stratification Elaborating mechanisms and biomarkers - Multi-omics and genetic discovery Modulating cell death and inflammation - Systems immunology and therapeutic targeting Maintaining vascular stability - Cell biology, biomechanics and regenerative approaches The programme leverages world-leading infrastructure including BHF Centres of Research Excellence at both institutions, large-scale clinical datasets (e.g. UK Biobank), advanced imaging platforms, spatial transcriptomics hubs, and cutting-edge immunophenotyping facilities. This structured 4-year doctoral training programme is designed to provide a level of integration and breadth beyond that of a traditional PhD. To establish a firm foundation for doctoral research, in year 1, students will undertake core research and bespoke specialist training, as well as two 12-week laboratory rotations in complementary disciplines, before progressing to a three-year main research project. Cross-institutional symposia and cohort-building activities will feature in all four years, and there will be opportunities to interact with pharmaceutical and biotechnology partners, and to undertake training in entrepreneurship, innovation and commercial translation. The programme aims to produce graduates equipped for careers in academia, industry, biotechnology, healthcare, policy and beyond. Each fully funded 4-year studentship includes: -A tax-free stipend at the standard BHF rate (currently £21,747/year; rising over the 4 years) -University tuition fees at the home rate -Consumables budget -Travel allowance for conferences -Additional funds to visit partners within the programme Studentships will fully fund those who meet the UK residency requirements (home fees). International students who are able to meet the additional costs of overseas tuition fees through other scholarships or funding schemes will also be considered. Students must commence in October 2027. Eligibility Applicants should hold, or expect to obtain, a first or upper second-class undergraduate degree (or equivalent) in a relevant discipline such as Biochemistry, Biomedical sciences, Bioengineering or related life science subject. We particularly encourage applications from candidates with interdisciplinary interests and from groups underrepresented in cardiovascular research. Equality, Diversity and Inclusion We are committed to fostering an inclusive research culture aligned with the BHF \"Igniting Change\" strategy. Recruitment will be conducted transparently and equitably. We actively encourage applications from candidates of all backgrounds. Application Process Applicants should submit: -CV -Academic transcripts -Covering letter/personal statement (<1000 words), outlining research interests and motivation. -Two academic references -Evidence of competence in English (if required) Applications should be submitted to Cambridge via: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvmdpdecr Selected applicants will be invited to interview in early January 2027. Application deadline: 8th Dec 2026 For further information Please contact:\nCambridge - pgmed@medschl.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference RC51350 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Medicine Salary Reference RC51350 Category Studentships Date published 8 October 2026 Closing date 8 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/building-services-technician-ps51301": {
      "title": "Building Services Technician",
      "description": "Building Services Technician | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Building Services Technician Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Building Services Technician Applications are open for a permanent full-time Building Service Technician role based in the Jeffrey Cheah Biomedical Centre (JCBC) - a state-of-the-art research building located on the Biomedical Campus which opened in 2019 and houses up to 900 staff in three separate research Institutes. This is an exciting opportunity to join the Building Services Team, supporting the efficient and effective functioning of the JCBC and the research groups based here. This is a dynamic role and it is expected that the post holder will work primarily on glasswash and media preparation, as well as any ad hoc duties as they arise. We are looking for motivated individuals with a can-do approach who is keen to meet the varied workload. Key duties and responsibilities include: assisting in cleaning and distribution of glassware assisting in media preparation and autoclaving distribution and collection of parcels and incoming mail collection of hazardous waste from labs and collection points filling liquid nitrogen vessels and changing gas cylinders water safety maintenance duties assisting the senior Building Service Technicians with minor maintenance tasks assisting with fire testing procedures providing furniture and managing room layouts for events. The successful candidates will need to demonstrate good organisational and prioritisation skills, excellent levels of communication and interpersonal skills, knowledge of laboratory environments and associated hazards and IT competence. The post holder may be required to attend training sessions associated with tasks relevant to this role. Previous experience in a similar role would be advantageous. The post is permanent and for 36.5 hours per week. For queries regarding this post, please contact Vasileios Kaimakis (JCBC Building Services Manager) email: vk286@cam.ac.uk . Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference PS51301 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars (PS51301) Key information Department/location Cambridge Stem Cell Institute (SBS) Salary £25,249-£27,319 pro rata Reference PS51301 Category Assistant staff Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 19 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/business-and-operations-manager-rn51269": {
      "title": "Business and Operations Manager",
      "description": "Business and Operations Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Business and Operations Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Business and Operations Manager We are seeking an experienced and highly motivated Business and Operations Manager to provide senior strategic and operational leadership for the newly integrated professional service teams for the Department of Psychiatry and Cambridge Mind and Brain Institute (CMBI). This is a pivotal role, working closely with the Heads of Institutions to shape and deliver the strategic aims of a vibrant academic community of approximately 220 staff and 175 PhD and MPhil students. You will act as professional service manager for the Institutions, overseeing the newly integrated professional services teams, overseeing governance, business planning and day-to-day operations in support of world-leading teaching and research, and taking responsibility for the management of income generated through grants, student fees, donations and investments of approximately £20m per year. About the role As Business and Operations Manager, you will advise the Heads of Institutions on strategy and support the planning and implementation of new developments. You will lead and manage an integrated professional services team, ensuring the Institutions' business, administration and services are delivered to a high standard and are geared towards continuous improvement. Key responsibilities include: Collaborating with and advising the Heads of Institutions on strategic development and sustainable management of the Institutions. Ensuring effective and efficient governance, including servicing or chairing Institutions Committees. Leading and managing the integrated professional services team supporting research, teaching and administration. Managing financial resources and business planning, including oversight of research grants and other income of approximately £20m. Leading change and business improvement projects, and overseeing internal and external communications. Ensuring the effective management of the Institutions' sites, buildings and facilities, including health, safety and regulatory compliance. About you You will be a senior professional services manager with demonstrable experience of managing large teams and delivering strategic planning and change at a tactical and operational level, together with a track record of supporting and developing staff. You will bring strong planning and relationship-building skills, and the ability to communicate persuasively and effectively with stakeholders at all levels of the organisation. Significant experience and knowledge of the UK Higher Education sector is desirable. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application.\nPlease include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing date: 25 October 2026 Interview date: 5 November 2026 Please quote reference RN51269 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Psychiatry Salary £63,606 - £67,468 Reference RN51269 Category Academic-related Date published 2 October 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/business-engagement-manager-physical-sciences-and-technology-ew49198": {
      "title": "Business Engagement Manager Physical Sciences and Technology",
      "description": "Business Engagement Manager Physical Sciences and Technology | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Business Engagement Manager Physical Sciences and Technology Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Business Engagement Manager Physical Sciences and Technology The University of Cambridge is seeking an exceptional relationship-builder and negotiator to help develop, manage and grow research and innovation partnerships between the University and industry, in the physical sciences disciplines. This is an exciting opportunity to join a newly established Business Engagement function within the central University's Research Office. You will play a role in shaping the new function and lead on strengthening collaboration between world-leading researchers in both the School of Physical Sciences and the School of Technology and industry partners. We are looking for someone who understands the power of strategic partnership management and has experience in both the academic and commercial worlds that will allow them to act as a bridge between different working cultures, priorities, and approaches to problem solving, and enhance the number and depth of research partnerships in these two schools. About the Role The University of Cambridge is internationally acclaimed for its contribution to physical sciences and technology research and innovation. The researchers supported by the Business Engagement function work on some of the world's most pressing challenges, and research collaborations with industry partners generates unique opportunities for powerful societal benefit. As Business Engagement Manager, you will lead on the Business Engagement function's support for the Schools of Physical Sciences and Technology, ensuring that activities are responsive to the needs of researchers in both schools. You will build close relationships with academic departments, senior research stakeholders and external partner organisations to support and enhance existing collaborative programmes, and you will work closely with researchers to initiate new partnership opportunities within target sectors. You will work collaboratively with colleagues in the research community, Cambridge University Development and Alumni Relations,  Cambridge Enterprise, and the University Research Office to coordinate support for opportunities and codevelop solutions to shared challenges. Your work will promote professionalism and best practice in partnership management across the physical sciences and lead to diversification of research funding streams. About you You will be comfortable engaging with senior stakeholders in both academia and industry, and have experience of developing, managing or supporting collaborative research partnerships at the interface of academia and industry. You will have education and training in a physical sciences discipline and may have experience of working in a relevant industry sector (e.g. quantum, AI/ML, semiconductors and advanced engineering, aerospace, deep tech, fintech, sustainability, advanced materials). You will have a good understanding of what it takes to bring together academic and corporate research teams, how to negotiate a contract, and how to deliver a programme of work independently. You will have a history of solving complex problems, managing complex stakeholder relationships or consortia, and influencing without authority; as well as a willingness to think outside the box and help others to do the same. Why join the team? This opportunity represents a unique chance to work alongside world leaders from across all disciplines of the physical sciences and play a role in some of the most impactful research and development partnerships in the world, with significant scope to influence how the University engages with industry and supports strategic research partnerships across the physical sciences. We are a supportive team in an excellent working environment within an exciting ecosystem. If this role appeals to you and is a great fit for your skills and experience, please get in touch. The department policy for hybrid working is 2 days/week in the office, but it is anticipated that to deliver well in this role 3+ days are likely to be necessary much of the time. It is important for candidates to be comfortable with that level of in-person attendance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. When applying, please upload your CV and cover letter which clearly sets out how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification (the knowledge, skills, and experience required for the role) along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted and invited to interview based upon these criteria. Interviews will be held in person in Cambridge.  There will be two rounds of interviews MS Teams interviews: 5th or 6th November 2026 In-person interviews: 20th or 23rd November 2026 Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Sarah Hull via Sarah.Hull@admin.cam.ac.uk Questions about the application process should be addressed to the Research Office Recruitment team at crorecruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference EW49198 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cambridge Research Office Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference EW49198 Category Academic-related Date published 21 September 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/cambridge-graduate-course-in-medicine-cgcm-tutor-part-time-2paweek-secondment-opportunity-ra51293": {
      "title": "Cambridge Graduate Course in Medicine (CGCM) Tutor (Part-time, 2PA/week) (Secondment Opportunity)",
      "description": "Cambridge Graduate Course in Medicine (CGCM) Tutor (Part-time, 2PA/week) (Secondment Opportunity) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Cambridge Graduate Course in Medicine (CGCM) Tutor (Part-time, 2PA/week) (Secondment Opportunity) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Cambridge Graduate Course in Medicine (CGCM) Tutor (Part-time, 2PA/week) (Secondment Opportunity) Applications are invited for this post which is to fill a recent vacancy. The Cambridge Graduate Course in Medicine Tutor will make a major contribution to the running of the Cambridge Graduate Course in Medicine and provide academic and pastoral student support.  A significant part of the role will be to be part of a team of two CGCM tutors delivering weekly 'Index Case' tutorials to year 1 and 2 CGCM students at St Edmunds College, Cambridge.  This case-base teaching is an integral component of the theoretical teaching of clinical medicine to CGCM Y1/2 students. The CGCM Tutor will be part of a team of eight CGCM Tutors and work in close partnership with the CGCM Director and Deputy Director, a General Practice CGCM Lead, and WSH Undergraduate Specialty Tutors.  Secretarial and other administrative support is provided by the CGCM team at the West Suffolk Hospital Education Centre. This is a three-year secondment, with the possibility of renewal for further periods of three years thereafter.  If the successful applicant is a GP, successful appointment would be on a fractional contract with the Cambridge School of Clinical Medicine. All candidates for the post should hold full GMC registration, a license to practice and substantive NHS appointment as a Senior Doctor (Consultant or Specialty Doctor) at West Suffolk Hospital or work as a General Practitioner in the Eastern region.  This post is not open to resident doctors.  The successful candidate's salary will be based on current experience level. The postholder will report to CGCM Director.  For further information, interested candidates should contact Dr Rachel Shute, CGCM Course Director, on rs879@medschl.cam.ac.uk . Fixed-term: 3 years. Closing Date: 18th October 2026 Interview Date: 9th November 2026 Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including e-mail address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RA51293 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Office of the School of Clinical Medicine Salary Clinical salary as per level of experience Reference RA51293 Category Academic Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/chief-animal-technician-aq51321": {
      "title": "Chief Animal Technician",
      "description": "Chief Animal Technician | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Chief Animal Technician Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Chief Animal Technician Applications are invited for a motivated and experienced Chief Animal Technician to be part of a hard working team in a busy University Biomedical Service facility situated in south Cambridge. As a member of a hard working team you will support the Biosciences Manager and Deputy Biofacility Manager with the daily operation of the barrier with a particular focus on providing technical support to researchers and technicians within the facility; undertaking regulated and non-regulated procedures as well as daily husbandry and animal welfare tasks. This will include the use of IVC technology, and maintenance of all necessary records. The role holder will also support the Biosciences Manager and Deputy Biofacility Managers in the daily coordination and supervision of technicians including daily supervision and oversight of the team carrying out daily care routines, training and assessment of new and existing staff, and supporting staff reviews. You will have experience working in a designated with laboratory animals and using IVC's and you should have a Home Office Personal Licence (Modules A - C) with up to date experience carrying out procedural work including regulated procedures. You will have good communication skills, able to work both independently and as part of a team, with experience supervising staff. You will have IAT Level 2 or equivalent (as a minimum), and be able to demonstrate in depth experience of relevant legislation, guidelines and animal technician work practices. You must be able to use a computerised data management system. The facility operates a rota system to provide animal care and essential husbandry at weekends and over Bank Holidays. In addition to the base salary stated above, the successful candidate for this post will receive an additional 2.5% supplement to their pay. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment and a security check. Please quote reference AQ51321 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference AQ51321 Category Assistant staff Date published 6 October 2026 Closing date 27 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/clinical-lecturer-in-gastroenterology-rc51286": {
      "title": "Clinical Lecturer in Gastroenterology",
      "description": "Clinical Lecturer in Gastroenterology | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Clinical Lecturer in Gastroenterology Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Clinical Lecturer in Gastroenterology Applications are invited for a Clinical Lecturer in Gastroenterology, Department of Medicine. Based on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus this post offers unrivalled opportunity to undertake high quality research within an outstanding academic and clinical environment. Applicants must be medically qualified, have full GMC registration, and demonstrate clinical competence for the award of a National Training Number (NTN/NTN(A)) in Gastroenterology. If a current NTN is not held, candidates will need to clinically benchmark before taking up the post. Clinical benchmarking can be achieved through application for specialty training within the national recruitment window where the minimal appointable score must be achieved. Candidates are responsible for ensuring that their clinical application is submitted within the appropriate recruitment round to ensure that they are benchmarked before the date that the post needs to commence. Candidates are required to hold or have submitted a PhD/DPhil/MD at the time of application and be of the standing required for an appropriate honorary contract of service with the NHS LETB (specialist trainee/LETB). Candidates who have not submitted their PhD/DPhil/MD at the time of application will not be considered. This is a fixed term appointment for a maximum of 4 years or until you obtain CCT (subject to any extension that you apply for and be granted by NIHR, which can be for a maximum of 24 months). Salary Scale: Nodal £67,325pa - £77,348pa (ST3 and above) This appointment requires an Honorary Clinical Contract. This is an integrated appointment between the University and relevant NHS Trust / Body. You will therefore also be issued with an Honorary Clinical contract by the NHS Trust. You should be aware that if your contract with the NHS Trust is terminated your employment with the University will be terminated, and vice versa. You will require GMC registration, Licence to Practice and Medical Defence Cover for the duration of your appointment. Informal enquiries about this post are welcomed and should be directed to Professor Arthur Kaser by email ak729@cam.ac.uk . We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department/institution/Faculty/School/University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter, CV and completed Additional Information Form in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RC51286 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Clinical Lecturer Additional Information Form Key information Department/location Department of Medicine Salary £67,325-£77,348 Reference RC51286 Category Academic Date published 2 October 2026 Closing date 30 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/clinical-research-training-fellow-x-3-fixed-term-rd51283": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Training Fellow x 3 (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Clinical Research Training Fellow x 3 (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Clinical Research Training Fellow x 3 (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Clinical Research Training Fellow x 3 (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for Clinical Research Training Fellowships leading to a PhD. The Fellowships are available to medically qualified individuals who are undertaking their specialist training in any clinical discipline, and who wish to obtain a PhD with a view to an academic career in any branch of cancer research. Eligibility to practice clinically within the UK is an essential qualification. Fellowships for 2027 are to be held in member research groups of the CRUK Cambridge Centre. For details of the projects on offer within these Programmes, please see: https://crukcambridgecentre.org.uk/education-and-training/ccc-clinical-research-fellowships Applicants will choose their preferred projects (first and second choices), and short-listed candidates will be invited to interview, at which their career intentions and preferred PhD project will be discussed. CRUK Cambridge Centre Clinical Research Fellowships are financed by Cancer Research UK and provide clinicians with funds to cover a full-time, 3-year PhD degree. This includes a clinical salary, generous consumables and University Composition Fees at the Home rate. Overseas applicants may apply for these positions and will usually be eligible to apply for the reduced staff University Composition Fee (UCF). This is one third of either the full- or part-time Home Student UCF, for all qualifying students, irrespective of whether they are classified as UK or Overseas. Successful international applicants would be required to apply for the reduced staff UCF via their host department, and if they are not eligible, they may be required to self-fund the cost difference between Overseas and Home fees; this is approximately £27,600 per year for 3 years. Award of the Fellowship is dependent on the successful applicant obtaining an offer of admission as a PhD student to the University of Cambridge. Please refer to the Further Particulars document for full details about programme employment and how to apply. Informal enquiries can be sent to Professor Daniel Hodson ( djh1002@cam.ac.uk ) or Dr Samantha Byers ( sb2251@cam.ac.uk ) Fellowships are usually expected to start on 1 October 2027. Fixed term: The funds for this post are available for 3 years. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a CV and statement of interest (this should include your first and second choice of PhD project from those on offer, as well as confirming your eligibility to practice) in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. The closing date for receipt of applications is 19 November 2026. Short-listed applicants will be invited to interview w/c 4th January 2027. You will be interviewed by a panel of Principal Investigators from the CRUK Cambridge Centre, and you will be given the opportunity to visit the relevant Department(s) or Institute(s) for your selected project(s). We strongly encourage informal interaction between short-listed candidates and the Principal Investigators and their laboratories before the formal interview. Please quote reference RD51283 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information File Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Oncology Salary £41,226 - £77,348 Reference RD51283 Category Research Date published 8 October 2026 Closing date 19 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/communications-coordinator-part-time-fixed-term-lc51228": {
      "title": "Communications Coordinator (Part-time, Fixed-Term)",
      "description": "Communications Coordinator (Part-time, Fixed-Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Communications Coordinator (Part-time, Fixed-Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Communications Coordinator (Part-time, Fixed-Term) The Department of Geography is seeking a Communications Coordinator to lead the delivery of communications and marketing for the Masters in Conservation Leadership, including the course website, annual report, social media profile and other communications content (e.g. commissioning videos and newsletters). The Masters is a high-profile programme for experienced conservation professionals from around the world, delivered in the David Attenborough Building by the partner organisations in the world-leading Cambridge Conservation Initiative. The purpose of this role is to manage the dissemination of information regarding the Masters and its alumni to external audiences, to members of the Cambridge Conservation Initiative and the wider conservation community, to the alumni network, funders and any other key stakeholders as required. This is to ensure that the Masters receives good quality applications during its annual marketing round, and to ensure that the profile of the Masters is maintained and understood by all of the target interest groups. The post holder will be based in the David Attenborough Building, home of the Conservation Research Institute. The successful applicant should be educated to HND, HNC, level 4/5 vocational qualifications or an equivalent level of practical experience.  They should possess demonstrable social media skills on LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook and other relevant channels.  They should have experience working in communications (e.g. in an NGO or university) coupled with experience of using web content management systems and social media platforms, as well as demonstrate factual and theoretical knowledge of Communications. Applicants should review the further particulars attached for more details on the role and its responsibilities. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 5 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcome and should be directed to Prof Chris Sandbrook, at cgs21@cam.ac.uk . If you have any questions about the application process, please email Mrs Alessandra Uomo, HR Coordinator, at hr@geog.cam.ac.uk . Interviews are anticipated to take place on Monday, 16 November 2026. Please quote reference LC51228 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Geography Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference LC51228 Category Assistant staff Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
