    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/fully-funded-phd-studentship-understanding-the-identity-and-function-of-tumour-initiating-states-sw51026": {
      "title": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Understanding the identity and function of tumour-initiating states",
      "description": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Understanding the identity and function of tumour-initiating states | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Understanding the identity and function of tumour-initiating states Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Understanding the identity and function of tumour-initiating states Supervisor: Prof Masashi Narita Department/location: Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Deadline for application: 16th October 2026 Course start date: 1st October 2027 Project details For further information about the research group, including their most recent publications, please visit their website at https://www.naritalab.com/ Our laboratory investigates the biology of preneoplastic cell states, with particular interest in how changes in cell functional identity and plasticity are controlled by the epigenome. We are interested in how oncogenic and metabolic signalling reshapes chromatin organisation and gene regulation, how these changes influence cellular fitness and tissue microenvironment, and which states ultimately suppress or promote tumour development. The PhD project will investigate the molecular and epigenetic mechanisms underlying transitions between preneoplastic cell states using diverse models, both in vitro and in vivo. The precise direction of the project will be developed according to the student's interests and emerging findings in the laboratory. Depending on the specific questions addressed, approaches may include mammalian cell culture, molecular and cell biology, genetic perturbation, imaging, epigenetic and chromatin profiling, and in vivo models of early tumorigenesis. Genomic and single-cell approaches may also be used, supported by collaboration with computational scientists. References/further reading Zhu H, Chan ASL & Narita M. The rise of RAS: how gradual oncogene activation shapes the OIS spectrum. Genes & Development (2025). Chan ASL et al. Oncogenic RAS drives a spectrum of cellular states with distinct tumour-propagating potential. Nature (2024). Olan I, Ando-Kuri M, Parry AJ et al. HMGA1 orchestrates chromatin compartmentalization and sequesters genes into 3D networks coordinating senescence heterogeneity. Nature Communications 15, 6891 (2024). Cassidy LD et al. Temporal inhibition of autophagy reveals segmental reversal of ageing with increased cancer risk. Nature Communications 11, 307 (2020). Olan I, Handa T & Narita M. Beyond SAHF: an integrative view of chromatin compartmentalization during senescence. Trends in Cell Biology (2023). Preferred skills/knowledge Previous hands-on laboratory research experience is highly desirable. Experience in one or more of the following would be advantageous but is not essential: mammalian cell culture, molecular biology, microscopy, genetic manipulation, chromatin or epigenetic assays, and cancer or ageing model systems. The successful candidate should have a strong interest in mechanistic biology and in using experimental approaches to understand fundamental questions in cancer development. Computational or bioinformatics expertise is advantageous but not required. Training and collaborative support will be available to analyse genomic datasets generated during the project. Eligibility We welcome applications from both UK and overseas students. Applications are invited from recent graduates or final-year undergraduates who hold or expect to gain a First/Upper Second Class degree (or equivalent) in a relevant subject from any recognised university worldwide. Applicants with relevant research experience, gained through Master's study or while working in a laboratory, are strongly encouraged to apply. How to apply Please apply via the University Applicant Portal. For further information about the course and to access the Applicant Portal, visit: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvcrpdmsc You should select to commence study in October 2027. References We would appreciate it if you could ask your referees to submit their references as soon as possible upon request, despite the longer University deadline for references. They will receive a request once you have completed the References section of your application. Please quote reference SW51026 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary Reference SW51026 Category Studentships Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/fully-funded-phd-studentship-unmasking-mechanisms-of-resistance-to-kras-inhibition-across-pancreatic-sw51012": {
      "title": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Unmasking mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition across pancreatic cancer genetic profiles",
      "description": "Fully-funded PhD studentship: Unmasking mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition across pancreatic cancer genetic profiles | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Unmasking mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition across pancreatic cancer genetic profiles Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Fully-funded PhD studentship: Unmasking mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition across pancreatic cancer genetic profiles Supervisor: Dr Giulia Biffi Department/location: Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Deadline for application: 16th October 2026 Course start date: 1st October 2027 Project details For further information about the research group, including their most recent publications, please visit their website at https://biffilab.wordpress.com Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) remains a formidable clinical challenge. A major recent advance has been the development of KRAS inhibitors, which have shown considerable promise in clinical trials (1). For decades, KRAS was considered undruggable; however, these new inhibitors represent a potential turning point for patients with PDAC. Despite this progress, rapid therapy resistance has already been observed in both patients and pre-clinical mouse models, highlighting the urgent need to define the mechanisms that drive resistance and to identify rational combination therapies. In our laboratory, we have established and validated a panel of genetically heterogeneous PDAC models. These studies have shown that malignant-cell genetics shape distinct, therapeutically actionable vulnerabilities through both cell-intrinsic programmes and changes in the surrounding tumour microenvironment [(2) and unpublished]. These observations create an important opportunity: we hypothesise that malignant¿stromal crosstalk within defined genetic contexts drives distinct mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition. In support of this, our work indicates that cancer-associated fibroblasts play diverse roles in PDAC progression and activate pathways that may contribute to therapy resistance (3, 4). This PhD project will therefore identify malignant cell-intrinsic and malignant cell-extrinsic mechanisms of resistance to KRAS inhibition, including fibroblast-mediated mechanisms, across genetically distinct PDACs. We will prioritise PDAC genotypes for which we already have preliminary data suggesting candidate mechanisms of resistance. The project will combine complementary models and techniques, including co-transplantation of organoids in mouse models, in vivo CRISPR, single-cell RNA sequencing, flow cytometry, multiplex immunofluorescence, spatial transcriptomics, and standard molecular biology approaches. A computational component may also be available, depending on the skills and interests of the student. Together, these strategies will define how intra-tumour genetic heterogeneity influences response and resistance to KRAS inhibition, complementing ongoing studies in the laboratory investigating inter-tumour genetic heterogeneity. References/further reading Daraxonrasib or Chemotherapy in Previously Treated Metastatic Pancreatic Cancer. N Engl J Med. 2026. (PMID: 42223072) SMAD4 and KRAS status shape malignant-stromal crosstalk in pancreatic cancer. Cancer Res. 2025. (PMID: 39841099) EGFR-activated myofibroblasts promote metastasis of pancreatic cancer. Cancer Cell. 2024. (PMID: 38157863) Functional heterogeneity of fibroblasts in primary tumours and metastases. Trends in Cancer. 2024. (PMID: 39674792) Preferred skills/knowledge We are looking for a motivated student who is excited by this project, enthusiastic about joining a collaborative and hard-working laboratory, and committed to the complementary experimental strategies required for its successful completion. As this project will rely heavily on mouse models, willingness to work with mouse models throughout the PhD is essential. Prior experience in a laboratory or industry research environment is required. Experience with tissue culture, including cell lines and/or organoids, common laboratory techniques such as immunohistochemistry, western blotting and/or PCR, and work with mouse models would be viewed favourably. Prior experience or knowledge of pancreatic cancer or cancer biology would also be advantageous. Eligibility We welcome applications from both UK and overseas students. How to apply Please apply via the University Applicant Portal at https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvcrpdmsc You should select to commence study in October 2027. Please quote reference SW51012 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary Reference SW51012 Category Studentships Date published 9 September 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/fundraising-assistant-part-time-fixed-term-dh51265": {
      "title": "Fundraising Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Fundraising Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Fundraising Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Fundraising Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) Are you an organised and proactive administrator looking to build a career in fundraising and higher education? This is your chance to support world-class fundraising at one of the globe's leading universities, helping to drive research, teaching, and innovation that changes lives. We are looking for a Fundraising Assistant to join us on a part-time basis for a three-year fixed-term contract, working 15 hours per week across three days. As a Fundraising Assistant , you'll provide high-level administrative and operational support to our Cambridge University Health Partners (CUHP) fundraising team. Working closely with senior fundraisers, academics, and external partners, you'll help manage donor relationships, support international travel and events, maintain accurate fundraising records, and contribute to projects that directly impact the University's future. What you'll do: Coordinate complex diaries, meetings, travel, and high-profile events. Act as the first point of contact for senior donors, volunteers, and University leaders. Support fundraising operations through data management, reporting, and process improvements. Assist with prospect research and briefing materials to support senior fundraisers. Provide administrative and financial process support across the team. What we're looking for: Strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to prioritise competing deadlines. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Confidence using Microsoft Office and databases (experience with fundraising databases is desirable). A professional, discreet approach to handling sensitive information. A collaborative team player with initiative and problem-solving ability. Why join us? This is a fantastic opportunity to grow your skills in a dynamic fundraising environment. At Cambridge Development and Alumni Relations, we support research, education and innovation tackling global challenges, from climate change to public health. Meaningful work Your role contributes to initiatives that make a real difference. Inclusive culture We value different perspectives and aim to create a place where everyone feels they belong. Development and growth Access training, mentoring and the wider Cambridge network to build your career. Wellbeing and flexibility We offer flexible working, generous leave and wellbeing support. Strong team and resources You'll work with experienced colleagues, good systems and a supportive environment. Our commitment to inclusion We welcome applications from all backgrounds and communities. If you need adjustments at any stage, we'll support you. Interested? If you're looking for a role where your work matters and you can grow, we'd like to hear from you. University of Cambridge Development and Alumni Relations - do your best work here. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure you read the candidate pack attached and that you detail your career history and outline your suitability for the role in your application. The closing date for this position is midnight on Sunday the 8th of November 2026. Interviews for this position are anticipated to take place week commencing the 16th of November 2026. Please quote reference DH51265 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Candidate Pack - Fundraising Assistant (Part Time, 3 year FTC) - October 2026 Key information Department/location University of Cambridge Development and Alumni Relations Salary £31,236-£35,608 pro rata Reference DH51265 Category Assistant staff Date published 9 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/head-of-development-department-of-medicine-dh51083": {
      "title": "Head of Development, Department of Medicine",
      "description": "Head of Development, Department of Medicine | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Head of Development, Department of Medicine Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Head of Development, Department of Medicine Head of Development, Department of Medicine The University of Cambridge is seeking an exceptional fundraiser to join Development and Alumni Relations as Head of Development for the Department of Medicine, the largest department within the School of Clinical Medicine. This is an exciting opportunity to help shape the next stage of the Department's philanthropic development. Its work spans cardiovascular and respiratory medicine, immunology and infectious disease, acute and critical care, traumatic brain injury and more, with outstanding research and powerful individual stories of discovery and impact. The opportunity now is to bring those stories together, identify where philanthropy can make the greatest difference and develop a clear and compelling case for support that reflects the breadth and ambition of the Department. Working closely with the Director of the Department of Medicine, senior academics and clinicians, you will be both an experienced fundraiser and a trusted adviser. You will bring the judgement and credibility to guide senior stakeholders, challenge constructively where appropriate and help turn complex ideas into propositions that inspire donors. In this role, you will: Shape and develop a compelling fundraising proposition for the Department, bringing together its breadth of research, ambition and impact. Build and manage relationships with major donors and prospective supporters, developing opportunities for significant philanthropic gifts. Work with senior academics and clinicians to identify and prioritise where philanthropy can make the greatest difference. Act as a trusted adviser, using your fundraising expertise and judgement to guide, influence and constructively challenge senior stakeholders. Work across the Department, School of Clinical Medicine, Development and Alumni Relations, NHS partners and wider Collegiate University to ensure fundraising is coordinated, strategic and supports Cambridge's wider priorities. We are looking for an accomplished major gifts fundraiser with excellent relationship-building, influencing and negotiation skills. You will be comfortable operating with senior stakeholders and high-net-worth individuals, able to navigate complexity and confident exercising your own judgement. You do not need to be a scientist, but you will need the intellectual curiosity to engage with complex research, ask the right questions and translate what you learn into something meaningful and compelling for a donor. You may already work in higher education, medicine or science, but we also welcome applications from experienced fundraisers who have developed relevant skills in other complex environments. What matters is your ability to build credibility, develop strong relationships and use your fundraising expertise to turn ambitious ideas into opportunities for philanthropic support. You will join a friendly, ambitious and collaborative Development and Alumni Relations community where your expertise will be recognised and valued. We work hard, support one another and value the different experiences, perspectives and ideas people bring. If you are excited by the opportunity to work with outstanding people, develop meaningful donor relationships and help unlock the philanthropic potential of one of Cambridge's largest and most scientifically diverse departments, we would love to hear from you. University of Cambridge Development and Alumni Relations - do your best work here. Please note: this is a 3-year fixed-term contract position. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date for this position is midnight on the 25th of October 2026. First round interviews for this position are anticipated to take place in the week commencing 2nd of November. Second round interviews for this position are due to take place in the week commencing 9th of November. Please quote reference DH51083 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Candidate Pack - Head of Development, Department of Medicine Key information Department/location University of Cambridge Development and Alumni Relations Salary £63,606-£67,468 Reference DH51083 Category Academic-related Date published 25 September 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/head-of-mfin-programme-student-learning-and-engagement-maternity-leave-cover-ns51264": {
      "title": "Head of MFin Programme (Student Learning and Engagement), Maternity leave cover",
      "description": "Head of MFin Programme (Student Learning and Engagement), Maternity leave cover | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Head of MFin Programme (Student Learning and Engagement), Maternity leave cover Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Head of MFin Programme (Student Learning and Engagement), Maternity leave cover Are you an experienced programme, operations or student services manager who can thrive in a fast-paced, customer-focused environment for a maternity cover position within a leading Business School? About the role: An exciting opportunity has arisen to join Cambridge Judge Business School as Head of MFin Programme (Student Learning and Engagement), providing maternity cover for 12 months for a pivotal leadership role within our highly regarded Master of Finance (MFin) programme. The MFin is a premium postgraduate degree attracting experienced finance professionals from around the world. With over 100 students representing 37 nationalities and a curriculum spanning areas such as AI, machine learning, sustainable finance, blockchain and digital assets, the programme is recognised for its academic excellence and innovative approach to business education. \nAs Head of Programme, you will lead the delivery of an outstanding student learning experience, managing all teaching operations, faculty engagement, programme communications, events and orientation activities. You will work closely with senior academic and professional services colleagues, helping to shape the strategic direction of the programme while ensuring exceptional service and support for students and faculty. About you: We're looking for an experienced programme, operations or student services leader who can thrive in a fast-paced, customer-focused environment.\nYou will have:\n- Significant experience in programme management, operations or administration, ideally within higher education or professional education. \n- Exceptional organisational and project management skills. \n- Experience managing complex stakeholder relationships and influencing at senior levels. \n- Strong leadership skills with experience managing and developing teams. \n- Experience delivering high-profile events and managing competing priorities. \n- Excellent communication skills and a commitment to delivering an outstanding student experience. \n- Confidence working with systems, data, reporting tools and online learning platforms Why work for Cambridge Judge Business School?\nWe are the business school of the University of Cambridge, advancing knowledge and leadership through people who leave a mark on the world.  Cambridge Judge Business School leverages the power of academia for real world impact to transform individuals, organisations and society. Since 1990, we have forged a reputation as a centre of rigorous thinking and high-impact transformative education, situated within one of the world's most prestigious research universities, and in the heart of the Cambridge Cluster, the most successful technology entrepreneurship cluster in Europe. Benefits of working for Cambridge Judge Business School: \nYou'll receive excellent benefits at Cambridge Judge Business School including:\n- Generous holiday allowance\n- Defined benefits pension schemes\n- Flexible working options\n- Family-friendly initiatives\n- Career development opportunities\n- Support for health and mental wellbeing\n- Discounts on shopping\n- Tax-efficient bicycle and charity-giving schemes\nMore information is available on our website - Staff benefits - Jobs at Cambridge Judge Business School - Cambridge Judge Business School Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. To view more details about the role and person specification, please click the Further Information link.\nClosing Date: 25th October 2026\nInterview Dates: w/c 2nd November 2026\nIf you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact: hrsupport@jbs.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference NS51264 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Judge Business School Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference NS51264 Category Academic-related Date published 10 October 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-administrator-fixed-term-2-yrs-full-time-or-0-8-fte-da51210": {
      "title": "HR Administrator (Fixed Term",
      "description": "HR Administrator (Fixed Term - 2 Yrs) Full-Time or 0.8 FTE | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR Administrator (Fixed Term - 2 Yrs) Full-Time or 0.8 FTE Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR Administrator (Fixed Term - 2 Yrs) Full-Time or 0.8 FTE We are seeking a HR Administrator to join our small, supportive HR team on a range of HR administrative duties, including assisting with recruitment and onboarding processes, carrying out right to work processes, dealing with day-to-day HR enquiries, and arranging training administration. The post holder will use their strong admin, IT and organisational skills to keep relevant databases and tracking tools up to date. The successful candidate will be able to demonstrate a knowledge HR administrative principles and processes, and their high level of awareness of personal impact paired with excellent communication and interpersonal skills will enable them to offer a fantastic level of customer service.    Strong attention to detail, an ability to prioritise effectively and an understanding of the importance of confidentiality in the role are key. In return we offer an encouraging and supportive environment both within the Museum and the wider University, a generous holiday allowance, an attractive pension scheme, health cash plan and many employee benefits. You will be based at the Fitzwilliam Museum, which is the principal museum of the University of Cambridge and the largest cultural venue in the region. Founded in 1816, the museum welcomes around 500,000 visitors a year and houses over half a million works of art and material culture spanning from 10,000BCE to the present day, principally from Europe, North Africa and Asia. To help support our staff to thrive both professionally and personally, we offer hybrid working, and the role holder will have the flexibility to work some days from home, subject to business needs. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please state in the suitability for role section if you have a preference for full-time or 0.8 FTE. Interviews for the role will be held Monday 2nd November 2026. Please quote reference DA51210 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particular Key information Department/location Fitzwilliam Museum Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference DA51210 Category Academic-related Date published 30 September 2026 Closing date 21 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-assistant-temporary-cover-ac51202": {
      "title": "HR Assistant [Temporary Cover]",
      "description": "HR Assistant [Temporary Cover] | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR Assistant [Temporary Cover] Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR Assistant [Temporary Cover] Fixed-term: 31 August 2027. Are you organised, approachable and interested in building your HR experience? We are looking for an HR Assistant to support colleagues across Education Services. As part of our friendly Business and Operations team, you will help provide a responsive, reliable and professional HR service. You will support recruitment, administer casual worker arrangements, maintain accurate records and respond to routine HR queries. This role would suit someone who: Enjoys providing a helpful and professional service Can organise and prioritise their workload effectively Communicates clearly and professionally Pays close attention to detail Works well with others Handles sensitive information with discretion Previous HR experience would be an advantage, but strong administrative skills, sound judgement and a willingness to learn are equally important. What we can offer You will join a friendly and supportive team, with opportunities to build practical HR experience and develop your skills. We offer: A varied role supporting colleagues across Education Services Training and ongoing development opportunities Support from experienced HR and Business and Operations colleagues Flexible working arrangements, where operationally possible A generous annual leave allowance and pension scheme Access to University wellbeing services and staff benefits We welcome applications from candidates seeking to work between 0.6 and 1.0 FTE. We will ask more information about your preferred pattern of employment if you are shortlisted. Applications are welcome  from both external and internal candidates, including internal candidates  who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact SSCjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AC51202 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Education Services Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference AC51202 Category Assistant staff Date published 28 September 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-business-partner-ah51216": {
      "title": "HR Business Partner",
      "description": "HR Business Partner | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR Business Partner Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR Business Partner We are looking for an experienced HR professional to join the University of Cambridge as an HR Business Partner within the Human Resources Division. The role focuses on providing trusted, proactive HR support that helps managers and leaders deliver their people and organisational objectives. We encourage early applications as they will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. About the role Working closely with managers and senior leaders across the University, you will provide expert advice on a broad range of people matters, including employee relations, organisational change, workforce planning and HR best practice. You will help translate people strategy into practical, evidence-based solutions that support a positive employee experience and high-performing teams. You will also contribute to HR projects, policy development and service improvement initiatives, working collaboratively with specialist HR teams and key stakeholders. What you will be doing Providing expert HR advice and consultancy to managers and leaders. Supporting employee relations casework and organisational change activity. Delivering high-quality, customer-focused HR services. Coaching and developing managers to build people management capability. Leading or contributing to HR projects and service improvement initiatives. Building strong relationships with stakeholders, including trade unions and specialist HR teams. We are looking for someone who has Significant HR experience in a generalist or specialist role. Strong knowledge of employment law, employee relations and organisational change. Experience delivering HR projects and supporting change initiatives. Strong communication, influencing and stakeholder management skills. Excellent analytical and problem-solving abilities. CIPD qualification or equivalent professional experience. Why join us? Alongside working in a collaborative and supportive environment, the University of Cambridge offers a range of excellent benefits, including: Development opportunities such as LinkedIn Learning, job shadowing and career pathways. 41 days annual leave (including bank holidays) and a highly competitive pension scheme. Hybrid working with flexibility in how you arrange your office days. A wide range of discounts at local retailers and thousands of national brands. Health and wellbeing support, including discounted gym access and the Staff Counselling Service. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for flexible working arrangements. How to Apply Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 02 October 2027. For this role, all applications will be managed by Spirehouse Recruitment. To apply, please send your CV to cambridge@spirehouse.co.uk For further information about the role, please contact Abbie Carr (Executive Consultant), abbie@spirehouse.co.uk Closing date: Monday 19 October 2026 Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Download further information HR Business Partner - Further Information Document Key information Department/location Human Resources Division Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference AH51216 Category Academic-related Date published 28 September 2026 Closing date 19 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-case-manager-ah51307": {
      "title": "HR Case Manager",
      "description": "HR Case Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR Case Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR Case Manager We are looking for an experienced HR professional to join the University of Cambridge as an HR Case Manager, specialising in complex employee relations casework. This is a key role within the HR Case Management team, providing expert advice and managing a portfolio of sensitive and high-risk cases across the University. We encourage early applications as they will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. About the role You will manage complex formal employee relations cases, including disciplinaries, grievances, appeals and Employment Tribunals, ensuring they are handled fairly, consistently and in line with policy and employment law. Working closely with HR Business Partners, managers, trade unions and Legal Services, you will assess risk, provide expert advice, and support effective resolution of cases. You will also contribute to policy development, service improvement and knowledge sharing across the HR community. What you will be doing Managing a portfolio of complex and sensitive employee relations cases Providing expert advice on employment law, policy and best practice Assessing risk and supporting Employment Tribunal activity where required Working collaboratively with stakeholders, including HRBPs and trade unions Coaching managers and contributing to training and guidance Supporting continuous improvement of HR case management processes We are looking for someone who has Strong experience in employee relations within a complex organisation Proven ability to manage high-risk and sensitive casework Up-to-date knowledge of UK employment law Excellent communication, judgement and influencing skills A proactive, organised and solution-focused approach CIPD qualification or equivalent experience Please review the 'Further Information' document for more details of the role and the skills we are seeking. Why join us? Alongside working in a collaborative and supportive environment, the University of Cambridge offers a range of excellent benefits, including: Development opportunities such as LinkedIn Learning, job shadowing and career pathways. 41 days annual leave (including bank holidays) and a highly competitive pension scheme. Hybrid working with flexibility in how you arrange your office days. A wide range of discounts at local retailers and thousands of national brands. Health and wellbeing support, including discounted gym access and the Staff Counselling Service. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for flexible working arrangements. How to Apply For this role, all applications will be managed by Spirehouse Recruitment. To apply, please send your CV to cambridge@spirehouse.co.uk For further information about the role, please contact Abbie Carr (Executive Consultant), abbie@spirehouse.co.uk Closing date: Friday 30 October 2026 Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Download further information HR Case Manager - Further Information Key information Department/location Human Resources Division Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference AH51307 Category Academic-related Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 30 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-coordinator-nm51358": {
      "title": "HR Coordinator",
      "description": "HR Coordinator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR Coordinator Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR Coordinator We have an exciting opportunity for an experienced and proactive HR Coordinator to join our established HR team within the Department of Engineering. As the team continues to evolve, this role offers the opportunity to contribute to the delivery of a high-quality HR service within one of the largest departments in the University, while helping to enhance and improve our people processes and ways of working. The Department of Engineering is the largest within the University of Cambridge (approximately 800 staff) and operates across a diverse range of Academic and Professional Services divisions. The role holder will play an important part in supporting the delivery of a professional, responsive and efficient HR service within a complex and fast-paced environment. We are seeking an organised and customer-focused HR professional who can build positive working relationships, provide accurate advice and guidance, and ensure HR processes are delivered effectively and compliantly. Reporting to an HR Manager and working as part of the wider Departmental HR team, you will collaborate closely with HR colleagues to support a broad range of HR activities across the employee lifecycle. The role encompasses coordinating recruitment and onboarding processes, managing visitor administration and compliance requirements, supporting probation and appraisal procedures, maintaining accurate HR records, and providing first-line advice to managers and staff on a variety of HR policies and employment-related matters. You will also work closely with colleagues across the Department and University to ensure compliance with employment legislation, immigration requirements and University policies. The successful candidate will have experience in HR administration or coordination, strong organisational skills, and exceptional attention to detail. You will be confident managing multiple priorities, handling sensitive information with discretion, and providing a high standard of customer service. Strong communication skills, a collaborative approach and the ability to work effectively in a complex environment are essential. Experience of recruitment, immigration or visitor administration, HR systems and providing HR advice would be advantageous. As part of the wider HR team, you will contribute to continuous improvement initiatives and support projects that enhance the experience of staff and visitors across the Department. The department offers a welcoming and inclusive environment, and the opportunity to work alongside supportive and collaborative colleagues. For more information about the department, please visit: https://www.eng.cam.ac.uk/ Benefits of working at the University include: 40.5 days' annual leave (inclusive of bank holidays and University closure days) Generous defined benefit pension scheme Enhanced family leave and pay policies Hybrid and flexible working arrangements Access to a comprehensive wellbeing programme, including EAP, Occupational Health and Staff Counselling Professional networking and access to Personal and Professional Development team Access to recognition and progression schemes Staff discount schemes Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date for applications is Sunday, 1st November 2026. Informal enquiries about the post are welcomed and should be directed to Leesa Osborn, HR Manager ( ld311@cam.ac.uk ). Enquiries regarding the application process should be directed to the HR Office on hr-office@eng.cam.ac.uk or 01223 332615. Please quote reference NM51358 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference NM51358 Category Assistant staff Date published 9 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
