      "title": "Junior Administrator",
      "description": "Junior Administrator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Junior Administrator Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Junior Administrator Join the Victor Phillip Dahdaleh Heart & Lung Research Institute (VPD-HLRI), part of the University of Cambridge, as our Junior Administrator and play a key role in supporting the Institute's senior leadership and day-to-day operations. The VPD-HLRI brings together researchers, clinicians and professional services staff from across the University, NHS and partner organisations, creating a collaborative environment focused on improving cardiovascular and lung health. As Junior Administrator , you will provide professional, proactive and confidential administrative and secretarial support to the Directors of both the Institute and the Clinical Research Facility (HLRI-CRF), helping to ensure the smooth and effective operation of their offices. This is a varied and fast-paced role that sits at the centre of Institute activity. You will manage complex and frequently changing diaries, coordinate meetings and events, organise domestic and international travel, and provide high-quality administrative support across a wide range of activities. Working closely with senior academic leaders, you will help ensure priorities are managed effectively and that information, correspondence and documentation are handled professionally and confidentially. The role involves regular contact with colleagues from across the University, Royal Papworth Hospital and a range of external organisations. You will act as a key point of contact for the Institute, responding to enquiries, coordinating activities and building effective working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders. Strong communication skills, sound judgement and a professional approach are therefore essential. Alongside supporting the Directors, you will contribute to a broad range of Institute operations. This includes organising seminars, meetings and visits, supporting governance and committee activity, preparing agendas and papers, taking minutes, tracking actions and maintaining records. You will also assist with recruitment administration, finance processes such as purchase orders, invoices and expenses, and the maintenance of accurate administrative records and databases. We are looking for someone who is highly organised, proactive and able to manage a varied workload while maintaining excellent attention to detail. You will be comfortable handling confidential information, working to competing deadlines and using your initiative to resolve issues and keep activities running smoothly. Previous administrative experience, including diary management and meeting coordination, would be advantageous. You should be confident using Microsoft Office applications including Outlook, Word and Excel, and be comfortable learning new systems and processes. Most importantly, you will enjoy providing excellent administrative support, building positive working relationships and contributing to a collaborative and supportive team environment. You will be joining a welcoming professional services team within a world-leading research institute, with opportunities to develop your skills and contribute to an environment that supports internationally recognised research and innovation. Interviews will be held on 6th November 2026 We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RC51303 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Medicine Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference RC51303 Category Assistant staff Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 21 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/kidani-professorship-of-integrated-cancer-medicine-rd50746": {
      "title": "Kidani Professorship of Integrated Cancer Medicine",
      "description": "Kidani Professorship of Integrated Cancer Medicine | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Kidani Professorship of Integrated Cancer Medicine Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Kidani Professorship of Integrated Cancer Medicine The Board of Electors to the Kidani Professorship of Integrated Cancer Medicine invite applications for this Professorship from persons whose work falls within the general field of the Professorship to take up appointment in 2027. Candidates will have an outstanding research record of international stature in leading multidisciplinary translational cancer research involving advanced computational approaches and the vision, leadership, experience and enthusiasm to help us realise an ambitious vision for digital enabled detection, prevention and treatment of cancer in the new Cambridge Cancer Research Hospital.  They will hold a PhD or equivalent postgraduate qualification. Standard professorial duties include leading a research team, postgraduate teaching, and supervision as well as some administration.  The Professor will be based in the Early Cancer Institute, University of Cambridge and space will be available in the CCRH from 2031.  A competitive salary and a generous start-up package will be offered. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Further information is available at http://www.hr.admin.cam.ac.uk/professorships or contact the Human Resources, University Offices, The Old Schools, Cambridge, CB2 1TT, (email: professorships@admin.cam.ac.uk ). Applications, consisting of a letter of application, a statement of current and future research vision and plans, a curriculum vitae and a publications list, along with details of three referees should be made online no later than 26 October 2026. Informal enquiries may be directed to Professor Rebecca Fitzgerald, Director of the Early Cancer Institute and academic lead for the CCRH : rcf29@cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference RD50746 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Oncology Salary Reference RD50746 Category Professorships and Directorships Date published 13 August 2026 Closing date 26 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/leverhulme-centre-for-life-in-the-universe-lclu-phd-studentship-fixed-term-ka51068": {
      "title": "Leverhulme Centre for Life in the Universe (LCLU) PhD Studentship (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Leverhulme Centre for Life in the Universe (LCLU) PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Leverhulme Centre for Life in the Universe (LCLU) PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Leverhulme Centre for Life in the Universe (LCLU) PhD Studentship (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a fully funded, four-year PhD studentship, available for home (UK) students starting in October 2027.  The successful candidate will be based within one of the Centre's participating departments and will undertake a research project aligned with the Centre's mission to develop a deeper understanding of life, its origins, and its distribution throughout the Universe. Projects will contribute to one or more of the Centre's core research questions: What chemical pathways could have led to the origins of life under conditions compatible with diverse planetary environments? How can we identify and characterise environments on Earth and beyond that may serve as cradles for prebiotic chemistry and life? What observational facilities, technologies, and analytical methods will enable the study of planets beyond our Solar System and the detection of signatures of geological and biological evolution? How can philosophical and mathematical approaches refine our understanding of life and inspire new interdisciplinary modes of scientific enquiry? We are dedicated to promoting diversity and inclusivity and widening participation in postgraduate study. Therefore, this studentship is only available to people who fulfil at least one of the eligibility criteria. For further information regarding eligibility see PhD Studentships ( https://www.lclu.cam.ac.uk/studentship-opportunities/lclu-phd-studentships ). You can find the list of proposed projects at https://www.lclu.cam.ac.uk/studentship-opportunities/lclu-phd-studentships/research-proposals-2026-2027 , but note that the list is not exhaustive, and applicants are encouraged to approach any of our supervisors with their own project ideas. If you would like to propose your own research topic, you can also browse through the list of supervisors with research interests ( https://www.lclu.cam.ac.uk/supervisors ) to find those that particularly interest you. You should contact prospective supervisors before applying, to further explore your mutual interests, and to get additional advice and information. Participating Departments at the University of Cambridge: Cavendish Laboratory, Institute of Astronomy, Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics, Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry, Department of Biochemistry, Department of Earth Sciences, Department of Zoology, Department of History and Philosophy of Science, Faculty of Divinity, and the MRC Laboratory of Molecular Biology. Application process To apply for a LCLU PhD Studentship, please apply through the University of Cambridge postgraduate admissions portal: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/ and note in the applying for funding section that you are eligible for LCLU funding.  The materials required for your application will depend on the department of the primary PhD supervisor with details available at: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/applying/how-do-i-apply . The deadlines for applications vary with the department, so please check what the relevant deadline is with the relevant PhD Supervisor, since some departments have earlier deadlines, such as 2 December 2026.  All applications will have to be in by 12:00 pm (noon) GMT 8 December 2026. Application closing date: 8 December 2026. Interviews will be held within the week of 15-19 February 2027. For further enquiries please email: se413@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference KA51068 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Physics Salary Reference KA51068 Category Studentships Date published 14 September 2026 Closing date 8 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/marketing-and-commercial-director-internal-candidates-only-ns51211": {
      "title": "Marketing and Commercial Director (internal candidates only)",
      "description": "Marketing and Commercial Director (internal candidates only) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Marketing and Commercial Director (internal candidates only) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Marketing and Commercial Director (internal candidates only) Are you an ambitious strategic marketing and commercial leader with the vision, commercial acumen and influence to help shape the next chapter of Cambridge Judge Business School's success? If so, we would love to hear from you. About the role: As Marketing and Commercial Director, you will provide strategic leadership and hold School-wide accountability for the direction and performance of marketing, brand, student recruitment, digital engagement and customer experience. Using market intelligence, customer insight and commercial analysis, you will advise the Dean and senior leaders on growth opportunities, portfolio performance, investment priorities and commercial risk. This newly created role offers the opportunity to shape the School's integrated marketing function, establishing its capabilities and ways of working while bringing together the expertise and activity that already exist across the School. You will lead a multidisciplinary team, driving innovation in digital marketing, CRM, automation and AI-enabled customer engagement while embedding a data-driven, customer-centred culture across the School. About you: We are looking for an accomplished senior leader with:\n- Significant experience leading integrated marketing and commercial functions within complex organisations.\n- A proven track record of enhancing brand reputation, customer engagement and commercial performance.\n- Strong strategic and commercial judgement, underpinned by data and insight.\n- Experience influencing senior stakeholders and leading through collaboration rather than direct authority.\n- Expertise in digital transformation, including CRM, marketing automation and AI-enabled marketing.\n- A passion for developing high-performing teams and building strategic partnerships.\n- Experience within a globally focused, reputation-led environment; experience in higher education, business education or international recruitment markets would be advantageous. Why work for Cambridge Judge Business School?\nWe are the business school of the University of Cambridge, advancing knowledge and leadership through people who leave a mark on the world.  Cambridge Judge Business School leverages the power of academia for real world impact to transform individuals, organisations and society. Since 1990, we have forged a reputation as a centre of rigorous thinking and high-impact transformative education, situated within one of the world's most prestigious research universities, and in the heart of the Cambridge Cluster, the most successful technology entrepreneurship cluster in Europe. Benefits of working for Cambridge Judge Business School: \nYou'll receive excellent benefits at Cambridge Judge Business School including:\n-   Generous holiday allowance\n-   Defined benefits pension schemes\n-   Flexible working options\n-   Family-friendly initiatives\n-   Career development opportunities\n-   Support for health and mental wellbeing\n-   Discounts on shopping\n-   Tax-efficient bicycle and charity-giving schemes\nMore information is available on our website - Staff benefits - Jobs at Cambridge Judge Business School - Cambridge Judge Business School Additional Information:\nThe University is supportive of hybrid working, where some work is undertaken on University premises and some in a remote working environment. The aim of our approach is to enable as many staff as possible to work in a hybrid way if they wish and where their role allows - we expect most colleagues to spend at least 50% of their time at their dedicated office location. This arrangement is subject to change and will not form a part of the contractual terms of the role. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Application process:\nTo view more details about the role and person specification, please click the Further Information link.\nClosing Date: 18 October 2026\nInterview Dates: weeks commencing 19 and 26 October 2026 If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact: hrsupport@jbs.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference NS51211 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information FPs Key information Department/location Judge Business School Salary £69,488 Reference NS51211 Category Academic-related Date published 2 October 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/mcms-user-support-specialist-aq51352": {
      "title": "MCMS User Support Specialist",
      "description": "MCMS User Support Specialist | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs MCMS User Support Specialist Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs MCMS User Support Specialist Applications are invited for the position of MCMS User Support Specialist. The role is part of a small team working on the development, delivery, and support of the Mouse Colony Management System (MCMS). The team sits within the University Biomedical Services division and provides the MCMS as Software as a Service (SaaS). Its customers are academic and commercial establishments that use model organisms (for example rodents) for scientific research. The MCMS User Support Specialist is part of a small team of Software Developers and User Support Specialists that develop, deliver, and support the MCMS software. The role holder is the primary point of contact between the MCMS platform and its user community with particular emphasis on supporting the University of Cambridge users. The role holder will: Coordinate and manage the helpdesk queue, triaging and resolving user enquiries across all aspects of the MCMS platform. Investigate and resolve account, access, and data issues, including writing SQL queries against the MCMS database. Create, maintain, and deliver user-facing training materials and documentation.\nParticipate in software testing as part of the development lifecycle, working closely with developers to feedback findings and support high-quality releases. Gather requirements from users and working groups, translating these into clear development issues for the team. Support the onboarding of new customer institutions and train new super-users and team members. We seek: Proven experience providing technical application support, troubleshooting user issues, and resolving helpdesk requests in a customer-focused environment. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with experience investigating software or system issues, working with databases (including SQL), and identifying when escalation is required. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to explain technical concepts clearly, produce user documentation, and deliver effective training to a range of audiences. Experience working within software development environments, including functional testing, documentation, and a willingness to learn new technologies and processes. Ideally experienced in a biological or scientific research setting, with knowledge of genetic research terminology, model organisms, and relevant regulatory frameworks such as the Animals (Scientific Procedures) Act (ASPA) 1986. The role is available immediately, subject to a 6 month probationary period. In addition to the base salary stated above, the successful candidate for this post will receive an additional 2.5% supplement to their pay. The successful candidate will undergo a health assessment and security check as part of the recruitment process. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment and a security check. Please quote reference AQ51352 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference AQ51352 Category Academic-related Date published 6 October 2026 Closing date 29 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/mechanical-design-engineer-ka50477": {
      "title": "Mechanical Design Engineer",
      "description": "Mechanical Design Engineer | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Mechanical Design Engineer Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Mechanical Design Engineer The Cavendish Laboratory is the place of pioneering physics, that's been at the forefront of scientific discovery for 150 years, and will continue to be, because we never sit still. The Cavendish Laboratory has made several important discoveries, including: the discovery of the electron (1897), the neutron (1932), the structure of DNA (1953); thirty Cavendish researchers have won Nobel Prizes. We're trailblazers who continue to extend the frontiers of physics. The Department has moved into our new state-of-the-art, multi-million pound Ray Dolby Centre which will be the centrepiece of the new Cavendish Laboratory. This is an exciting and challenging time for the Department, and we would like to find a highly motivated individual with a positive attitude towards change and development to join us. A reorganisation and merger of several professional services teams within the Department of Physics has led to the creation of Cavendish Engineering ¿ one of the largest, most advanced multidisciplinary university workshops within the UK. An opportunity has arisen that requires a candidate with a very strong mechanical understanding, excellent communication, and multi-tasking skills to be considered for this critical position.   Reporting to the Deputy Head of Cavendish Engineering, you will provide a consultation, design and manufacture service to our customers.  We would expect proficient use of EDM, 5-Axis, Milturn, and manual machinery, along with a strong Autodesk Fusion CAD/CAM background.  Great communication skills and a knowledgeable approach enable you to work with your customer at every step of the journey.  If this sounds like you, we'd love to hear from you. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcome and should be sent to Ceri Williamson, Head of Cavendish Engineering ( caw204@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference KA50477 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Department of Physics Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference KA50477 Category Assistant staff Date published 25 September 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/mst-course-administrator-fixed-term-jl51108": {
      "title": "MSt Course Administrator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "MSt Course Administrator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs MSt Course Administrator (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs MSt Course Administrator (Fixed Term) We are seeking a proactive and highly organised Administrator to play a key role in supporting the delivery of our two part-time Master of Studies (MSt) programmes: Applied Criminology & Police Management and Applied Criminology, Penology & Management. This role is offered full-time and will be fixed-term for 2 years in the first instance. Working closely with the Graduate Programmes Manager, Course Directors and academic staff, you will provide high-quality administrative support across the full student lifecycle, from initial enquiry and admissions through to assessment, qualification and graduation. You will also contribute to the planning and delivery of residential teaching blocks, ensuring a seamless experience for students, academics and external contributors. The role offers the opportunity to work with a diverse range of stakeholders, including senior professionals from policing, probation, prisons and the wider criminal justice sector. You will coordinate course administration, manage admissions processes, maintain programme information and learning resources, support assessments and examinations, and help organise teaching and programme events. We are looking for an experienced administrator with excellent organisational skills, strong attention to detail, and the ability to manage competing priorities in a busy environment. You will be confident communicating with a wide range of colleagues and external partners, comfortable working independently, and committed to delivering a professional and responsive service. The University of Cambridge Institute of Criminology is one of the world's leading criminology departments, with an international reputation for excellence in research and teaching. In addition to two MSt programmes for senior criminal justice practitioners, the Institute delivers an MPhil and a PhD programme. The Institute is also home to the Radzinowicz Library, which houses the most comprehensive criminology collection in the United Kingdom. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal queries about the role can be directed to Lydia Romero, Graduate Programmes Manager ( sgc@crim.cam.ac.uk ) or Fiona Harrison, Institute Manager ( admin.officer@crim.cam.ac.uk ). Interviews are expected to be held in week commencing 2nd November 2026 (subject to change). Please quote reference JL51108 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Institute of Criminology Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference JL51108 Category Assistant staff Date published 24 September 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/museum-education-and-outreach-coordinator-part-time-fixed-term-ge51353": {
      "title": "Museum Education and Outreach Coordinator (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Museum Education and Outreach Coordinator (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Museum Education and Outreach Coordinator (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Museum Education and Outreach Coordinator (Part Time, Fixed Term) The Museum of Classical Archaeology (MOCA) are looking to recruit a part-time (0.6 FTE) Museum Education and Outreach Coordinator. The role will be fixed term for 12 months due to limited funding. Main responsibilities of the role include: facilitating and delivering approximately 150 classes p.a. (with approximately 2,500 children), plus in person and online events with students and members of the public assisting with training and managing volunteers advising the Museum Committee, and the Faculty, on possible expansion of provision including resource implications selecting and managing specialist outside providers and staff where needed providing advice and training for teachers as well as identifying and contributing to local and national opportunities contributing to Faculty access initiatives The successful candidate will have enthusiasm for and knowledge of the classical world, a first degree with training in the humanities, and experience in teaching in a museum environment. MOCA is home to a collection of more than 600 plaster casts of Greek and Roman sculpture, as well as ancient artefacts. MOCA is part of the University of Cambridge Museums (UCM), a consortium of the eight University Museums and Cambridge University Botanic Garden. We work closely with other University of Cambridge collections as well as with museums regionally, nationally and internationally. Our programmes focus on unlocking the University's collections, and the research activities which underpin them, for a larger and more diverse audience. Further information about the Museum of Classical Archaeology is available at http://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/museum/ . Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and should be directed to Dr Susanne Turner, Curator on smt41@cam.ac.uk . For questions on the application process, please contact the School's HR Team on sahhr@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is midnight (GMT) on Sunday 08 November 2026. interviews are planned for Monday 23 November 2026, subject to change. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference GE51353 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Faculty of Classics Museum of Classical Archaeology Salary £31,236-£35,608 pro rata Reference GE51353 Category Assistant staff Date published 12 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/ncpp-technical-engineer-fixed-term-nm51351": {
      "title": "NCPP Technical Engineer (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "NCPP Technical Engineer (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs NCPP Technical Engineer (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs NCPP Technical Engineer (Fixed Term) The National Centre for Propulsion and Power (NCPP) is looking for an outstanding Technical Engineer to join the growing NCPP Engineering Office to underpin its capability in custom rotating machinery engineering. This is an exciting opportunity to work at the interface of research, engineering and industry, developing and delivering bespoke rotating machinery and experimental systems that enable cutting-edge turbomachinery research. Based within the NCPP, the UK's new flagship mid-TRL test facility for turbomachinery research and development, at the Whittle Laboratory in West Cambridge, you will play a key role in enabling rapid, safe and high-quality experimental programmes for both academic and industrial partners. Our work supports innovation in propulsion and power technologies and contributes to the wider ambition to deliver world-leading zero-emission research and help accelerate the transition to net zero. You will take technical ownership of academic and industrial research projects, working closely with researchers, academics, technicians, and industrial partners to turn research requirements into practical engineering solutions and ensuring experiments can be delivered safely, efficiently and to a high standard. From concept and detailed design through manufacture, instrumentation, assembly and commissioning, you will lead the development of complete rotating machinery test sections and their instrumentation and ancillary systems. You will also play a key role during test campaigns, acting as the subject matter expert in rotating machinery, supporting operation and contributing to data collection and analysis. The role includes significant hands-on engineering and workshop activity, with physical work on the experimental facility or in the workshop Occasional travel and, rarely, out-of-hours or weekend working may be required to support urgent deliverables or unforeseen equipment failures. We are looking for an engineer who combines strong technical knowledge with practical, hands-on engineering experience. The successful candidate will hold a bachelor's degree or Level 6 vocational qualification in mechanical engineering or a related technical discipline, or possess equivalent experience. Professional registration at IEng or CEng level, or a commitment to working towards professional registration, would be desirable. You will have experience in rotating machinery engineering, with expertise in one or more areas such as design, analysis, manufacturing, instrumentation and controls, commissioning, servicing or diagnosis. Specialist knowledge and hands-on experience working with rotating machinery and in a mechanical workshop is particularly important. This is more than a conventional engineering role so if you are an engineer who enjoys taking a problem from concept through to hardware, commissioning and successful experimental operation, we would love to hear from you. Join us and help turn ambitious research ideas into working engineering solutions. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 14 months in the first instance. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please contact the HR Office at the Department of Engineering ( hr-office@eng.cam.ac.uk , 01223 332615). Please quote reference NM51351 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference NM51351 Category Academic-related Date published 9 October 2026 Closing date 9 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/network-operations-technician-fixed-term-vc51369": {
      "title": "Network Operations Technician (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Network Operations Technician (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Network Operations Technician (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Network Operations Technician (Fixed Term) University of Cambridge Information Services (UIS) Network Operations team is offering an exciting opportunity (9 months) for an on-site Network Operations Technician to be part of our team. Under the guidance of the Network Operations Manager, you will play a critical role in supporting the network environments within the University, including: The University Data Network, the main backbone network. Access networks in departments and colleges. The University's internet connections to its ISP, Janet. The University Wireless Service. The Data Centre Network located in multiple sites. The University DDI (DNS, DHCP and IP Address Management) system. As a member of the team, your responsibilities will include: Supporting network monitoring and maintenance systems to ensure the smooth operation of the networks. Responding quickly and efficiently to network outages, participating in on-site remediation, and maintaining good communication with customers and colleagues. Handling incidents raised by customers, providing second/third line support for tickets escalated to the network team. Ensuring processes are followed, updated, and shared throughout the team. You will have the following skills and experience: Basic knowledge of IT and networking. Experience of troubleshooting IT problems. Ability to communicate with colleagues and customers, and excellent written and verbal communication skills. As this role supports physical infrastructure operations, it is primarily on-site and will involve travel around the University based in Cambridge, UK. Benefits include a generous holiday allowance, competitive pension plan, parental leave, training and development opportunities, flexible working arrangements, and wellbeing support. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. In-person interviews are expected to be scheduled soon after the job closes, and we are looking to appoint as soon as possible. If you have any queries before applying, please contact the Network Operations Manager (Networks), Lawrence Howlett ( lh821@cam.ac.uk ). We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online Application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. The closing date for applications is: Sunday 25th October 2026 The interview date for the role is: To be confirmed Please quote reference VC51369 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location University Information Services Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference VC51369 Category Assistant staff Date published 12 October 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/next-generation-fellow-fixed-term-pm50284": {
      "title": "Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term) The Loke Centre for Trophoblast Research (CTR) was founded in 2007 as a Centre of excellence to promote scientific study of the placenta, early development and maternal-fetal interactions during pregnancy. It brings together over 30 Principal Investigators, many of whom are recognized international experts in their fields. The Loke CTR has an opening for a Next Generation Fellow who will complement and synergise with our existing research programmes. Next Generation Fellowships are a springboard to a long and successful career in the field of placental and reproductive biology and highly flexible to suit individual needs, to enable the postholder to develop as an independent researcher. Holders are expected, and will be supported, to apply for Research Fellowship/Career Development Awards from research councils or other funders, or a faculty position during their tenure. Find out more about the fellowship on our website: https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/ngf-overview The Next Generation Fellowships are a springboard to a long and successful career in the field of placental and reproductive biology. Many of our Next Generation Fellows have used the opportunity as a stepping stone to independent group leader positions: https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/next-generation-fellow-alumni . What to expect Next Generation Fellows will be mentored to develop and establish an independent research program that will complement that of other groups within the Centre for Trophoblast Research. Fellows will be expected to contribute fully to the activities of the Loke CTR and to their host department. They will supervise MPhil/masters, summer and/or undergraduate students. They may be a co-applicant on research grant applications to recruit, train and co-supervise postdoctoral researchers, research assistants, technical staff and PhD students. The post holder will be expected to create a strong track record of publishing high impact papers and disseminate the results of their work in any ways that seem appropriate and effective (e.g. via conferences and seminars). They will be responsible for compliance with all the relevant laws (Home Office, HTA, HFEA). Candidates should hold a PhD and have a high-quality publication track record in academic journals or equivalent.  We welcome applications from across a diverse range of research relevant to our Grand Challenges https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/about-us/objectives . Contract details Appointments will be made on a fixed-term, full-time, basis for a period of 3 years (Grade 7, £37,694- £46,049 for appointments in 2027). There is a possibility of up to 1-year extension. The contract further includes research consumables/equipment of £60,000 and travel support up to £750 over the duration of the fellowship. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. How to apply To apply to this post, candidates should first identify a research Sponsor from the list of Principal Investigators https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/people/principal-investigators within the Centre for Trophoblast Research, and discuss their research plans with them. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. In addition to this, please ensure that you upload the application documentations as follows: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Up to 3 letters of reference Letter of support from your chosen Loke CTR sponsor Loke CTR Next Generation Fellowship application form Closing date: midnight on 31st January 2027. Please quote reference PM50284 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Application Form Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PM50284 Category Research Date published 15 July 2026 Closing date 31 January 2027 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/office-administrator-and-senior-receptionist-jl51263": {
      "title": "Office Administrator and Senior Receptionist",
      "description": "Office Administrator and Senior Receptionist | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Office Administrator and Senior Receptionist Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Office Administrator and Senior Receptionist The Institute of Criminology is looking for a proactive, welcoming, and efficient Office Administrator and Senior Receptionist.  You will be joining a friendly and welcoming professional services team in one of the world's leading criminology departments, with an international reputation for excellence in research and teaching. This key role combines front-of-house customer service with varied administrative support across the Institute. You will oversee the day-to-day running of reception, working alongside a part-time Receptionist, welcome guests, and assist senior administrators and academic teams with essential daily tasks and discrete projects. This role is ideal for a warm, organised administrator who enjoys being the welcoming face of a department while keeping back-of-house operations structured and effective. If you are a natural problem-solver who enjoys variety, stays calm under pressure, and takes a proactive approach to supporting others, we would love to hear from you. Benefits of working at Cambridge include: Competitive rates of pay with automatic service-related pay progression and annual cost of living increases; Generous annual leave allowance of 36 days paid leave (including bank holidays); Generous maternity, adoption and shared parental leave entitlement and other family friendly schemes (e.g. workplace nurseries and salary exchange schemes for childcare); An auto-enrolment pension scheme , with a generous employer contribution; Travel benefits and retail discounts at over 2,000 local and national stores. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal queries about the role can be directed to Meg Horobin ( HRCoordinator@crim.cam.ac.uk ). Interviews are planned for 19th November 2026 (subject to confirmation) Please quote reference JL51263 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Institute of Criminology Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference JL51263 Category Assistant staff Date published 8 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/operations-coordinator-part-time-nm51320": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator (Part Time)",
      "description": "Operations Coordinator (Part Time) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Operations Coordinator (Part Time) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Operations Coordinator (Part Time) The Aviation Impact Accelerator (AIA) is seeking an experienced and highly organised Operations Manager to play a central role in supporting the team and ensuring the smooth and effective running of its day-to-day operations. Based at the Whittle Laboratory in West Cambridge, you will work closely with the AIA Director, Operations Lead and wider team to coordinate and deliver a broad range of operational and administrative priorities. These will include finance, HR, recruitment, procurement, office and calendar management, and other activities essential to the effective functioning of the team. This is a hands-on role requiring someone who is comfortable taking ownership of important operational processes, managing competing priorities and ensuring that tasks are followed through accurately and efficiently. Working under the supervision of the Operations Lead, you will exercise sound judgement in handling routine matters independently, escalate non-routine or sensitive issues appropriately, and provide clear, accurate and timely information to support effective decision-making. We are looking for someone with excellent organisational and coordination skills, exceptional attention to detail and a strong sense of personal responsibility. You will be a proactive self-starter who takes pride in delivering work to a high standard, anticipates what needs to be done and is willing to take ownership of the vital administrative and operational functions that enable the wider team to perform effectively. The successful candidate will be able to communicate clearly and professionally with a wide range of colleagues and stakeholders, manage multiple tasks and deadlines without losing sight of detail, and approach problems calmly and practically. Good financial awareness and experience of working across several operational functions are important, as is the ability to work effectively in a busy and evolving environment. We particularly value reliability, initiative, good judgement and a genuine commitment to getting things right. The role would suit someone who enjoys bringing structure and organisation to a team, who is comfortable working both independently and collaboratively, and who understands the importance of strong operational support to the success of a wider programme. Experience of working within a university, research organisation or similarly complex environment would be an advantage. For full details of the role, responsibilities and person specification, please see the further particulars document. Working pattern: Tuesday to Thursday, office-based. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 6 months in the first instance. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date for applications is Wednesday 21 October 2026. Informal enquiries are welcomed and if you have any queries regarding either the vacancy or the application process, please contact the HR Office in the first instance on hr-office@eng.cam.ac.uk or 01223 332615. Please quote reference NM51320 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £33,951-£39,906 pro rata Reference NM51320 Category Assistant staff Date published 7 October 2026 Closing date 21 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/pa-to-hod-and-da-pj51154": {
      "title": "PA to HoD and DA",
      "description": "PA to HoD and DA | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs PA to HoD and DA Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs PA to HoD and DA We are looking for an experienced PA to support the Head of Department and the Departmental Administrator in the Department of Psychology, based in historic central Cambridge. We offer a welcoming, friendly work environment where you will feel valued, encouraged to develop and supported to achieve your full potential. The Department of Psychology is a large teaching and research Department in the School of Biological Sciences with approximately 30 Academics, 130 members of staff and 100 postgraduate students. You will provide a comprehensive and proactive PA service to the Head of Department and the Departmental Administrator to support the efficient and effective management of their time and work. You will ensure the successful management of collaborative/administrative projects involving a range of university departments and external institutions, by putting in place all requisite processes and procedures. You will also prepare for and attend meetings, record the minutes and act on administrative tasks that may arise out of committee meetings. You will oversee department visitor applications, organise and manage events, seminars and publicity for the department, as well as providing administrative assistance to departmental projects, and welcoming departmental guests. This is an interesting and varied role, which includes some HR administration duties in conjunction with the HR team, as well as event management and occasional cover for reception. You will have a proactive and flexible approach to your work and will have relevant PA/administrative experience. You will have excellent IT skills and exceptional written, verbal and organisational skills. You will be able to use your own initiative as well as having the ability to work both independently and as part of a friendly and supportive team. What we Offer Our Professional Services staff play a fundamental role in the School's academic vision of the pursuit of education and research at the highest levels of excellence. The University of Cambridge offers excellent benefits, extensive opportunities in a stimulating environment. The University salary structure includes automatic service-related pay progression in many of its grades and an annual cost of living increase. In addition to this, employees are rewarded for outstanding contribution through a number of regular pay progression schemes. Staff also benefit from a generous annual leave entitlement. The University offers employees a wide range of competitive benefits, from health care cash plans to childcare, a cycle to work scheme, to shopping and insurance discounts. University-led initiatives in the areas of equality, diversity and wellbeing include staff and students networks. These include the Women's Staff Network, the Disabled Staff Network, the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic Staff Network, Parents and Carers and the LGBT+ Network. We also hold a wide range of Equality and Diversity events on a regular basis. Wellbeing at Cambridge is a university-wide initiative aiming at supporting and maximising the health and wellbeing of staff. It encompasses a network of Wellbeing Advocates, who provide guidance and general signposting about wellbeing issues, including mental or physical health and Dignity @ Work concerns. The University offer a range of family-friendly policies, including maternity, adoption and shared parental leave. In addition, workplace nurseries, childcare vouchers, a childcare salary sacrifice scheme and a high-quality holiday play scheme are available to help support University employees with caring responsibilities Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: Louise Balshaw ( bom@psychol.cam.ac.uk ) Please quote reference PJ51154 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Psychology Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference PJ51154 Category Assistant staff Date published 23 September 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/phd-studentship-in-human-computer-interaction-and-critical-computing-fixed-term-nr50923": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship in Human-Computer Interaction and Critical Computing (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "PhD Studentship in Human-Computer Interaction and Critical Computing (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs PhD Studentship in Human-Computer Interaction and Critical Computing (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs PhD Studentship in Human-Computer Interaction and Critical Computing (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3.5 years. A doctoral studentship is available in the Aspirational Computing Lab in the Department of Computer Science and Technology (CST) at the University of Cambridge. The goal of this PhD programme is to launch one \"deceptive by design\" project that combines the perspectives of human-computer interaction (HCI) and critical computing. Topics of interest include: Persuasion and deception in conversational agents across and beyond text and voice modalities Drivers of influence and over trust in conversational agents that are charming and disarming Interventions against specific cases of interface deception, digital addiction, and/or overreliance on technology across digital platforms and products Other critical computing topics--including all matters of equality, diversity, and inclusion (ED&I or DEI), accessibility and inclusive design, feminism and intersectionality, trans and gender diversity studies, and misinformation and information deception--will also be considered. However, the topics must fall under the purview of the Aspirational Computing Lab and be supervisable by Dr. Seaborn. The applicant must propose a specific research question with an accompanying methodology and research plan that demonstrates their interest, knowledge, and ability to think as a researcher within the joint context of HCI and critical computing. In the first half year, the candidate will work closely with Dr. Seaborn and senior researchers to train and gain experience on the research topic and relevant methods. By the second half, the candidate will take on a leading role and begin carrying out the research comprising their doctoral dissertation. The candidate is expected to join other projects, participate in research communication and outreach, and take on other supporting roles. Notably, the candidate can be trained in small group teaching and invited to take on undergraduate supervisions.  Candidates must be self-motivated, collaboration-oriented, and open to interdisciplinary work and team contexts. Qualifications/Skills Holding a first-class honours degree or equivalent; a Master's-level degree (or other postgraduate work) is highly desirable; these should be relevant to HCI and/or critical computing and the proposed topic Solid understanding of HCI fundamentals and user-centred design Solid communication and interpersonal skills, including the ability to work with people of diverse backgrounds and across disciplinary conventions Solid understanding of research methods involving human participants, especially experimental design Basic understanding of data analysis approaches and tools, including statistics platforms like R and/or methods like thematic analysis Familiarity with deception within the context of computing and HCI Relevant publications, industrial output, or portfolio items, including open-source projects Practical skills in at least two of (a) programming and/or machine learning, (b) interaction design, and/or (c) research design Applicants must contact Dr. Seaborn ( katie.seaborn@cst.cam.ac.uk ) in advance to assess topic and background fit. In doing so, applicants must show evidence of engagement with this advert, Dr. Seaborn's research, and familiarization with the lab website (that is a hint). Further information on the PhD in Computer Science programme can be found on the \"PhD in Computer Science\" webpage. All applications are made online via the University's Applicant Portal; refer to the https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cscspdpcs webpage. Please quote the reference NR50923 in the Research Topic field so that your application can be routed directly to Dr. Seaborn. Applications should include all of the following, without AI assistance: Academic transcripts A CV 2 academic references In the online form, similarly without AI assistance: Research Summary: A brief outline of your research proposal (like an abstract) Research Experience: An outline of your research interests and relevant background, notably describing the qualities and experience you will bring to the role; succinct but with sufficient detail to understand what you did, especially in team contexts Career Goals: And notably why a PhD would help you with these Additional Information to Support Your Application: Please describe circumstances where you faced hindrances or circumstances which mean that you cannot safely carry out work on topics like diversity in your nation of residence Course Specific Questions > Core-reasons for applying: A motivation statement covering why you wish to pursue a PhD in this area and in my lab specifically The research proposal in one PDF that includes: A ~2-page research proposal A ~1-page outline of first year deliverables (be sure to do your research on the programme) Bibliographic references on a fourth page An application is only complete when all supporting documents, including the 2 academic references, are submitted. It is your responsibility to ensure that both referees submit their references before the closing date. Applicants are expected to apply for the University Funding Competitions, but, in the event of an unsuccessful funding application, Dr. Seaborn may have the ability to award the successful candidate with a Home Rate Studentship. Note that a successful international student would need to self-fund the difference between the home and international fees. The University actively supports equality, diversity, and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Please quote reference NR50923 on your application and in any correspondence about this studentship. Please note that applications for academic year 2027/2028 open mid-September 2026. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Please quote reference NR50923 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary Reference NR50923 Category Studentships Date published 2 September 2026 Closing date 2 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/phd-studentship-the-biochemistry-and-chemical-biology-of-the-genome-and-epigenome-ma51367": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship: The Biochemistry and Chemical Biology of the Genome and Epigenome",
      "description": "PhD Studentship: The Biochemistry and Chemical Biology of the Genome and Epigenome | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs PhD Studentship:  The Biochemistry and Chemical Biology of the Genome and Epigenome Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs PhD Studentship:  The Biochemistry and Chemical Biology of the Genome and Epigenome We invite applications from creative and motivated individuals to join Professor Sir Shankar Balasubramanian's group for a 4-year PhD studentship, working on multidisciplinary projects exploiting both chemistry and biology to explore fundamental mechanisms of genome function ( http://www.balasubramanian.co.uk ). Our projects develop and use cutting edge technologies in chemical biology, molecular biophysics, structural biology and genomics to answer critical questions in genome function. Students will contribute to one or more of our primary research areas below: A key interest is the structure and function of DNA secondary structures called G-quadruplexes (G4s). G4s are important in gene and chromatin regulation and our aims are to use chemical biology strategies to elucidate their role in genome biology and to develop small molecule-based intervention strategies for cancer [e.g. see Genome Biology, 2025, 25, 155; Nature Chemistry, 2025, 17, 875; PNAS, 2024, 12(7), e2320240121; Nature Communications, 2022, 13:142; Genome Biology, 2021, 22, 117] We are also developing techniques to map where small molecules bind to DNA and chromatin and to identify the proteins associated with their binding sites with a view to understanding therapeutic mechanisms [e.g. see Nature Biotechnology, 2023, 41, 1265]. Our aim is to characterise small molecule interactions with DNA, RNA and associated proteins and to enable the development of bifunctional small molecules that target the genome and chromatin-associated proteins to understand genome mechanisms and explore their therapeutic potential in diseases such as cancer. The design of bifunctional modalities will encompass new modalities of proximity-inducers that target contextual biology. A recent example being degraders that act at G-quadruplex locations in chromatin [e.g. see Nature Chemistry, 2026, 18, 1092]. This position will be primarily based in the Balasubramanian lab at the Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry and will involve collaborative interactions with the group's sister lab at the CRUK Cambridge Institute. The student will be highly motivated, capable of independent thought and have excellent communication skills with the ability to work collaboratively. Ideally, the candidate will have a strong background in biochemistry/chemistry and/or molecular biology. A good knowledge of genomics and the chemistry/chemical biology of nucleic acids is highly desirable. The studentship commences in October 2027 and provides a maintenance grant and tuition fees at the UK rate.  However, we could support outstanding overseas students in their application for a suitable scholarship. To apply, please email a cover letter, CV, detailed academic transcripts and the contact details for two academic referees direct to:  Jo Lockhart, Balasubramanian Group Science Administrator ( BalasubramanianRecruitment@ch.cam.ac.uk ), including 'PhD October 2027' in the subject.  Any other documents sent will not be considered when assessing your application. Interviews will take place on Wednesday 11th November 2026. Your cover letter should explain why you wish to be considered for the studentship and describe the relevant qualities and experience you have to undertake a PhD. Please also state how you learned of the studentship. For queries regarding the studentship, please contact Jo at BalasubramanianRecruitment@ch.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference MA51367 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary Reference MA51367 Category Studentships Date published 10 October 2026 Closing date 29 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/phd-studentship-comparative-innate-immune-sensing-of-zoonotic-viruses-pk51376": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship: Comparative innate immune sensing of zoonotic viruses",
      "description": "PhD Studentship: Comparative innate immune sensing of zoonotic viruses | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs PhD Studentship: Comparative innate immune sensing of zoonotic viruses Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs PhD Studentship: Comparative innate immune sensing of zoonotic viruses Project title: Comparative innate immune sensing of zoonotic viruses - Professor Brian Ferguson Why do some viruses cause severe disease after they cross into a new species, while others don't? This project will investigate how differences in pattern recognition receptor (PRR) signalling between host species influence virus infection, adaptation and zoonotic emergence. We will compare PRR pathways and downstream immune responses across different host species to determine how variation in pathogen sensing, signalling strength and immune regulation shapes the outcomes of virus/host interactions. Using molecular virology, immunology and comparative approaches, the research will identify conserved and species-specific mechanisms that determine the outcome of cross-species infection. Understanding how PRR signalling establishes or restricts host range could provide new insights into predicting and preventing the emergence of novel viral pathogens. The Department of Pathology has an excellent reputation in pure biological and biomedical research and is offering fully funded* studentships from October 2027 to work with one of the Research Group Leaders in this large and vibrant Department. All applications should be made online via the University's Applicant Portal for a PhD in Pathology (BLPA22). A completed application must be submitted by the closing date below. An application is only complete when all supporting documents, including the 2 academic references, are submitted. It is the applicant's responsibility to ensure their referees submit their references before the closing date of the studentship. The studentships are available to students who qualify for UK HOME FEES ONLY Please check for eligibility details using this website: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/finance/fees/what-my-fee-status Funding will cover the student's stipend at the current Research Council rate and Home University Fees. The studentship will be funded for 4 years from October 2027.\nApplicants should hold (or expect to obtain) the equivalent of a UK upper 2.1 or higher in an undergraduate honours or Masters degree in a relevant subject. The studentship is open to those eligible for the Home rate of University fees .  Additionally, the \nDepartment requires that by the time of interview all potential students must have fulfilled the language requirements for admission. ( http://www.graduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/blpapdpth/requirements ) Please also explain your motivation why you wish to pursue a PhD in this area, outline your research interests and background, and describe the qualities and experience you will bring to the role. Applications from ineligible candidates will not be considered. Further information about the course can be found here. Closing date: 2nd December 2026 Please quote reference PK51376 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Pathology Salary Reference PK51376 Category Studentships Date published 12 October 2026 Closing date 2 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
