      "title": "Physical Examination Skills Lead (Part-time; 1PA/week) (Secondment Opportunity)",
      "description": "Physical Examination Skills Lead (Part-time; 1PA/week) (Secondment Opportunity) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Physical Examination Skills Lead (Part-time; 1PA/week) (Secondment Opportunity) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Physical Examination Skills Lead (Part-time; 1PA/week) (Secondment Opportunity) Applications are invited for this new post. This new post will develop and lead the delivery of all aspects of physical examination skills teaching in the Cambridge Clinical Course.  This post has been created out of recognition that there needs to be overall academic and strategic oversight of the teaching of the core professional skill of physical examination, which is currently delivered across multiple parts of the course. The postholder will report to the Clinical Sub-Dean for Curriculum, and work closely with other members of the teaching team, in particular leads of the Undergraduate Clinical Supervisor Programme, the curriculum integration lead, the three Intimate examination skills leads and the student academic support lead. The postholder will work with the curriculum and assessment teams to maximise alignment between course intended learning outcomes, teaching and assessment of physical examination. This post is 1PA/week (0.1wte).  This is a three-year secondment, with the possibility of renewal for further periods of three years thereafter.  If the successful applicant is a GP, successful appointment would be on a fractional contract with the Cambridge School of Clinical Medicine.  The work will involve a mixture of in-person meetings at the Clinical School and remote working. All candidates for the post should hold full GMC registration, a license to practice and substantive NHS appointment as a Consultant doctor or a General Practitioner in the Eastern region, delivering patient-facing care that requires regular use of physical examination skills. The postholder will report to Prof Mark Lillicrap, Professor of Medical Education Practice and Sub-Dean for Curriculum.  For further information, interested candidates should contact Prof Lillicrap on msl29@medschl.cam.ac.uk . Fixed-term: 3 years Closing date: 18th October 2026 Interview Date: 9th December 2026 Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including e-mail address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RA51292 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Office of the School of Clinical Medicine Salary Clinical Salary Scale Reference RA51292 Category Academic Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/policy-consultant-ah51097": {
      "title": "Policy Consultant",
      "description": "Policy Consultant | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Policy Consultant Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Policy Consultant We are looking for an experienced Policy Consultant to join the University of Cambridge's Human Resources Division. This is an exciting opportunity to help shape and deliver HR policy across one of the world's leading universities, supporting policy development, stakeholder engagement, and cultural change initiatives that impact staff across the institution. We encourage early applications as they will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. About the role Reporting to the Strategic Project Consultant,  you will support the development, implementation and review of University-wide HR policies, ensuring they are legally compliant, aligned with strategic priorities and embedded effectively across the employee lifecycle. Working closely with HR colleagues, trade unions and stakeholders across the University, you will provide specialist policy advice, lead consultation activity, manage projects and contribute to the delivery of innovative people practices. What you will be doing Developing, reviewing and implementing HR policies and procedures. Providing advice and guidance on policy interpretation and application. Leading consultation and negotiation with trade unions and key stakeholders. Managing policy projects and coordinating cross-functional working groups. Preparing reports, committee papers, business cases and communications. Designing and delivering policy-related training, briefings and workshops. We are looking for someone who has Experience drafting and implementing HR policies in a complex or regulated environment. Strong knowledge of UK employment law and HR best practice. Experience working with trade unions and undertaking consultation and negotiation activity. Excellent written communication and report-writing skills. Strong project management and stakeholder engagement skills. CIPD qualification or equivalent experience. Why join us? Alongside working in a collaborative and supportive environment, the University of Cambridge offers a range of excellent benefits, including: Development opportunities such as LinkedIn Learning, job¿shadowing and career pathways. 41 days annual leave (including bank holidays) and a highly competitive pension scheme. Hybrid working with flexibility in how you arrange your office days. A wide range of discounts at local retailers and thousands of national brands. Health and wellbeing support, including discounted gym access and the Staff Counselling Service. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for flexible working arrangements. For this role, all applications will be managed by Spirehouse Recruitment. To apply, please send your CV to cambridge@spirehouse.co.uk For further information about the role, please contact Abbie Carr (Executive Consultant), abbie@spirehouse.co.uk Closing date: Friday 23 October 2026 Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Download further information Policy Consultant - Further Information Document Key information Department/location Human Resources Division Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference AH51097 Category Academic-related Date published 25 September 2026 Closing date 23 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-in-oceanography-fixed-term-le51157": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Research Associate in Oceanography (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Research Associate in Oceanography (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post Doctoral Research Associate in Oceanography (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post Doctoral Research Associate in Oceanography (Fixed Term) We invite applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate at the University of Cambridge. The successful applicant will join the Ocean Dynamics group ( https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/jrt51/ ) at the Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics under the supervision of Professor John Taylor and Associate Professor Oscar Branson. The post is available from 15 November 2026 or another mutually agreed start date. This project is part of a wider programme coordinated by Frontier Climate to study ocean alkalinity enhancement (OAE). The Cambridge team will build a high-resolution model of the near-field environment surrounding the site of the 2025 OAE field trial conducted by the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution. The team will work closely with Dr Adam Subhas and other colleagues to investigate potential alkalinity loss through secondary mineral precipitation. The simulations will also help assess sampling strategies. The simulations will use Oceananigans.jl ( https://github.com/Oceananigans ) to represent physical processes at the field site and OceanBioME.jl ( https://github.com/OceanBioME ) to model how dissolving and sinking particles interact with seawater carbon chemistry. Experience in ocean modelling, particularly with Oceananigans, is highly desirable. Knowledge of seawater carbon chemistry is desirable but not essential. The role holder will be expected to plan and manage their own research and work closely with others. They must be able to communicate technical material clearly and build internal and external collaborations. The post holder may also be asked to help supervise student projects, develop students' research skills, provide instruction, or plan and deliver seminars related to the research area. The successful candidate will have (or expect to soon be awarded) a PhD in Oceanography, Physics, or a related field and experience in ocean modelling. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries, please contact Professor John Taylor at J.R.Taylor@damtp.cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact: LE51157@maths.cam.ac.uk . Please indicate the contact details of two academic referees on the online application form and upload a full curriculum vitae, a list of publications and a description of your recent research (not to exceed two pages). Please ensure that at least one of your referees is contactable at any time during the selection process and is made aware that they will be contacted by the Mathematics HR Office Administrator to request that they upload a reference for you to our Web Recruitment System; and please encourage them to do so promptly. Fixed term: The funds for this post are available for 24 months in the first instance. Closing date: 19 October 2026 Please quote reference LE51157 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference LE51157 Category Research Date published 25 September 2026 Closing date 19 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-custom-optical-microscopy-development-fixed-term-ma50992": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Research Associate: Custom Optical Microscopy Development (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Research Associate: Custom Optical Microscopy Development (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post Doctoral Research Associate: Custom Optical Microscopy Development (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post Doctoral Research Associate: Custom Optical Microscopy Development (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 18 months in the first instance, if project milestone targets are met at 18 months, a further 18 months of funding will be allocated. Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) to develop custom optics integrated with microfluidic ultra-high-throughput screening (uHTS) for label free screening of phase separation in de novo designed inorganic-protein hybrid materials. Techniques developed include polarised optical microscopy (POM), fluorescence microscopy, and darkfield microscopy, coupled to droplet microfluidics for screening for libraries of 10K to >1M samples. Scientific objectives are to map the phase space of ordered inorganic hybrid materials. Screens will explore the multidimensional parameter sampling of reaction conditions and libraries of de novo inorganic-templating interfaces. The PDRA will be hosted in the group of Professor Knowles in the Department of Chemistry at Cambridge University, funded by an Advanced Research + Invention Agency (ARIA) international consortium. The PDRA will be responsible for designing, assembling, and maintaining microscopes which integrate polarisation-resolved and fluorescence optics with existing custom microfluidic droplet screening platforms including PhaseScan and cell free expression.\nTraining in microfluidics will be provided. The Knowles group brings expertise in droplet-based screening, soft matter physics, biophysics, and phase separation, and the post holder is not expected to arrive with that background. Consortium partners bring expertise in de novo protein design and high-throughput inorganic synthesis. Collaborating closely with these partners, the PDRA will design, build, and utilise systems to enable uHTS to be extended to de novo designed inorganic-protein composites, identifying and optimising conditions which produce functional hybrid materials. Further duties include contributing to the supervision of PhD and Master's students and assisting with the maintenance of the group's custom instruments. Candidate requirements: Demonstrated experience of building a bespoke optical microscope from individual components, including optical design, sourcing, assembly, alignment and characterisation of performance. Applicants must describe at least one instrument they have built and their specific personal contribution to it. A PhD (or be about to obtain) in Optical Engineering, Physics, Chemistry, Materials Science, Mineralogy, or a related discipline. The ideal candidate will possess: Experience studying the synthesis and/or characterisation of crystalline inorganic materials. A sound understanding of polarisation optics (e.g. Jones/Mueller calculus) and practical experience of polariser/analyser alignment. Knowledge of fluorescence optics, including excitation/emission filtering and the selection of dichroics and filter sets. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. For queries relating to your application or the application process, please contact Echo Wu via email on jw825@cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference MA50992 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The Department holds an Athena SWAN silver award for women in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics, and Medicine. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference MA50992 Category Research Date published 24 September 2026 Closing date 20 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-fixed-term-su51024": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate  (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate  (Fixed Term) We are seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) in auditory cognitive neuroscience based at the CBU (with its transition into CMBI). The PDRA will work on a Wellcome Trust funded project led by Dr Guangting Mai studying the neural basis of audiovisual speech processing in adult cochlear implant (CI) users. Main advisors/collaborators include Drs Matt Davis and Bob Carlyon (CBU) and Prof Doug Hartley (Nottingham BRC). The project aims to study how different sensory modalities (audition and vision) work together to support day-to-day speech comprehension by CI users. We also aim to address an imminent and critical clinical question of whether processing of visual speech (talker's lip movement during face-to-face communication) is associated with adaptative or maladaptative changes in auditory processing and audiovisual integration of speech during CI rehabilitation. We will address these questions through sets of cross-sectional and longitudinal experiments in adult CI users combining sophisticated brain imaging of electroencephalography (EEG) and high-density diffuse optical tomography (HD-DOT). Findings of the project would provide important implications for clinicians to consider appropriate strategies as to how patients should be instructed to use visual speech and what their impacts may be for speech and language development during CI rehabilitation. The findings may also provide evidence to use brain imaging to better predict post-implantation outcomes and/or guidance for neuromodulation to improve CI users' speech comprehension. An ideal candidate is expected to have a strong background (PhD in neuroscience, psychology or auditory/speech sciences) and extensive experience in conducting EEG/MEG and/or functional neuroimaging experiments (fNIRS/DOT/fMRI) in human participants. Experience in working with clinical populations (e.g., participants with hearing, speech or language disorders) is highly desirable. Relevant analyses expertise (including Matlab or Python programming) will also be a strong asset. Informal inquiries about the post can be sent to guangting.mai@mrc-cbu.cam.ac.uk Funding for this post is available for a fixed term of 3 years full time or extended pro rata for part time (preferably at least 0.7 FTE). Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment, a security check and a standard Disclosure and Barring Service check. This appointment also requires a Research Passport application. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 16th October 2026 Interview Date: to be confirmed (expected November 2026) Please quote reference SU51024 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location MRC Cognition and Brain Sciences Unit Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference SU51024 Category Research Date published 14 September 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-fixed-term-pp51339": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate  (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate  (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Applications are invited for a postdoctoral position to start from January 2027 or as soon as possible after this date. This project is funded by a grant from the ERC and aims to elucidate mechanisms of antibacterial and antiviral immunity in birds.\nThe main focus of the research will be to characterise the innate immune complexes which form in avian cells in response to infection and to discover how they resemble or differ from human systems. Recent advances have found a critical role for protein concentration in the formation of such molecular assemblies which requires investigation of genes using precise gene editing. Isolation of signalling complexes will be followed by proteomic analysis of their contents in a time- and stimulus-dependent manner.\nApplicants should have a PhD in cell biology, evolutionary biology, immunology or a related discipline. Essential wet lab skills will be experience with cell culture, CRISPR-based gene editing and in vitro infection studies. A good understanding of human and avian innate immunity and evolutionary bioinformatics would be an advantage. The project is in collaboration with other postdocs within and outside the department. The candidate must value teamwork and collaboration, and have good administrative, interpersonal and presentational skills.\nWe are committed to a proactive approach to equality, which includes supporting and encouraging equally all groups of individuals, promoting an inclusive culture and valuing diversity. Further particulars for the role and information about the Department www.vet.cam.ac.uk Informal enquiries should be directed to Julie Ingham email ji217@cam.ac.uk Applicants should submit a CHRIS/6, CV, covering letter outlining suitability for the role and contact details for two references. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV) and a covering letter in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents, which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Closing date: 21 October 2026 Interviews will be held: 3 November 2026 Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Enquiries about the application process can be directed to hr.enquiries@vet.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PP51339 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PP51339 Category Research Date published 7 October 2026 Closing date 21 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-fixed-term-pc51215": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate position to join the research group of Dr Felipe Karam Teixeira ( https://www.gen.cam.ac.uk/research-groups/research-groups/karam-teixeira ) in the Department of Genetics at the University of Cambridge. The Project We are seeking an outstanding and highly motivated scientist to investigate the molecular mechanisms that drive germline differentiation in vivo. Using the Drosophila female germline as a model, the successful candidate will explore how multiple layers of gene regulation coordinate the developmental switches required for oocyte formation.\nOur group combines high-throughput molecular and genomic, including short-read and long-read sequencing, with computational analysis, developmental biology, advanced microscopy, and Drosophila genetics. Experimental approaches may include CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing, tissue-specific RNA interference, transgenesis, transcriptomic and epigenomic profiling, immunofluorescence, single-molecule RNA imaging, and quantitative confocal microscopy. Our recent work generated a systematic roadmap of transcriptional and translational changes that accompany germline stem cell differentiation in vivo (Samuels et al., EMBO Journal 2024; Samuels et al., Development 2025). This work revealed that differentiation does not occur through the simple accumulation of molecular changes. Instead, it is driven by distinct, developmentally ordered waves of gene-expression remodelling that progressively transform a germline stem cell into a terminally differentiated cell. Building on these findings, the successful candidate will investigate how transcriptional, post-transcriptional, translational, and chromatin-associated mechanisms cooperate to control key developmental switches during oogenesis. The position offers substantial scope for the successful candidate to shape the project's direction, introduce complementary approaches, and develop an independent scientific profile. Candidate profile Applicants should hold, or be close to completing, a PhD in molecular biology, genetics, developmental biology, biochemistry, genomics, or a related discipline. We encourage candidates with research experience in germline, stem cell, or developmental biology. Prior expertise in next-generation sequencing-based methods to interrogate gene expression/chromatin (RNA-seq, ATAC-seq, ChIP-seq, CUT&Tag, Hi-C) and/or confocal microscopy (IF, smFISH, or HCR) would be particularly valuable. Previous experience with Drosophila genetics would be advantageous but is not essential, as appropriate training can be provided. We therefore welcome applications from excellent candidates working in other experimental systems who wish to apply their expertise to an experimentally tractable in vivo model of stem cell differentiation.\nWe are looking for candidates who can demonstrate a solid academic and research record appropriate to their career stage; the ability to design, execute, and troubleshoot complex experiments; rigorous and quantitative approaches to data analysis; evidence of scientific creativity and intellectual independence; excellent written and verbal communication skills; and the ability to work effectively in an interdisciplinary and collaborative research environment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Research environment and development The successful candidate will join a collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment spanning genetics, developmental biology, genomics, microscopy, and computational biology. The postholder will be encouraged to contribute intellectually to the direction of the research, present findings at international conferences, develop collaborations, participate in manuscript preparation, and pursue appropriate fellowship and career-development opportunities. Duration The funds for this post are available for three years in the first instance. The preferred start date is January/February 2027, although some flexibility may be possible. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be addressed to Dr Felipe Karam-Teixeira fk319@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PC51215 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Genetics Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PC51215 Category Research Date published 30 September 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-fixed-term-go51218": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) The Leverhulme Centre for the Future of Intelligence (CFI) at the Institute for Technology and Humanity are inviting applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate. This is an exciting opportunity to work within a research project in trustworthy AI, led by Professor Adrian Weller. This is a fixed-term post where funding is available for two years. The research project investigates how to develop fundamental theory and methods that enable machine learning systems to be deployed in a trustworthy manner. The project spans human-AI interaction, fairness, transparency, privacy and other approaches to optimising deployed AI systems as human-machine teams. The postdoc will conduct research developing and evaluating methods at the frontier of trustworthy AI, validating them on real-world problems in partnership with domain experts and stakeholders, and publishing and presenting results at leading venues in machine learning (such as NeurIPS, ICML and ICLR), AI ethics and society (such as FAccT and AIES), as well as policy fora. Applicants should have completed, or be near completion of, a PhD in Computer Science, Information Engineering, Statistics, Machine Learning or a closely related field, with expertise in one or more areas of trustworthy AI, such as human-AI interaction, fairness, transparency and interpretability, privacy, or robustness. A strong understanding of machine learning methods, together with excellent mathematical and programming skills, is required. CFI is a highly interdisciplinary research centre addressing the challenges and opportunities posed by artificial intelligence (AI). Based at the University of Cambridge, the Centre has close links with industry, policymakers, and many academic disciplines. The role holder will also be able to have a desk in the Machine Learning Group in Engineering. In order for your application to be considered, please upload the following: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Covering Letter explaining your interest in and aptitude for the position Sample of professional writing Names and contact details of two referees who are familiar with your work If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and should be directed to Professor Adrian Weller at aw665@cam.ac.uk . For questions on the application process, please contact the School's HR Team on sahhr@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is midnight (GMT) on Sunday 08 November 2026. Interviews are planned for the second half of November, subject to change. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference GO51218 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Cambridge Institute for Technology and Humanity Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference GO51218 Category Research Date published 1 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
