      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Fixed Term) The Deane and Rayner laboratories at the Cambridge Institute for Medical Research are recruiting a Postdoctoral Research Associate to work as part of an ongoing team project that explores the function of a multi-gene family that is expanded in the genomes of Plasmodium vivax and other related neglected human malaria parasites. The project builds on a recent publication that established that these tryptophan-rich antigens (TRAgs) have lipid binding activity (PMID: 37709739). This is a short-term position to produce high-quality purified TRAg proteins for structural and functional studies. It will be absolutely essential that the successful candidate has demonstrable experience in: protein expression using HEK293F-based mammalian expression systems; structure determination including X-ray crystallography of protein-ligand complexes; and biophysical characterisation of protein interactions such as using biolayer interferometry or isothermal titration calorimetry. Additional desirable skills include computational modelling of protein complexes and protein-ligand complexes as well as experience with molecular biology approaches for cloning and mutagenesis of new expression constructs. An understanding of how pathogens hijack host-cell pathways will also be advantageous. The successful applicant will also have demonstrated their capacity to produce high-quality data by having first author peer-reviewed publications. The primary focus will be on carrying out outstanding academic research which will contribute directly to collaborative papers. The project provides substantial scope for innovation, creativity and exercising scientific judgement. You will work as part of a vibrant interdisciplinary team with colleagues in both the Rayner and Deane laboratories in the Cambridge Institute for Medical Research. You are expected to contribute to that positive team environment, supporting and training others where needed and working together to create the best possible outputs within the time available. The Cambridge Institute for Medical Research (CIMR) is located on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus and it is a collaborative institute at the interface between fundamental research and the clinic, with a mission to determine the molecular mechanisms of disease in order to advance human health. This full-time fixed-term post is available for 6 months from start date. Informal enquiries to Janet Deane jed55@cam.ac.uk Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 8th November 2026 Interview Date: to be confirmed Please quote reference SD51362 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location C.I.M.R. Clinical Neurosciences Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference SD51362 Category Research Date published 9 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-honorary-resident-doctor-fixed-term-rr51252": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Fixed Term) This is a unique opportunity to carry out a period of clinical postdoctoral research focused on developing and validating new treatments and biomarkers for mitochondrial disorders, as part of a new national rare disease centre funded by LifeArc and MDUK. The post is specifically aimed at practicing clinicians with a PhD and relevant specialty training, who have extensive prior clinical and research experience with neuromuscular or neurogenetic conditions. The Cambridge Clinical Mitochondrial Research Group (MitoCAMB) studies the genetic basis, clinical presentation and molecular mechanisms of mitochondrial diseases and other rare inherited neurodegenerative/neuromuscular disorders. Our research group is embedded within the Department of Clinical Neurosciences and the MRC Mitochondrial Biology Unit, University of Cambridge. We develop new and effective treatments for affected patients through investigator-led experimental medicine studies, novel gene therapy approaches, and early phase clinical trials. The group was recently awarded a £7.5 million LifeArc Translational Rare Disease Centres Award for the next 5 years with our partners at UCL, the University of Newcastle, the Lily Foundation and MDUK.  Together, we have established a national platform to translate novel treatment strategies and biomarkers into clinical trials. This platform will improve mitochondrial disease diagnosis nation-wide, connecting patient groups, knowledge and infrastructure to accelerate the clinical translation of new treatments.  We have an extensive pre-clinical discovery science programme focussed on understanding the genetic basis of undiagnosed mitochondrial and neurogenetic disorders. Our laboratory research is translational, generating new insights that we develop for clinical impact through MitoCAMB. We are recruiting a Postdoctoral Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) under the supervision of Professor Patrick Chinnery, Dr Jelle van den Ameele and Professor Rita Horvath.  The post-holder will support and drive several ongoing and new academic-led and commercial studies and clinical trials in mitochondrial and neurodegenerative diseases. They will receive extensive support, supervision and training where required, but will be expected to have a high level of independence and enthusiasm to proactively take responsibility for ongoing and new clinical studies. This will also give them the opportunity to develop clinical, laboratory and computational skills that would underpin their own emerging research programme. The post-holder will have the opportunity to participate in the mitochondrial/neurogenetics NHS clinic at Addenbrooke's Hospital and additional NHS services. The successful applicant must have a PhD and have completed general medical training, hold MRCP or equivalent, and preferably be a registered medical practitioner permitted to practise in the UK or be willing to work towards this.  We encourage applications from any medical specialty and from any career grade, but clear evidence of prior clinical and research interest in neuromuscular or neurogenetic conditions is desirable. Enquiries for further information are welcome and should be sent to Professor Patrick Chinnery, or Dr Jelle van den Ameele or Professor Rita Horvath at: pfc25@medschl.cam.ac.uk , jv361@medschl.cam.ac.uk or rh732@medschl.cam.ac.uk Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Closing Date: 1st November 2026 Interview Date: TBC This is an integrated appointment between the University and Cambridge University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust (\"NHS Trust\").  You will therefore also be issued with an Honorary Clinical Contract by the NHS Trust for which an Enhanced DBS check will be required. You should be aware that if your contract with the NHS Trust is terminated your employment with the University will be terminated, and vice versa. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter, CV and a completed Additional Information for Clinical Researchers form in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RR51252 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Clinical Researcher - Additional Information Form Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Clinical Neurosciences Salary £40,190-£76,582 Reference RR51252 Category Research Date published 30 September 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-in-mathematical-and-statistical-methods-for-genome-privacy-fixed-le51229": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mathematical and Statistical Methods for Genome Privacy (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mathematical and Statistical Methods for Genome Privacy (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mathematical and Statistical Methods for Genome Privacy (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mathematical and Statistical Methods for Genome Privacy (Fixed Term) We invite applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate in Mathematical and Statistical Methods for Genome Privacy in the Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics at the University of Cambridge. The successful candidate will join the research group of Dr Gamze Gursoy and work on developing new mathematical and computational methods for privacy-preserving human pangenome references. Human pangenome references provide increasingly rich representations of human genetic diversity, but their scale and structure also raise important questions about how information contributed by individuals can be protected. This project will develop new approaches at the intersection of graph algorithms, statistical methodology, optimization, privacy, and computational genomics, with the broader goal of enabling useful population-scale genomic references while limiting disclosure of sensitive information about the individuals represented within them. Depending on the successful candidate's interests and background, research directions may include differentially private construction and analysis of pangenome graphs, privacy-aware graph algorithms and representations, statistical characterization of privacy-utility trade-offs, optimization of algorithms for large genomic graphs, and development and benchmarking of methods on human pangenome data. There will be considerable freedom for the successful candidate to help shape the scientific direction of the project. We are particularly interested in candidates with a strong background in one or more of the following areas: - statistics; - graph theory or graph algorithms; - mathematical or numerical optimization; - differential privacy or related areas of data privacy; - algorithms and theoretical computer science; - computational genomics, bioinformatics, or pangenomics. We do not expect candidates to be experts in all of these areas. Applicants with deep expertise in one area and a strong interest in learning and working across the others are strongly encouraged to apply. In particular, we welcome applications from statistics, mathematics, optimization, privacy, or algorithms who are excited about applying their methods to fundamental problems in human genomics, as well as researchers from computational genomics who are interested in developing expertise in privacy. The project is highly interdisciplinary. The successful candidate will have opportunities to collaborate with Professor Po-Ling Loh in the Statistical Laboratory, Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics (DPMMS), particularly on statistical, optimization, and differential privacy aspects of the work. There will also be opportunities for interaction and collaboration with researchers in the broader Human Pangenome Project community. The postholder will be expected to develop and manage their own research with guidance from the PI, contribute actively to the intellectual life of the research group, and communicate their work through publications, presentations, seminars, and conferences. They will join a collaborative group working broadly across computational genomics, statistical genetics, privacy-preserving computation, and trustworthy methods for genomic and biomedical data. Applicants should have completed, or be close to completing, a PhD in statistics, mathematics, computer science, computational biology, bioinformatics, genomics, or a related field. Evidence of strong quantitative, computational, or methodological research is more important than prior experience in any particular application area. Strong programming skills and an interest in interdisciplinary research are desirable. The funding requires that candidates must not have been UK residents during the 24 months before the start date, nor be currently employed by a UK organisation or its overseas campuses. The appointment is for two years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please indicate the contact details (including their email addresses) of two academic referees on the online application form and upload a full curriculum vitae and a description of your recent research (not to exceed two pages) . Please ensure that at least one of your referees is contactable at any time during the selection process and is made aware that they will be contacted by the Mathematics HR Office Administrator to request that they upload a reference for you to our Web Recruitment System; and please encourage them to do so promptly. For informal enquiries, please contact Dr Gamze Gursoy at gg584@cam.ac.uk . If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact: LE51229@maths.cam.ac.uk . Start date: As soon as possible, or by negotiation. Interviews will be held soon after the closing date. Please quote reference LE51229 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference LE51229 Category Research Date published 6 October 2026 Closing date 31 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/principal-application-specialist-cognosdatamart-developer-fixed-term-vc51313": {
      "title": "Principal Application Specialist (Cognos/DataMart Developer) (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Principal Application Specialist (Cognos/DataMart Developer) (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Principal Application Specialist (Cognos/DataMart Developer) (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Principal Application Specialist (Cognos/DataMart Developer) (Fixed Term) The Finance and HR departments at the University enhances their reports with IBM Cognos Analytics reports. The design of the DataMarts uses dimensional modelling, largely using ETL. Oracle PL/SQL is the predominant tool in building the DataMarts, but there is some ODI use, primarily for scheduling builds. The Principal Application Specialist will take a leading role in Oracle data engineering, supporting system integrations and reporting processes. The role holder will work closely with stakeholders across the University to ensure that data contained within enterprise systems is surfaced reliably and consistently, collaborating with developers and system maintainers to support system integrations and federated reporting. The role holder will help build a taxonomy of information concepts and data entities which are relied upon for critical University business - helping to document the existing data landscape and connect the dots between business process and data engineering. The role holder will lead a combination of 2nd/3rd line operational support and project and programme activities in line with strategic initiatives. What you will be doing: Be a subject matter expert in the DataMart Reporting applications within assigned business areas. Lead the development of new ETL pipelines using Oracle Data Integrator and PL/SQL to extract and transform data sourced from enterprise systems into the University's managed Oracle DataMart.¿ Review business requirements and use these to design and develop suitable technical software solutions using a wide variety of methods, software products and software tools. Oversee testing of completed software, e.g. through user training, and the production of documentation. Migrate fixes, patches and other developments between development environments in accordance with relevant change control processes. Provide quality assurance for data extract and transform activities, reviewing pipelines and documentation to ensure that data lineage is ensured. Support the operational availability of the data engineering technology stack, leading patching activities, resolving technical debt and contributing to improved observability and monitoring of pipelines. Provide 2nd and 3rd line support for data load and transform activities supporting system integrations and reporting, including operational oversight of the University's IBM Cognos instance. Document the existing data engineering landscape, information architecture and data entities, and contributing to the backlog of continuous improvement activities. Drive organizational evolution by enhancing operational efficiency, expanding automation capabilities, and systematically minimizing technical debt. What you will bring: Experience of ODI version 12.2.4.1 or above. In-depth knowledge of Oracle PL/SQL. In particular, the ability to analyse and quickly correct DataMart build issues. A good understanding of DataMart design. Able to create and maintain IBM Cognos Framework Manager models/packages. It would be great if you had: Ability to learn new skills independently and proactively and are constantly striving to raise technical excellence. Ability to debug complex problems effectively, including in new unexplored applications. Drive and commitment to see activities through to completion meeting deadlines matching pace to the urgency of the situation. Ensure that business requirements and processes are fully understood and clearly documented. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department/institution/Faculty/School/University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online Application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. The closing date for applications is: Sunday 18th October 2026 The interview date for the role is: To be confirmed Please quote reference VC51313 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location University Information Services Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference VC51313 Category Academic-related Date published 6 October 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/pro-vice-chancellor-innovation-ae51267": {
      "title": "Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Innovation)",
      "description": "Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Innovation) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Innovation) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Innovation) The University of Cambridge is one of the world's leading universities, renowned for the excellence of its teaching, research and innovation. Our distinctive collegiate University brings together a global community of scholars, students and professional staff who are committed to advancing knowledge, addressing the world's most pressing challenges, and making a positive impact on society. With more than 24,000 students and over 12,000 staff, Cambridge combines a tradition of academic excellence with a commitment to fostering an inclusive, supportive and ambitious community. The Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Innovation) is an exciting opportunity to play a pivotal role in enabling entrepreneurial activity across the University to deliver Cambridge's innovation agenda. As a member of the University's senior leadership team, you will oversee the infrastructure, policies and resources that support members of the University to translate and commercialise their discoveries and will build on Cambridge's position as a global hub for discovery and enterprise. The Pro-Vice-Chancellor will be supported by the Office of Research and Innovation. The successful candidate will be a recognised leader in the academic and innovation communities, with strong academic background (including a PhD) and an outstanding personal track record. The successful individual will have experience of building and leading academic-industry partnerships and collaborations, working effectively with professional services colleagues to deliver institutional priorities, and achieving outcomes that create measurable value for institutions, partners and society. An inclusive leadership style, strong collaborative skills, and the ability to inspire, motivate and align colleagues around a shared vision will be highly valued. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date for applications is Monday 26 October 2026. Stakeholder meetings are anticipated to take place during the week commencing 9 November 2026, with interviews scheduled for Monday 16 November and Thursday 19 November 2026. If you would like an informal conversation about the role, please contact the Vice-Chancellor's Office at VCO.Enquiries@admin.cam.ac.uk If you have any queries about the recruitment process, please contact Rachel Hughes,\nRecruitment Adviser, Senior Professional Appointments at Rachel.Hughes@admin.cam.ac.uk Apply online Download further information Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Innovation) - Further Information Key information Department/location Vice-Chancellor's Office Salary Reference AE51267 Category Professorships and Directorships Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 26 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/pro-vice-chancellor-staff-and-community-ae51282": {
      "title": "Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Staff and Community)",
      "description": "Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Staff and Community) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Staff and Community) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Staff and Community) The University of Cambridge is one of the world's leading universities, renowned for the excellence of its teaching, research and innovation. Our distinctive collegiate University brings together a global community of scholars, students and professional staff who are committed to advancing knowledge, addressing the world's most pressing challenges, and making a positive impact on society. With more than 24,000 students and over 12,000 staff, Cambridge combines a tradition of academic excellence with a commitment to fostering an inclusive, supportive and ambitious community. The Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Staff and Community) is an exciting opportunity to lead the development and implementation of strategy and policy relating to all staff (academic and professional services). You will be an inclusive and inspirational leader, who is committed to ensuring that all staff can flourish in their role and to building and sustaining trust in the University's policies and processes. This senior academic will work across academic departments, human resources and other central offices, and the unions and will liaise with the Colleges to ensure that policies and processes are fit for purpose, understood, trusted and consistently applied. The Pro-Vice-Chancellor will be supported by the Human Resources Division. The successful candidate will be a recognised leader in the academic community, with an outstanding personal track record, and inclusive approach to leadership and the ability to inspire colleagues to make a difference in how staff feel about working at the University. The successful individual will have direct experience in the selection, recruitment, development and retention of academic and professional services staff and a proven record of managing people and achieving outcomes that create measurable value for institutions. A keen understanding of how the Collegiate University works and the ability to work across it to join up parts will be highly valued. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date for applications is Monday 26 October 2026. Stakeholder meetings are anticipated to take place during the week commencing 9 November 2026, with interviews scheduled for Monday 16 November and Thursday 19 November 2026. If you would like an informal conversation about the role, please contact the Vice-Chancellor's Office at VCO.Enquiries@admin.cam.ac.uk If you have any queries about the recruitment process, please contact Rachel Hughes,\nRecruitment Adviser, Senior Professional Appointments at Rachel.Hughes@admin.cam.ac.uk Apply online Download further information Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Staff and Community) - Further Information Key information Department/location Vice-Chancellor's Office Salary Reference AE51282 Category Professorships and Directorships Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 26 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/professorship-of-mechanical-engineering-1997-nm51179": {
      "title": "Professorship of Mechanical Engineering (1997)",
      "description": "Professorship of Mechanical Engineering (1997) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Professorship of Mechanical Engineering (1997) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Professorship of Mechanical Engineering (1997) The Board of Electors to the Professorship of Mechanical Engineering (1997) invite applications for this Professorship to take up appointment on 1 October 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. The Department of Engineering is one of the few truly integrated engineering departments in the world. It is also the largest department in the University of Cambridge, with this breadth and scale bringing about unique advantages. The research portfolio develops pinnacles of world-class excellence, which adapt and combine to address a vast array of engineering challenges. Cambridge is consistently ranked as a world leader in Mechanical Engineering. Candidates will have an outstanding research record of international stature in mechanical engineering. They will provide vision, leadership, experience, and enthusiasm to the Engineering Department's Mechanics, Materials and Design Division to maintain and develop a leading research presence. Candidates will hold a PhD or equivalent postgraduate qualification. The following potential research areas are highlighted: experimental mechanics, robotics, energy materials, medical devices, dynamics and vibrations and modelling and simulation, although applicants in other research areas within the broad field of mechanical engineering with an emphasis on solids are also welcomed. Standard professorial duties include teaching and research, examining, supervision and administration. The Professor will be based in Cambridge. A competitive salary will be offered. The University of Cambridge will be advised and supported in this appointment process by their executive search partner Perrett Laver. For further details, including the job description, person specification, and information on how to apply, please visit https://plusportal.perrettlaver.com/ quoting the reference number 8564. Applications should consist of: a covering letter explaining how you meet the criteria in the Person Specification (see Further Particulars); a curriculum vitae (CV); a full publications list; a statement of no more than three pages in length covering professional, teaching and research experience and your future research plans; details of three referees. The closing date for applications is 09.00am (BST) on Friday 30th October 2026. For an informal and confidential discussion about this position, please contact Calum Convey at Perrett Laver via calum.convey@perrettlaver.com . The Board of Electors will meet soon after the closing date to produce a short list of candidates. Shortlisted candidates will be invited to the Department to take part in informal meetings with staff, give a short seminar/lecture and attend a formal interview. This process is expected to conclude in early 2027. Please quote reference NM51179 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary Reference NM51179 Category Professorships and Directorships Date published 24 September 2026 Closing date 30 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/professorship-of-veterinary-science-pp51038": {
      "title": "Professorship of Veterinary Science",
      "description": "Professorship of Veterinary Science | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Professorship of Veterinary Science Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Professorship of Veterinary Science The Board of Electors to the Professorship of Veterinary Science invite applications for this Professorship from persons whose work is connected with veterinary science (ideally in research aligned with One Health) to take up appointment in 2027. In addition to serving as Professor of Veterinary Science, the postholder will serve as Head of Department of Veterinary Medicine (for five years in the first instance, with the option for renewal), providing strategic vision, intellectual direction, and transformational leadership for one of the world's leading centres of veterinary teaching, research, and clinical excellence. The Department of Veterinary Medicine is the Veterinary School of the University of Cambridge, the highest-rated research university in the United Kingdom, and one of the top five research universities in the world. The Department has a national and international reputation for excellence in veterinary science and education and recently ranked fourth in the world in the Top QS Universities rankings. With a renewed commitment to veterinary education from the University, the Department is entering a period of significant change and renewal, providing an opportunity for the new Head to shape the future of veterinary medicine at Cambridge and reinforce its position as a global leader in veterinary science, research and clinical innovation. Candidates will have an outstanding research record of international stature in veterinary science or a related area, together with demonstrable academic and research leadership experience, including substantial experience of leading successful research programmes, delivering high-quality teaching, and fostering excellence across research, education and ideally also clinical service environments. Candidates should also bring demonstrable experience leading, or contributing significantly to, successful external accreditation processes. The ideal candidate will bring an understanding of veterinary medicine and the evolving needs of the veterinary profession, together with the vision, ambition and enthusiasm to lead the Department through transformational change and renewal. They will hold a PhD or equivalent postgraduate qualification. Standard professorial duties include teaching and research, examining, supervision and administration. As Head of Department, the postholder will provide strategic and intellectual leadership, setting the Department's academic direction and long-term vision. They will foster a culture of excellence, innovation, collegiality and inclusive, values-driven leadership across teaching, research and clinical domains.  The Professor will be based in Cambridge.  A competitive salary will be offered. More information, including a detailed role description and person specification, and how to apply is available at: https://opportunities.mcleanpartnership.com/vetsci/ or contact Dr Graham Little at McLean Partnership on +44 (0)203 725 3757 or via email at grahamlittle@mcleanpartnership.com . Applications, consisting of a letter of application, a statement of current and future research plans, a curriculum vitae and a publications list, along with details of three referees should be sent no later than midnight on 19 October 2026. Please quote reference PP51038 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary Reference PP51038 Category Professorships and Directorships Date published 10 September 2026 Closing date 19 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/programme-coordinator-centre-for-climate-repair-fixed-term-le51260": {
      "title": "Programme Coordinator, Centre for Climate Repair (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Programme Coordinator, Centre for Climate Repair (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Programme Coordinator, Centre for Climate Repair (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Programme Coordinator, Centre for Climate Repair (Fixed Term) About us The Centre for Climate Repair is a multi-disciplinary Centre hosted in the Department of Applied Mathematics & Theoretical Physics (DAMTP) led by a Director and three Deputy Directors who, together with support from a University Board, provides strategic oversight and direction of the Centre. The Centre for Climate Repair (CCR) is focused on supporting research to further knowledge in three main areas: emissions Reduction; greenhouse gas Removal; and Refreezing the Arctic or Climate Interventions. However, most of the research activities are in the Removal and Refreeze area. The Centre develops interdisciplinary research projects in these areas, raises funds for the projects, and then oversees their delivery. The programmes/projects that fall under CCR's umbrella each contain research staff and students, each with their own budgets and grant milestones/deadlines. The Centre's Programme Coordinators will work to oversee and manage the programmes to ensure their successful delivery and completion. The role The successful candidate will play an important role in supporting the delivery and coordination of key research programmes, working closely with the Senior Programme Coordinator and Project Leads. This is an exciting opportunity for an organised, proactive and collaborative individual to contribute to a range of programme activities and help ensure that projects are delivered effectively and successfully. The role will provide a broad range of administrative and project coordination responsibilities, including coordinating meetings and events, supporting grant reporting, and helping to deliver programme-related activities. Working with colleagues and stakeholders across the Centre, the postholder will have the opportunity to build strong relationships and gain experience across a diverse portfolio of research activity. Around 50% of the role will be dedicated to supporting the Communications team, under the supervision of the Communications Coordinator. This will involve contributing to the delivery of CCR's communications and engagement strategy across the Centre's activities, helping to raise the profile of its research, programmes and impact and ensuring that its work reaches a wide range of audiences. This is a varied and engaging opportunity to contribute across both programme coordination and communications, playing an important role in supporting the delivery, visibility and impact of CCR's work. About you We are looking for an organised, proactive and enthusiastic individual who enjoys working across a varied range of projects and activities. You will have excellent organisational and communication skills, strong attention to detail and the ability to manage competing priorities and deadlines. You will be a collaborative and approachable colleague who can build positive relationships with colleagues and stakeholders, while also working independently and taking initiative. An interest in research, communications and engagement, together with a flexible and collaborative approach, will be important in supporting the successful delivery and promotion of CCR's work. All CCR staff follow a hybrid working pattern, and although CCR is located within the Department of Applied Maths and Theoretical Physics (DAMTP) on Wilberforce Road, some travel to the Department of Engineering on Trumpington Street will be required. A scientific background is not required for this role, but an interest in supporting research in this area would be beneficial. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal inquiries can be made by contacting Ari Brooks (Senior Programme Coordinator) at eb792@cam.ac.uk . If you have any queries regarding the application process, please email LE51260@maths.cam.ac.uk . In the Suitability for the Role section, please describe how your skills and experience meet the selection criteria as described in the Further Particulars, providing specific and relevant examples to illustrate this. Please indicate the contact details of two professional referees (including e-mail addresses); please note that we will assume that you are happy for us to approach your referees at any stage unless you indicate otherwise in the space provided on the form. Closing date for application is 01 November 2026. We will be reviewing applications for this position on a regular basis. We encourage you to apply as soon as possible as we may call you for a tour of the site and meetings with the team before the closing date. Interviews will take place as soon as possible following the closing date. Limit of tenure: The funds for this post are available until 31st December 2027 in the first instance, and the post may, subject to the availability of funding, be further renewed for a period. Please quote reference LE51260 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference LE51260 Category Assistant staff Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/programme-lead-in-autism-and-physical-health-at-the-k-lisa-yang-centre-for-autism-research-rn51296": {
      "title": "Programme Lead in Autism and Physical Health",
      "description": "Programme Lead in Autism and Physical Health at the K. Lisa Yang Centre for Autism Research | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Programme Lead in Autism and Physical Health at the K. Lisa Yang Centre for Autism Research Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Programme Lead in Autism and Physical Health at the K. Lisa Yang Centre for Autism Research The newly created K. Lisa Yang (KLY) Centre for Autism Research at Cambridge seeks to appoint an outstanding Assistant Research Professor (Grade 9) to lead the Autism and Physical Health Programme within the KLY Centre. This is an exceptional opportunity for an ambitious researcher to establish and develop an internationally recognised programme addressing the significant physical health inequalities experienced by autistic people. Autistic people die, on average, 16 years earlier than non-autistic people and experience elevated rates of physical health conditions across nine organ systems. The programme will investigate the causes of this premature mortality, including genetic and lifestyle risk factors and inequalities in access to healthcare. Using large-scale epidemiological health records, population cohorts and family-based international datasets, the postholder will lead research to identify risks associated with mortality, including cardiovascular disease, cancer and diabetes, among autistic people and those with higher numbers of autistic traits, regardless of formal diagnosis. By linking complementary datasets, the programme will investigate how physical health and mortality risks vary by sex, age at diagnosis and biological clusters identified through the Precision in Autism Diagnosis programme. The programme has a strong translational focus, aiming to prevent avoidable premature death through improved identification of health risks, more effective screening and better access to healthcare. The postholder will work with collaborators, clinicians, policymakers and the autistic community to ensure findings inform policy and practice, while shaping the programme's future direction. The successful candidate will provide scientific leadership for the Autism and Physical Health Programme and contribute to the wider development of the KLY Centre. They will lead high-quality research, develop competitive grant applications, supervise research staff, build national and international collaborations, and support the translation of findings into policy and healthcare improvements. Applicants should have: (1) a PhD in a relevant discipline; (2) a strong track record of internationally recognised research in autism, physical health, health inequalities, epidemiology or population health; (3) an excellent publication record appropriate to career stage; (4) evidence of obtaining or substantially contributing to external research funding; and (5) strong quantitative, methodological and analytical skills. Experience of leading research using health records, population cohorts, linked administrative data, family-based datasets or other large-scale health data is essential. Experience of interdisciplinary collaboration, community engagement, and translating research findings into health policy or clinical practice is desirable. The K. Lisa Yang Centre for Autism Research at Cambridge is part of the global Yang Tan Collective, alongside sister centres at Harvard and MIT. The Centre is closely linked to the Autism Research Centre (ARC) in Cambridge (see www.autismresearchcentre.com ) and contributes to a collaborative international effort to accelerate discovery and translation in autism research. Please submit a CV (including a full publication list), a cover letter outlining your suitability for the role, a research vision statement (maximum three pages) describing your plans for the Autism and Physical Health Research Programme over the next five years, and contact details for two academic referees. Applications close at midnight on 18 October 2026. For further information, contact Professor Simon Baron-Cohen at sb205@cam.ac.uk . Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 5 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and a CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. The closing date for applications is: 18th October 2026 The interview date for the role is: TBC Please quote reference RN51296 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Psychiatry Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference RN51296 Category Research Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/purchasing-and-facility-technician-rc51379": {
      "title": "Purchasing and Facility Technician",
      "description": "Purchasing and Facility Technician | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Purchasing and Facility Technician Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Purchasing and Facility Technician Cambridge University's Victor Phillip Dahdaleh Heart & Lung Research Institute (VPD-HLRI) is seeking a dedicated and detail-oriented Purchasing and Facilities Technician to join our team at the forefront of biomedical research. In this pivotal role, you will be responsible for receiving, inspecting, and recording all incoming goods, ensuring that vital laboratory and operational supplies are managed efficiently. You will assist with regular stock checks and support all services running through the Goods In department, maintaining accurate inventory records to facilitate the smooth operation of our institute. Your responsibilities will extend beyond inventory management, as you will also support the Laboratory and Buildings team with a range of operational tasks. These include ensuring a steady supply of clean laboratory glassware, helping to move equipment as needed, setting up meeting rooms for various events, and assisting with general building maintenance. As an integral member of our Building Services Team, your contribution will help sustain a facility that delivers research with a real and meaningful impact on global health. At VPD-HLRI, you will be joining a dynamic and collaborative team within the Department of Medicine, working alongside over 400 dedicated staff members. Our institute is committed to delivering research that addresses some of the most pressing challenges in cardiovascular and respiratory diseases worldwide. Within our impressive, sustainably designed facility - which houses advanced laboratories, a Clinical Research Facility, office spaces, and versatile seminar and conference rooms - you will find an environment that encourages learning and professional growth. This is an exciting opportunity to work with experienced professionals and play a crucial role in supporting world-leading research. If you are proactive, organised, and enthusiastic about helping us maintain the highest standards in facility operations, we invite you to apply and contribute to our mission of improving global heart and lung health. We encourage early applications as they will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RC51379 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Medicine Salary £25,249-£27,319 Reference RC51379 Category Assistant staff Date published 12 October 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/quality-and-policy-manager-ak51248": {
      "title": "Quality and Policy Manager",
      "description": "Quality and Policy Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Quality and Policy Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Quality and Policy Manager The Education Quality and Policy Office (EQPO) seeks a dedicated individual to join our friendly, enthusiastic team. EQPO is responsible for the quality assurance of all taught higher education provision, and for University-wide education-related policies. The team is part of the Education and Student Outcomes Department within Education Services. It comprises the Head, four Quality and Policy Managers, and an Administrator (0.5fte).  We work closely with other teams across Education Services and beyond, particularly the Postgraduate Research Office (PGRO) which is responsible for non-taught postgraduate education across the University, both the Undergraduate and Postgraduate Admissions Offices, Student Registry and the International Student Office. Subject to the General Board's oversight, EQPO is charged with the development and implementation of educational and student-related strategies and policies as they apply to those of the University's 25,000 students (undergraduate, postgraduate and part-time) studying taught courses. The role-holder supports the Head of EQPO in the development and implementation of student-centred education policy and quality assurance functions and, in doing so, will develop a wide-ranging knowledge of national developments in Higher Education. They will become familiar with the University's Quality Assurance Framework, using it to inform their QA work and signposting it to internal contacts when necessary.  The role holder also acts as the key EQPO contact for a set of Faculties and Departments, providing advice and guidance around internal quality assurance arrangements for teaching, learning and assessment, their means of addressing University policies and guidance, and other matters which come under EQPO's remit. The liaison aspect requires the role-holder to develop and maintain excellent working relationships with key contacts to encourage mutual trust, and the provision of general and specific advice to staff at all levels. Benefits A competitive salary with automatic service-related progression and annual cost of living increases A generous annual leave allowance Access to a hybrid pension scheme with attractive employer contributions Access to a range of shopping and travel discounts through the Cambens scheme. When applying, please upload your CV and a cover letter outlining how you meet the criteria set out in the Person Specification of the further information document Informal enquiries are encouraged and should be directed to the Head of EQPO, Jane Clare at Jane.Clare@admin.cam.ac.uk Closing date: 13th November2026 Interview date: 19th & 20th November 2026 Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact SSCjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AK51248 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Education & Student Outcomes Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference AK51248 Category Academic-related Date published 8 October 2026 Closing date 13 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistant-fixed-term-pm51164": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) The Research Luke Harland is establishing a new research group based in the Loke Centre for Trophoblast Research within the Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience at the University of Cambridge. The group investigates how specialised blood and vascular cell types arise during mammalian development and uses this knowledge to generate defined cell populations from pluripotent stem cells for mechanistic studies and tissue modelling. We aim to understand how developmental history shapes blood and vascular cell identity and function, and to apply this knowledge to build improved vascularised tissue models. By recreating the developmental processes that generate specialised blood vessels, we hope to build more physiologically relevant models for studying human development and disease. Our initial focus is on reconstructing the fetal placental exchange interface, which enables the transfer of oxygen and nutrients required for fetal growth and development. Our research combines embryology, stem cell biology, single-cell genomics, genome engineering, advanced imaging and computational biology. Further information about Luke Harland and key publications relevant to the ongoing research programme can be found on the Loke Centre for Trophoblast Research website ( https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/people/luke-harland ) The Opportunity We are seeking an enthusiastic and motivated Research Assistant to join our growing Wellcome-funded research programme. This is an exciting opportunity to become one of the founding members of a new research group and contribute to its development and culture. The successful candidate will contribute to research spanning developmental and stem cell biology, with opportunities to work with pluripotent stem cell culture and directed differentiation, molecular biology, flow cytometry, microscopy, mouse developmental models and single-cell genomics. About You Applicants should hold a BSc or Master's degree in a relevant biological discipline and have practical laboratory experience in cell biology, developmental biology, stem cell biology, molecular biology or a related field. We are particularly interested in candidates with experience in the culture and directed differentiation of mouse and/or human pluripotent stem cells. Experience with R or Python is desirable, and previous analysis of single-cell RNA-sequencing data would be an advantage. Experience with flow cytometry, microscopy or mouse developmental biology would also be beneficial. Training will be provided, and experience across all of these areas is not expected. We are looking for an organised, enthusiastic and collaborative individual with excellent attention to detail and an interest in combining experimental and computational biology. For more information please see the further particulars. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Due to the nature of the work, you will be required to work on site. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Applications should include a current CV, a cover letter outlining your interest in the position and relevant experience, and contact details for two professional referees. Informal enquiries can be made to Dr Luke Harland ( ltgh2@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference PM51164 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £33,002-£35,608 Reference PM51164 Category Research Date published 1 October 2026 Closing date 30 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantassociate-fixed-term-nr51332": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Associate (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance with possible extension. We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate in the general area of wearable sensing systems for the head, wearable signal processing and sensing data machine learning to join the Mobile Systems Research Laboratory at the University of Cambridge. The main responsibilities for this position are to innovate on wearable sensing for the head, devise new machine learning paradigms for robust in-the-wild analytics targeting health and fitness applications with special emphasis of translating the research into practice. We are seeking candidates with a background in wearable and (bio)sensing systems, possibly with experience in advanced wearable sensor data machine learning. Requirements: Candidates should have been awarded a PhD or be close to one and a research record in wearable sensing systems with specific focus on the head. Experience with current sensor data machine learning modelling also very appreciated. The ideal candidates should also have excellent programming skills. Good communication, presentation and management skills are also desirable given the size of the team. Experience in industry and industrial research also welcome. Appointment at the Research Associate level is dependent on the award of a PhD. This is a postdoctoral-level appointment, those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at the Research Assistant -Grade 5, which will be amended to Research Associate-Grade 7 once the award of the PhD has been conferred. Applicants should contact Prof Cecilia Mascolo for further information. http://cl.cam.ac.uk/~cm542 Deadline: Starting date: 1st February 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure you upload a covering letter, a curriculum vitae, a brief research statement, two key publications and contact information of at least 2 referees. If you upload any additional documents, which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please quote reference NR51332 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary £33,002 - £35,608 or £37,694- £46,049 Reference NR51332 Category Research Date published 7 October 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantassociate-in-ai-for-materials-and-manufacturing-fixed-term-nm51326": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate in AI for Materials and Manufacturing (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Associate in AI for Materials and Manufacturing (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Associate in AI for Materials and Manufacturing (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Associate in AI for Materials and Manufacturing (Fixed Term) A position exists for a Research Assistant/Associate in AI for Materials and Manufacturing in the Department of Engineering, University of Cambridge, to work on a multidisciplinary research programme developing protein-guided, droplet-based routes to architected functional materials, as part of an Advanced Research + Invention Agency-funded project, subject to contract negotiations. The post holder will be located in West Cambridge, Cambridgeshire, UK. The project, collaborating across seven research groups, delivers a new pathway and manufacturing process towards multi-functional architected materials across length scales, studying: (i) how to create rare-earth free magnetic materials at scale and in useful formats (ii) how to create advanced, nanoporous electrodes for high capacity, long-life battery technologies The key responsibilities and duties are to develop the project's uncertainty-aware hybrid digital twin and AI control architecture. The appointee will combine protein and formulation descriptors, reduced-order or physics-informed droplet and deposition models, multimodal in-line metrology and process-structure-property machine learning. They will develop information-efficient experiment selection, diagnose process drift, recommend quantitative parameter corrections and support route down-selection and statistical process control. The appointee will collaborate closely with researchers in protein design, nanocrystal synthesis, flow chemistry, printing, biomineralisation, advanced characterisation, magnetics, batteries and scale-up. They will help establish shared data and model interfaces, integrate multimodal in-line metrology, communicate uncertainty and model limitations, and contribute to reports, publications, presentations and programme stage gates. Applicants must have (or be close to obtaining) a PhD in engineering, computer science, applied mathematics, physics, data science, materials science or a closely related discipline. Essential capabilities include machine learning or statistical modelling for physical systems, quantitative model validation, scientific programming and reproducible data workflows, and effective multidisciplinary collaboration. Experience in hybrid modelling, uncertainty quantification, active learning or optimisation, digital twins, process control, multimodal metrology data, materials informatics or advanced manufacturing is desirable rather than required across every area. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD.\nThose who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at Research Assistant level, which will be amended to Research Associate once the PhD has been awarded. Salary Ranges: Research Assistant £33,002 - £35,608; Research Associate £37,694 - £46,049 Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 December 2027 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV) and a covering letter in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please submit your application by midnight on the closing date. If you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please contact Prof. Ronan Daly ( rd439@cam.ac.uk ) or Holly Shaw ( has50@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference NM51326 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information HR7_FurtherParticulars Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference NM51326 Category Research Date published 6 October 2026 Closing date 21 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantassociate-in-climate-compatible-growth-x-2-fixed-term-nm51366": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate in Climate Compatible Growth x 2 (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Associate in Climate Compatible Growth x 2 (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Climate Compatible Growth x 2 (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Climate Compatible Growth x 2 (Fixed Term) Two Research Assistant/Associate positions have become available as part of the Climate Compatible Growth (CCG) programme, an exciting research programme involving leading UK universities and KTH, Sweden. CCG is funded by the UK's Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO), which aims to support investment in sustainable energy and transport systems to meet development priorities in the Global South. The key partner countries in CCG are Kenya, Zambia, Ghana, Lao PDR, Viet Nam and India where joint research projects are in place. The specific focus of this post is critical materials (such as lithium, cobalt, graphite) to support low-carbon transitions globally. The post holders will be located in Cambridge, UK. These posts are full-time and offered on a fixed-term contract basis until 31.03.27, starting as soon as possible. The key responsibilities and duties are: 1) Research critical material production & supply chains\n2) Model supply chain changes techno-economically\n3) Academic & policy writing \n4) Collaborate with institutions in selected CCG partner countries The skills, qualifications and experience required for the roles: \n- Applicants must have (or be close to obtaining) a PhD in Engineering or a field relating to mining, supply chains, or similar. \n- Knowledge and experience in: environmental assessment, emissions accounting, tools of Industrial Ecology; cost estimations of supply chains and production processes of minerals; programming languages (i.e. Python, MATLAB, R), software control (i.e. GitHub) and GIS-type software. \n- Collaboration and communication with multicultural partners, policymakers research institutes, colleagues. \n- Evidence of potential to produce and clearly communicate high-quality research outputs. \n- Experience of managing own workload, sticking to deadlines, and ability to constantly update knowledge. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at Research Assistant level, which will be amended to Research Associate once the PhD has been awarded. Salary ranges: Research Assistant: £33,002 - £35,608 Research Associate: £37,694- £46,049 Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 March 2027 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publication list in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please submit your application by midnight on the closing date. If you have any questions about this vacancy, please contact Prof Jonathan Cullen at jmc99@cam.ac.uk for\nqueries of a technical nature related to this role, and please contact Kirsten Saward at khs29@cam.ac.uk for queries about the application process. Please quote reference NM51366 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information HR7_RA in CCG Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference NM51366 Category Research Date published 9 October 2026 Closing date 24 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantassociate-in-emulsion-templating-of-battery-materials-fixed-term-nm51354": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate in Emulsion Templating of Battery Materials (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Associate in Emulsion Templating of Battery Materials (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Emulsion Templating of Battery Materials (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Emulsion Templating of Battery Materials (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for an experimental postdoctoral researcher to create architected materials using protein guided assembly in micro-emulsion templates. The researcher will be part of the NanoManufacturing group in the Department of Engineering at the University of Cambridge ( http://www.nanomanufacturing.eng.cam.ac.uk/ ), which looks at various aspects of nanotechnology and manufacturing processes as well as energy storage systems. This work will be carried out in close collaboration with other leading research teams in Cambridge as well as Liverpool and the Netherlands.  Collaborating with these groups will create a stimulating work environment with exciting opportunities for personal development and learning about different fields of research. This is a diverse team that values collaboration, scientific excellence and research integrity and the project's goal is to reduce our reliance on rare earths and critical minerals in Li-Ion battery cathodes and magnets. The selected candidate will play a key role in the development of scalable droplet generation tools such as membrane emulsification and ink-jet printing as templates for protein guided assembly and bio-mineralisation.  These structured materials will then be evaluated in Li-ion batteries, which is a research area in which the host group specialises. We will consider candidates with various research backgrounds including physical chemistry, engineering and material science. We are particularly interested in candidates with experience in emulsion-templating, colloidal self-assembly, and/or Li-ion batteries.  Applicants are required to have a PhD or being close to obtaining a PhD. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at Research Assistant level, which will be amended to Research Associate once the PhD has been awarded. Salary Ranges: \nResearch Assistant: £33,002 - £35,608\nResearch Associate: £37,694 - £46,049 Fixed-term: The funding is for 18 months or through until 01/03/2028, whichever\nof these comes soonest. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV) and a covering letter and your 2 best papers in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please submit your application by midnight on the closing date. If you have any questions about this vacancy please contact Holly Shaw, has50@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference NM51354 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information HR7 Research Assistant/Associate in Emulsion Templating of Battery Materials Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £33,002 - £35,608 or £37,694- £46,049 Reference NM51354 Category Research Date published 8 October 2026 Closing date 29 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantassociate-in-nanobio-printing-and-assembly-fixed-term-nm51291": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate in NanoBio Printing and Assembly (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Associate in NanoBio Printing and Assembly (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Associate in NanoBio Printing and Assembly (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Associate in NanoBio Printing and Assembly (Fixed Term) A position exists for a Research Assistant/Associate in NanoBio Printing and Assembly in the Department of Engineering, University of Cambridge, to work on a multidisciplinary research programme developing protein-guided, droplet-based routes to architected functional materials, as part of an Advanced Research + Invention Agency-funded project, subject to contract negotiations. The post holder will be located in West Cambridge, Cambridgeshire, UK. The project, collaborating across seven research groups, delivers a new pathway and manufacturing process towards multi-functional architected materials across length scales, studying: (i) how to create rare-earth free magnetic materials at scale and in useful formats\n(ii) how to create advanced, nanoporous electrodes for high capacity, long-life battery technologies The appointee will take ownership of instrumented printing, droplet-generation, membrane-emulsification and controlled-flow systems. They will integrate protein-containing formulations, compare magnetic, electric, shear, flow and drying-assisted alignment, and test protein retention or removal to lock anisotropic order into stable materials. They will define process windows, interrogate data, maintain reproducible standard operating procedures, and support quality control and scale-up. The role involves close collaboration across protein, mineralisation, nanocrystal, chemistry, magnetics, AI and manufacturing teams. The role also includes supporting the project lead as a technical project lead, which includes aspects of project management, coordination, risk management and mentoring. Applicants must have obtained, or be close to obtaining, a PhD in chemical engineering, materials science or materials engineering, applied physics, fluid mechanics, colloid or interface science, or a closely related discipline, with additional expertise in biotechnology. Essential capabilities include strong experimental skills and transferable experience in one or more printing, droplet, microfluidic, or flow processes. Experience of field-assisted processing, high-speed or quantitative imaging, magnetic or colloidal materials, and protein production and handling are desirable. Expertise across every area is not expected. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD (or equivalent experience). Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at Research Assistant level, which will be amended to Research Associate once the PhD has been awarded. Salary Ranges: \nResearch Assistant £33,002 - £35,608 \nResearch Associate £37,694 - £46,049 Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 1 March 2028 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV) and a covering letter in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please submit your application by midnight on the closing date. If you have any questions about this vacancy, or the application process, please contact: Prof. Ronan Daly ( rd439@cam.ac.uk ), or Holly Shaw ( has50@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference NM51291 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further particulars Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £33,002 - £35,608 or £37,694- £46,049 Reference NM51291 Category Research Date published 2 October 2026 Closing date 19 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantassociate-in-theoretical-computer-science-fixed-term-nr51230": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate in Theoretical Computer Science (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Associate in Theoretical Computer Science (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Theoretical Computer Science (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Theoretical Computer Science (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 year. The Department of Computer Science and Technology is an academic department that encompasses computer science along with many aspects of engineering, technology and mathematics. We have a worldwide reputation for academic research with consistent top research ratings. The Department has an open and collaborative culture, supporting revolutionary fundamental computer science research, strong cross-cutting collaborations internally and externally, and ideas which transform computing outside the University. Please follow the link at: https://www.cst.cam.ac.uk to find out more about our department. Applications are invited for a postdoctoral researcher to join Professor Tom Gurs research group in the Department of Computer Science and Technology at the University of Cambridge, UK. This position is part of a broader effort to advance fundamental research in classical and quantum complexity theory. The successful candidate will conduct cutting-edge research on  the interplay of theoretical computer science, mathematics, and quantum mechanics.  Particular topics include classical and quantum aspects of: Sublinear algorithms and property testing Cryptography, zero-knowledge proofs, and probabilistically checkable proofs Locally testable and locally decodable codes Computational learning theory Structure-versus-randomness and local-to-global phenomena Harmonic analysis, additive combinatorics, and their applications in theoretical \ncomputer science There will be opportunities for collaboration with faculty members and PhD students and to engage with the broader quantum computing and theoretical computer science communities at Cambridge. Essentia criteria Successful candidates will hold (or be close to completing) a PhD in Computer Science or a relevant subject area. Appointment at the research associate level depends upon having a PhD or equivalent skills and experience through non-academic routes. Where a PhD has yet to be awarded appointment will initially be made as a Research Assistant and amended to Research Associate when the PhD is awarded.  Additional information about the skills required for the position can be found in the further particulars. Appointment at the Research Associate level is dependent on the award of a PhD. This is a postdoctoral-level appointment, those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at the Research Assistant -Grade 5, which will be amended to Research Associate-Grade 7 once the award of the PhD has been conferred. In addition to the base salary, the successful candidate for this post will receive an additional 2.5% supplement to their pay. For further information please contact Professor Tom Gur: tom.gur@cst.cam.ac.uk Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Required documents include a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, a brief research statement, and contact information for two references. If you upload any additional documents, which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please quote reference NR51230 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary £34,608-£35,608 £37,694-£46,049 Reference NR51230 Category Research Date published 29 September 2026 Closing date 1 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantresearch-associate-fixed-term-nq50329": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate (Fixed Term) Membrane-based separations are central to addressing major sustainability challenges, from securing critical metal supplies for the energy transition to enabling energy-efficient water treatment. Developing the next generation of membrane processes requires predictive computational models that connect molecular-scale phenomena to system-level performance. However, the thermodynamics and transport behaviour of concentrated, multi-ion aqueous solutions remain poorly understood, limiting rational membrane design and process optimisation. The Department of Chemical Engineering and Biotechnology (CEB) at the University of Cambridge welcomes applications for a Postdoctoral Research Associate to work on a UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship project developing computational models for selective ion transport in complex aqueous mixtures. The research combines molecular simulation, thermodynamic modelling, and continuum transport theory to build predictive frameworks for membrane-based ion separations. The role will involve molecular dynamics (MD) simulations and density functional theory (DFT) calculations to understand ion-solvent and ion-membrane interactions, alongside the development of continuum-scale transport models. This is an opportunity to be a member of a new research group working at the interface of molecular simulation, chemical thermodynamics, and membrane science. You will report to Dr Akshay Deshmukh (PI), a UKRI Future Leaders Fellow at CEB. You will work alongside an experimental PDRA and collaborate with international project partners. You will have access to CEB's world-leading facilities and the broader Cambridge research ecosystem, including the Cambridge Centre for Data-Driven Discovery. You will be expected to publish in high-impact journals, develop and release open-source computational tools, and present findings at international conferences. You will also gain experience in research leadership, grant writing, and mentoring, with support from the PI to help you achieve your career development goals. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 24 months in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference NQ50329 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Chemical Engineering and Biotechnology Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference NQ50329 Category Research Date published 9 July 2026 Closing date 30 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
