    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-pc51194": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) How do mutant clones compete in healthy tissues as animals age, and how do some mammals suppress cancer across remarkably different lifespans and body sizes? The Cagan Lab is recruiting a Postdoctoral Research Associate to investigate these questions through comparative somatic genomics. The Cagan Lab uses natural variation across species as a powerful experiment. Mammals differ dramatically in lifespan, body size, life history and cancer incidence. Comparing them can reveal the general rules of clonal evolution, expose lineage-specific solutions to cancer suppression, and provide a principled way to interpret emerging findings from human tissues. This pioneering project will map age-related clonal dynamics across mammals with diverse lifespans and body-sizes including rodents, elephants and cetaceans. The aim is to uncover principles governing somatic evolution in mammals. Comparing somatic and germline tissues will reveal how clones arise and expand, which genes and pathways are selected, and how tissue context and species biology influence these processes. The role is predominantly computational, with an experimental data-generation component and substantial intellectual ownership. You will combine high-depth error-corrected sequencing (NanoSeq), species-specific targeted gene panels, histology and quantitative evolutionary analysis. The balance of computational and experimental work will vary as the project develops. Key responsibilities Develop reproducible genomic workflows; call and quality-control somatic variants; quantify mutation rates and signatures; infer clone sizes and dynamics; and test for selection. Design targeted gene panels and comparative analyses accounting for species, tissue, age, sex and sampling structure. Model clonal growth in relation to lifespan, body size, tissue architecture and cancer risk, including comparisons with human datasets. Contribute to sample collection, histology, laser-capture microdissection and DNA extraction, with training and support. Maintain accurate sample metadata and quality-control records. Collaborate with colleagues in Cambridge, at the Wellcome Sanger Institute and across partner institutions; prepare manuscripts and reusable data and code; present findings; and support students and junior colleagues. Manage day-to-day work independently, communicate progress and risks, and help shape the project's scientific direction. What the role offers You will have substantial ownership of a comparative genomics project linking evolution, ageing and cancer biology, with access to diverse mammalian tissues and an interdisciplinary collaborative network. Training will include project-specific experimental methods and analysis of somatic clonal dynamics. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcome and should be directed to Dr Alex Cagan, atjc2@cam.ac.uk Further information about the Cagan Group can be found here https://www.gen.cam.ac.uk/cagan-group Please quote reference PC51194 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Genetics Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PC51194 Category Research Date published 25 September 2026 Closing date 21 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-pk51315": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) We are looking for a postdoctoral scientist to join Prof Christina Zielinski's group to work in the field of human T-cell immunology. This role offers the opportunity to work at the forefront of human T-cell immunology using cutting-edge cellular, molecular and gene-editing approaches. The candidate will explore the molecular regulation of a novel cytotoxic pathway in T cells and its impact in settings of cancer and infection. This will involve investigations into the role and regulation of T cell-derived extracellular vesicles and membrane repair mechanisms. The ability to work both independently and as part of a team, coupled with excellent communication, organizational and problem-solving skills is required. Expertise in molecular techniques, gene-editing of human primary cells (CRISPR-based approaches), and microscopic imaging technologies are required. Experience with cytotoxic T cells is a plus. Other duties/responsibilities include developing and driving research objectives and writing up work for presentation and publication. Fixed-term: The post is available for two years in the first instance starting as soon as possible and subject to a 6-month probationary period. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a motivation letter detailing project and career development plans and include a publications list in the upload section of the online application. Please provide contact details, including an email address of two referees. At least one of your referees should be someone who is familiar with your work in your primary research field. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our Department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries and requests for further information are very welcome and should be addressed to Prof Christina Zielinski cez22@cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact: hr@path.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PK51315 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Pathology Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PK51315 Category Research Date published 9 October 2026 Closing date 29 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-honorary-resident-doctor-in-respiratory-medicine-rc51139": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) in Respiratory Medicine",
      "description": "Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) in Respiratory Medicine | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) in Respiratory Medicine Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) in Respiratory Medicine Cambridge-GSK Translational Immunology Collaboration (CG-TIC) We seek a highly motivated Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) to contribute to cutting-edge research that will provide novel insights into the pathogenesis of respiratory disease, with a particular emphasis on collaborative work with the Cambridge-GSK Translational Immunology Collaboration ( https://www.med.cam.ac.uk/cg-tic ). This is a new interdisciplinary partnership between the University of Cambridge, Cambridge University Hospital and Royal Papworth Hospital NHS Trusts and GSK, bringing together expertise from these partners. The Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) will conduct high-quality research as part of a clinical cohort research study that is using endophenotyping and laboratory studies to investigate respiratory disease mechanisms in Interstitial Lung Diseases. In the clinical cohort research study, the research associate will provide clinical support, covering a weekly research clinic and bronchoscopy list. The lab studies will include integration of multi-omics and clinical data and will involve close collaboration with colleagues across CG-TIC and at GSK. The position is based in the Department of Medicine. The chosen candidate will work in a dynamic, supportive team under the direct supervision of Dr Jenny Dickens and Dr Gauri Saini (Royal Papworth Hospital NHS Foundation Trust), who will supervise the clinical research study. Further mentorship will be provided by Prof. James Nathan and the CG-TIC Respiratory Mechanisms of Disease research team. The successful applicant will also have the potential option to undertake a PhD within this post. The higher degree will be based within the Department of Medicine. Applicants should have a medical degree (MBBS or equivalent) and necessary clinical training experience in respiratory medicine. Experience of collaborative working will be extremely valuable. Informal enquiries regarding this position are strongly encouraged: Dr Gauri Saini ( gauri.saini@nhs.net ) ILD Phenotyping Lead and/or Prof. James Nathan CG-TIC Respiratory lead ( jan33@cam.ac.uk ). Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. This appointment requires an Honorary Clinical Contract. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. This is an integrated appointment between the University and Cambridge University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust (\"NHS Trust\").  You will therefore also be issued with an Honorary Clinical Contract by the NHS Trust. You should be aware that if your contract with the NHS Trust is terminated your employment with the University will be terminated, and vice versa.\nPlease ensure that you upload a covering letter, CV and a completed Additional Information for Clinical Researchers form in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application.\nPlease include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RC51139 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Additional Information Form Key information Department/location Department of Medicine Salary £40,190-£76,582 Reference RC51139 Category Research Date published 21 September 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-in-music-and-ai-fixed-term-gt51091": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Music and AI (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate in Music and AI (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate in Music and AI (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate in Music and AI (Fixed Term) The Faculty of Music at the University of Cambridge wishes to appoint a Research Associate in Music and AI, starting on 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. This is a fixed-term, five-year post. The appointee will have strong computational skills, with experience in developing, adapting, or evaluating machine-learning systems. We envisage that the appointee's research will focus on one of the three research topics below, but are open to alternative fields of specialism that fall under the guiding ideal of advancing artificial intelligence for the benefit of humanity. [1] Empowerment vs automation: how can generative AI enhance musical creativity and agency for expert musicians rather than simply automate creative decisions? [2] Artificial creativity: how can music AI models be taught to be 'truly' creative, embracing the irrational, the unexpected, the quirks of human arbitrariness that might later be defined as creative breakthroughs? [3] Music AI for health and wellbeing: in what ways can music AI be used to enhance health and wellbeing through sound and music's proven capacity to enhance such mechanisms as emotion induction, memory stimulation, and physical entrainment? Applicants should have a relevant PhD in machine learning, computer science or a related discipline and a record of, or clear potential for, outstanding research. We seek a scholar of disciplinary depth who wishes to forge intellectual and creative synergies with colleagues working in other areas. The appointee will work alongside colleagues in the Centre for Music and Science, with further opportunities for collaborations with the Centre for Human-Inspired Artificial Intelligence (CHIA), the Leverhulme Centre for the Future of Intelligence, and the Department of Computer Science and Technology. The successful applicant will primarily pursue a multi-year research project in the field of Music and AI. They will also have the opportunity to deliver teaching at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels. At the heart of a vast network of musical study, research and practice, the Faculty of Music is a world-leading centre of research. Our facilities include a professional concert hall and music library as well as an extensive collection of historical instruments and a Javanese gamelan. Integral to the Faculty is the Centre for Music and Science, housing a fully equipped studio, computer room and research room, and fostering a vibrant research community addressing key topics in music cognition, computational musicology, and music AI. Further information about the Faculty of Music is available at www.mus.cam.ac.uk . For information on what to include in your application, please see the further information document on the top right-hand side of this advertisement. Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and should be directed to Professor David Trippett at chair@mus.cam.ac.uk . For questions on the application process, please contact the School's HR Team on MusicHR@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is midnight (BST) on Wednesday 21 October 2026. Interviews are planned for Friday 30 October 2026, subject to change. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote vacancy reference number GT51091 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Faculty of Music Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference GT51091 Category Research Date published 21 September 2026 Closing date 21 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-behaviour-and-evolution-group-fixed-term-pf51056": {
      "title": "Research Associate: Behaviour and Evolution Group (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate: Behaviour and Evolution Group (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate: Behaviour and Evolution Group (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate: Behaviour and Evolution Group (Fixed Term) A Research Associate post is available in the Behaviour and Evolution Group directed by Rufus Johnstone in the Department of Zoology at the University of Cambridge. The applicant will join an exciting, inter-disciplinary team investigating the role of intergroup conflict in the evolution of animal societies. Informed by data collected from banded mongoose and termite study systems, the applicant will develop new theoretical models of intergroup conflict, as part of an ERC Advanced Grant (INTERGROUP) awarded to Rufus Johnstone and Michael Cant and funded by the UKRI under the Horizon Guarantee programme. Intergroup conflict is recognised as a potentially powerful force shaping the evolution of social behaviour in animal societies. However, we have limited theory to explain and predict when intergroup fights will break out, how long they will last, how they end, or their consequences for individual behaviour and life history. This successful applicant will address these questions by developing new theoretical models of conflict initiation,\ndynamics, and consequences for life history allocation. The goal of the modelling work is to generate predictions that can be tested using data from the mongoose and termite systems or published data in other taxa. The successful applicant will join the Behaviour and Evolution research group based in the Department of Zoology, directed by Rufus Johnstone. They will form part of the larger group of researchers working on the INTERGROUP project based at the universities of Exeter, Swansea, Liverpool John Moores and York. They will participate in regular meetings with the team, with potential opportunities for collaboration and short visits as the project develops. Candidates will be highly motivated and keen to interact with other components of the wider project. An interest in and aptitude for evolutionary theory and modelling is desirable, although training in formal modelling techniques will also be provided. Close teamwork and a collaborative spirit will be essential, but team members will be expected to demonstrate scientific independence matching their technical expertise. There will be opportunities to contribute to training new team members and to general project management, as well as to participate in public engagement activities. For more information, please refer to the Further Particulars document. The closing date for applications is midnight (GMT) on 18 October 2026 The interview for this role is expected to take place during the week commencing Monday, 26 October 2026 (subject to change) and may be conducted remotely in exceptional circumstances. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available up to 31 May 2029, with a possibility of extension subject to the project status and funding. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact Professor Rufus Johnstone [email: raj1003@cam.ac.uk ]. For any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Zoology HR Office [email: hr@zoo.cam.ac.uk ] Please quote reference PF51056 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Research Associate - PF51056 Key information Department/location Department of Zoology Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PF51056 Category Research Date published 24 September 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-bumblebee-conservation-fixed-term-pf51346": {
      "title": "Research Associate: Bumblebee Conservation (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate: Bumblebee Conservation (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate: Bumblebee Conservation (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate: Bumblebee Conservation (Fixed Term) Moss carder bee recovery post-doc opportunity We are looking for an exceptional, driven postdoctoral researcher to lead a high-impact, 1-year project bridging the gap between machine learning for species identification and real-world insect conservation. You will focus on recovery of the moss carder bee (Bombus muscorum), an iconic bumblebee species classed as 'vulnerable' in the latest European Red List of bees.  You will join a Species Recovery Programme project led by the Bumblebee Conservation Trust, funded by Natural England. This is an exciting opportunity to develop tools with direct impact on nature recovery and biodiversity policy. To drive targeted monitoring programmes and inform targeted field surveys, you will build and evaluate a visual recognition tool for this morphologically cryptic species. To achieve this, you will harness machine learning approaches, combined with DNA barcoded species photos. This will require fieldwork, coordination of scientists and volunteers from our partner, the Bumblebee Conservation Trust, and lab work. This tool will enable real-world conservation interventions. Joining the vibrant Brown Lab within the Department of Zoology, you will be embedded in a world-leading scientific network as a member of the Cambridge Conservation Research Institute (CCRI) and the Cambridge Conservation Initiative (CCI) - one of the largest clusters of biodiversity researchers and policy experts on Earth. Close teamwork and a collaborative spirit will be essential, but team members will be expected to demonstrate scientific independence matching their technical expertise. There will be opportunities to contribute to training new team members, to supporting and supervising undergraduate and postgraduate students and to general project management, as well as to participate in public engagement activities. The post will be supervised by Professor Mark Brown. The preferred start date is 1 April 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. For more information, please refer to the Further Particulars document. The closing date for applications is midnight (GMT) on 8 November 2026 The interview for this role is expected to take place between 30 November 2026 and 4 December 2026 (subject to change) and may be conducted remotely in exceptional circumstances. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for up to 12 months. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Flexible working requests will be considered (such as part-time). Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact Professor Mark Brown [email: mb2805@cam.ac.uk ]. For any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Zoology HR Office [email: hr@zoo.cam.ac.uk ] Please quote reference PF51346 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Research Associate - Bumblebee Conservation - Further Particulars - PF51346 Key information Department/location Department of Zoology Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PF51346 Category Research Date published 9 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-coordinator-fixed-term-sm51167": {
      "title": "Research Coordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Coordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Coordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Coordinator (Fixed Term) The Medical Research Council Mitochondrial Biology Unit (MRC MBU), University of Cambridge wishes to appoint a Research Coordinator to join the ARIA-funded Programmable mitochondria (Pro-MiT) project team under the leadership of Professor Michal Minczuk. A world-renowned centre for research, the MRC MBU ( www.mrc-mbu.cam.ac.uk ) is a vibrant community focused on the involvement of mitochondria and their dysfunction in an ever-increasing range of human diseases. From 2027, the ProMiT programme will transition with the MBU research activities into the Cambridge Institute for Medical Research (CIMR, www.cimr.cam.ac.uk ), remaining on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus and becoming part of a broader interdisciplinary research environment. The postholder will contribute to ProMiT, a five-year international, multi-institutional project funded through the ARIA Precision Mitochondria ( aria.org.uk/opportunity-spaces/programmable-physiology/precision-mitochondria ) initiative and aimed at developing and rigorously testing new technologies for the delivery and functional use of genetic material in mitochondria. The Research Coordinator will support the day-to-day operational delivery of the programme, acting as a central coordination point between the Project Lead, scientific workstreams, partner organisations, ARIA and University professional services. Responsibilities include maintaining project plans and milestones; coordinating quarterly reporting and Go/No-Go reviews; financial monitoring; partner communications; programme meetings and visits; and compliance with ARIA-University requirements. The post holder will need to be a strong team player and effective communicator in order to work with a range of partner teams and external stakeholders. The nature of the role will require the post-holder to have excellent organisational skills, with the ability to plan and prioritise for themselves and others. Candidates should be educated to degree level / Level 6 vocational qualification or have an equivalent level of practical experience. Essential experience includes working in a project, programme or complex administrative role involving multiple stakeholders, workstreams and deadlines; maintaining project plans, milestones and progress information; and financial monitoring or budget administration. Strong organisational, communication and analytical skills, the ability to synthesise scientific or technical information, and confidence using Microsoft 365 and shared project-management systems are also required. Experience in a higher-education or research environment, supporting an externally funded international programme, or research-grant compliance would be desirable. Informal enquiries about this position should be directed to Professor Michal Minczuk ( mam201@cam.ac.uk ). This is a fixed-term position. The funds for this post are available until 31 August 2031 in the first instance. The University of Cambridge is committed, in its pursuit of academic and research excellence, to a pro-active and inclusive approach to equality, which supports and encourages all under-represented groups, promotes an inclusive culture, and values diversity. The MBU is part of the School of Clinical Medicine, which holds an Athena Swan Silver Award and supports an Academic Women's Forum. The University particularly encourages women and candidates from Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic backgrounds to apply. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Closing date: 22 October 2026 Interview date: w/c 2 November 2026 Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of three professional referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. These should be contactable prior to interview where possible. Please quote reference SM51167 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location MRC Mitochondrial Biology Unit Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference SM51167 Category Academic-related Date published 23 September 2026 Closing date 22 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
