    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-office-projects-manager-ew51121": {
      "title": "Research Office Projects Manager",
      "description": "Research Office Projects Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Office Projects Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Office Projects Manager This is an exciting time to join the Research Office. Sitting at the heart of the University's research mission, we are continuing to develop how we work and how we deliver our services, with an ambitious programme of change and improvement ahead of us. We are looking for an experienced Projects Manager to help turn those ambitions into reality. You will lead a varied portfolio of strategic projects across the Research Office, taking projects from initial scoping and planning through to delivery and implementation. Working closely with senior leaders, subject matter experts and operational teams, you will bring structure and momentum to complex initiatives, coordinate people and resources, manage risks and dependencies, and keep delivery on track. This is a role for someone who enjoys making things happen. You will be comfortable working across organisational boundaries, bringing together colleagues with different perspectives and priorities, and finding practical ways forward when the answer is not always straightforward. You will also work closely with the Transforming Research Support Programme, making sure that projects align with and contribute to wider organisational change. We are looking for someone with significant project management experience, excellent organisational and interpersonal skills, and the confidence to communicate effectively with a wide range of stakeholders. You will be able to manage competing priorities, navigate complexity and translate plans into tangible results. You do not need to be an expert in research administration. What matters is that you are curious, collaborative and pragmatic, with strong project management judgement and a genuine commitment to improving how services work. If you are excited by the opportunity to lead meaningful change in a complex organisation and want your work to contribute to an environment that enables research to change lives, we would love to hear from you Limited funding: The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date is 16th October 2026.  Interviews will be held in person, in Cambridge during w/c 2nd November. We reserve the right to close the advert early if sufficient applications are received, so welcome early applications. Questions about the application process should be addressed to the Research Office Recruitment team at crorecruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference EW51121 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cambridge Research Office Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference EW51121 Category Academic-related Date published 25 September 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-programme-manager-fixed-term-sj51268": {
      "title": "Research Programme Manager (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Programme Manager (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Programme Manager (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Programme Manager (Fixed Term) This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced and motivated Research Programme Manager to take a lead role in the operational delivery of a multi-disciplinary research programme to evaluate an acceptance-based guided self-help intervention to support weight loss maintenance and improve the physical and mental health of people living with obesity. This research programme is led by Professor Amy Ahern and is funded by an NIHR Programme Grant for Applied Research. It builds on a successful feasibility study of the Supporting Weight Management (SWiM) programme and will further refine, adapt and evaluate this intervention to provide evidence needed for implementation within the NHS. You will have responsibility for the operational management of the programme, including co-ordinating and ensuring timely delivery of all work-packages, line-managing research and administrative staff employed on the programme, managing the budget, monitoring progress and capturing impact, and preparing and submitting reports to funders and other stakeholders. You will also take a lead role in co-ordinating dissemination activities and maintaining excellent working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders including internal and external collaborators, relevant R&D offices, the Clinical Research Network, NHS England, Local Authorities, participating weight management services, industry partners, and patient/public representatives. The successful candidate will have a graduate qualification in a related field and demonstrable experience in successful planning and coordination of multiple applied research projects running in parallel across different institutions. Applicants will have excellent written and verbal communication skills, and the ability to build and maintain good working relationships with collaborators, stakeholders and team members. The Institute of Metabolic Science Epidemiology is a division of the Department of Clinical Biochemistry within the University of Cambridge's School of Clinical Medicine and is situated on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus. Its mission is to study the genetic, developmental and environmental factors that cause obesity, diabetes and related metabolic disorders and develop strategies for their prevention. IMS Epidemiology benefits from a number of large-scale, cross programme epidemiological studies with detailed datasets allowing a wide range of research from genetics to public health. Highly experienced information teams and other specialist teams support the scientists in the delivery of their research aims. The appointment will be made on a fixed-term basis for a period of 3 years in order to undertake a specific project of finite duration. Please contact Professor Amy Ahern ( Amy.Ahern@ims.cam.ac.uk ) for informal enquiries. The closing date is Tuesday 20th October 2026 and interviews will likely be held on 29th and 30th October 2026. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application.\nPlease include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference SJ51268 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Clinical Biochemistry (Institute of Metabolic Science Epidemiology) Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference SJ51268 Category Academic-related Date published 1 October 2026 Closing date 20 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-programme-manager-cambridge-compiler-lab-fixed-term-nr51238": {
      "title": "Research Programme Manager",
      "description": "Research Programme Manager - Cambridge Compiler Lab (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Programme Manager - Cambridge Compiler Lab (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Programme Manager - Cambridge Compiler Lab (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. The Department of Computer Science and Technology is an academic department that encompasses computer science along with many aspects of engineering, technology, and mathematics. We have a world-wide reputation for academic research with consistent top research ratings. The Department has an open and collaborative culture, supporting revolutionary fundamental computer science research, strong cross-cutting collaborations internally and externally, and ideas which transform computing outside the University. Please follow the link at: https://www.cst.cam.ac.uk to find out more about our Department. We are seeking an experienced Programme Manager to design, implement, and oversee the research programme of the Cambridge Compiler Lab in collaboration with the head of the research group and the entire research team. They will ensure that the research group defines clear and ambitious objectives and timelines, leading to research outputs with measurable impact for broader communities. Through the establishment of workflows, systems, collaborative workshops and strategy sessions, they will track progress and maintain accountability for shared research goals, facilitate research activity and productivity and ensure compliance with relevant policies. The Programme Manager will develop and manage operational systems, collaborative workshops and strategy sessions to ensure active engagement of academic stakeholders, empowering them to contribute effectively to deliverables. They will foster innovative collaboration, building and sustaining relationships with and between internal and external stakeholders, industry collaborators, policy makers, practitioners and community groups to drive the delivery of impactful outcomes, ensuring open communication and shared understanding. The ideal candidate will be educated to degree level, or possess a Level 6 vocational qualification or equivalent experience. They will bring proven expertise in research administration, having previously established governance and monitoring systems that support high-performing research environments. Experience in financial oversight is essential, including budget preparation, expenditure tracking, and detailed reporting. Strong project management skills are crucial, with the ability to plan, monitor, and evaluate large-scale initiatives while managing multiple priorities to measurable outcomes. Proactivity, a keen problem-solving mindset, and the ability to work independently under pressure are key attributes for success in this role. This post is available on a full-time basis; however, we are committed to supporting flexible working and will consider part-time applications from candidates able to work a minimum of 40% FTE. Informal queries can be directed to Dr Tobias Grosser at tcg40@cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact hr-admin@cst.cam.ac.uk Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please upload your CV and covering letter when applying for the role. If you upload any additional documents, which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please quote reference NR51238 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference NR51238 Category Academic-related Date published 8 October 2026 Closing date 31 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-software-engineer-part-time-fixed-term-lg50949": {
      "title": "Research Software Engineer (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Software Engineer (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Software Engineer (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Software Engineer (Part Time, Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 5 years in the first instance. If additional funding is acquired, extension may be possible. The Institute of Astronomy (IoA) and the co-located, interdepartmental Kavli Institute for Cosmology, Cambridge (KICC) at Cambridge University are internationally renowned for a uniquely stimulating and multi-disciplinary research environment.  One topic where Cambridge has a long history is black holes, the most extreme environments in nature, and laboratories where strong gravity, plasma physics and high-energy astro(particle)/physical processes meet. The research group of Prof. Sera Markoff (Plumian Professor) studies these systems across the full black hole mass scale, combining GPU-accelerated supercomputer simulations and semi-analytic modelling with data from world-leading facilities including the Event Horizon Telescope (EHT). Her group sits at the nexus of several international efforts: a growing Cambridge node, where this post is based (currently expanding over the coming years into a fully-fledged team of students and postdocs); an established group at the University of Amsterdam of several postdocs and students; her ongoing ERC Synergy Grant project 'Blackholistic', with partner groups at Oxford, Radboud University in Nijmegen, the University of Turku, the University of Namibia and the University of South Africa; and major international collaborations including the EHT and the Cherenkov Telescope Array Observatory (CTAO). Via the ERC project, the group is also involved in commissioning a new 14-metre radio telescope in Namibia, the Africa Millimetre Telescope. The group's research is built on a developing portfolio of in-house software: a GPU-accelerated general-relativistic magnetohydrodynamics (GRMHD) simulation code (in C, with GPU acceleration primarily on NVIDIA and AMD hardware); a GR ray-tracing code (in Julia) that produces images and spectra from those simulations, and is designed to conduct Bayesian parameter inference; semi-analytic jet and accretion modelling codes (in C++, with a Python interface) that are being integrated into the open-source Gammapy framework for use across the CTAO community and beyond; and increasingly building on other imaging and analysis frameworks used within the EHT Collaboration. These codes run on local, national and international high-performance computing (HPC) facilities. This part-time 0.5 FTE Research Software Engineer (RSE) role will provide the professional software engineering capability at the centre of this network and will suit someone excited to work at the interface between modern software engineering and some of the most cutting-edge research in astrophysics. The role-holder will establish and manage version-controlled repositories for all group software, deploy and optimise the codes on available HPC facilities, and embed good software practice across a distributed, international team through documentation, testing, code review and training. The role has a strong outward-facing dimension: coordinating shared code development with partner institutions and contributing the group's codes to community open-source frameworks used by international collaborations. The purpose of the role is to ensure that the group's research software is reliable, performant, well-documented and reproducible; that development remains coordinated with partner groups as shared codes evolve; and that software engineering expertise is retained within the group as projects and personnel change. The role also offers clear scope for growth: the role-holder will be encouraged to engage directly in the group's research, helping develop new computational methods and contributing to publications, with responsibilities that can expand as the Cambridge node grows. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. In particular, the role-holder's responsibilities will include: Establishing and managing reliable version-control infrastructure for the group's codes, including migration of legacy repositories. Defining repository structure, branching, access and release workflows so that all group software is secure, documented, citable and reproducible. Liaising with partner development groups at other institutions to track external development branches of shared codes. Planning and carrying out the integration of selected external developments into the group's versions, coordinating builds and testing. Setting up, testing and optimising the group's numerical simulation codes on local, national and international (US/CAN/EU) HPC clusters using AMD and/or NVIDIA GPUs (e.g., via CUDA, HIP, OpenCL, or similar). Carrying out application profiling and benchmarking, and contributing technical cases and support to group proposals for acquiring more HPC computing time. Designing, coding, verifying, testing and documenting new physics capabilities within the group's codes, together with the research team, profiling and optimising their performance. Investigating, prototyping and implementing machine-learning and advanced statistical methods (e.g. improved sampling for Bayesian parameter inference, surrogate models/emulators, simulation-based inference) to accelerate the group's modelling and inference workflows; evaluating their performance and implements improvements. Supporting development of the group's semi-analytic modelling codes (C++ with a Python interface) through performance optimisation and branch management, and planning and carrying out their integration into the open-source Gammapy framework, adhering to community coding standards, so that they can be adopted across the CTAO community and beyond. Implementing automated testing, continuous integration and documentation across the group's codebases. Providing advice, guidance and training to researchers and students in both Cambridge and Amsterdam to promote adoption of software engineering best practice. Actively maintaining specialist knowledge and providing detailed advice to group leader Prof. Markoff on software strategy, computing resource planning and data-management plans for grant proposals and reports. Within the group's research goals, building on and refining ideas for developing and evaluating new computational methods, collaborating with researchers on their application to open problems; reporting on work carried out and potentially contributing material of publication quality to group publications. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please indicate the contact details of three professional referees on the online application form and upload a full curriculum vitae (CV), list of publications (if you have such) and a research/technical experience statement (this research/technical experience statement being three pages max in 11pt font). The closing date for applications is: 23:59 BST on Monday 9th November 2026. Referees will be requested to provide references by the same date. Where permission is given by the applicant, references will be requested prior to the vacancy closing. Applicants are asked to inform their referees that they will receive a reference request communication including a link to upload a reference to the University of Cambridge recruitment system by the application closing date. Applications will be reviewed after the closing date and interviews are provisionally planned for: w/c 30th November 2026. We anticipate that this 0.5 FTE post is equivalent to circa 18.5 hours per week, flexibility in exact working hours per week may be required. As a guide, the General Board expects the average full-time working week for academic-related employees to be in the region of 37 to 40 hours, therefore a part-time, 0.5 working week can be considered on average to be 18.5 to 20 hours per week. For any queries regarding the application please contact: hr@ast.cam.ac.uk . Informal enquiries may be addressed to Professor Sera Markoff via her EA, Joanna Carruthers, at plumianprofea@ast.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference LG50949 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University of Cambridge is a signatory to the San-Francisco Declaration on Research Assessment (DORA), and in recruitment or promotion evaluations will assess research on the basis of its merits rather than the journal or venue in which it is published. Applicants should not include Journal Impact Factors or uncontextualized metrics in their applications. Full details are at: https://www.research-strategy.admin.cam.ac.uk/research-policy/DORA The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University of Cambridge thrives on the diversity of its staff and students. Applications from underrepresented groups are particularly welcome. We have an active Equality and Diversity Committee which continually works to further the aims of the Athena SWAN charter. The University has a number of family-friendly policies and initiatives, including a returning-carer scheme, childcare costs support, university workplace nurseries, university holiday play-schemes, and a shared parental-leave policy.\nThe University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Institute of Astronomy Salary £35,608-£46,049 pro rata Reference LG50949 Category Academic-related Date published 22 September 2026 Closing date 9 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/richard-elphick-studentship-in-modelling-progression-risks-in-barretts-oesophagus-rd51277": {
      "title": "Richard Elphick Studentship in Modelling progression risks in Barrett's oesophagus.",
      "description": "Richard Elphick Studentship in Modelling progression risks in Barrett's oesophagus. | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Richard Elphick Studentship in Modelling progression risks in Barrett's oesophagus. Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Richard Elphick Studentship in Modelling progression risks in Barrett's oesophagus. Applications are invited for the Richard Elphick Studentship, a fully funded PhD studentship at Trinity College, established by friends and family in memory of Richard. The successful candidate will undertake research on \"Modelling progression risks in Barrett's oesophagus\" under the supervision of Professor Rebecca Fitzgerald (Department of Oncology) and Professor Nora Pashayan (Department of Public Health and Primary Care). This is an opportunity to work with Professor Fitzgerald and her research group, who are internationally renowned for their work on Barrett's oesophagus including the development of a novel, non-endoscopic capsule sponge test, and Professor Pashayan's research group who are leading in quantitative methods to develop risk-based approaches to cancer screening. Project Outline: Oesophageal adenocarcinoma (OAC), the most common oesophageal cancer in the UK and US, has a 5-year survival rate below 20%, though outcomes improve with early detection. Barrett's oesophagus, an asymptomatic precursor, is monitored to detect and treat dysplasia before cancer develops or to identify cancer at an earlier stage. However, most cases remain undiagnosed. The Cytosponge, developed by Prof Fitzgerald's lab, offers a cheaper, GP-based alternative to endoscopy, increasing Barrett's detection and the number of people entering surveillance. This project will use large multicentre datasets to develop a Markov multistate model of Barrett's oesophagus progression, estimating transition risks and incorporating patient and disease factors to predict individual cancer risk and personalise surveillance intervals. Microsimulation studies will compare fixed and risk-based surveillance strategies and evaluate endoscopy versus Cytosponge-based approaches. Findings will be translated into a clinical decision-support and visualisation tool that communicates personalised progression risk and supports tailored surveillance recommendations for patients and clinicians. Eligibility and Funding: Applications are welcomed from UK and Overseas candidates who meet the entry requirements for the PhD in Oncology. The studentship will commence in the 2027-28 academic year and will provide full funding for 3.5 years, including tuition fees at either the UK Home or Overseas rate, a maintenance stipend at the applicable UKRI rate, and a research emolument. More details of the funding amounts can be found on the University's postgraduate funding webpages and the Trinity College website. How to Apply Applicants should apply for the PhD in Oncology via the University's Applicant Portal and enter the project title and supervisor(s)' names in the relevant section of the application form. In their statement of interest, applicants should demonstrate how their skills align with the requirements of the project. A research proposal is not required for this application; however, to satisfy system requirements, applicants should upload a blank document in the designated research proposal section.\nApplicants must select \"Yes - I wish to be considered for funding\" within the application and choose the Richard Elphick Studentship from the list of available funding opportunities and complete any additional questions. Applicants are advised to indicate Trinity as the College of first preference on their application to the University. Further Information Enquiries regarding the research project, application process or the PhD in Oncology should be directed to the Postgraduate Administration Team, Department of Oncology (email: postgradadmin@oncology.cam.ac.uk ) Enquiries relating to the Richard Elphick Studentship should be directed to Trinity College (email: gradfunding@trin.cam.ac.uk ). The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK Please quote reference RD51277 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Oncology Salary Reference RD51277 Category Studentships Date published 2 October 2026 Closing date 8 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
