      "title": "Senior Research Fellowship Sponsorship Scheme",
      "description": "Senior Research Fellowship Sponsorship Scheme | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Research Fellowship Sponsorship Scheme Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Research Fellowship Sponsorship Scheme The Department of Zoology at the University of Cambridge seeks Expressions of Interest from early career researchers who wish to be sponsored for externally funded fellowships that will allow them to start an independent research group in our Department. Are you planning to submit a senior research fellowship* application for funding competitions with deadlines after March 2027? We offer a programme of support that will help establish you as an independent academic researcher, including assistance with your fellowship application, setting up your lab and recruiting personnel, as well as mentoring and training to help you develop your career. Research Fellows will also have access to the comprehensive package of ongoing support available from the School of Biological Sciences https://www.bio.cam.ac.uk/research/research-fellowships . We have an excellent track record in progressing our Senior Research Fellows into lectureships or equivalent positions at Cambridge and other leading international universities and research institutes. About the Department of Zoology We are a thriving community of researchers investigating how animal life evolved, how it functions and persists today and how we can conserve it for future generations. The Department comprises around 30 research groups, working at every level of biological organization from genes to ecosystems. The Department and the wider University of Cambridge are committed, in their pursuit of academic and research excellence, to a pro-active and inclusive approach to equality, which supports and encourages all under-represented groups, promotes an inclusive culture, and values diversity. Our dynamic intellectual environment includes the world-leading Cambridge Conservation Institute and University Museum of Zoology, and provides opportunities for collaboration in research themes that span the School of Biological Sciences ( https://www.bio.cam.ac.uk/research/research-themes ) and the wider university ( https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/research-at-cambridge/strategic-research-initiatives-networks ). The School of Biological Sciences has state of the art facilities in imaging, high performance computing and bioinformatics. The Department's research strengths currently lie in behaviour and neuroscience, conservation science, ecology and all aspects of evolutionary biology. How to apply for sponsorship Please address informal enquires to the Head of Department Professor Rebecca Kilner [email: hod@zoo.cam.ac.uk ] To make an application, please download and complete the very short application form ( https://tinyurl.com/tpv27mx2 ) and send it with your publications list and contact details for two referees to admin@zoo.cam.ac.uk by 17:00 GMT on Tuesday 10th November 2026. We will only contact you, and your referees, if you are shortlisted. We will shortlist by Tuesday 8th December 2026. Shortlisted candidates will be invited to the Department to meet the academic staff and give a seminar in the week beginning 11 January 2027. We typically sponsor two people each year and sponsorship is for two years. *Target fellowship schemes include: NERC Independent Fellowship, ERC Starting / Consolidator Grant, The Wellcome Trust Career Development Awards, Royal Society University Research Fellowship, Royal Society Dorothy Hodgkin Fellowship, Branco-Weiss Research Fellowship. If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Paulina Lasocka or Anastasia Nezhentseva  [email: hr@zoo.cam.ac.uk ] Please quote reference PF50279 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Zoology Salary Reference PF50279 Category Research Date published 7 July 2026 Closing date 10 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-teaching-associate-fixed-term-ka50205": {
      "title": "Senior Teaching Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Senior Teaching Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Teaching Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Teaching Associate (Fixed Term) The Department of Physics invites applications for a Senior Teaching Associate in the field of Computational Continuum Modelling and High Performance to support the teaching and assessment of the MPhil in Scientific Computing https://mphil.csc.cam.ac.uk/ The MPhil is a full-time course which provides education on high performance computing and advanced algorithms for numerical simulation at continuum and atomic-scale levels. The MPhil has a taught and a research element which carry equal examination credit. Students attend lecture modules on theory, algorithms and methods for modelling and numerical simulation, as well as research computing, software development and high-performance computing. This theoretical knowledge, which is complemented by hands-on practical sessions and written assignments (both involving coding), provides a solid foundation for the research project carried out during the second part of the academic year. The successful candidate will provide support and contribute to the continuum and high performance computing streams and their respective teaching schedule, and are therefore expected to have extensive experience in those areas. In particular, they should be able to demonstrate a comprehensive understanding of, and show practical experience with, the mathematics of systems of nonlinear inhomogeneous partial differential equations, and shock-capturing, Riemann problem based numerical algorithms for their solution. They must also be proficient in C++ and the techniques required for software development best practice.  Familiarity with methods for high-performance computing such as parallelisation is essential. For more information on the course modules, please see: https://mphil.csc.cam.ac.uk/course_structure/ They should be an excellent communicator and it is also desirable, but not essential, to have a track record of delivering lectures or computational practical classes. They must be highly organised and motivated and should demonstrate aptitude for programme coordination.  It is expected that they will hold, or be imminently completing, a PhD in mathematics or physics (or a cognate discipline). Due to the fast-moving nature of the topics covered in the MPhil, the role holder would be expected to participate in the research activities of the research group associated with the programme (The Laboratory for Scientific Computing), to maintain their knowledge of the current status of research, and techniques used within them. The successful candidate will be expected to support the MPhil primarily by: contributing to the creation and delivery of course modules, the supervision, assessment and examination of modules, the provision, supervision and assessment of written assignments and research projects across a range of continuum and HPC topics and interviewing applicants. They will also be expected to participate in the administration of tasks associated with the academic delivery of these programmes like overseeing the seminar series and industry led modules, and the coordination and delivery of non-examined short courses on advanced topics or transferable skills. A new Academic Career pathway scheme for Teaching and Scholarship staff (ACP T&S) has been developed to recognise and reward outstanding contributions and celebrate academic achievement through promotion and/or pay progression. This post falls within this career pathway scheme. Further details of the scheme are available at https://www.acptands.hr.admin.cam.ac.uk/acp-overview The Departments are active in promoting policies to address historic under-representation of women and minority groups in its workforce. Candidates from under-represented groups, as well as candidates with a track record in addressing barriers to equality and diversity in education, are particularly encouraged to apply. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 12 months in the first instance, with the possibility to extend subject to funding. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Applicants should submit the following documents with their application: Cover letter summarising their suitability for this post Curriculum Vitae (CV) An experience statement of up to two pages setting out their past work in scientific computing related to computational continuum modelling and high performance computing and how it relates to the skills required for this position. Optional: Evidence of teaching contribution at undergraduate or postgraduate levels (e.g. lecture notes, computing practical sheets etc). Please include details of three referees, including e-mail address and phone number, whom the University of Cambridge can contact for references. The deadline for applications is 1st November 2026. Shortlisted applicants will be invited to visit Cambridge to give a teaching presentation, for informal discussions and formal interview. Informal enquiries are welcome and should be sent to Prof. N. Nikiforakis ( nn10005@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference KA50205 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Department of Physics Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference KA50205 Category Academic Date published 28 August 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/service-desk-technician-ns51302": {
      "title": "Service Desk Technician",
      "description": "Service Desk Technician | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Service Desk Technician Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Service Desk Technician About the role: The Service Desk team provide first line support to all staff and students. This role is predominantly customer facing and requires a good all-round knowledge of modern computing systems and services with excellent troubleshooting and fault-finding skills. You will need a solid understanding of the administrative systems in use at the school and wider University as well as the internal, custom developed applications and processes that are in place to support teaching & learning. About you: Taking direction from the Service Desk Team Leader, your work will be varied and cross functional including project-based activities. Your day-to-day work will consist of deskside and remote support with tickets proactively picked up or allocated through the service desk or because of internal processes and procedures. You will also work occasional weekends, providing programme support on a rota to support teaching delivery. To meet the demands of the staff and students, the role requires the ability to quickly assimilate new ideas, embracing technology changes and having the confidence and initiative to solve problems independently. The role holder will be fully trained and supported through on the job, commercial courses, and personal development programmes. Why work for Cambridge Judge Business School? We are the business school of the University of Cambridge, advancing knowledge and leadership through people who leave a mark on the world.  Cambridge Judge Business School leverages the power of academia for real world impact to transform individuals, organisations and society. Since 1990, we have forged a reputation as a centre of rigorous thinking and high-impact transformative education, situated within one of the world's most prestigious research universities, and in the heart of the Cambridge Cluster, the most successful technology entrepreneurship cluster in Europe. Benefits of working for Cambridge Judge Business School: You'll receive excellent benefits at Cambridge Judge Business School including:\n- Generous holiday allowance\n- Defined benefits pension schemes\n- Flexible working options\n- Family-friendly initiatives\n- Career development opportunities\n- Support for health and mental wellbeing\n- Discounts on shopping\n- Tax-efficient bicycle and charity-giving schemes More information is available on our website - Staff benefits - Jobs at Cambridge Judge Business School - Cambridge Judge Business School Application process: To view more details about the role and person specification, please click the Further Information link. Closing Date: 23rd October 2026 Interview Dates: TBD If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact: hrsupport@jbs.cam.ac.uk Additional Information: The University is supportive of hybrid working, where some work is undertaken on University premises and some in a remote working environment. The aim of our approach is to enable as many staff as possible to work in a hybrid way if they wish and where their role allows we expect most colleagues to spend at least 50% of their time at their dedicated office location. This arrangement is subject to change and will not form a part of the contractual terms of the role. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference NS51302 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Judge Business School Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference NS51302 Category Academic-related Date published 7 October 2026 Closing date 23 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/specialist-veterinary-nurse-in-anaesthesia-and-theatre-pp51288": {
      "title": "Specialist Veterinary Nurse in Anaesthesia and Theatre",
      "description": "Specialist Veterinary Nurse in Anaesthesia and Theatre | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Specialist Veterinary Nurse in Anaesthesia and Theatre Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Specialist Veterinary Nurse in Anaesthesia and Theatre Are you keen to expand and develop your veterinary nursing skills in a fast-paced and dynamic environment? If so, we have an exciting opportunity to join our Veterinary Theatre Nursing Team at the Queens Veterinary School Hospital which is an integral part of the Department of Veterinary Medicine and is an RCVS accredited teaching Hospital that provides veterinary care 24/7 365 days a year. We are seeking individuals with a passion for nursing, and the ability to communicate with all levels of staff, students and clients for this varied and interesting position. This role is offered on a full-time basis; however, we also welcome applications from candidates seeking part-time work (minimum 4 days per week). Working as part of a multi-skilled team of Veterinary Clinicians and Nurses the Specialist Nurse Anaesthesia and Theatre role is essential in supporting our specialist veterinary teams to deliver outstanding patient care and manage workflow throughout the hospital working in surgical theatres, medicine procedure rooms maintenance and monitoring of patients under general anaesthesia or sedation whilst having procedures, including diagnostic imaging. You will be available to assist specialist nurses with the care of their patients and teams and if necessary to the wards teams e.g. to monitor an intensive recovery or provide one-to-one patient care. The referral hospital is a very fast-paced environment which requires the role holder to be adaptable with the ability to work on your own initiative with minimal supervision and working well under pressure is essential. In return, we offer an encouraging and nurturing environment and have a dedicated team of clinicians and nurses who are committed to providing the best care for our patients. The role is offered with a salary of between £31,236.00-£35,608.00 per annum depending on experience, plus on call allowance and an abundance of benefits such as: Competitive salary Friendly and caring environment, including plenty of cakes for birthdays Enhanced annual leave allowance of 36 days including bank holidays Enhanced pension scheme Access to CAMbens employee discounts, including discounted travel across Cambridge by bus Cycle to work scheme and travel to work loan scheme Family friendly benefits and enhanced leave Health and Wellbeing benefits such as discounted membership to the University's Sport Centre Training and Development and Career Development Both full-time and part-time candidates may apply for this post. Working patterns can be discussed with candidates, so please include your preference in your application. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries about this role and to arrange informal visits, please contact James Gasson via email: jpg74@cam.ac.uk Further particulars for the role and information about the Department are available in the Further Particulars document enclosed with this advert. If you are viewing this advert on an external site, please visit the University's job pages for access. Applications will be monitored regularly, and we may contact candidates prior to the closing date. Therefore, if you are interested, please submit your application as early as possible. Interviews will take place within two weeks of the closing date. Please quote reference PP51288 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Specialist Veterinary Nurse in Anaesthesia and Theatre Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference PP51288 Category Assistant staff Date published 2 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/spld-screening-adviser-temporary-cover-part-time-aw51247": {
      "title": "SpLD Screening Adviser [Temporary Cover] (Part Time)",
      "description": "SpLD Screening Adviser [Temporary Cover] (Part Time) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs SpLD Screening Adviser [Temporary Cover] (Part Time) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs SpLD Screening Adviser [Temporary Cover] (Part Time) Temporary cover: This post is fixed-term until 12 July 2028 or the return of the post holder, whichever is the earlier. The Accessibility & Disability Resource Centre (ADRC) invites applications for a part time (0.5 FTE) Specific Learning Difficulties (SpLD) Screening Adviser. The post holder will provide an initial screening service to students who identify with traits of SpLD. You will work as part of the ADRC team and also work closely with academic and support staff within university departments and within colleges, as well as with other professional services staff within the University's Student Services Centre. The post-holder will report to the Head of Centre\nYou will be qualified at degree level (or with equivalent relevant experience). and hold a post-graduate qualification in assessing and diagnosing specific learning difficulties. You should have recent experience screening or assessing students for SpLDs and an up-to-date and comprehensive knowledge of legislation, regulatory guidance and debate in relation to disability equality in a tertiary education setting. An understanding of and commitment to the principles of Universal Design for Learning (UDL) would be an advantage.\nYou will need excellent verbal and written communication skills, and be able to work with people from a wide variety of backgrounds, experiences and expectations, demonstrating a commitment to equality of opportunity. The ability to work to deadlines in a busy support service environment is essential. Benefits of working at the University of Cambridge include: Competitive rates of pay with automatic service-related pay progression and annual cost of living increases; Generous annual leave allowance; Flexible and hybrid working opportunities; Generous maternity, adoption and shared parental leave entitlement and other family friendly schemes (e.g. workplace nurseries); An auto-enrolment pension scheme, with a generous employer contribution; Travel benefits and retail discounts at over 2,000 local and national stores; Schemes to support with relocation / the provision of accommodation Information for disabled applicants is contained within the Further Particulars.\nAdditional information regarding the role can be found within the Further Particulars. When applying, please upload your CV and cover letter which clearly sets out how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification of the Further Particulars (the knowledge, skills, and experience required for the role) along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted and invited to interview based upon these criteria. If you are unable to apply online, please contact disability@admin.cam.ac.uk Applications are welcome  from both external and internal candidates, including internal candidates  who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. The closing date for application is: Monday 9th November The interview date is: Weds 18th November We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact SSCjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AW51247 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Student Support Salary £35,608-£46,049 pro rata Reference AW51247 Category Academic-related Date published 6 October 2026 Closing date 9 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/student-wellbeing-training-and-development-lead-aw50967": {
      "title": "Student Wellbeing Training and Development Lead",
      "description": "Student Wellbeing Training and Development Lead | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Student Wellbeing Training and Development Lead Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Student Wellbeing Training and Development Lead The University Counselling and Wellbeing Service (Student Support) is a dynamic, high-demand service driven by our mission to support and empower students in fulfilling their academic and personal potential. \nWe are at an exciting stage of the service's development. Following a transformative Student Mental Health and Wellbeing Plan (2022-2025) at the Collegiate University, we are recruiting to a number of roles to support our service's work and objectives under a new Student Support strategic plan (2026-2029). As part of an integrated Student Support Department, we work in partnership with the Mental Health Advice Service, the Harassment and Violence Support Service, and the Accessibility and Disability Resource to offer students joined-up support. These services contribute to a wider stepped care approach at Collegiate Cambridge, working in partnership with College Health and Wellbeing teams who support students in their College community.\nWe are seeking to appoint an exceptional candidate to the role of Student Wellbeing Training and Development Lead. The successful candidate will drive forward an ambitious programme of highly contextualised training for staff at the Collegiate University drawing on considerable expertise and specialist knowledge of student mental health contexts. This exciting role also includes practitioner and direct student-facing work as a Psychological Wellbeing Adviser, and working closely with the Head of Counselling and Wellbeing to support the delivery of an agreed portfolio of strategic project work. The role holder will actively engage with regular clinical supervision, service meetings and maintain appropriate standards of continuing professional development as part of the service's commitment to the highest standards of professional development, ethical practice and quality assurance. Please note that this post requires a professional who can demonstrate appropriate qualifications, training and experience in delivering psychological wellbeing support within an organisational context, alongside demonstrable experience in delivering mental health-related training and complex, multi-stakeholder projects. Applications which are unable to demonstrate these essential requirements for the post will not be progressed. When applying, please upload your CV and a cover letter outlining how you meet the criteria set out in the Person Specification of the further information document above. Additional notes: Please see the Further Particulars role brochure on this page for full details on the role. We particularly encourage candidates from a Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic background to apply for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level within our University. Benefits of working at the University of Cambridge include: Competitive rates of pay with automatic service-related pay progression and annual cost of living increases; Generous annual leave allowance; Flexible and hybrid working opportunities; Generous maternity, adoption and shared parental leave entitlement and other family friendly schemes (e.g. workplace nurseries); An auto-enrolment pension scheme, with a generous employer contribution; Travel benefits and retail discounts at over 2,000 local and national stores; Schemes to support with relocation / the provision of accommodation If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact: sscjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is: Friday 16th October 2026 The interview date for the post is: Friday 30th October 2026 Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact SSCjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AW50967 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Student Wellbeing  Training and Development Lead FPs Key information Department/location Student Support Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference AW50967 Category Academic-related Date published 5 October 2026 Closing date 16 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/study-manager-part-time-fixed-term-rp51380": {
      "title": "Study Manager (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Study Manager (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Study Manager (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Study Manager (Part Time, Fixed Term) Join the NIHR-funded NeWTS programme to lead delivery of a portfolio of pioneering neonatal medical device clinical investigations. The University of Cambridge, Department of Paediatrics, NeWTS team is delivering a complex, high profile medical device development programme comprising multiple inter-related clinical investigations involving adults, neonates, NHS organisations, academic partners and commercial collaborators. The programme is central to generating the clinical and usability evidence required to support regulatory approval and adoption of the NeWTS device into routine clinical practice. Working with Dr Kathryn Beardsall and partners across the NHS, academia and industry, the post holder will oversee study delivery from set-up to close-out and play a key role in bringing an innovative neonatal technology towards regulatory approval and clinical adoption. Successful delivery of these investigations is critical to supporting regulatory approval and adoption of the NeWTS device into routine clinical practice.\nThe post holder will have a key role in the operational leadership and delivery of the NeWTS clinical investigation portfolio, which includes usability studies, clinical investigations and implementation research conducted across multiple sites and stakeholder groups. The role requires coordination of complex study activities, management of regulatory and governance processes, oversight of study performance and risk management, and engagement with internal and external stakeholders to ensure delivery of programme objectives. Working closely with the Chief Investigator and wider programme team, the post holder will act as a central operational contact for collaborating organisations, NHS partners, regulatory authorities and industry stakeholders, providing oversight of study delivery from set-up through to close-out. The role will contribute to the development of systems, processes and partnerships that support high-quality, compliant and efficient delivery of the programme within agreed timelines, budgets and governance frameworks. The post holder will provide operational leadership for the NIHR-funded programme of clinical investigations, with responsibility for planning, coordinating and monitoring delivery across multiple interdependent studies involving NHS organisations, academic collaborators, commercial partners and regulatory authorities. Working with delegated authority from the Chief Investigator, the role holder will identify risks, develop mitigation strategies, oversee study governance, manage stakeholder relationships, coordinate resources and budgets, and make operational decisions necessary to achieve programme milestones. The role holder will represent the programme internally and externally, establish effective working relationships across partner organisations, lead operational meetings and contribute to the continuous improvement of study processes and systems. The post holder will be accountable for maintaining regulatory compliance, participant safety, data integrity and delivery of study objectives within agreed timelines and funding requirements. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 August 2027 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RP51380 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars (RP51380) Key information Department/location Department of Paediatrics Salary £35,608-£46,049 pro rata Reference RP51380 Category Academic-related Date published 12 October 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/surveyor-grade-3-surveyor-grade-4-jd51317": {
      "title": "Surveyor Grade 3 / Surveyor Grade 4",
      "description": "Surveyor Grade 3 / Surveyor Grade 4 | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Surveyor Grade 3 / Surveyor Grade 4 Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Surveyor Grade 3 / Surveyor Grade 4 The Cambridge Archaeological Unit (CAU) is recruiting an archaeological Surveyor to work on a range of archaeological excavations, evaluations and surveys throughout East Anglia. Projects include prehistoric landscapes, major Iron Age and Roman settlements, and various urban sites. The Role:\nThe archaeological Surveyor is responsible for the digital recording of archaeological evaluations, excavations and watching briefs. Working as part of a small team, you will collect data in the field, using equipment including a GNSS, TPS and drone, and process that data in the office, using programmes including AutoCAD and ArcGIS Pro. The post holder will assist site directors during the fieldwork and post-excavation phases and provide plans, contour maps etc for the illustration team for the production of client reports and publications. This role will be based both in the field on archaeological projects and in the office. Please note that lone working will be required from time to time. The archaeological Surveyor will assist in delivering archaeological services to a professional standard and to strict deadlines in a resource efficient way. The post holder will be part of the CAU's Survey Team and report to the Survey Section Head Jane Matthews. Requirements:\nThe Role-holder should hold a primary degree in Archaeology. Candidates without such a qualification degree may be considered, dependant on the level of relevant experience. Experience using GNSS, TPS and drones in an archaeological context is essential. General computer literacy is essential as is demonstrable experience using AutoCad, GIS, photogrammetry and Microsoft Office software. A good / excellent understanding and experience of archaeological fieldwork is essential, including knowledge of onsite archaeological drawing, especially planning. The role holder will be used to working independently, and problem-solving in the field. It is crucial that the role holder can prioritise and complete work to strict deadlines and be flexible to respond to the rapidly changing needs of archaeological sites. It is important that the role holder is comfortable lone working. Successful applicants must have a clean UK/EU Driving Licence and a willingness to drive off-road vehicles is essential, due to the remote nature of archaeological sites. One or more of the following would also be an advantage: valid CSCS card, Membership of CIfA or a First Aid at Work qualification.\nOther relevant H&S qualifications (eg: documented Manual Handling training, Pipe & Cable Detection training, Lantra 4x4 driver training), would also be viewed favourably. The successful candidate must also be willing to undertake mandatory University training and any training deemed essential for the role by the Departmental Safety Officer. Please include information regarding projects you have worked on and proven surveying skills, together with post survey processing experience. Photogrammetry examples can be submitted by email to cauhr@arch.cam.ac.uk . Please include the Job reference in the subject field of your email. Please review the Further Particulars document for each grade for details regarding the main responsibilities, person profile, and relevant experience. We have need for a Surveyor at either Grade 3 or Grade 4. Please note there is only one vacancy. If you are uncertain which role would best suit you, please submit applications for both grades. Please note in order to qualify for the Grade 4 post you must be able to evidence the full range of essential criteria listed in the Further Particulars document. Though the post is being advertised at full time (37.5hrs/week), for the right candidate the CAU would consider a 0.80FTE contract (30.0hrs/week). We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date for this application is 23.59 BST on Friday 30 October 2026. Applications must consist of a CV (listing full employment history) and a cover letter detailing your suitability for the role. In-person interviews will be held on Tuesday 17 November 2026 at our offices at 34 & B Storey's Way, Cambridge, CB3 0DT. Informal queries regarding this post may be sent to: \n - Jane Matthews ( cauhr@arch.cam.ac.uk ) for queries about the role and relevant skills.\n - HSPS HR team ( hr@hsps.cam.ac.uk ) for queries about the application process. It is expected that the successful candidate will start before or on 4th January 2027. Please quote reference JD51317 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Grade 3 & Grade 4 Key information Department/location Department of Archaeology (CAU) Salary Grade 3 £31,370 or Grade 4 £33, 945 Reference JD51317 Category Research Date published 6 October 2026 Closing date 30 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/teaching-professor-designated-principal-radiologist-pp50690": {
      "title": "Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist",
      "description": "Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist A leadership opportunity has arisen for an experienced specialist radiologist to advance their career by heading up the Diagnostic Imaging service team at the Queen's Veterinary School Hospital, Cambridge. We are a large, multidisciplinary small animal referral hospital, sitting within the University of Cambridge Department of Veterinary Medicine. The Hospital operates as a commercially competitive hospital, whilst remaining central to supporting teaching of veterinary undergraduate and postgraduate students, as well as delivering and supporting veterinary clinical and one health research. The QVSH also works collaboratively with our small animal and equine primary care practices and is supported by our in-house Clinical Pathology and Anatomical Pathology services. This role will be head of the Imaging service within the QVSH, responsible for leading, developing and supporting a high standard of clinical service provision in the Imaging team. The role holder will be responsible for ensuring this team works effectively as part of the wider referral hospital. Supporting clinical research is an important part of this role, as required for postgraduate training programmes, whilst balancing research impacts on the wider hospital requirement to provide a financially viable service.\nThe department currently consists of 4 part-time Imaging Diplomates (in addition to this post), 3 ECVDI residents, 1 Imaging Intern and 5 radiographers. The service provides referral level MRI, CT, Ultrasound and Radiography to the QVSH both in and out of hours, as well as offering advice and support to our small animal and equine primary care practices. The role primarily provides a specialist diagnostic imaging clinical service while contributing to the clinical teaching of undergraduate (Year 6) veterinary students and postgraduate Clinical Training Scholars working within the QVSH. The postholder will support the development and delivery of undergraduate and postgraduate teaching and assessment in diagnostic imaging, in collaboration with academic colleagues. Although this is not primarily a teaching post, there will be opportunities to contribute to both clinical and didactic teaching. Time will be allocated for the administrative and service leadership components of the role. The role holder will be required to participate in an out-of-hours rota, for which an additional payment is provided. Applicants should be a Diplomate of the European College of Veterinary Diagnostic Imaging (Dip ECVDI) or American College of Veterinary Radiology (Dip ACVR) and be a member of the Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons (RCVS) or hold a veterinary degree registerable with the RCVS. Significant experience of working in a multidisciplinary veterinary referral hospital is essential along with in depth understanding of the operation of a commercial veterinary referral hospital. We offer a supportive and engaging environment with generous paid annual leave (41 days including the public holidays). In addition, we are able to provide competitive staff benefits and services, including family friendly policies, numerous discounts on shopping, health care, financial services, and public transport. We also offer a generous pension scheme, annual CPD allowance and tax-efficient bicycle, car lease and charity-giving schemes. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Prof. Nick Bexfield, Hospital Director, by email: nb289@cam.ac.uk Further particulars for the role and information about the Department are available: https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/term/Department-of-Veterinary-Medicine If you are viewing this advert on an external site, please visit the University's job pages for access. Please quote reference PP50690 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £69,488-£80,525 plus Market Pay Supplement subject to approval Reference PP50690 Category Academic Date published 29 September 2026 Closing date 29 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
