      "title": "Technical Workshop Manager",
      "description": "Technical Workshop Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Technical Workshop Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Technical Workshop Manager A position exists for a Technical Workshop Manager in the Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics. The role holder will play a pivotal role in the organisation and operation of the GK Batchelor Laboratory and its associated Workshop. About the Role The Technical Workshop Manager is responsible for the day-to-day management of the Laboratory Workshop, working closely with the GKB Laboratory Manager. Acting as the first point of contact for technical queries by research personnel for the development and/or procurement of new equipment and general support in the Laboratory, the Technical Workshop Manager will take the lead in the creative design, specification, construction and/or procurement of new (often bespoke) equipment. You will be responsible for purchasing specialist materials and equipment for the workshop in a timely manner and within the budgetary constraints of the projects. Additionally, you will ensure the adoption of new technologies, develop novel in-house capabilities, and help the laboratory to respond rapidly to emerging fields and new scientific challenges. Managerial responsibility includes coordinating and supervising a team of three technicians, to maintain the Department's experimental research and teaching activities in the Laboratory and maintain its unique world-leading reputation. About The Laboratory The Laboratory, providing nearly 900m2 of research space, houses diverse research with experiments ranging from the biological physics to robotics and soft matter physics and planetary scale and industrial fluid mechanics. The Laboratory houses multiple diverse scientific research groups with different research focuses and agenda, hosts a steady stream of visitors and summer students, and provides support for educational efforts. The apparatus employed ranges from microfluidic devices and microscopes to AI-assisted automated robotic systems to flow tanks of up to 12m3. In a typical year more than 50 distinct research projects are undertaken with durations ranging from eight weeks to multiple years. About You You will have practical experience of specialist workshop technical work, including the operation of a broad range of computer-controlled and traditional workshop machinery, hand tools and manufacturing processes. You will have experience in designing and building bespoke scientific equipment. You will have demonstrable experience of managing or coordinating a technical workshop, including planning activities, maintaining equipment and resources, and ensuring safe and efficient operations. You will have experience of line management, supervision or leadership of technical staff. It is expected that you will have experience of mechanical design and technical drawing using CAD software (e.g. Fusion 360 or equivalent). You will be educated to degree level/level 6 vocational qualification or have an equivalent level of practical experience. Alternatively, you will have a recognized and certified apprenticeship in Engineering with proven workplace experience. For further essential criteria for the position, please see the further particulars attached to the advert webpage. Informal enquiries can be made by contacting Professor Raymond E. Goldstein FRS ( R.E.Goldstein@damtp.cam.ac.uk ) If you have any queries about the application process, please email LE51160@maths.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is 27 October 2026 (Midnight UK Time). Interviews are expected to be held in person at the Centre for Mathematical Sciences, Wilberforce Road, Cambridge. How to Apply Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please complete the details on the application form, upload your CV and submit a covering letter clearly stating how your skills and experience match the criteria specified in the person profile section of the further particulars. Please also provide the contact details of two professional referees, at least one of whom should be contactable if you are invited to attend an interview. Please quote reference LE51160 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Technical Workshop Manager FP's Academic-related staff_hr7 Key information Department/location Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference LE51160 Category Academic-related Date published 7 October 2026 Closing date 27 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/unestablished-teaching-associate-part-time-fixed-term-ge51076": {
      "title": "Unestablished Teaching Associate (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Unestablished Teaching Associate (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Unestablished Teaching Associate (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Unestablished Teaching Associate (Part Time, Fixed Term) The Faculty of Classics is seeking to appoint a part time (0.6 FTE) Teaching Associate from 01 January 2027 (or as soon as possible). The role will be fixed term for 24 months due to limited funding. The post offers an opportunity for early career starters to increase their skills through a role in a vibrant and distinguished community of Classicists.  Applications are invited from those with an interest in teaching Ancient Greek and Latin. The teaching will principally involve intensive reading classes in Greek and Latin for students without A level qualification or equivalent at entry.  The successful applicant will also be expected to take a full part in the Pre-term IG language teaching and as marker and/or assessor of language papers across the Tripos. Information about the Faculty can be found at www.classics.cam.ac.uk . The undergraduate and postgraduate student body in Classics has become increasingly diverse in recent years and we aim to achieve similar diversity among academic staff. Accordingly, the Faculty particularly encourages women and candidates from a Black, Asian, and Minority Ethnic background to apply for these vacancies. The University was awarded the Race Equality Charter Bronze award in 2019; the Faculty of Classics was awarded the Athena SWAN Bronze award in 2021. Candidates will need to show evidence of the following qualifications, skills, and experience: Evidence of ability to teach Greek and Latin language effectively, and at all relevant levels, including ab initio; Evidence of ability to teach Greek and Latin texts from a variety of genres; Ability to play an effective role in the life and work of the Faculty as a whole; Ability to work as part of a team. Informal enquiries about the post are welcomed and can be directed to Dr Rosanna \nOmitowoju at rso20@cam.ac.uk . Questions about the application process can be directed to the Schools HR Team at sahhr@admin.cam.ac.uk . For your application to be considered, please ensure that you upload the following: CV (including details of language teaching experience at all levels and listing any \npublications) Covering letter Please do not upload any additional documents to those specified above as these will not be considered as part of your application. Applicants are requested to ask two referees to write directly to recruitment@classics.cam.ac.uk by the closing date. The closing date is midnight (BST) on Sunday 18 October 2026. Interviews are planned \nfor Friday 06 November 2026, subject to change. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference GE51076 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Faculty of Classics Salary £33,951-£39,906 pro rata Reference GE51076 Category Academic Date published 22 September 2026 Closing date 18 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/urological-cancers-translational-research-manager-fixed-term-internal-applicants-only-re51338": {
      "title": "Urological Cancers Translational Research Manager (Fixed Term) (Internal Applicants Only)",
      "description": "Urological Cancers Translational Research Manager (Fixed Term) (Internal Applicants Only) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Urological Cancers Translational Research Manager (Fixed Term) (Internal Applicants Only) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Urological Cancers Translational Research Manager (Fixed Term) (Internal Applicants Only) Internal applications are invited for an  enthusiastic and self-motivated Translational Research Manager. The successful candidate will manage the Urological Malignancies Virtual Institute (UMVI) translational research team. The Translational Research Manager (TRM) will ensure all clinical samples are appropriately recorded and stored in line with relevant legislation and local practices/guidelines. The TRM will lead, delegate, conduct and audit sample collection, sample records and lab work. The role holder will also maintain robust, real-time records on sample analysis. We expect the TRM to contribute scientifically to sampling and analytical plans and to manage the operational deployment of the translational team to meet goals and milestones. The TRM should have a strong scientific background with extensive lab experience alongside excellent project management skills. The Urological Malignancies Programme is an important component part of the CRUK Cambridge Cancer Centre which is led by Professor Richard Gilbertson FRS. Led by Professor Grant Stewart and Dr Brent O'Carrigan, the UMP members are working together to improve outcomes for patients with urological cancers. The CRUK Cambridge Centre is renowned nationally and internationally and benefits from a major commitment from Cancer Research UK, recognising Cambridge's position at the forefront of cancer research. Our main work centres around translational clinical studies, which obtain blood, tissue, stool and urine samples from patients with urological cancers (i.e. kidney, prostate and bladder), and provides them to our researchers to help us understand the mechanisms by which urological cancers arise, progress and evade detection. Our team is small and focused, with a busy daily schedule. Daily activities include consenting patients to research studies, obtaining biological samples, completing case report forms, entering data onto clinical research databases, and maintaining study documentation. You will need the ability to work in a self-directed, independent and flexible manner, and enjoy working in a small team where members have a wide range of skills and backgrounds. Some out of hours working is required to collect and process samples from early morning and late running surgeries. Informal enquiries may be made to Mrs Carley Batley, the Urological Malignancies Programme Manager in the first instance ( cb2147@cam.ac.uk ). Further information on our group can be found at our website: https://www.cambridge-urologicalmalignancies.org.uk Funding is available to support this post untill 31 March 2028 in the first instance. Closing date for applications is 21 October 2026 with interviews dates TBC. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a standard Disclosure and Barring Service check. This appointment also requires a Research Passport application. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RE51338 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars (RE51338) Key information Department/location Department of Surgery Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference RE51338 Category Academic-related Date published 8 October 2026 Closing date 21 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/veterinary-nurse-small-animal-wing-pp51258": {
      "title": "Veterinary Nurse",
      "description": "Veterinary Nurse - Small Animal Wing | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Veterinary Nurse - Small Animal Wing Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Veterinary Nurse - Small Animal Wing Based on August 2025 salary scale - £27,319.00 to £31,236.00\nSalary £26,226.24 to £29,986.56 (pro rata for 0.96 FTE) + 15% Shift Allowance =\n£30,160.18 to £34,484.54 per annum, working an average of 35 hours a week.\nThis equates to approximately an hourly rate of £14.32 ¿ £16.38 (£16.47-£18.84 with 15% uplift). We are delighted to offer an exciting opportunity for a Registered Veterinary Nurse (RVN) to join the dedicated nursing team at the Queens Veterinary School Hospital. This is a permanent position, available to start as soon as possible. The referral hospital is a very fast-paced environment which requires you to be adaptable with the ability to work well under pressure. The role will be to provide nursing to an excellent standard in the Small Animal Wing. The small animal wing consists of four dog, two cat, critical care and isolation wards housing an average of 15-25 patients during the overnight period. We are looking for a nurse, who is able to manage routine and complex situations confidently, drawing on experience gained during at least 12 months of post-registration practice, who can cover 24/7 working on a 7 week, generous and attractive rotating shift pattern, covering core, early, late, night shifts and time off (includes 10 day off shift period after weeks of nights). Weekend working (2 in 7). Rotas are planned well in advance to help employees maintain a healthy work-life balance. The role in Small Animal inpatients will include surgical, medical and critical care nursing (training can be given in critical care procedures). Cleaning, hygiene and administration duties linked to inpatient care will form a reasonable percentage of the time. This is a varied role where no two days are the same, so we are seeking individuals with a passion for nursing, with the ability to communicate with all levels of staff, students and clients. The ability to work on your own initiative with minimal supervision is essential. Shifts have student nurse and Veterinary Care Assistant support. In return, we offer an encouraging and nurturing environment and have a dedicated team of clinicians and nurses who are committed to providing the best care for our patients. Benefits: \n - Generous paid annual leave including bank holidays\n - Defined benefit pension scheme\n - Enhanced family friendly policies\n - Access to a dedicated Personal and Professional Development team\n - Wellness programme including Occupational Health team and Staff counselling\n - Staff discount scheme including shopping vouchers\n - Cycle to work scheme\n - Travel to work loans \n - Eye care voucher scheme\n - Discounted gym membership \n - CPD allowance Applications will be monitored regularly, and we may contact candidates prior to the closing date.  We reserve the right to close this vacancy early if we receive sufficient applications for the role. Therefore, if you are interested, please submit your application as early as possible. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this role, please contact the QVSH Clinical HR Team on qvsh.hr@vet.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference PP51258 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. Please quote reference PP51258 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Veterinary Nurse - Small Animal Wing Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £27,319-£31,236 pro rata Reference PP51258 Category Assistant staff Date published 6 October 2026 Closing date 3 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/widening-participation-coordinator-at51306": {
      "title": "Widening Participation Coordinator",
      "description": "Widening Participation Coordinator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Widening Participation Coordinator Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Widening Participation Coordinator Student Admissions and Access is looking for a professional with experience in the education or charity sector to join the Widening Participation (WP) team. The WP team works with partners and colleagues across the University and the UK to coordinate a range of outreach programmes to support students from underrepresented and less advantaged backgrounds with their consideration of and application to Cambridge and other leading universities. In this role, you would be part of a national agenda to ensure that higher education is open to all students, regardless of their background. The role As one of seven Widening Participation Coordinators, you will coordinate at least one outreach programme and contribute to the development and delivery of the team's wider WP activity. Our aim is for the successful applicant to lead on projects aimed at supporting prospective applicants from ethnicities currently underrepresented at Cambridge. This includes co-ordinating our established Target Oxbridge programme and scoping a new project to support British Bangladeshi and British Pakistani students. You will research, design, plan and deliver programmes of activities that support students to develop key academic skills, instil confidence and develop self-efficacy, while helping them to explore higher education and understand the pathways available to achieve their aspirations. You will work with students and their parents, carers and teachers; staff and students from Cambridge departments and Colleges; as well as other partners to deliver programmes and evaluate their effectiveness and impact. About you We are looking for a well-organised and adaptable professional with experience in education, the charity sector or a related field. You will have excellent communication and interpersonal skills, strong IT and organisational skills, and the ability to manage competing priorities and work effectively both independently and as part of a team. An understanding of widening participation and barriers to higher education would be advantageous. The role may involve occasional evening and weekend work and travel to locations within our target areas. We operate agile working practices, providing flexibility in where and how you work, in agreement with your line manager. Benefits A competitive salary with automatic service-related progression and annual cost of living increases A generous annual leave allowance of 41 days per year (including bank holidays) Access to a hybrid pension scheme with attractive employer contributions Access to a range of shopping and travel discounts through the Cambens scheme. Application process Further information about the role and the criteria for appointment can be found in the accompanying further information pack. When applying, please upload your CV and a cover letter outlining how you meet the criteria set out in the Person Specification of the further information document Application deadline: 8 November 2026 Expected interview date: 17 November 2026 For an informal discussion about the role, please contact Paul Potter, Deputy Head of Widening Participation, at paul.potter@admin.cam.ac.uk . Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment and an enhanced DBS check. Student Admissions and Access is committed to safeguarding the young people we work with and expects all employees to share this commitment. Further information is available in the University safeguarding policy. The University values diversity and is committed to equality of opportunity. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact SSCjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AT51306 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Student Admissions & Access Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference AT51306 Category Academic-related Date published 6 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/accounts-assistant-helpdesk-fixed-term-ag51309": {
      "title": "Accounts Assistant",
      "description": "Accounts Assistant - Helpdesk (fixed term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Accounts Assistant - Helpdesk (fixed term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Accounts Assistant - Helpdesk (fixed term) An exciting opportunity has arisen to join the Finance Division Helpdesk team as an Accounts Assistant - Helpdesk. The Finance Division manages the financial resources of the University and the support services for the financial functions carried out by the University's departments. This role is part of the Accounting Services section of the Finance Division which performs a range of key strategic and operational accounting and administrative duties. If you are passionate about Finance, keen to develop your skills, and looking for an exciting opportunity to deliver a high quality service, then this is the role for you. The Role The Helpdesk team provides specialist first-line support for various aspects of the Cambridge University Financial System (CUFS), including areas such as: User Administration, Accounts Payable, Accounts Receivable, iProcurement and Expenses, advising departments on best practice as well as being a point of contact for suppliers and customers of the University, ensuring calls and enquiries are dealt with in a timely and professional manner. In addition to providing first-line support in the areas above you will also assist the wider Accounting Services team in areas such as assisting with allocation of payments received by the University, verification of International Payments and helping teams where short-term support is needed. Your Skills The successful candidate will be expected to have: Experience with the processing of accounting transactions Able to work independently and as part of a team Have excellent communication skills and telephone manner, and be able to relay information to a wide range of people of all levels Intermediate knowledge of excel and working knowledge of Oracle Financials Experience of dealing with complex reconciliations Strengths of time management and attention to detail Benefits The University offers an excellent benefit package including: 36 days annual leave, inclusive of Bank Holidays Defined benefits pension schemes Family-friendly initiatives Career development opportunities Support for health & mental wellbeing Discounts on shopping Rental deposit scheme Public transport season ticket loans\n-Tax-efficient bicycle and charity-giving schemes Location The Finance Division has embraced hybrid working and individuals are currently working with their team two days a week from a University site, principally Greenwich House, and three days worked remotely (for those working a standard Monday to Friday week). This arrangement is subject to change and will not form a part of the contractual terms of the role. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 December 2027 in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check, a health assessment and a security check. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you would like more information about this role, informal enquiries can be directed by email to Simon Miller, Supervisor, Finance Helpdesk & Cash Management\nvia Simon.Miller@admin.cam.ac.uk If you have any questions the application process, please contact a Recruitment Administrator at Finhr_recruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is 25 October 2026. The interviews date for the role are planned 03 November 2026. Please quote reference AG51309 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Accounts Assistant, Helpdesk Key information Department/location Finance Division Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference AG51309 Category Assistant staff Date published 9 October 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/assistant-librarian-psychology-temporary-cover-part-time-wt51368": {
      "title": "Assistant Librarian",
      "description": "Assistant Librarian - Psychology [Temporary Cover] (Part Time) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Assistant Librarian - Psychology [Temporary Cover] (Part Time) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Assistant Librarian - Psychology [Temporary Cover] (Part Time) Ever wanted to work in an institution that is home to one of the world's great collections of cultural treasures and research materials? Spanning thousands of years of human thought and discovery, Cambridge University Libraries and Archives house unique treasures and research materials made up of nearly ten million books, maps, manuscripts, photographs and priceless objects. We are seeking a collaborative and proactive Assistant Librarian to join the Biological Sciences Libraries Team. This is an exciting opportunity to take a leading role in managing and developing the Department's web presence while supporting its teaching, learning, and research activities. The role We are seeking an experienced and proactive library professional to lead departmental engagement for the Psychological and Behavioural Sciences Tripos (PBST). This varied role encompasses library management, webmastering, collection development, and academic liaison activities, supporting the Department of Psychology's teaching, learning, and research priorities. The role is fixed term until March of 2028 and part time  - FTE 0.5 which equates to 18.25 hours per week. Candidates internal to the University are welcome to apply on the basis of a secondment. As Assistant Librarian (PBST) you will Design and deliver training sessions in partnership with staff and colleagues across SBS and SBS Libraries Team. Deliver library services across Biological Science Libraries. Lead on Academic liaison with the designated Department. Lead on the management, development and maintenance of both the Department of Psychology and Biological Sciences Libraries websites. Contribute to shared team goals through collaboration a supportive team culture. What we are looking for Proven ability to develop strong relationships and partnerships with stakeholders. Experience delivering teaching, training, or learning support both online and in-person. Knowledge of academic information resources and databases, with experience in developing user-focused STEM library collections and services. An innovative problem-solver with a proven ability to work collaboratively and a strong commitment to delivering high-quality customer service. Proven ability to manage and enhance departmental web presence, including websites, digital resources, and online information platforms. What we offer Flexible working arrangements are available upon discussion. Hybrid working and flexible hours are limited due to the operational requirements of the role. If needed, please contact us to discuss. Generous annual leave allowance of 36 days - depending on days worked across the week. Exceptional parental leave (including maternity, paternity / co-parent, adoption and shared parental leave). Discounts on major retailers. Professional development opportunities such as apprenticeships and access to a variety of training courses. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries please contact: - \nFunmike Ifie, Biological Sciences Librarian\nTel: 0776 1044287 \nEmail: yoi20@cam.ac.uk . Application closing date: Sunday 25 October 2026.\nInterview date: Monday 16 November 2026 For further details please refer to the Further Particulars. Please quote reference WT51368 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Psychology Library Salary £31,236-£35,608 pro rata Reference WT51368 Category Assistant staff Date published 13 October 2026 Closing date 25 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/bhf-4-year-phd-studentships-in-cardiovascular-research-prevention-rh51427": {
      "title": "BHF 4-Year PhD Studentships in Cardiovascular Research (Prevention)",
      "description": "BHF 4-Year PhD Studentships in Cardiovascular Research (Prevention) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs BHF 4-Year PhD Studentships in Cardiovascular Research (Prevention) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs BHF 4-Year PhD Studentships in Cardiovascular Research (Prevention) University of Cambridge, University of Edinburgh, Imperial College London and University of Oxford. Applications are invited for 10 funded 4-year PhD studentships starting in October 2027, as part of the British Heart Foundation (BHF) doctoral training programme focused on preventing cardiovascular disease through innovative, data-driven approaches. This interdisciplinary PhD programme brings together leading researchers from the University of Cambridge, University of Edinburgh, Imperial College London, and the University of Oxford. Students will be trained to work at the intersection of population health, systems biology, and data science/AI, combining expertise across these fields to tackle one of the most pressing challenges in global health. By harnessing large-scale multimodal data and emerging technologies, students will develop new approaches to understand, predict, and prevent heart and circulatory diseases. Students will be based in one of the following institutes:  Cambridge (5 studentships), Edinburgh (2 studentships), Imperial (1 Studentship) or Oxford (2 studentships) while benefiting from training, co-supervision, and collaboration across the partner institutions. Projects will span a range of topics including health data science, AI and machine learning, multi-omics, epidemiology, and translational cardiovascular research. Students will be part of a cohort-based programme, gaining access to shared training activities, collaborative networks, and state-of-the-art data resources across the multi-institute partnership. This four-year PhD programme combines interdisciplinary training with in-depth research. In year 1, students will undertake training courses and research rotations (at their host institution) before selecting their PhD project and supervisory team. In years 2-4, students will carry out their PhD research with interdisciplinary supervision. Additional opportunities will include advanced training courses, programme-wide workshops and symposia, and optional placements with our academic or industry partners, including EMBL-European Bioinformatics Institute, Wellcome Sanger Institute, AstraZeneca, Novo Nordisk, Genomics Ltd, Owlstone Medical, ThermoFischer Scientific and Flagship Pioneering. The co-leads of the programme are: \nCambridge - Professor Angela Wood Edinburgh - Professor Nicholas Mills Imperial - Associate Professor Michela Noseda Oxford - Professor Sarah Lewington Eligibility Applicants should hold, or expect to obtain, a first or upper second-class undergraduate degree (or equivalent) in a relevant discipline such as statistics, mathematics, computer science, data science, engineering, bioinformatics, epidemiology, biomedical sciences, genetics, public health, or another quantitative or biomedical field, and may also hold a Master's degree in a related subject. Applicants should also demonstrate strong analytical skills, experience working with data or computational methods, and an interest in interdisciplinary research.\nStrong communication and teamwork skills are essential, and international applicants may need to provide evidence of English language proficiency. Funding The studentship provides: University tuition fees (UK home rate only) A tax-free stipend at the BHF rate Research training and support We invite applications from students who meet the UK residency requirements (home fees). International students who confirm that additional costs of all overseas tuition fees will be covered through other scholarships or funding schemes will also be considered. How to apply Please do not use the University application portals. To apply, please email bhfphdadmin@medschl.cam.ac.uk with the following documents. Curriculum vitae (CV) Two letters of recommendation from your referees, signed Transcripts A cover letter (maximum 2500 characters) outlining your suitability for the PhD programme and indicating your preferred host institutions in order of preference. \nPlease list only institutions that you would be willing to attend if offered a place, even if they are not your first choice. Evidence of competence in English - If required - please see our Competence in English guidance for more information. AI must not be used when writing personal statements and CVs. This prohibition covers the use of AI to help your use of English. Using AI in your application ' Postgraduate Study https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/apply/how/ai-postgraduate-applications Application deadline: 8th December 2026 Please quote reference RH51427 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Public Health and Primary Care Salary Reference RH51427 Category Studentships Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 8 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/business-and-operations-manager-ra51381": {
      "title": "Business and Operations Manager",
      "description": "Business and Operations Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Business and Operations Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Business and Operations Manager The Cambridge Stem Cell Institute (CSCI) and the Department of Haematology are world-leading academic research institutions, home to outstanding scientists and clinicians working at the forefront of stem cell biology, regenerative medicine and blood disease. We are seeking an experienced and highly motivated Business and Operations Manager to provide senior strategic and operational leadership across the Cambridge Stem Cell Institute (CSCI) and the Department of Haematology, and to lead the professional services and facilities teams based in the Jeffrey Cheah Biomedical Centre (JCBC). Reporting to the Director of CSCI and the Head of the Department of Haematology, you will support them in achieving their strategic aims for governance, policy and business operations in support of teaching and research. You will manage a research grant and external income portfolio of over £120m, with an annual turnover of over £20m, and lead a professional services team of 63 staff, with 12 direct reports. About the role As Business and Operations Manager, you will advise the Director and Head of Department on overall strategy and support the planning and implementation of new developments. You will oversee the delivery of business, administration and services across both organisations, striving for continuous improvement. With a reporting line to the Head of General Division in the School Office, you will also have the opportunity to contribute to School-wide developments through project and functional area management, where workload permits. Key responsibilities include: Advising and assisting the Director and Head of Department on strategic development and the sustainable management of the Institute and Department. Leading the joint Institute and Department professional services administration team (25 staff), supporting research programmes, over 90 postgraduate students including a recently started Masters course. Leading the Core Research Facilities teams (24 staff), covering Scientific Operations Management, Advanced Microscopy, Histology, Genomics, Cell Culture, and Bioinformatics. Leading the JCBC Building Services Team, ensuring the day-to-day running, maintenance and safety of an 18,000m² state-of-the-art research facility that also houses CITIID and the Milner Therapeutics Institute. Leading and coordinating support (HR, Contracts, H & S, etc) for Department of Haematology staff outside of JCBC. Ensuring effective governance, financial planning, and the management and monitoring of research grants and external income. Working with academic colleagues and the School to deliver learning, teaching and examinations, including the undergraduate medical curriculum and postgraduate provision. Leading change and business improvement projects, overseeing communications and external affairs, supporting fundraising and the implementation of Enhanced Financial Transparency (EFT). Ensuring a safe and secure working environment and overseeing regulatory inspections and audits (for example HSE and HTA). About you You will be a senior administrator with demonstrable experience of leading and managing large teams of support staff for quality results, and of delivering strategic planning and change at a tactical and operational level, together with a track record of supporting and developing staff. You will bring strong planning and relationship-building skills, and the ability to communicate persuasively and effectively with stakeholders at all levels of the organisation. Significant experience and knowledge of the UK Higher Education sector, particularly the delivery of Life Sciences, is desirable. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RA51381 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars (RA51381) Key information Department/location Office of the School of Clinical Medicine Salary £63,606-£67,468 Reference RA51381 Category Academic-related Date published 13 October 2026 Closing date 4 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/communications-lead-change-and-impact-yv51402": {
      "title": "Communications Lead: Change and Impact",
      "description": "Communications Lead: Change and Impact | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Communications Lead: Change and Impact Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Communications Lead: Change and Impact The Role The University of Cambridge is seeking an experienced communications professional to join the Change and Impact team in the University Change Support Office (UCSO). This role will lead and coordinate communications for a portfolio of complex change programmes and projects, helping staff and stakeholders understand and engage with institutional change. UCSO provides expertise in change and communications, benefits realisation, portfolio planning, common standards, strategic reporting and business-case appraisal. It supports the Change and Programme Management Board in directing and overseeing the University's portfolio of major change programmes, for which communications is a key enabling function. Working within the strategic communications direction and the established operational priorities, the post-holder will develop, implement and evaluate communications approaches for UCSO-supported change activity. The role will provide specialist advice on stakeholder engagement, messaging, timing, channels, listening mechanisms and communications risks, including where issues are sensitive or operationally significant. The post-holder will also serve as the UCSO communications practice lead for change activity, developing standards, tools and templates; coordinating communications across programmes; coaching contributors; and using audience insight and feedback to improve communications impact. The role will work closely with programme teams, Change Partners, communications colleagues and the University's Internal Communications Team within the Office of External Affairs and Communications. Key responsibilities Develop, implement and evaluate internal communications strategies and stakeholder engagement plans for UCSO-supported change activity, translating programme objectives into clear messages, channels, listening arrangements and impact measures. Provide specialist communications advice to programme boards, senior responsible officers, committees, managers and programme teams on sensitive messaging, timing, stakeholder confidence, reputational considerations and adoption risks. Define and embed communications standards, tools, templates and quality criteria, and support consistent practice across programmes through coaching, feedback and practical guidance. Coordinate communications across UCSO-supported programmes, Reimagining Professional Service activity and other major change initiatives, aligning messages and schedules and resolving duplication or conflicting communications needs. Produce, commission, edit and assure high-quality communications products, and use audience insight, channel information and stakeholder feedback to identify risks and improve communications effectiveness. About you You will: Have significant experience of leading or coordinating communications for complex organisational change, transformation, policy implementation or institutional programmes involving multiple stakeholder groups. Bring advanced knowledge of internal and change communications, with experience of advising senior stakeholders on sensitive communications, engagement, messaging, timing and reputational considerations. Have excellent written, verbal and editorial judgement and experience of producing or assuring high-impact content for specialist and non-specialist audiences. Be able to develop practical communications strategies, standards and evaluation approaches, interpret audience insight and feedback, and influence effectively through matrix working arrangements. Be educated to degree level, or have equivalent practical experience, in communications, engagement, public relations, journalism, marketing, organisational change or a related field. A recognised professional communications qualification is desirable. The Benefits In return, you will receive an extensive reward and benefits package including: 41 days annual leave, including bank holidays. Potential for flexible and hybrid working, subject to the operational needs of the role. A generous pension scheme, discount portal, cycle to work scheme, access to the University accommodation service, relocation expenses scheme, employee assistance programme and family-friendly policies. The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. When applying, please upload your CV and cover letter which clearly sets out how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification (the knowledge, skills, and experience required for the role) along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted and invited to interview based upon these criteria. Interviews will be held in late November 2026. Additional information regarding the role can be found within the further information file. If you wish to discuss this role in more detail, or have questions about the application process, enquiries are welcome. Please contact the team via change@admin.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference YV51402 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Communications Lead: Change and Impact Key information Department/location University Change Support Office Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference YV51402 Category Academic-related Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 11 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
