      "title": "Control of Works Supervisor",
      "description": "Control of Works Supervisor | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Control of Works Supervisor Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Control of Works Supervisor * The Role: * The University of Cambridge is seeking a highly motivated Control of Works Supervisor to join the Estates Division Asset Management team. Working across one of the UK's largest and most complex operational estates, comprising over 370 buildings, 723,000m² of academic, teaching and research space, and assets valued at more than £3.4 billion, you will play a critical role in ensuring contractors and Estates teams operate safely and compliantly across the University estate. As a key member of the Control of Works team, you will oversee the implementation of safe systems of work, manage permit-to-work processes, undertake contractor audits, review risk assessments and method statements, and support compliance with health and safety legislation and best practice. You will work closely with Estates colleagues, University departments, contractors and Health and Safety professionals to promote a strong safety culture, deliver contractor inductions, provide expert guidance on control of works processes, and drive continuous improvement across permit and compliance systems. This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced health and safety or building services professional looking to make a significant impact within a world-leading institution. About you: To excel in this role, you will bring: Practical experience of health and safety management within a maintenance, facilities management, engineering or construction environment. Experience reviewing and assessing risk assessments and method statements (RAMS), permits to work, COSHH documentation and safe systems of work. Strong knowledge of contractor management, including pre-start audits and ensuring safe working practices on operational sites. Excellent communication skills with the ability to engage confidently with contractors, technical specialists, operational teams and senior stakeholders. The ability to deliver safety briefings, toolbox talks and training sessions to both small and large groups. Strong organisational skills, attention to detail and the ability to prioritise effectively in a fast-paced environment. Proficient IT skills and experience working with digital systems and documentation. IOSH Managing Safely (or equivalent) and a sound understanding of current health and safety legislation. A recognised building services qualification or equivalent technical knowledge. \nA NEBOSH qualification and appointment as an Authorised Person (AP) or Authorising Engineer (AE) would be advantageous. Please be aware that a Full UK Driving Licence is essential for this role (a full EU Driving Licence is acceptable on appointment, but the successful applicant must transfer to a UK Licence prior to confirming a start date). Benefits of working at the University of Cambridge include: Competitive pay with automatic annual cost of living increases and service-related progression. Generous annual leave entitlement, helping you maintain a healthy work-life balance. Flexible and hybrid working arrangements to support your lifestyle and wellbeing. Family-friendly policies, including enhanced maternity, adoption, and shared parental leave, plus access to workplace nurseries. Excellent pension scheme with a generous employer contribution through auto-enrolment. Travel and shopping discounts at over 2,000 local and national retailers. Relocation support for eligible new starters. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please email Estates Division on EDR@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference BA51385 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Control of Works Supervisor Key information Department/location Estates Division (Facilities) Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference BA51385 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/digital-performance-coordinator-ea50199": {
      "title": "Digital Performance Coordinator",
      "description": "Digital Performance Coordinator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Digital Performance Coordinator Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Digital Performance Coordinator Reaching prospective learners at the right time, with the right message, is central to achieving our recruitment goals. We're looking for someone  who can use data, insight and experimentation to continually improve campaign performance and maximise the impact of our marketing investment. This is a chance to shape and optimise digital marketing at a world-leading educational institution with roots dating back to 1873. At University of Cambridge Professional and Continuing Education (PACE), our mission is to make lifelong learning accessible to all. We deliver a diverse portfolio of courses and focus on widening participation for adult learners at every stage of life. The Role As Digital Performance Coordinator, you'll deliver and optimise paid media and organic social activity across PACE. Working within the Growth Marketing team and wider External Engagement Division, you'll collaborate with colleagues across content, web and communications to support integrated marketing campaigns and ensure activity aligns with wider recruitment objectives. This pivotal role offers ownership of day-to-day performance across paid media and organic social channels. Using data, insight and creativity, you'll plan, execute and optimise campaigns across the prospective learner journey, driving engagement and ensuring activity is targeted, on-brand and aligned with recruitment priorities. You'll monitor campaign performance, identify opportunities to improve results and make evidence-based recommendations that maximise the effectiveness of marketing investment. What you'll do: Plan, deliver and optimise paid social campaigns across platforms aligned to audience targeting and campaign priorities Support the delivery and optimisation of paid search and display  campaigns Manage end-to-end campaign execution, including audience targeting, ad creation, budget pacing, and optimisation Monitor performance against key metrics such as CPA, CTR, conversions and ROI, implementing improvements where required Manage organic social activity, including content scheduling, engagement, and performance tracking Collaborate with marketing colleagues  across content and web to delevop compelling, performance-led creative assets Analyse campaign performance and provide insights and recommendations to improve outcomes Run A/B tests and explore new tools, formats and platform features including AI driven campaign solutions to enhance performance Ensure all marketing activity follows brand, accessibility, and compliance standards Work with external partners or agencies to support campaign delivery and achieve campaign objectives Your skills: Experience delivering and optimising digital marketing campaigns, particularly across paid social and PPC Familiarity with platforms such as Meta Ads Manager, LinkedIn Campaign Manager, and Google Ads Strong understanding of audience segmentation and full-funnel digital marketing principles Analytical mindset, with the ability to interpret data and turn it into actionable insights Experience using reporting tools (e.g. GA4) Attention to detail in campaign setup and QA Ability to manage multiple campaigns simultaneously Confident communication skills, including presenting insights clearly to colleagues Proactive approach, with a focus on testing, learning, and continuous improvement Desirable: Experience using AI-driven campaign tools (e.g. Meta Advantage+, Google PMax) Experience working in higher education or a similar The starting salary would typically be the entry-point of the band advertised, increasing annually. Elements of this role are suitable for hybrid working, where staff can split their time between in-person collaboration and remote working. The frequency of the remote elements of the role can be discussed with the recruiting manager. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Interviews will take place on 18 November (online), followed by a second round of interviews for shortlisted applicants on 25 November (in person). Informal enquiries can be made to Sophie.Sanghera@pace.cam.ac.uk Enquires about the application process should be directed to HR@pace.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference EA50199 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Digital Performance Coordinator Key information Department/location University of Cambridge Institute of Professional and Continuing Education Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference EA50199 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 9 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/esprc-idla-studentship-research-into-low-order-modelling-of-rotating-detonation-engines-nm51193": {
      "title": "ESPRC IDLA Studentship",
      "description": "ESPRC IDLA Studentship - Research into Low order modelling of Rotating Detonation Engines | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs ESPRC IDLA Studentship  - Research into Low order modelling of Rotating Detonation Engines Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs ESPRC IDLA Studentship  - Research into Low order modelling of Rotating Detonation Engines Applications are invited for a fully funded 4-year PhD studentship at the University of Cambridge to conduct research into low order models of a rotating detonation engine, in collaboration with Rolls-Royce under the Industrial Doctoral Landscape Award (IDLA) scheme. Rotating Detonation Engines offer a potential improvement towards lower weight/higher power propulsion systems compared to to conventional gas turbine engines. This project involves the development of a skeleton simplified model for a rotating detonation engine (RDE), using existing models, which will be coded it in a way that makes it simple to access potential performance using realistic thermodynamics. Validation will be conducted using higher fidelity models (RANS and/or LES) and potential experiments. The model will eventually extend to liquid fuels and integrate with a gas turbine simulation platform to simulate the overall system. The project is funded by Rolls-Royce and EPSRC, and belongs to a larger overall initiative on advanced propulsion concepts. The student is expected to undertake the following activities: Training in thermodynamic modelling of compressible and supersonic systems Translate existing thermodynamic codes for chemical modelling and detonation toolbox into open source code Implement existing eigenvalue simulation for determination of RDE frequency and cycle properties as a function of operating condition Validate the implemented code against existing higher fidelity simulations and experimental results Extension of the code to include different fuels, geometries and interfaces with compressor and turbine Produce periodic progress reports and present and engage with company specialists Attend International Conferences and submit papers to Journals Attend Annual Doctoral Conference/Symposia organised by Rolls-Royce and other related organisations Qualifications: \nApplicants should have (or expect to be awarded) a good UK Master's degree (or overseas equivalent) in a relevant science subject (Engineering, Physics, Computer Science, Mathematics), preferably with a focus on fluid mechanics and numerical methods, with some programming experience (Python/C++). The applicant should be self-motivated, but able to work as part of a team, and effectively communicate their research findings. EPSRC IDLA studentships are four-year awards that cover full fees and maintenance, and are available for eligible home students and a limited number of international students. To apply for this studentship, please send your two page CV to Prof Simone Hochgreb ( sh372@cam.ac.uk ) to arrive no later than 28 February 2027. Applications may close early if the position is filled before this date. Please note that any offer of funding will be conditional on securing a place as a PhD student. Candidates will need to apply separately for admission through the University's Graduate Admissions application portal; this can be done before or after applying for this funding opportunity. The applicant portal can be accessed via: www.graduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/egegpdpeg . The final deadline for PhD applications is 14 May 2027, although it is advisable to apply earlier than this. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary Reference NM51193 Category Studentships Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 28 February 2027 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
