    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-in-physical-biology-mitotic-mechanisms-pt51399": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Physical Biology",
      "description": "Research Associate in Physical Biology - Mitotic Mechanisms | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate in Physical Biology - Mitotic Mechanisms Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate in Physical Biology - Mitotic Mechanisms Applications are invited for a Post-doctoral Research Associate position in the group of Dr. Francois Nedelec at the Sainsbury Laboratory Cambridge University (SLCU). This call is open to carry out fundamental research in the field of physical biology, specifically studying mitotic spindle assembly and chromosome segregation. This post is funded by a prestigious ERC Synergy project BioMecaNet in collaboration with the experimental groups of Andrea Musacchio (MPI of Molecular Physiology, Dortmund, DE) and Thomas Surrey (CRG, Barcelona, SP). The project's primary focus is the comprehensive analysis of the fundamental mechanisms underlying chromosome segregation, employing advanced mathematical models and computer simulations. The research will concentrate on a thorough investigation into the mechanics of the cytoskeleton, particularly in relation to the formation of a functional bipolar mitotic spindle. A central goal of the project is to integrate filament mechanics and cellular chemistry into the modeling process, with a specific aim to develop physically accurate simulations of chromosome bi-orientation and segregation. Applicants must have or be close to obtaining a PhD in computational biology, physics, applied mathematics, computer science or related field and have a proven track record of scientific publication. Prior experience in modelling of biological systems, or expertise in systems biology and/or theoretical biophysics, in general, would be advantageous. The successful applicant will have the opportunity to develop skills in mathematical modelling, advanced numerical simulations and machine learning. Fully involved in the basic research in these areas whilst also writing up their research work for presentation and publication, the post holder will have the opportunity to assist in the supervision of student projects, and to engage in teaching if desired. Good and effective communication skills are essential (e.g. presentations, outreach activities). The Laboratory provides a welcoming and collaborative environment with a wide range of family-friendly benefits and development opportunities. More about the Sainsbury Laboratory and details of what the University offers to employees, can be found at: http://www.slcu.cam.ac.uk/ . Please ensure that you upload a copy of your full CV with publications list, cover letter highlighting your suitability for the position and contact information of three referees. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 30 June 2028. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Scientific enquiries apart from the formal application should be directed to Dr Francois Nedelec at Francois.Nedelec@slcu.cam.ac.uk For questions regarding the application process, please email HR@slcu.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PT51399 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information PT51399 - Research Associate Further Information Key information Department/location Sainsbury Laboratory Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PT51399 Category Research Date published 14 October 2026 Closing date 6 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-in-the-photophysics-of-battery-materials-molecular-systems-fixed-term-ka51428": {
      "title": "Research Associate in the Photophysics of Battery Materials & Molecular Systems (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate in the Photophysics of Battery Materials & Molecular Systems (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate in the Photophysics of Battery Materials & Molecular Systems (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate in the Photophysics of Battery Materials & Molecular Systems (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 36 months in the first instance. Applications are invited for a Research Associate to join the group of Professor Akshay Rao in the Optoelectronics Group, Department of Physics (Cavendish Laboratory), University of Cambridge. The role holder will study the photophysics of and battery materials & molecular systems, using optical spectroscopy and optical microscopy to investigate charge and ion transport, and the structural and chemical changes that occur in these materials during operation. The role holder will design and carry out experiments that combine optical spectroscopy and optical microscopy, analyse and interpret the resulting data, and publish and present the findings. They will be expected to take part in collaborations with other researchers and PhD students in the group, as well as with national and international partners. Appointment as a Research Associate depends on having been awarded, or close to being awarded, a PhD in Physics, Chemistry, Materials Science or a related discipline. Candidates must have experimental experience in both optical spectroscopy and optical microscopy. Experience with molecular materials, battery or electrochemical materials, optical instrument development and the analysis of large datasets is essential. Candidates must provide the names and contact details of two referees who know their work or studies in the relevant field. The position is available from 4 January 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter, for 36 months. Please see the attached further information file for role-specific details. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Enquiries should be sent to Professor Akshay Rao ( ar525@cam.ac.uk ). Applications should be made online and include a full CV, a list of publications and the names and contact details of two referees. Please quote reference KA51428 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physics Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference KA51428 Category Research Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-grants-administrator-part-time-pj51345": {
      "title": "Research Grants Administrator (Part Time)",
      "description": "Research Grants Administrator (Part Time) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Grants Administrator (Part Time) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Grants Administrator (Part Time) We are looking for an enthusiastic and motivated individual to join the Department of Psychology as a part-time Research Grants Administrator. The Department holds around 70 active research grants and has a busy and varied research funding portfolio.\nThe post holder will contribute to the provision of a comprehensive and efficient research management service across the full lifecycle of research grants, supporting pre-award and post-award activities and helping to ensure the effective administration of all research funding activity within the Department. The successful candidate will be the first point of contact for research grant related queries from researchers and professional service colleagues, including finance and HR. They will be expected to develop close working relationships with academic staff and with the University's Research Operations Office to support the delivery of research projects and ensure compliance with university, funder and regulatory requirements. The successful candidate will ideally have experience in an administrative role, preferably within a research, higher education, finance, accounting or grant management environment. However, we also welcome applications from candidates with administrative, financial or organisational experience who are looking to develop their career in research administration. This is a varied and rewarding position requiring excellent organisational skills, attention to detail and the ability to manage competing priorities and work effectively to deadlines. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills are essential, together with a collaborative approach and a willingness to solve problems and support colleagues in a busy and dynamic environment. Candidates should be educated to 'A' level standard, to include GCSE English language and mathematics/NVQ level 3 or have an equivalent level of practical experience. Good IT skills, including Word, Excel and emails are a requirement. Use of bespoke financial, grant or reporting systems is desirable. Previous experience in a fast-paced environment is essential. In return, we offer an encouraging, supportive and inclusive environment with an opportunity for the role holder to cultivate and progress their career. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: Petra Georgoulis Hluzova ( ph344@cam.ac.uk ) Please quote reference PJ51345 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Psychology Salary £27,319-£31,236 pro rata Reference PJ51345 Category Assistant staff Date published 14 October 2026 Closing date 4 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-laboratory-technician-fixed-term-pk51245": {
      "title": "Research Laboratory Technician (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Laboratory Technician (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Laboratory Technician (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Laboratory Technician (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. We wish to recruit a highly motivated Research Laboratory Technician to join the Department of Pathology, University of Cambridge. The successful applicant will provide support for experimental research and media/ equipment preparation within the Department in order to support the effective delivery of teaching and research. Applicants should be educated to GCSE level grade A-C /NVQ level 2 or equivalent in a science subject/ equivalent level of practical experience. Duties will include supporting laboratory administration, assisting lab managers with monitoring consumable stock items, assisting with the Business administration duties, assisting with maintenance of laboratory tidiness and organisation, assisting research workers and project students in their work, particularly to ensure their safety, and providing support with preparing practical classes. For more information please see the Further particulars.\nOnce an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please provide contact details, including an email address of two referees. At least one of your referees should be someone who is familiar with your work. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: Dr Samuel Odeyemi on swo28@cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the HR team on hr@path.cam.ac.uk Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference PK49372 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Pathology Salary £25,249-£27,319 Reference PK51245 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 13 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-personnel-administrator-part-timeinternal-applicants-only-ma51323": {
      "title": "Research Personnel Administrator (Part time)(Internal applicants only)",
      "description": "Research Personnel Administrator (Part time)(Internal applicants only) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Personnel Administrator (Part time)(Internal applicants only) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Personnel Administrator (Part time)(Internal applicants only) The part time Research Personnel Administrator role is a key role in the Department of Chemistry as it provides an efficient and effective administrative support in a very busy office dealing with staff, post graduate students and visitors.  The role involves research personnel administration as well as assisting with general HR administration. This includes processing visitor agreements, generating letters, organising small scale events and meetings, taking meeting minutes, managing stationary supplies and liaising with other departments and organisations. Educated to at least GCSE-level or equivalent, the ideal candidate must be able to work on their own initiative, be well organised in prioritising their work, be flexible in their approach and have excellent communication and IT skills along with a strong sense of team playing and working flexibly across a varied workload.  Recent experience of working in a department in a similar role is essential.  As is being able to deal sympathetically and effectively with a wide range of people at all levels with tact and diplomacy, observing the need for confidentiality where appropriate. The role is for 30 hours per week, days and hours to be worked will be negotiable on appointment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal queries please contact Emma Graham ( ejg49@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference MA51323 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The School of Physical Sciences holds an Athena Swan Silver Award which recognises and supports progress in gender equality. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £25,249-£27,319 pro rata Reference MA51323 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 23 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-counsellor-aw51276": {
      "title": "Senior Counsellor",
      "description": "Senior Counsellor | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Counsellor Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Counsellor The University Counselling and Wellbeing Service (Student Support) is a dynamic, high-demand service driven by our mission to support and empower students in fulfilling their academic and personal potential. We are at an exciting stage of the service's development. Following a transformative Student Mental Health and Wellbeing Plan (2022-2025) at the Collegiate University, we are recruiting to a number of roles to support our service's work and objectives under a new Student Support strategic plan (2026-2029). As part of an integrated Student Support Department, we work in partnership with the Mental Health Advice Service, the Harassment and Violence Support Service, and the Accessibility and Disability Resource to offer students joined-up support. These services contribute to a wider stepped care approach at Collegiate Cambridge, working in partnership with College Health and Wellbeing teams who support students in their College community. We are seeking to appoint a highly-motivated, experienced professional in the field of counselling and psychotherapy to the new role of Senior Counsellor (Access and Wellbeing). The Senior Counsellor (Access and Wellbeing) plays a crucial operational role within a high-demand student support service, supporting the diverse needs of Cambridge's student population. The role holder will join the Senior Management Team of the University Counselling and Wellbeing Service, reporting to the Head of Counselling and Wellbeing, and working alongside two Deputy Heads of Counselling and Wellbeing. The Senior Counsellor's key contribution will be as a first point of contact for daily service operations, ensuring the day-to-day processes of delivering a high volume of appointments runs efficiently and effectively. The successful candidate will also provide line management and professional development support to staff in the Wellbeing Service, alongside maintaining a caseload of students, support our data-driven approach to service access and improvement, and actively participate in the service's work responding to risk where it arises, including concerns related to self-harm, suicidality and/or safeguarding duties. Please note that this post requires accreditation (or registration, where this confers an accreditation-level standard) with your appropriate professional regulatory body in the UK, such as BACP, UKCP, BABCP or HCPC. We particularly encourage candidates from a Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic background to apply for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level within our University. Benefits of working at the University of Cambridge include: Competitive rates of pay with automatic service-related pay progression and annual cost of living increases; Generous annual leave allowance; Flexible and hybrid working opportunities; Generous maternity, adoption and shared parental leave entitlement and other family friendly schemes (e.g. workplace nurseries); An auto-enrolment pension scheme, with a generous employer contribution; Travel benefits and retail discounts at over 2,000 local and national stores; Schemes to support with relocation / the provision of accommodation The interview date for the post is Monday 9th November 2026 Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo an enhanced Disclosure and Barring Service check. When applying, please upload your CV and cover letter outlining how you meet the criteria set out in the person specification in the Further Information document. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact SSCjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AW51276 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Student Support Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference AW51276 Category Academic-related Date published 13 October 2026 Closing date 22 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-research-assistant-internal-only-fixed-term-rg51395": {
      "title": "Senior Research Assistant (Internal Only) (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Senior Research Assistant (Internal Only) (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Research Assistant (Internal Only) (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Research Assistant (Internal Only) (Fixed Term) We are seeking a senior research assistant to work within the Group of Dr. Clémence Blouet at the Institute of Metabolic Science, within a programme aimed at understanding the mechanisms through which central nutrient sensing contributes to metabolic homeostasis. This position offers an ideal opportunity for motivated scientists wishing to develop their research skills in neurobiology within a world-class research environment. \nWe are looking for an experienced Senior Research Assistant to lead the histology and imaging that underpins the laboratory's programme, and to help run the laboratory itself.\nYou will develop, optimise and validate multiplexed immunofluorescence panels and related tissue-based assays in mouse brain; acquire confocal datasets; and build reproducible quantitative image analysis pipelines in HALO, Imaris or Fiji. You will work across several projects and with several people, from undergraduates to postdoctoral fellows, and you will have genuine ownership of the imaging methods the lab depends on - including the chance to bring in new approaches such as RNAscope, tissue clearing and light-sheet imaging, or electron microscopy. Alongside the science, you will take on core laboratory management: ordering and inventory, instrument maintenance, inductions, and health and safety, as well as training and supervising students and junior staff. This is a senior post with a long horizon, suited to someone who wants to be the technical and organisational backbone of a research group rather than to move on after two years. The Institute of Metabolic Science offers a highly collaborative environment with outstanding imaging and in vivo facilities on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus.\nRequirements. Applicants should hold a BSc in physiology, biochemistry, neuroscience or a related discipline, with substantial post-qualification research experience in histology, imaging and quantitative image analysis. Experience of multiplexed immunofluorescence, confocal imaging and mouse brain histology is essential, as is a demonstrated ability to develop, optimise and validate new protocols independently, and competence in quantitative image analysis software such as HALO, Imaris or Fiji/ImageJ. You should be comfortable with basic mouse husbandry and standard dosing procedures, and hold a Home Office personal licence or be eligible to obtain one. Experience of laboratory management and of training and supervising others is essential. An MSc or MRes, advanced imaging experience (RNAscope, tissue clearing and light-sheet, electron microscopy), basic molecular biology, and a track record of contributing to publications or grant applications are all advantageous. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 18 months in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RG51395 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Clinical Biochemistry Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference RG51395 Category Research Date published 14 October 2026 Closing date 28 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-research-associate-in-translational-biophysical-chemistry-and-imaging-fixed-term-ma51403": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate in Translational Biophysical Chemistry and Imaging (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Senior Research Associate in Translational Biophysical Chemistry and Imaging (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Research Associate in Translational Biophysical Chemistry and Imaging (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Research Associate in Translational Biophysical Chemistry and Imaging (Fixed Term) Fixed term: Funds are available for 12 months in the first instance. Applications are invited for a Senior Research Associate to work with Professor Steven F. Lee in the Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry, University of Cambridge. The post forms part of a translational research programme developing an imaging based drug discovery platform for neurodegenerative disease. Using quantitative single molecule imaging, the programme will characterise small protein assemblies and determine how candidate compounds alter their formation, structure and abundance. The initial focus is alpha synuclein aggregation in Parkinson's disease. The programme will develop, miniaturise and validate a higher throughput assay, undertake compound screening and generate quantitative datasets linking compound treatment to changes in protein assembly. Working with Cambridge researchers and external academic and translational partners, the successful applicant will help develop the technology into a robust, externally deployable drug discovery platform. The successful applicant will take substantial independent responsibility for: Developing and validating quantitative single molecule imaging assays for protein aggregation. Establishing reproducible workflows for higher throughput compound screening. Performing screening experiments and developing quantitative approaches to classify and prioritise compound responses. Building robust analysis pipelines for large imaging datasets and interpreting and presenting results. Coordinating research across academic collaborators and external partners. Working with commercial suppliers and technology partners to integrate compound libraries, instrumentation and assay formats. Contributing to publications, funding applications and scientific leadership. A central aim is to establish a commercially credible opportunity with a clear route to market. The successful applicant will refine the customer proposition, test the business model and undertake customer discovery with pharmaceutical companies, biotechnology companies and contract research organisations. They will also contribute to intellectual property strategy and commercial positioning, building an evidence base around assay performance, workflow, cost and differentiation. Applicants should hold a PhD in chemistry, biophysics, biochemistry, neuroscience or a related discipline, with substantial postdoctoral or equivalent research experience and expertise in quantitative biological or biophysical research. Experience in single molecule microscopy, advanced fluorescence microscopy, protein aggregation, neurodegeneration, high throughput screening or quantitative image analysis would be particularly valuable. Applicants must demonstrate scientific independence, experience of working across disciplinary boundaries and responsibility for complex research projects. Experience involving external organisations, technology translation, innovation, commercialisation, industry engagement or research strategy would be advantageous. The successful applicant will contribute to the programme's strategic direction and represent the project in discussions with academic, commercial and translational partners. Excellent communication, organisational and project management skills are essential. The post offers an opportunity to combine advanced physical chemistry, neurodegeneration research and drug discovery, contributing directly to the translation of single molecule technologies towards therapeutic discovery. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. For informal enquiries, please contact Professor Steven Lee at sl591@cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference MA51403 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The School of Physical Sciences holds an Athena Swan Silver Award which recognises and supports progress in gender equality. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference MA51403 Category Research Date published 15 October 2026 Closing date 12 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-research-software-engineer-nm50695": {
      "title": "Senior Research Software Engineer",
      "description": "Senior Research Software Engineer | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Research Software Engineer Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Research Software Engineer Are you ready to drive innovation at the intersection of advanced software engineering and sustainable aviation research? The Aviation Impact Accelerator (AIA) at the University of Cambridge is seeking a Senior Research Software Engineer to join our team at Whittle Laboratory in West Cambridge. As part of this team, you will lead efforts to develop the scientific software, infrastructure and interactive tools that underpin our research and communicate its findings to government, industry and the public. This is a broad senior engineering role which is technically demanding and intellectually engaging spanning new tool development, support for analyst-led projects, cloud infrastructure, data pipelines and public-facing web applications. We are particularly interested in candidates with an engineering or physical-science background, an understanding of low-order physical systems modelling, and experience deploying and operating web services, ideally using AWS. The AIA combines physics-based and data-driven modelling to study aviation as an interconnected system. Our software includes modern, typed Python modelling toolboxes and Bilt, an in-house framework for building a causal, composable and unit-aware engineering models, which are deployable to JAX. Much of our development is greenfield, giving team members significant influence over software architecture, engineering practices and technical direction. The role also offers opportunities to mentor students and colleagues, contribute to reports and publications, and engage with external partners and software contractors where appropriate. We value code quality, testability, automation, and maintainable research software. This role is suitable for engineers looking to deepen their expertise in scientific and data-intensive software while maintaining industry-aligned practices. If you are eager to contribute your skills to a meaningful mission, we would love to hear from you. Apply now to join the AIA team and play a key role in advancing sustainable aviation solutions. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 12 months in the first instance. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you include both a CV and covering letter within your application which should detail how you feel you meet the criteria in the person specification. The closing date for applications is Sunday 1 November 2026. Informal enquiries are welcomed and if you have any questions about this vacancy or the application process, please contact the HR Office at the Department of Engineering: hr-office@eng.cam.ac.uk , +44 (0)1223 332615. Please quote reference NM50695 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference NM50695 Category Academic-related Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-teaching-associate-in-archaeological-science-temporary-cover-jd51383": {
      "title": "Senior Teaching Associate in Archaeological Science [Temporary Cover]",
      "description": "Senior Teaching Associate in Archaeological Science [Temporary Cover] | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Teaching Associate in Archaeological Science [Temporary Cover] Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Teaching Associate in Archaeological Science [Temporary Cover] The Department of Archaeology is seeking to appoint a full- or part-time Senior Teaching Associate in Archaeological Science, on an 8-month contract from 1st December 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. The Senior Teaching Associate will be based in the Department of Archaeology on the Downing Site, Cambridge. The post involves: Coordination of and contributions to undergraduate and graduate courses in archaeological science, which involve lectures, practicals and seminars; Acting as the Director of the MPhil in Archaeological Science, responsible for the oversight of ca. 10-15 students per year; Contribution to other option papers as appropriate depending on the post-holder's interests; Some undergraduate and/or Master's dissertation supervision; Examination and assessment. Candidates will need to show evidence of the following qualifications, skills and experience: Good first degree and a doctorate or equivalent experience in an archaeological materials science subject. Evidence of ability to teach archaeological science effectively at undergraduate and Master's level, with an emphasis on materials science. Demonstrable experience with a range of scientific techniques employed in archaeomaterials. Good organisational abilities. Ability to play an effective role in the life and work of the Department. Ability to work as part of a team. Experience and knowledge of using digital teaching platforms. Temporary cover: This post is fixed-term until 31 July 2027 or the return of the post holder, whichever is the earlier. The start date for this role is 1st December 2026, or as soon as possible thereafter. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Professor Marcos Martinon-Torres ( M.Martinon-Torres@arch.cam.ac.uk ) or the Department Administrator ( administrator@cam.ac.uk ). The deadline for application is 28 October 2026. The interview date is to be confirmed. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please upload a Curriculum Vitae and covering letter, to include your work experience, qualifications and how your profile fits the position, plus the contact details of two referees.  After shortlisting, where you have given permission, your nominated referees will be asked to provide a reference via the on-line system. Please quote reference JD51383 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Department of Archaeology Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference JD51383 Category Academic Date published 14 October 2026 Closing date 28 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/staff-counsellor-temporary-cover-part-time-secondment-as51295": {
      "title": "Staff Counsellor [Temporary Cover] (Part Time Secondment)",
      "description": "Staff Counsellor [Temporary Cover] (Part Time Secondment) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Staff Counsellor [Temporary Cover] (Part Time Secondment) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Staff Counsellor [Temporary Cover] (Part Time Secondment) The University of Cambridge Staff Counselling Centre is inviting applications from accredited and experienced counsellors and psychotherapists to join our dynamic, embedded higher education counselling service. We are seeking a Staff Counsellor to join our team for 18.5 hours per week. This role operates within our hybrid working model, with attendance at the Centre required for a minimum of two days each week to meet service needs. This is a temporary two-year fixed-term position to provide cover for a secondment. This is an exciting opportunity for a qualified and experienced practitioner seeking a rewarding and fulfilling role within a supportive, inclusive and collaborative working environment. The successful candidate will have experience of working within a counselling service, in accordance with the BACP Ethical Framework, supporting clients with a broad range of workplace and personal issues. You will be able to work in a culturally sensitive manner as part of a team of 15 counselling professionals, using your own initiative while recognising when to seek guidance from senior colleagues. You will be proficient in delivering brief therapeutic interventions and capable of managing a caseload that includes clients presenting with a range of complex issues. You must hold, or be able to demonstrate equivalence with, the accreditation standards of one of the following professional bodies: BACP, UKCP, BABCP, BPC, or, for counselling psychologists, registration with the BPS and HCPC. In addition to the base salary stated above, the successful candidate for this post will receive an additional 2.5% supplement to their pay. The closing date for applications is 8th November 2026 Interviews will be held week commencing 16th November 2026 Fixed-term: 2 years. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a Disclosure and Barring Service check (enhanced with adults' barred list) and a health assessment. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment and a security check. Please quote reference AS51295 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Occupational Health and Safety Service Salary £35,608-£46,049 pro rata Reference AS51295 Category Academic-related Date published 13 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/talent-exchange-project-coordinator-fixed-term-ak51141": {
      "title": "Talent Exchange Project Coordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Talent Exchange Project Coordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Talent Exchange Project Coordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Talent Exchange Project Coordinator (Fixed Term) About the Role The Global Engagement Office is seeking a Talent Exchange Project Coordinator to support delivery of the Cambridge-AstraZeneca-Beijing Life Science Ecosystem Collaboration, with a focus on the talent exchange strand. You will help coordinate a range of activities that support academic exchange, knowledge sharing and international collaboration between Cambridge and partner organisations in Beijing. Why Join Us? This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a high-profile international collaboration and support meaningful engagement between academic, clinical and institutional partners. The role offers the chance to work across a diverse network of stakeholders and play an important part in developing international partnerships and knowledge exchange activities. About You You will be an organised and proactive project professional who enjoys building relationships and working collaboratively with a wide range of stakeholders. You will have experience of project coordination, stakeholder engagement and organising events, visits, exchanges or similar activities. You will be confident managing competing priorities, coordinating activity across multiple partners and maintaining accurate project records and documentation. You will have excellent communication and organisational skills, strong analytical abilities and experience using Microsoft 365. Experience of working with Chinese partners and an understanding of the cultural sensitivities involved in supporting effective collaboration are essential, as is fluency in written and spoken Mandarin. Further Information For full details of the role, responsibilities and selection criteria, please refer to the Further Particulars document. We encourage early applications, as the vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 24 months in the first instance. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to GlobalEngagement.HR@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AK51141 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Talent Exchange Project Coordinator Key information Department/location Global Engagement Office Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference AK51141 Category Assistant staff Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 6 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/veterinary-radiographer-pp51410": {
      "title": "Veterinary Radiographer",
      "description": "Veterinary Radiographer | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Veterinary Radiographer Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Veterinary Radiographer Are you an experienced RVN or Radiologist looking to develop your specialist imaging skills and take the next step in your career? The Queen's Veterinary Hospital is seeking a Veterinary Radiographer to join our dedicated Diagnostic Imaging team. As an RCVS-accredited teaching hospital, we provide exceptional patient care 24/7 and offer a unique opportunity to work alongside specialist radiologists, imaging residents, veterinary nurses and clinicians in a stimulating referral and teaching environment. This varied role will involve producing high-quality diagnostic images, supporting radiation safety across the hospital, teaching veterinary students and nurses, and assisting with sedation monitoring and patient care. Full training in advanced imaging modalities, including MRI and CT, will be provided, making this an excellent opportunity for someone looking to expand their expertise in diagnostic imaging. This position would particularly suit an experienced RVN who is keen to advance their clinical skills, develop specialist knowledge, or progress their career within a leading veterinary teaching hospital. Weekend and on-call participation is required as part of this role. What we offer: 36 days annual leave, inclusive of Bank Holidays, plus CPD allowance University employee benefits including family friendly policies and CAMbens Training and development in advanced imaging techniques including CT and MRI Opportunities to teach and support veterinary students A collaborative team environment with specialist support Career development within a world-renowned veterinary hospital Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Alice Bird, Associate Hospital Director, by email: arb59@cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the QVSH Clinical HR Team: qvsh.hr@vet.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference PP51410 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. Further particulars for the role and information about the Department are available in the Further Particulars document enclosed with this advert. If you are viewing this advert on an external site, please visit the University's job pages for access. Applications will be monitored regularly, and we may contact candidates prior to the closing date. Therefore, if you are interested, please submit your application as early as possible. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Veterinary Radiographer Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference PP51410 Category Assistant staff Date published 15 October 2026 Closing date 12 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/astrazeneca-funded-non-clinical-phd-studentship-in-improved-2a-peptides-for-co-translational-pk51527": {
      "title": "AstraZeneca funded Non-clinical PhD Studentship in Improved 2A peptides for co-translational separation of a polyprotein",
      "description": "AstraZeneca funded Non-clinical PhD Studentship in Improved 2A peptides for co-translational separation of a polyprotein | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs AstraZeneca funded Non-clinical PhD Studentship in Improved 2A peptides for co-translational separation of a polyprotein Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs AstraZeneca funded Non-clinical PhD Studentship in Improved 2A peptides for co-translational separation of a polyprotein Applications are invited for a 4-year PhD studentship starting on 1 October 2027 and based in the Department of Pathology, University of Cambridge, and the AstraZeneca Discovery Centre at Cambridge. The student will be working on a collaborative project jointly supervised by Prof. Andrew Firth at the University of Cambridge and Dr Giovanni Ciotta at AstraZeneca and will have the opportunity to work across the two sites. The project focuses on manipulating peptide-bond skipping sequences to aid protein engineering. 2A peptides mediate co-translational separation of a polyprotein via a mechanism known as \"ribosome skipping\" or \"StopGo\". These ~20 amino acid peptides adopt an unusual conformation within the translating ribosome that prevents peptide bond formation at a specific site, but instead promotes release of the nascent peptide followed by continuation of translation. These sequences have proved to be exceptionally useful in biotechnology because they allow the introduction of all components of a pathway or multi-subunit complex in a single step, with stable stoichiometric co-expression from a single promoter. However, 2A peptides have some caveats ¿ including ribosome drop-off, ribosome pausing/stalling, and some amount of separation failure ¿ which constrain their functional utility. We have identified new classes of 2A sequences, some of which have improved properties compared to traditional 2A peptides. This PhD project will contribute to our work investigating these new 2A sequences and optimising them for biotechnological applications. The UCam team has expertise in molecular biology including ribosome profiling, ribosome structure and function, cell biology, cryo-electron microscopy, and bioinformatics. The AZ team has expertise in cell therapy, CRISPR/Cas9 technologies, and viral vectors. The PhD project will focus on molecular biology techniques but there will also be opportunity for bioinformatic skill development and there may be scope to obtain structural biology experience. At the AstraZeneca site, the student will focus on implementing optimised sequences in specific biotechnological applications, e.g. in cell therapy. Candidate We are looking for a highly motivated and enthusiastic individual capable of thinking and working independently. Applicants should have or shortly expect to obtain a first or upper second-class degree from a UK university, or an equivalent standard from an overseas university, in a relevant subject such as Biochemistry, Microbiology, Cell Biology or Genetics. These positions are open to UK citizens or overseas students who meet the UK residency requirements (home fees) or are able to augment the funds to cover the extra costs associated with international student fees through scholarships or funding schemes. Students will not be allowed to supplement fees via self funding. Full details of the University's entrance requirements and scholarships are specified on the following link: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/ Additionally, the Department requires that by the time of interview all potential students must have fulfilled the language requirements for admission: http://www.graduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/blpapdpth/requirements Please check for further eligibility details using this website: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/finance/fees/what-my-fee-status Application Process All applications should be made online via the University's Applicant Portal for a PhD in Pathology (BLPA22). A completed application must be submitted by the closing date below. Please also explain your motivation why you wish to pursue a PhD in this area, outline your research interests and background, and describe the qualities and experience you will bring to the role. Please also download our equal opportunities monitoring form from this link and return the completed form by email to postgrad@path.cam.ac.uk An application is only complete when all supporting documents, including the equal opportunities monitoring form, and the two academic references, are submitted. It is the applicant's responsibility to ensure their referees submit their references before the closing date of the studentship. Funding Full funding covering Maintenance fees (expected to start at approx. £23,805 p.a.) and the University Composition Fee is provided for the studentship, with effect from 1 October 2027. Student support and training As a graduate student at Cambridge, you will have access to a wide range of training opportunities. During their PhD, there is no taught or examined coursework, but students are encouraged to attend the wide variety of lectures and training courses available to them across the Department and wider University. This includes a centrally run Statistics course and the University Core Skills Training Programme, which includes sessions on Time Management, Presentation and Performance and Scientific Writing. The University's training platform ( https://training.cam.ac.uk ) offers a wide range of professional development courses, including in management, communication, career planning, well-being, entrepreneurship, technology, programming, data analysis and AI. Additionally, the University has an evolving range of practical workshops (e.g. microscopy, flow cytometry, chromatin mapping). All students are expected to attend internal and external seminars held within the Department of Pathology. As this studentship is part of a collaborative programme between AstraZeneca and the University of Cambridge, students will also be encouraged to attend and present at the annual AstraZeneca students symposium. Applications from ineligible candidates will not be considered. Informal enquiries may be addressed to Prof. Andrew Firth ( aef24@cam.ac.uk ). Deadline Applications close Monday 30 November 2026. Interviews for shortlisted candidates are scheduled for Monday 14th and Tuesday 15th (and possibly Thursday 17th) of December 2026. Diversity and Inclusion The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. We place major emphasis on the importance of teamwork and an enjoyable work environment as a foundation for performing internationally leading research. This will allow the student to acquire cutting edge research methodologies in a supportive environment. Please quote reference PK51527 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Pathology Salary Reference PK51527 Category Studentships Date published 27 October 2026 Closing date 30 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/astrazeneca-funded-non-clinical-phd-studentship-fixed-term-physics-guided-representation-learning-lf51434": {
      "title": "AstraZeneca-Funded Non-Clinical PhD Studentship (Fixed Term): Physics-Guided Representation Learning for Drug Discovery",
      "description": "AstraZeneca-Funded Non-Clinical PhD Studentship (Fixed Term): Physics-Guided Representation Learning for Drug Discovery | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs AstraZeneca-Funded Non-Clinical PhD Studentship  (Fixed Term): Physics-Guided Representation Learning for Drug Discovery Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs AstraZeneca-Funded Non-Clinical PhD Studentship  (Fixed Term): Physics-Guided Representation Learning for Drug Discovery Applications are invited for a four-year, AstraZeneca-funded PhD studentship in the Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics, commencing in October 2027. The successful candidate will be jointly supervised by Prof Sergio Bacallado (University of Cambridge), Dr Dino Oglic (AstraZeneca), and Dr Jon Paul Janet (AstraZeneca). The studentship offers an opportunity to conduct cutting-edge research at the interface of statistical machine learning, molecular simulation and drug discovery. Project description The project, entitled Physics-Guided Representation Learning from Free Energy Perturbation Data for Affinity Prediction and Candidate Down-Selection , aims to develop novel machine-learning methods for predicting molecular binding affinities and supporting decision-making in drug discovery. Free energy perturbation (FEP) calculations provide valuable information about how strongly candidate drug molecules bind to their biological targets, but these calculations are computationally expensive. This project will investigate how machine-learning models can extract richer information from FEP data and experimental measurements, combining physical principles with modern representation-learning techniques. The research will involve developing and evaluating new statistical and machine-learning methods, with access to both public benchmark datasets and AstraZeneca's industrial drug-discovery expertise and data. Candidate requirements Applicants should have, or expect to obtain, a first-class or upper second-class honours degree (or equivalent), normally following four years of university study. We welcome applicants with backgrounds in Mathematics, Statistics, Machine Learning, Scientific Computing, or any closely related discipline. Experience with computational chemistry would be desirable but is not essential . We particularly welcome applications from candidates with strong mathematical or machine-learning backgrounds who are interested in applying their expertise to problems in drug discovery. Funding The studentship provides funding for four years , covering a maintenance stipend and University tuition fees at the UK/Home rate. The studentship is open to candidates eligible for Home-fee status. International candidates may also be considered, provided that additional funding can be secured through scholarships or other approved funding schemes to cover the difference between Home and international tuition fees. This difference cannot be covered through student self-funding. Training and supervision The student will benefit from joint academic and industrial supervision, participating in the research activities of the Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics and collaborating closely with AstraZeneca researchers to gain exposure to industrial applications of artificial intelligence in pharmaceutical development. How to apply Applications must be submitted through the University of Cambridge Postgraduate Applicant Portal , selecting the course PhD in Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics : https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/mapmpdpms/apply Applicants should indicate Dr Sergio Bacallado as their proposed supervisor and use the following project title: Physics-Guided Representation Learning from Free Energy Perturbation Data for Affinity Prediction and Candidate Down-Selection. Applications should include the supporting documentation required by the University, including academic transcripts, a CV, two references, and the relevant research summary and reasons for applying. The deadline for applications is 6 January 2027 (23:59 UK time) . Shortlisted candidates will be invited to interview in January 2027 . Informal enquiries about the project are welcome and may be addressed to Dr Sergio Bacallado at sb2116@cam.ac.uk . The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Key information Department/location Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics Salary Reference LF51434 Category Studentships Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 6 January 2027 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/building-services-technician-fixed-term-pm51450": {
      "title": "Building Services Technician (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Building Services Technician (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Building Services Technician (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Building Services Technician (Fixed Term) A short term opportunity has arisen for a versatile Building Services Technician to join our friendly facilities team in the Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience, providing an efficient and reliable service to our buildings located in central Cambridge. The role holder will provide day to day maintenance across our two buildings which consist of laboratories, classrooms, offices and lecture theatres. The role holder will support the team with planned and reactive maintenance of our buildings to ensure the provision of a safe, legal and compliant working environment. This will include the maintenance and monitoring of heating, plumbing, and ventilation systems, dealing with gas canisters, general building infrastructure repairs, assisting with the installation of equipment and portering. The role will also include work which could be part of a refurbishment or remodelling and would include painting, carpentry and metal work. The successful candidate will have good organisational and prioritising skills, as well as excellent problem solving and fault-finding skills.  A flexible working approach is a must and you should have good interpersonal, team-working and customer service skills. You will have experience in a maintenance role, and experience in a University or laboratory environment would be advantageous Informal enquiries about the role and working at the University are encouraged, and these can be directed to Giulia Iadevaia, gi229@cam.ac.uk . Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 6 months. Apply: Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. On the online form, in the suitability for the role section, you should explain the reasons for your application and how your knowledge, skills and experience match the requirements of the role. The full contact details for two professional referees should be provided, one of who should be your most recent line manager. Please note that we will assume that you are happy for us to approach your referees at any stage, unless you indicate otherwise in the space provided on the form. The closing date for applications is 5 November 2026. Please quote reference PM51450 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £25,249-£27,319 Reference PM51450 Category Assistant staff Date published 21 October 2026 Closing date 5 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/change-and-engagement-manager-fixed-term-ag51483": {
      "title": "Change and Engagement Manager (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Change and Engagement Manager (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Change and Engagement Manager (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Change and Engagement Manager (Fixed Term) We reserve the right to close this vacancy early if we receive sufficient applications for the role. Therefore, if you are interested, please submit your application as early as possible If you're looking for a new challenge with excellent benefits, extensive learning opportunities and a stimulating working environment, this is the place for you. The Finance Division are looking for a Change and Engagement Manager. The role is key to the delivery of the University's Finance Transformation Project. You will work closely with sponsors, internal and external stakeholders to support change initiatives in meeting their objectives. Sitting within the Finance Division on The Finance Transformation Programme (FTP). FTP is part of a series of change initiatives at Cambridge University, which aim to embrace new ways of working to better support our staff and academic mission. These initiatives are closely linked to the University's priorities and will help ensure that the University continues to thrive in the future. The Role You will work with stakeholders and internal teams to support change initiatives in meeting objectives by increasing employee adoption and usage. Focusing on the people side of change - including changes to business processes, governance, data, technology and ways working. The primary responsibility will be the management of engagement and adoption of multiple university schools and departments to ensure that they are ready to embrace the new ways of working both at go live and beyond. You will work to drive faster adoption, higher utilisation and greater proficiency of the changes that impact staff to increase benefit realisation and the achievement of results and outcomes. Your Skills The right candidate will: Be pro-active and self-motivated with the ability to build and maintain relationships with both internal team members and wider university stakeholders. Experienced in change management principles and methodology and able to apply those in practice. Be able to manage multiple complex projects and stakeholders whilst maintaining sight of the overall project outcomes deadlines. The ability to engage with a complex internal team whilst delivering easy to understand, engaging interactions with the wider schools and stakeholders. Excellent communication & listening skills - both written and verbal, combined with the ability to prepare and conduct high impact workshops and presentations. Benefits The University offers an excellent benefit package including: 41 annual leave, inclusive of Bank Holidays Defined benefits pension schemes Flexible working options Family-friendly initiatives Career development opportunities Support for health & mental wellbeing Discounts on shopping Rental deposit scheme Public transport season ticket loans Tax-efficient bicycle and charity-giving schemes Location The University is supportive of hybrid working, where some work is undertaken on University premises and some in a remote working environment where the role allows. Specific arrangements will be discussed at interview and will not form a part of the contractual terms of the role. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 30 September 2027 in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check, a health assessment and a security check. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you would like more information about this role, informal enquiries can be directed by email to Heather Ajimi, Senior Change and Engagement Manager at Heather.Ajimi@admin.cam.ac.uk If you have any questions the application process, please contact a Recruitment Administrator at Finhr_recruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is 06 November 2026. The interviews date for the role are planned 20 November 2026. Please quote reference AG51483 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Change and Engagement Manager Key information Department/location Finance Division Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference AG51483 Category Academic-related Date published 23 October 2026 Closing date 6 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube 