    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/epsrc-fibe3-cdt-studentship-with-national-highways-extreme-weather-implications-for-concrete-nm51441": {
      "title": "EPSRC FIBE3 CDT Studentship with National Highways: Extreme weather implications for concrete behaviour",
      "description": "EPSRC FIBE3 CDT Studentship with National Highways: Extreme weather implications for concrete behaviour | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs EPSRC FIBE3 CDT Studentship with National Highways:  Extreme weather implications for concrete behaviour Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs EPSRC FIBE3 CDT Studentship with National Highways:  Extreme weather implications for concrete behaviour This is a four-year (1+3 MRes/PhD) studentship funded through the Cambridge EPSRC Centre for Doctoral Training in Future Infrastructure and Built Environment: Unlocking Net Zero (FIBE3 CDT). Further details can be found at https://www.net-zero-fibe-cdt.eng.cam.ac.uk/ The project is funded in collaboration with National Highways.  National Highways is the UK government company which plans, designs, builds, operates and maintains England's motorways and major A-roads, known as the strategic road network. Due to geological abundance of its constituents, and its strength and resilience, concrete is used widely across National Highway's strategic road network in both ancillary and primary structures.  However, conventional ordinary Portland cement concrete is carbon intensive so any new construction must have embodied carbon reduction at the fore and, wherever possible, the lifetime of existing infrastructure must be extended.  In parallel, external weather demands on concrete during placement, and in service, are escalating due to climate change. This project therefore has elements of both concrete climate change mitigation, and adaptation.  One particular area of uncertainty is whether lower carbon concretes may exhibit greater sensitivity to harsher early age curing conditions leading to detrimental longer-term chemical or microstructural effects, and thus an undesirable reduction in quality.  Acceleration of concrete exposure conditions in practice will also be investigated to consider the effects on concrete of more extreme temperatures and repeated wetting and extended drying not previously seen in modern history. The project objectives include: to exploit advanced digital measurement techniques in tandem with concrete characterisation experiments under controlled and/or accelerated exposure conditions e.g. temperature, humidity, carbonation; to investigate macro-scale and micro-scale models for prediction (AI tools will be used as appropriate) to infer correlation and for physics-informed modelling; to conduct evidence-based analyses of trade-offs between quality and embodied carbon. The new knowledge derived through the project will frame guidance on acceptable weather conditions during placement and enable pre-emptive prediction of any detrimental effects due to more severe in-service exposure conditions to thereby future-proof both new and existing infrastructure. For project-specific enquiries please e-mail Professor Janet Lees: jml1010@cam.ac.uk .  For general enquiries, please email cdtcivil-courseadmin@eng.cam.ac.uk . Applicants should have (or expect to obtain by the start date) at least a high 2.1 degree and preferably a 1st class degree.  The degree should be in engineering, sciences or a relevant discipline, and will preferably be at a Master's level.  Previous expertise in concrete materials is desirable. Fully-funded studentships (fees and maintenance) are only available for eligible home students in the first instance. A limited number of international students can be considered for funding at a later stage in the recruitment process.\nFurther details about eligibility and funding can be found at: https://www.ukri.org/councils/esrc/career-and-skills-development/funding-for-postgraduate-training/eligibility-for-studentship-funding/ https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/finance/fees https://www.cambridgetrust.org/scholarships/ Applications should be made online via the University of Cambridge Applicant Portal: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/egegpdfib stating project title and supervisor's name. Please note there is a £20 application fee.\nEarly applications are strongly encouraged as an offer may be made before the stated deadline. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary Reference NM51441 Category Studentships Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 13 April 2027 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/epsrc-fibe3-cdt-studentship-with-national-highways-rapid-low-risk-adoption-of-lower-carbon-concrete-nm51440": {
      "title": "EPSRC FIBE3 CDT Studentship with National Highways: Rapid, low risk, adoption of lower carbon concrete",
      "description": "EPSRC FIBE3 CDT Studentship with National Highways: Rapid, low risk, adoption of lower carbon concrete | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs EPSRC FIBE3 CDT Studentship with National Highways:  Rapid, low risk, adoption of lower carbon concrete Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs EPSRC FIBE3 CDT Studentship with National Highways:  Rapid, low risk, adoption of lower carbon concrete This is a four-year (1+3 MRes/PhD) studentship funded through the Cambridge EPSRC Centre for Doctoral Training in Future Infrastructure and Built Environment: Unlocking Net Zero (FIBE3 CDT). Further details can be found at https://www.net-zero-fibe-cdt.eng.cam.ac.uk/ The project is funded in collaboration with National Highways.  National Highways is the UK government company which plans, designs, builds, operates and maintains England's motorways and major A-roads. Concrete has traditionally used Portland cement as the primary binder material but the high embodied carbon penalty associated with high proportions of Portland cement is no longer tenable in a climate emergency. The construction industry is responding by actively replacing cement with other constituents with lower carbon footprints.  The challenge is to rapidly adopt promising binder alternatives while ensuring that mix compositions with a relatively short performance track record will successfully meet the demands of infrastructure over 50-100 years. This project seeks to derisk the adoption of newer lower carbon concretes. Binders with higher levels of cement replacement through supplementary cementitious materials (SCMs) and/or filler materials will be scrutinised in the context of concrete material properties e.g. microstructure, and performance measures e.g. durability. A combination of digitally enhanced experimental work and multi-physics modelling will help establish performance comparisons for compositions with SCM replacement to thereby identify and validate appropriate limits on proportion and composition thresholds. The project objectives include: to quantify the alignment of lower carbon concrete combinations against current design standards; to identify promising multi-component binder concretes that sit within, or outside, design standards e.g. through combinations of limestone, calcined clay, fly ash and GGBS replacement, but for which testing compliance is needed to address knowledge gaps to provide confidence in future performance; to undertake comprehensive experimentation and theoretical investigations to support service-life modelling. The project outcomes will give greater confidence in the fundamental behaviour of lower carbon concrete mixes to accelerate uptake in UK infrastructure to help meet net zero aspirations. For project-specific enquiries please e-mail Professor Janet Lees: jml1010@cam.ac.uk .  For general enquiries, please email cdtcivil-courseadmin@eng.cam.ac.uk . Applicants should have (or expect to obtain by the start date) at least a high 2.1 degree and preferably a 1st class degree.  The degree should be in engineering, sciences or a relevant discipline, and will preferably be at a Master's level.  Previous expertise in concrete materials is desirable. Fully-funded studentships (fees and maintenance) are only available for eligible home students in the first instance. A limited number of international students can be considered for funding at a later stage in the recruitment process.\nFurther details about eligibility and funding can be found at: https://www.ukri.org/councils/esrc/career-and-skills-development/funding-for-postgraduate-training/eligibility-for-studentship-funding/ https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/finance/fees https://www.cambridgetrust.org/scholarships/ Applications should be made online via the University of Cambridge Applicant Portal: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/egegpdfib stating project title and supervisor's name. Please note there is a £20 application fee.\nEarly applications are strongly encouraged as an offer may be made before the stated deadline. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary Reference NM51440 Category Studentships Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 13 April 2027 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/eu-research-support-facilitator-ew51475": {
      "title": "EU Research Support Facilitator",
      "description": "EU Research Support Facilitator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs EU Research Support Facilitator Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs EU Research Support Facilitator Are you an experienced research support professional with an interest in European and international research funding? Are you confident providing specialist advice to academics and supporting complex funding applications and awards? The Cambridge Research Office is seeking an EU Research Support Facilitator to join its EU team and provide expert support for European Commission research funding. The University of Cambridge is one of the world's leading research-intensive universities. The Research Office  provides strategic and operational support across the research funding lifecycle. The Role: Working closely with academics, Departments and colleagues across the Research Office, you will provide specialist advice and support throughout the lifecycle of European and international research grants, from application development and submission through to award management and reporting. You will advise researchers and professional services colleagues on funding requirements, budgeting, costing and pricing, support the transition of successful applications from pre-award to post-award management, and maintain expert knowledge of European Commission and international funder requirements, translating these into clear and practical guidance. You will also: support European Commission funding applications, grant agreements and award amendments; liaise with funding bodies, particularly the European Commission, and develop effective relationships with internal and external stakeholders; provide advice on budgeting, costing, pricing and compliance in line with funder requirements and University policies; analyse funding outcomes and feedback to identify future opportunities; contribute to the development and improvement of processes and guidance for European research funding; support the management of risks associated with research funding applications and awards; and support training, induction and staff development activities relating to European Commission funding. About you You will have experience of research support activities, preferably within Higher Education, together with a background in research funding or grants administration. You will have the financial awareness to advise on research grant budgeting, costing and pricing, alongside an understanding of financial systems and budgeting processes. You will be self-motivated, able to work independently and as part of a team, and capable of managing competing priorities. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills are essential. You will be comfortable communicating complex information to a range of audiences and building productive relationships with academics, researchers, professional services colleagues and external organisations. You will also have: excellent written communication skills, including experience preparing papers and reports; the ability to develop and participate effectively in internal and external networks; good working knowledge of Microsoft Excel and PowerPoint; an understanding of the Higher Education sector and research environment; and a relevant degree or an equivalent combination of experience, education and training. Knowledge and experience of European Commission or other international research funding is desirable. What we can offer: This is an opportunity to join a specialist team at the heart of the University's research support activity. The role offers variety across research funding advice, grant administration, financial management, stakeholder engagement, process improvement, funding intelligence and staff development. You will have the opportunity to develop expertise in European and international research funding while contributing directly to the University's ability to secure and manage externally funded research. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: \nReuben Almeida at: reuben.almeida@admin.cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact:\nCambridge Research Office Recruitment at: crorecruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk Please see the Further Particulars document for more details of this vacancy. Your application should clearly set out how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification (the skills and experience required for the role) along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted based on these criteria. Please include details of your referees, including email address, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference EW51475 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cambridge Research Office Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference EW51475 Category Academic-related Date published 22 October 2026 Closing date 22 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
