    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/faculty-position-in-statistics-lf51433": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Statistics",
      "description": "Faculty Position in Statistics | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Faculty Position in Statistics Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Faculty Position in Statistics The Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics is seeking to recruit a new Faculty member who can contribute to research and teaching in Statistics from September 2027 (or by agreement). Statistics is broadly interpreted, and includes theoretical aspects of mathematical statistics through to foundations of machine learning. Appointment will be made at Assistant Professor (Grade 9), which, subject to satisfactory completion of a probation period (3-5 years), leads to a tenured position. Candidates should hold a PhD or equivalent in Statistics, Mathematics, or a closely related subject. The ideal candidate will have a strong track record of research excellence and be able to lecture a range of courses in statistics at undergraduate and master's levels. They will have the ability, or potential, to secure research funding to support their research vision and build a network of collaborators. The post-holder will be part of the world-leading Statistical Laboratory with strong interdisciplinary connections within Cambridge, the UK, and internationally. The University of Cambridge has been ranked in the top 5 for Mathematics in the QS World Rankings for each of the last 5 years. The Mathematics departments occupy a modern, purpose-built campus, also including the Isaac Newton Institute. As well as relocation costs, start-up packages (up to 5 years) are provided to assist the post-holder in establishing their research in Cambridge, and regular sabbatical leave enables the Faculty to focus on research and scholarship. For further information please see the Further Particulars. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. You will need to upload a full curriculum vitae, research and teaching statements, list of publications, (please indicate your view of your strongest publications or preprints, up to a maximum of six) and the contact details, including email addresses, of three academic referees. Please ensure that your referees are aware that they will be contacted at any time during the process by the Mathematics HR Office Administrator to request that they upload a reference for you via our Web Recruitment System, and please encourage them to do so promptly. Informal enquiries should be directed in the first instance to Professor Po-Ling Loh via email at LF51433@maths.cam.ac.uk . Closing date for applications is Monday, 7 December 2026 . References will be contacted promptly upon receipt of applications, and all reference letters must be submitted by Monday, 14 December 2026. Interviews will provisionally take place between 25 January and 05 February 2027 . Please quote reference LF51433 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics Salary £48,337-£61,165 Reference LF51433 Category Academic Date published 22 October 2026 Closing date 7 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/finance-assistant-ve51481": {
      "title": "Finance Assistant",
      "description": "Finance Assistant | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Finance Assistant Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Finance Assistant Ever wanted to work in an institution that is home to one of the world's great collections of cultural treasures and research materials? Spanning thousands of years of human thought and discovery, Cambridge University Libraries and Archives house unique treasures and research materials made up of nearly ten million books, maps, manuscripts, photographs and priceless objects. The role An opportunity has arisen to join the Finance Team at Cambridge University Libraries and Archives (CULA) as a Finance Assistant. Reporting into the Finance Lead, you will work closely with all members of the Finance team, undertaking transaction processing and providing administrative support. As Finance Assistant you will Provide accounts payable and accounts receivable support to our colleagues in CULA. Be responsible for banking income received. Assist with the running of both month end and year end processes. Act as a contact for our CULA colleagues for accounting enquiries. Monitor stationery stock levels, ordering and distributing stationery as required. What we are looking for Experience of working in an accounts environment. Experience of using standard Microsoft Office packages, particularly Excel. Good organisational skills. Attention to detail. Good communication skills and a collaborative approach. What we offer Flexible and hybrid working opportunities such as part-time hours, job shares, and staggered hours. Generous annual leave allowance of 36 days. Exceptional parental leave (including maternity, paternity / co-parent, adoption and shared parental leave). Discounts on major retailers. Professional development opportunities such as apprenticeships and access to a variety of training courses. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. We particularly welcome applications from candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries please contact Bethany Hales, Finance Lead at bh587@cam.ac.uk Application closing date: Sunday 8 November 2026. Interview date: Thursday 19 November 2026 For further details please refer to the Further Particulars. Please quote reference VE51481 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location University Library Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference VE51481 Category Assistant staff Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/finance-manager-ra51445": {
      "title": "Finance Manager",
      "description": "Finance Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Finance Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Finance Manager We are looking to recruit a highly motivated and experienced Finance Manager to join the School of Clinical Medicine Cluster manage and oversee the financial matters of five academic departments. The Departments of Radiology, Surgery, Paediatrics, Obstetrics and Gynaecology and Genomic Medicine have clustered their Professional Services staff to form one integrated team, with specialist groups in HR, Finance and Education. This team manages all administrative matters across the five departments. The departments are housed across different locations in the Biomedical Campus. This is a senior position in the Cluster professional services team, reporting directly to the Business and Operations Manager. The role holder will lead the finance team with four direct reports. The post holder's duties will include overseeing monthly, quarterly and annual management accounts; the preparation of annual financial submissions; financial data analysis; and the provision of financial, budgetary and planning advice to senior management and Departmental staff, including the five Heads of Department. The successful candidate will take responsibility for financial controls and planning within the Departments. This is an exciting time to join, as Departments continue to expand through active capital projects and academic recruitment. The role holder will have the opportunity to develop and implement new financial processes and procedures to enhance management of the expanding financial requirements. The post holder will hold a degree in accounting, or equivalent level of qualification. They will be able to demonstrate advanced knowledge of accounting, and research grants, and have substantial experience in a senior finance management role in a similar environment. Informal enquiries can be made to James Barrett, Business and Operation Manager at jwab3@cam.ac.uk Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RA51445 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars (RA51445) Key information Department/location School of Clinical Medicine Cluster Team Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference RA51445 Category Academic-related Date published 20 October 2026 Closing date 15 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/functional-analyst-fixed-term-vc51424": {
      "title": "Functional Analyst (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Functional Analyst (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Functional Analyst (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Functional Analyst (Fixed Term) We are pleased to announce that an opportunity has become available for a Grade 7 Functional Analyst role to join the Teaching Learning & Assessment team, primarily to support the UIS Digital Exam service and the platform Inspera. In the first instance this is a fixed 2 year post. The expected start date is 'as soon as possible'. As a Functional Analyst, you will support the University's digital exam systems and UIS digital exams service as part of the TLA team, and as part of a group of internal functional analysts. You will be responsible for the functional change evaluation and change management arising from user support queries and vendor change. You will also be responsible for supporting product investigations, testing, configuration, data manipulation and supporting reporting, patching, compliance, policy and procedure, evaluations and audits. You will also provide user support (2nd and 3rd line support calls). You will also support the evaluation & implementation of new vendor driven functionality and continuous service improvements. This role will sit within the TLA team and will be expected to liaise with teams outside of UIS, including teams within Educational Services and departmental users. In addition the role may liaise with stakeholders, portfolio teams and the vendor, on product and functional matters. The role will work alongside, and in partnership, with other roles within TLA and the wider digital exams service, including the Head of TLA, TLA team lead, digital exams operations management, technical specialists and support analysts. The successful candidate will have experience with functional application support, change evaluation and management, application testing and investigation, 2/3rd line user support. An ideal candidate will have experience supporting (from a functional and product point of view) digital education platforms and/or SaaS products. The successful candidate will also have strong analytical, problem-solving and communication skills. You should be organised, customer-focused and keen to learn new technologies. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you would like more information about this role, informal enquiries can be directed by email to recruitment@uis.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference VC51424 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Functional Analyst Further Information Key information Department/location University Information Services Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference VC51424 Category Academic-related Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 2 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/genomics-bioinformatician-fixed-term-rb51512": {
      "title": "Genomics Bioinformatician (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Genomics Bioinformatician (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Genomics Bioinformatician (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Genomics Bioinformatician (Fixed Term) Do you have great computational, analytical and organisational skills?  Interested in a role which is contributing to changing lives for the better? Looking for your next challenge? We are recruiting a Genomics Bioinformatician who has formal bioinformatics and programming experience or training. You will need to be familiar with statistical analysis and data visualisation techniques and have the ability to manage and organise the priorities of a small team to succeed in this role. About NIHR BioResource: Our mission is to facilitate human health research and its transformation into medical practice. NIHR BioResource is a unique and powerful resource for studying disease mechanisms and for investigating the links between genes, the environment, health and disease, enabling scientific discoveries as well as facilitating translational medicine for the benefit of patients. Further details on the NIHR BioResource, including some of our COVID-19 research studies, can be found on our website https://bioresource.nihr.ac.uk . About the job: The post holder will manage a small sub team in charge of fulfilling data requests, by providing genomic data and/or defining cohorts based on genomic profiles. NIHR BioResource aims to substantially increase the number of research studies it supports, by adopting new tools and opening this position. The data sets are currently hosted at the University's Cambridge Service for Data Driven Discovery (CSD3) but will soon be migrated to the public cloud (AWS) and integrated in a commercial bioinformatics platform that includes a variant database and a Secure Desktop Environment (SDE) for data sharing. The post holder is also expected to contribute when needed to other activities of the Bioinformatics team. The post requires proficiency in programming, bioinformatics tools, and database management. Collaborative and detail-oriented approach essential. The postholder will ideally have experience in bioinformatics, computational genomics, software development, rare or complex disorder genetics and have a keen interest in translational research. About you: Ideally, you will have: Good all-round informatics skills in LINUX/UNIX, HPC, data management, scripting (python, R, Bash) and version control (Git) Experience of team or project management, including initiative and delivery Excellent time-management skills with ability to work to deadlines Experience of Cohorts and Biobanks What we offer: The University offers a wide range of benefits including flexible working, family friendly policies, an attractive pension scheme (employer's contribution is 14.5%). and 41 days' annual holiday (inclusive of public holidays). We encourage career development by providing extensive training & development opportunities. This appointment is full-time, 37 hours per week; part-time to 80% will be considered. Work / life balance is important, and we are open to discussion on flexible working, including hybrid working. The post is available on a fixed-term basis until 31 March 2028 in the first instance. NIHR BioResource is funded by the National Institute for Health & Care Research (NIHR) on a 4-year funding cycle. Our offices are on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus CB2 0QQ (approximately 2 miles south of the city centre). See the Further Information link below for full details and Person Specification. This appointment requires a Research Passport application. We encourage informal queries about the job or the application process - please contact Stephane Ballereau, Lead Bioinformatician, Stephane.Ballereau@bioresource.nihr.ac.uk . How to apply: Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Closing date:  3 weeks from Advert Interview date: w/c 30th November 2026 Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RB51512 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars (RB51512) Key information Department/location Department of Haematology Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference RB51512 Category Academic-related Date published 27 October 2026 Closing date 17 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/glaxosmithkline-funded-phd-studentship-immune-ageing-fixed-term-rc51518": {
      "title": "GlaxoSmithKline-funded PhD studentship: Immune Ageing (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "GlaxoSmithKline-funded PhD studentship: Immune Ageing (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs GlaxoSmithKline-funded PhD studentship: Immune Ageing (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs GlaxoSmithKline-funded PhD studentship: Immune Ageing (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for fully funded, four-year PhD studentships based at the Cambridge Institute for Therapeutic Immunology and Infectious Disease (CITIID), within the Department of Medicine at the University of Cambridge. The studentship programme is part of CG-AGE, a collaborative project between the Department of Medicine and GlaxoSmithKline, focused on the mechanisms and phenotypes of immune ageing . Project The UK population is getting older, with nearly 1 in 5 individuals over the age of 60 and over half a million aged 90 or older (2021 Census). This is important, because ageing is strongly associated with altered immune function, leading to increased susceptibility to infectious and inflammatory diseases, which are major contributors to morbidity and mortality in older adults. A major limitation to our understanding of human immune age has been a reliance on analysis of few immune traits in comparatively small cohorts with limited representation of the oldest individuals. To overcome this, we have integrated multiple datasets to identify and validate latent age-related trajectories, capturing quantitative traits reflecting immune exposure and response, and uncovering highly stereotyped and non-linear trajectories of immune ageing. Here, we propose to build on our existing work to quantify multiple immune age metrics in a longitudinal population of ageing individuals undergoing annual immune challenge in the form of vaccination. We will relate these immune age metrics to a variety of outcomes including correlates of vaccine protection and clinical outcomes with the aim of inferring mechanistic processes that drive immune ageing. Students will use multi-omic single cell methods to identify cell subpopulations associated with both chronological and immunological ageing (defined by a range of different ageing metrics), their associated differentiation trajectories and multi-omic characteristics and will relate these to the magnitude, breadth and efficacy of vaccine-induced protection. These data will deeply characterise the aged immune response, explore the mechanism of changes occurring and support development of experimental medicine interventions to modulate aged immunity. The successful candidates will each undertake a four-year research project with the overall aims of (1) characterising aged immune responses, (2) exploring how they change longitudinally in an ageing population, and (3) investigating how this knowledge can be exploited to better modulate aged immunity. Depending on the specific interests of the student, individual projects may include exploitation of computational machine learning approaches in concert with wet-lab experiments on aged samples. Wet-lab experiments will include generation of multi-omic single cell datasets alongside immune organoid models, while dry-lab work will seek to interpret age-related changes and their impact on immunity using advances in the capabilities of machine learning/AI techniques. Students will be a core part of CG-AGE, a five-year collaboration between the University of Cambridge and GSK, led by Prof. Eoin McKinney, that seeks to better understand immune ageing and how to modulate it. Individual studentships may have (co)supervision from investigators affiliated with the CG-AGE programme. PhD programme The studentships are 4 years in length, starting from October 2027, and will include an optional 6-month sabbatical period to be spent in another laboratory either in Cambridge or in GSK to gain additional skills and knowledge (this may optionally include a sabbatical GSK placement). Studentships include fees and maintenance for students eligible for Home fee status. For eligibility please check: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/finance/fees/what-my-fee-status . The programme will include several studentships and aims to equip the next generation of scientists with an in depth understanding of the molecular and cellular basis underlying immune ageing together with how that intersects with the pathogenesis of immune and infectious disorders. Students will have access to training in scientific communication, critical thinking, career development, and other transferrable skills through: -One-to-one supervision: you will receive direct guidance from your supervisors and attend weekly journal clubs. -Presentations: give one poster presentation per year to the Department, and present your research at our seminar series and annual symposia. -Networking: hear from recent alumni and non-academic representatives on their career paths. -Attend Principal Investigator-led core topic sessions covering fundamental techniques (bioinformatics, microscopy, flow cytometry, data analysis, and statistics), cutting-edge technologies (genetic screens, new proteomic approaches, and mathematical modelling) and key transferable skills (communication, scientific writing, public engagement, and ethics). Students will submit a thesis of no more than 60,000 words within five years of commencing study in the programme. Candidate We are looking for highly motivated, enthusiastic and industrious individuals who can work both independently and within a team. The studentships would be suitable for students with a background in either molecular biology or computational biology/informatics. Thorough training will be provided in both fields and will be adapted depending on the candidate's previous experience. This PhD offers the unique opportunity to work at the interface of basic science and drug discovery in both academic and industry settings, and by the end of the studentship you will have gained a broad range of key experimental, computational and transferable skills that will serve as a springboard for a successful research career. Applicants should have or should expect to obtain a minimum of a UK 2:1 Honours Degree (or equivalent) in a relevant subject and should be passionate about discovering new biology with the goal of improving human health. To be appointed applicants must meet the minimum entry criteria which can be found at: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvmdpdmed Environment CG-AGE is led by Professor Eoin McKinney and students will be closely aligned with work taking place across the CG-AGE programme. Professor Eoin McKinney is the Versus Arthritis Professor of Clinical Autoimmunity, Director of Studies for Pembroke College and faculty of the Cambridge Centre for Artificial Intelligence in Medicine (CCAIM) and a Principal Investigator based in the Cambridge Institute for Therapeutic Immunology and Infections Disease ( https://www.citiid.cam.ac.uk/ ), situated within the Jeffrey Cheah Biomedical Centre at the heart of the Cambridge Biomedical Campus. The McKinney Lab are interested in exploring the phenotype and mechanism of autoinflammatory disease and how that understanding can be used to develop novel approaches for immunomodulation.\nAs a graduate student at the University of Cambridge, you will have access to a wide range of training opportunities and benefit from close supervision provided by a primary and secondary PhD supervisor from amongst affiliated CG-AGE investigators as well as a personal mentor. You will also become a member of one of the 31 Cambridge Colleges, through which you will have access to accommodation and a wide variety of student clubs, societies and activities. Funding The position is fully funded. Beginning on 1st October 2027, you will receive an annual stipend and your University tuition fees (at the home rate) will be fully covered for four years. Applications are welcomed from overseas students who are able to cover the additional costs associated with international student fees through scholarships or other funding schemes, such as the Cambridge Commonwealth, European & International Trust ( https://www.cambridgetrust.org/scholarships ) or Gates Cambridge ( https://www.gatescambridge.org/ ); a database of all funding opportunities can be found at https://www.student-funding.cam.ac.uk/ . Further information on how fee status is determined is available at https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/finance/fees/what-my-fee-status Application Process Interested applicants should apply through the central University of Cambridge Postgraduate Admissions portal ( https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cvmdpdmed ). The closing date for applications for this specific PhD opportunity is 15th January 2027. During the first stage of the selection process we will consider your academic record, the comments from your referees, and your research experience and achievements to date. We are mindful that the extent of research opportunity varies greatly between candidates. Candidates may be asked to complete a brief written assessment to establish their suitability for the position, and shortlisted candidates will be invited to attend an interview. Please direct informal enquiries to Professor Eoin McKinney ( efm30@cam.ac.uk ) by email. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RC51518 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Medicine Salary Reference RC51518 Category Studentships Date published 27 October 2026 Closing date 15 January 2027 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/gpeg-operations-manager-sn51516": {
      "title": "GPEG Operations Manager",
      "description": "GPEG Operations Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs GPEG Operations Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs GPEG Operations Manager Operations Manager - General Practice Education Group (GPEG) The General Practice Education Group (GPEG), within the University of Cambridge's Primary Care Unit, is looking for a dynamic Operations Manager to play a central role in delivering high-quality primary care education. This is a varied and influential position at the heart of GPEG's work with students, academic colleagues, NHS partners and GP practices. You will lead the day-to-day delivery of administrative services, oversee placement and teaching activity, coordinate projects and events, manage business/financial processes, and provide the information needed to support effective decisions. You will have real scope to shape how the team works. Alongside supporting and developing the roles direct report and colleagues, you will strengthen processes, introduce practical improvements and make effective use of digital tools and automation. You will also build trusted relationships across a wide network, helping to resolve operational challenges and ensure that students and teaching partners receive a responsive, reliable service. We are looking for someone with excellent organisational and communication skills who can balance competing priorities, use sound judgement and turn complex requirements into clear, workable solutions. If you enjoy taking ownership, improving systems and contributing to education with a direct impact on future healthcare professionals, this role offers an excellent opportunity to make a difference. The successful candidate will be based in the Department of Public Health and Primary Care at the East Forvie Building, University Forvie Site, Robinson Way, Cambridge Biomedical Campus, Cambridge CB2 0SR. The University supports hybrid working where the requirements of the role allow, although this post includes other activities that will require attendance on University premises and at teaching sessions. This post is full-time (37 hours per week - 1.0 FTE) The funds for this post are available until 05 November 2029 Informal enquiries can be made via email to Angus Ramus: phpc.hr@medschl.cam.ac.uk Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 22nd November 2026 Interview Date: 8th December 2026 Please quote reference SN51516 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Department of Public Health and Primary Care, The Primary Care Unit Salary £36,320 - £46,970 Reference SN51516 Category Academic-related Date published 27 October 2026 Closing date 22 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/head-of-higher-degrees-and-ppd-office-jr51471": {
      "title": "Head of Higher Degrees and PPD Office",
      "description": "Head of Higher Degrees and PPD Office | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Head of Higher Degrees and PPD Office Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Head of Higher Degrees and PPD Office About Us The University of Cambridge is one of the world's leading academic institutions, renowned for its excellence in education, research, and innovation. For nearly 800 years, we've been at the forefront of groundbreaking discoveries, shaping global knowledge across diverse fields. We boast 31 Colleges, over 150 faculties and departments, and a thriving ecosystem of museums, libraries, and research centres. We are now looking for a Head of Higher Degrees and PPD Office to join our Faculty of Education on a full-time, permanent basis. The Benefits Salary of £36,320 - £46,970 per annum Generous annual leave allowance Generous maternity, adoption and shared parental leave entitlement and other family-friendly schemes, including workplace nurseries Auto-enrolment pension scheme with a generous employer contribution University Staff Counselling Service Travel benefits and retail discounts at over 2,000 local and national stores Relocation support schemes Development opportunities This is an excellent opportunity for a senior administrator with superb people management skills and a focus on improvement and operational procedures to join our world-renowned university. You'll have the chance to join one of the country's leading centres for educational research and teaching, and play a key supporting role in the delivery of qualifications for individuals who will lead thinking and innovation around the world. The Role As our Head of Higher Degrees and PPD Office, you will oversee the delivery of an effective admission and administration service for Faculty of Education staff to support Postgraduate and PPD courses as well as several senior members of the faculty. Specifically, you will support the Director of Learning and Teaching, the Business and Operations Manager and the Head of Faculty in their work, as well as supporting the administration of MPhil, MEd, EdD, PhD and Professional Practitioner Development (PPD) courses. You will also take responsibility for admissions for UK and international students for PPD courses, totally around 600 students, whilst leading a team of seven staff, and working closely with academic programme leaders and the Director of Learning and Teaching. Additionally, you will: Take responsibility for student registration and assessment and examination admin Act as the escalation point for non-routine correspondence and issue resolution Work closely with the Education Quality & Policy Office (EQPO), the learning and teaching team and the Student Registry Support staff with the student complaints process Allocate work to the team, train them on new processes and monitor performance Maintain the Single Central Record process, and manage student records Act as the Disability Liaison Officer for Postgraduate and PPD courses About You To be considered as our Head of Higher Degrees and PPD Office, you will need: Experience in a senior administrative role Experience managing and supervising staff Advanced knowledge of administration and operational management principles Experience leading change and continuous improvement Knowledge of safeguarding principles and safer recruitment practices, with the ability to apply relevant policies and procedures Excellent leadership and people management skills Strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills Excellent organisational, planning and workload management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and deadlines The ability to work independently, solve problems, use initiative and adapt to changing circumstances Strong digital skills, including proficiency in Microsoft Office A degree, Level 6 vocational qualification or equivalent level of practical experience Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For detailed information about the role and working for the University, please see the 'Further Information' link at the bottom of the advert. Informal enquiries can be addressed to Thury Agustsdottir\nFaculty Business and Operations Manager, at BOM@educ.cam.ac.uk If you have any questions about the application process, or have any difficulties with applying for the vacancy, please contact the Faculty of Education HR team at hr@educ.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference JR51471 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Faculty of Education Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference JR51471 Category Academic-related Date published 23 October 2026 Closing date 15 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-recruitment-administrator-ve51359": {
      "title": "HR & Recruitment Administrator",
      "description": "HR & Recruitment Administrator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR & Recruitment Administrator Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR & Recruitment Administrator Ever wanted to work in an institution that is home to one of the world's great collections of cultural treasures and research materials?\nThe HR team at the Cambridge University Libraries and Archives (CULA) support 450+ colleagues in managing and housing unique treasures and research materials made up of nearly ten million books, maps, manuscripts, photographs and priceless objects. The role This is a varied role offering the opportunity to develop your administrative knowledge and experience, working across HR administration, recruitment, and providing employee support. The HR & Recruitment Administrator plays an important role in ensuring the HR processes across CULA run smoothly and efficiently, providing a positive experience for our colleagues and candidates. As HR & Recruitment Administrator you will Provide a high level of administrative support for our hiring managers and candidates throughout the entire recruitment process. Respond to routine HR queries through both written and verbal communication, signposting colleagues to relevant guidance and escalating complex queries where appropriate. Ensure employee records, local trackers, HR systems, and intranet pages are accurately maintained. Process paperwork relating to contractual and payroll related changes for our colleagues. Carry out general administrative tasks such as organising meetings, note taking, and distributing documents. What we're looking for Experience of working in an administrative position. A customer-focussed attitude with the ability to build effective working relationships. Experience of exercising own judgement in organising and prioritising workload. Excellent attention to detail. Comfortable working with Microsoft Office packages (particularly Outlook and Excel). What we offer Flexible and hybrid working opportunities. Generous annual leave allowance of 36 days. Exceptional parental leave (including maternity, paternity / co-parent, adoption and shared parental leave). Discounts on major retailers. Professional development opportunities such as apprenticeships and access to a variety of training courses. For further details please refer to the Further Particulars. When applying, please ensure that you outline how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification of the Further Particulars, along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted based upon these criteria. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries please contact Rob Millington, HR Manager, on rm940@cam.ac.uk . Interview date: Monday 16 or Tuesday 17 November 2026 Please quote reference VE51359 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information HR & Recruitment Administrator - Further Particulars Key information Department/location University Library Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference VE51359 Category Assistant staff Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-policy-team-coordinator-part-time-internal-applicants-only-ah51501": {
      "title": "HR Policy Team Coordinator (Part Time Internal Applicants Only)",
      "description": "HR Policy Team Coordinator (Part Time Internal Applicants Only) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR Policy Team Coordinator (Part Time Internal Applicants Only) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR Policy Team Coordinator (Part Time Internal Applicants Only) The Human Resources Division is seeking an organised and proactive HR Policy Team Co-ordinator to provide high-quality administrative, project co-ordination and communications support to the HR Policy Team. This is an exciting opportunity to play a key role in supporting the development and implementation of University-wide HR policies and initiatives. Working closely with colleagues across the University, you will help ensure the efficient delivery of policy projects by coordinating activities, maintaining project documentation, managing communications and supporting stakeholder engagement. Reporting to the HR Adviser (Policy and Industrial Relations), the postholder will provide comprehensive administrative support to the HR Policy Team, including coordinating meetings and consultations, managing overarching project plans and documentation, maintaining team communications and supporting the management of the team's shared inbox. The role will also involve collecting, analysing and presenting data to inform policy development and supporting the maintenance of HR policy information on the University's website. The successful candidate will have relevant administrative experience, ideally gained within an HR environment, together with excellent organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities. You will be confident working with data, producing accurate reports and maintaining detailed records, while exercising discretion when handling confidential information. Strong communication and interpersonal skills are essential, as is the ability to work both independently and collaboratively with a wide range of stakeholders. You will be educated to A Level standard, NVQ Level 3 or equivalent, or possess an equivalent level of practical experience. Experience of supporting projects, maintaining databases and spreadsheets, and taking accurate meeting notes or minutes is essential. Knowledge of HR processes and experience of dealing with HR-related queries would be advantageous. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Closing date for applications: Midnight, Sunday 1 November 2026 Interviews for the role will be held: Week commencing 2 November 2026 Please quote reference AH51501 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Human Resources Division Salary £31,236-£35,608 pro rata Reference AH51501 Category Assistant staff Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/interim-laboratory-manager-fixed-term-le51413": {
      "title": "Interim Laboratory Manager (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Interim Laboratory Manager (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Interim Laboratory Manager (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Interim Laboratory Manager (Fixed Term) A position exists in the Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics for an Interim Laboratory Manager to play a pivotal role in the organisation and management of the GK Batchelor Laboratory. About the Role We are seeking an experienced and proactive Laboratory Manager to lead the operation and development of a world-class experimental research facility. This is a highly visible and influential role, providing technical leadership, strategic oversight, and operational management across a diverse portfolio of cutting-edge research and teaching activities. Working closely with academic staff, researchers, students, and technical colleagues, you will oversee laboratory facilities, support the design and delivery of innovative experiments, manage specialist equipment and infrastructure, and ensure the highest standards of health, safety, and compliance. You will play a key role in shaping the future development of the Laboratory, leading refurbishment projects, enhancing facilities and services, and enabling outstanding research outcomes. You will be involved in a broad and varied portfolio of projects spanning a wide range of experimental methods, from microscopy and robotics to large-scale fluid mechanics installations. A central part of the role is problem solving and the creative design of bespoke experimental setups, working in close collaboration with the GK Batchelor Laboratory workshop to turn ambitious research ideas into working experiments. About the GK Batchelor Laboratory The role holder is central to the successful operation of the GK Batchelor Laboratory, a world-class research facility spanning nearly 900m² and supporting a diverse range of experimental research, from the physics of microorganisms to large-scale industrial and environmental flows. Equipped with advanced imaging, laser, and experimental technologies, the Laboratory hosts multiple research groups, supports teaching activities, welcomes international visitors, and delivers more than 50 research projects each year. About You You will combine strong technical expertise with excellent leadership, project management, and problem-solving skills. You will thrive in a dynamic environment, providing expert advice, supporting a wide range of users, and ensuring the Laboratory remains a safe, efficient, and inspiring place for research, teaching, and collaboration.\nYou will hold a Master's or PhD in engineering or physical sciences or demonstratable equivalent experience. A minimum of three years of experience in academic research is also essential. Informal enquiries can be made by contacting Professor Raymond E. Goldstein FRS ( R.E.Goldstein@damtp.cam.ac.uk ) If you have any queries about the application process, please email LE51413@maths.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is 22 November 2026 (Midnight UK Time) Interviews are expected to be held during the week commencing 7 December 2026 (in person at the Centre for Mathematical Sciences, Wilberforce Road, Cambridge). How to Apply Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please complete the details on the application form and submit a covering letter clearly stating how your skills and experience match the criteria specified in the person profile section of the further particulars. Please also provide the contact details of two professional referees, at least one of whom should be contactable if you are invited to attend an interview. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Applications from experienced staff already working within the University of Cambridge who would be interested in taking up this role on a secondment basis are welcomed. This is a fixed term position for 12 months in the first instance. Please quote reference LE51413 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference LE51413 Category Academic-related Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 22 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/investigator-ah51480": {
      "title": "Investigator",
      "description": "Investigator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Investigator Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Investigator We are looking for an experienced Investigator to join the Human Resources Division, specialising in complex and sensitive staff investigations. This role plays a key part in ensuring fair, thorough and proportionate investigation processes across a range of staff misconduct and complaint cases. We encourage early applications as they will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. About the role You will lead investigations into a range of staff matters, including complex and sensitive cases such as serious misconduct and complaints, ensuring findings are evidence based and clearly presented. Working closely with HR colleagues and stakeholders, you will provide expert advice, support decision making processes, and contribute to continuous improvement of investigation practices. What you will be doing Managing complex investigations from planning through to final report Interviewing parties, gathering evidence and assessing credibility Producing clear, balanced investigation reports with findings of fact Presenting findings to senior decision makers at hearings Advising stakeholders on investigation processes and risk Delivering training and contributing to improvements in practice We are looking for someone who has Strong experience conducting complex and sensitive workplace investigations Excellent report writing and analytical skills Ability to handle sensitive issues with professionalism and discretion Strong communication and stakeholder management skills Knowledge of UK employment law, investigations and best practice High attention to detail and ability to manage competing priorities Why join us? Alongside working in a collaborative and supportive environment, the University of Cambridge offers a range of excellent benefits, including: Development opportunities such as LinkedIn Learning, job shadowing and career pathways. 41 days annual leave (including bank holidays) and a highly competitive pension scheme. Hybrid working with flexibility in how you arrange your office days. A wide range of discounts at local retailers and thousands of national brands. Health and wellbeing support, including discounted gym access and the Staff Counselling Service. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for flexible working arrangements. How to Apply Temporary cover: This post is a fixed-term maternity cover for 13.5 months or the return of the post holder, whichever is the earlier. For this role, all applications will be managed by Spirehouse Recruitment. To apply, please send your CV to cambridge@spirehouse.co.uk For further information about the role, please contact Abbie Carr (Executive Consultant), abbie@spirehouse.co.uk Closing date: Sunday 08 November 2026 Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Download further information Investigator - Further Information Document Key information Department/location Human Resources Division Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference AH51480 Category Academic-related Date published 22 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/it-technician-part-time-fixed-term-lb50890": {
      "title": "IT Technician (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "IT Technician (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs IT Technician (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs IT Technician (Part Time, Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a part-time (0.6 FTE) IT Technician to support the IT team in the Department of Earth Sciences. This is an exciting opportunity where the post holder will work as part of a team providing IT service delivery to the Department and will be responsible for providing first and second line support, including the problem solving of routine technical issues. Acting as first point of contact, the post holder will respond to a broad range of service requests for support, investigate problems in systems, install / remove hardware and also assist with website administration. The Department of Earth Sciences is a complex and dynamic department within the School of the Physical Sciences.  It is based at two main sites, one on Downing Street in central Cambridge and the other is the Bullard Laboratories at Madingley Rise in West Cambridge; it also incorporates the Sedgwick Museum which has sites both in central Cambridge and in West Cambridge.  The person appointed will be expected to work flexibly across all areas of the Department and will need to travel between the central Cambridge and West Cambridge sites on a regular basis. The successful candidate will be educated to HND, HNC, level 4/5 vocational qualification or have equivalent level of practical experience, with recent experience providing user support and troubleshooting Windows and macOS clients. Previous experience of working in an IT department is also required, with demonstrable knowledge of IT support. The ability to manage and prioritise tasks, with strong communication skills and the ability to effectively work as part of a team are essential. Previous experience of working in Higher Education would be advantageous. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 October 2027 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please see the role description for more information and email Neil Marjoram ( nem41@cam.ac.uk ) if you have any questions. We hope to hold interviews on Wednesday 02 December 2026. Please quote reference LB50890 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Earth Sciences Salary £31,236-£35,608 pro rata Reference LB50890 Category Assistant staff Date published 27 October 2026 Closing date 17 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/learning-and-engagement-manager-lb51447": {
      "title": "Learning and Engagement Manager",
      "description": "Learning and Engagement Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Learning and Engagement Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Learning and Engagement Manager Are you passionate about the power of museums to change lives? Do you share our commitment to addressing inequality in science?\nAre you an excellent communicator able to take a strategic approach to engaging a range of audiences? \nThen we'd like to hear from you! The Sedgwick Museum of Earth Sciences is looking for a full-time Learning and Engagement Manager to take the lead on the development and delivery of a programme of activities aimed at increasing, diversifying and deepening audience engagement and participation both in person and online. The programme will draw on current research and researchers and have a strong emphasis on current themes in Earth, climate and environmental science, on addressing inequality and challenging stereotypes within the Earth sciences, and on the legacies of empire within the Museum's collections. The Museum is in the early stages of planning a major capital development, and we anticipate the Learning and Engagement Manager will contribute to shaping this project. The Museum welcomes around 90,000 visitors a year, with staff of 17 and a valued team of volunteers. Its collections are of national and international historic and scientific importance, comprising more than a million rocks, fossils, minerals and associated archival material acquired over more than 250 years. The Museum is embedded within the active research community of the Department of Earth Sciences, and benefits from being part of the cross-disciplinary University of Cambridge Museums (UCM) consortium, an Arts Council National Portfolio Organisation. Key responsibilities of this role include: \n- Development of an imaginative and strategic programme across a range of audiences, and developing projects, partnerships and networks to ensure it is relevant and audience-led;\n- Ensuring delivery to a high standard, including planning, budgeting and evaluation;\n- Contributing to the duty management rota for public opening of the museum, including supervising front of house staff and taking responsibility for safety and security;\n- Management of the Learning and Engagement team (3 staff) and a wider team of volunteers and students. The successful candidate will have previous experience of planning and running successful public programmes, and of working with a range of audiences.  With demonstrable effective teamworking skills, they will be an expert communicator and confident engaging with Earth and environmental science and current global issues. An understanding of current good practice in museums or similar environments is essential for the role, alongside previous experience of managing staff and/or volunteers. Previous experience of reporting and advocating to funders and stakeholders would also be advantageous. Please note the role holder will be required to contribute to the Museum's Duty Management rota, including working approx. 11 Saturdays a year and may be required to occasionally work out of hours / offsite to deliver activities or events, with prior agreement. Limited funding: The funds for this post are available for 5 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo an enhanced Disclosure and Barring Service check. We particularly welcome applications from candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV) and covering letter, setting out how you meet the requirements of the role, in the upload section. Please see the role description for more information.  If you have any questions or would like an informal chat about the role, please contact the Director, Liz Hide on; eah12@cam.ac.uk We understand that some applicants may want to use AI tools to help them draft their application.   We are looking for a Learning and Engagement Manager who has very strong communication skills, so please make sure your own authentic voice, skills and communication style can shine through in your application. We hope to hold interviews on Tuesday 01 December 2026. Please quote reference LB51447 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Earth Sciences Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference LB51447 Category Academic-related Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 15 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/library-assistant-acquisitions-metadata-fixed-term-vh51477": {
      "title": "Library Assistant (Acquisitions & Metadata) (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Library Assistant (Acquisitions & Metadata) (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Library Assistant (Acquisitions & Metadata) (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Library Assistant (Acquisitions & Metadata) (Fixed Term) Ever wanted to work in an institution that is home to one of the world's great collections of cultural treasures and research materials? Spanning thousands of years of human thought and discovery, Cambridge University Libraries and Archives house unique treasures and research materials made up of nearly ten million books, maps, manuscripts, photographs and priceless objects. The role The Squire Law Library is seeking to appoint an enthusiastic and customer-focused library colleague to the role of Library Assistant (Acquisitions and Metadata). This is an exciting opportunity to join our dedicated team and to play an important part in the operation of one of the largest libraries in the University and one of the leading academic law collections in the UK. As Library Assistant (Acquisitions and Metadata) you will Organise all print book purchases for the Squire. Record all book purchases on Alma Acquisitions. Catalogue all print book purchases, legal deposit material and donations. Classify print material. As part of a team, staff the enquiry desk in both the Squire Law and Radzinowicz Criminology Libraries. What we are looking for An extremely customer-focused individual. A team-player. Someone with library or administrative experience. And has attention to detail and accuracy. What we offer Flexible opportunities such as part-time hours, job shares, and staggered hours. Generous annual leave allowance of 36 days - if worked across 5 days. Exceptional parental leave (including maternity, paternity / co-parent, adoption and shared parental leave). Discounts on major retailers. Professional development opportunities such as apprenticeships and access to a variety of training courses. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. We particularly welcome applications from candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries please contact Kate Faulkner, Acting Squire Law Librarian at kef39@cam.ac.uk or 01223 330064. Application closing date: Sunday 8 November 2026. Interview date: Friday 20 November 2026 For further details please refer to the Further Particulars. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 18 months in the first instance. Please quote reference VH51477 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Squire Law Library Salary £25,249-£27,319 Reference VH51477 Category Assistant staff Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/part-iii-co-ordinator-lf51370": {
      "title": "Part III Co-ordinator",
      "description": "Part III Co-ordinator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Part III Co-ordinator Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Part III Co-ordinator We are inviting applications for an enthusiastic and highly organised Part III Co-ordinator to lead on the coordination and delivery of the Part III programme. The role holder will be part of the Postgraduate Office team which supports all aspects of postgraduate education for the two Mathematics Departments. Part III is a world-famous postgraduate course which is taught primarily through an unparalleled number of lecture courses (~80) spanning all aspects of mathematics and theoretical physics. Students who are undergraduates at Cambridge may progress to Part III to work towards their MMath degree, additionally the Faculty admits postgraduate students from outside Cambridge onto the MASt programme. There is a total cohort of ~290 students on this programme each year. The Part III Co-ordinator will provide effective support for the maintenance and development of the Part III programme and will work in conjunction with the Director and Associate Director of Taught Postgraduate Education (DTPE + ADTPE) and supports Committee Chairs as required. The role holder will be responsible for the accurate and effective management of applicants and current students' data, ensuring that records are maintained consistently, securely and readily available to support operational and academic decision-making. Administration of the Part III programme currently sits across two teams within the Faculty's shared services, the Postgraduate Office and Undergraduate Office. This role acts as a bridge between these two offices and has a crucial role in ensuring effective communication and collaboration between the teams. The post is line managed by the Postgraduate Office Manager but in certain aspects of their work supervision will be provided by the Undergraduate Office Manager. In turn the role holder will be expected to contribute to the knowledge and experience of the team, and to provide supervision support for others as required. The successful candidate will be educated to degree level/level 6 vocational qualifications or equivalent level of practical experience and have experience of developing and reviewing administrative processes and systems. It is essential to have demonstratable experience and competence in all Microsoft Office packages, as well as excellent communication and interpersonal skills. The right candidate will have excellent organisational and time management skills, the ability to work efficiently with a high level of accuracy and attention to detail and demonstrate knowledge of administration involving a critical understanding of operational requirements ideally in an educational environment. You will have an ability to work flexibly and be supportive of colleagues. Informal inquiries can be made by contacting the Postgraduate Office Manager, Pilar Alonso on grad-administrator@maths.cam.ac.uk . If you have any queries about the application process, please email LF51370@maths.cam.ac.uk . Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. In the Suitability for the Role section, please describe how your skills and experience meet the selection criteria as described in the Further Particulars, providing specific and relevant examples to illustrate this. The full contact details of two professional referees (including email addresses) should also be included; please note that we will assume that you are happy for us to approach your referees at any stage unless you indicate otherwise in the space provided on the form. Interviews will take place as soon as possible following the closing date. Please quote reference LF51370 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information FP's Part III Co-ordinator_Assistant Staff_hr7_ Key information Department/location Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics Salary £34,630-£40,704 Reference LF51370 Category Assistant staff Date published 21 October 2026 Closing date 18 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/phd-studentship-with-the-leverhulme-trust-experimental-investigation-of-phase-change-mechanisms-in-nm51442": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship with the Leverhulme Trust",
      "description": "PhD Studentship with the Leverhulme Trust - Experimental Investigation of Phase Change Mechanisms in Aircraft Contrail Formation | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs PhD Studentship with the Leverhulme Trust  - Experimental Investigation of Phase Change Mechanisms in Aircraft Contrail Formation Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs PhD Studentship with the Leverhulme Trust  - Experimental Investigation of Phase Change Mechanisms in Aircraft Contrail Formation Contrail formation remains one of the least understood yet most climate-relevant impacts of aviation. Persistent contrails can evolve into cirrus-like clouds that significantly affect Earth's radiative balance, but the physical mechanisms governing their formation particularly in the near-field region immediately downstream of aircraft engines remain poorly characterised. This fully funded PhD studentship is part of a Leverhulme Trust Research Project entitled \"Bridging the scale gap: From non-equilibrium thermodynamics to contrails\". The project combines laboratory-scale experiments with high-fidelity numerical modelling to investigate vapour-liquid-solid phase transitions in aircraft exhaust plumes under controlled conditions, with particular relevance to emerging low-soot propulsion technologies such as sustainable aviation fuels and hydrogen. The PhD project focuses on the experimental investigation of early contrail formation, using a dedicated facility that generates contrail-relevant exhaust flows by injecting a high-speed moist jet into a controlled, cold environment. The student will contribute to the development and application of advanced optical and laser-based diagnostics to characterise condensation, freezing, and particle populations in the evolving plume.\nThe project includes close interaction with numerical simulations, enabling quantitative comparison between experiment and CFD and supporting physical interpretation of the measured processes. While the primary focus of the PhD is experimental, the student will gain experience working at the interface of experiment and high-fidelity modelling in a collaborative research environment. The 3-year studentship is fully funded for home students and includes tuition fees and a tax-free stipend at the UKRI rate. The expected start date is 1 October 2027. Applications should include a CV, academic transcripts, and a brief statement outlining the applicant's motivation and relevant background. Informal enquiries are welcome. Applicants should have (or expect to obtain by the start date) at least a good 2.1 degree in Engineering or a closely related subject. A strong interest in experimental fluid mechanics and thermodynamics is essential. The project involves multiphase and particle-laden flows relevant to contrail formation; prior experience in these areas is welcome but not required. Experience with laboratory instrumentation, data acquisition, or optical diagnostics is desirable but not essential. Familiarity with coding or data analysis (e.g. MATLAB, Python, LabVIEW) would be an advantage. Good written and spoken English communication skills are expected. Applications should be submitted via the University of Cambridge Applicant Portal https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/egegpdpeg/finance , with Dr Katharina Tegethoff identified as the potential supervisor. Applications may close early if the position is filled before the advertised date. Please note there is a £20 application fee attached to using the Cambridge Applicant Portal. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary Reference NM51442 Category Studentships Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 31 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postgraduate-administrator-temporary-cover-secondment-lc51429": {
      "title": "Postgraduate Administrator [Temporary Cover] (Secondment)",
      "description": "Postgraduate Administrator [Temporary Cover] (Secondment) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postgraduate Administrator [Temporary Cover] (Secondment) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postgraduate Administrator [Temporary Cover] (Secondment) The Department of Geography is seeking an experienced and dynamic administrator to coordinate its postgraduate course provision. This is a fixed-term vacancy to cover for a colleague on secondment, offering an exciting opportunity to join a small, friendly team and experience a key role supporting the work of the Department of Geography. The Postgraduate Administrator is the administrative lead for postgraduate matters in the Department, covering five MPhil (Masters) programmes and two PhD programmes. They are responsible for the management and coordination of the Postgraduate Office, working collaboratively with the dedicated MPhil course administrators, whilst being directly responsible for the administration of one research MPhil and two PhD programmes. They also work closely with the Director of Postgraduate Studies, providing advice and support to ensure that all of our postgraduate students progress through their pathway to successful completion of their courses. The Postgraduate Administrator is the administrative lead for postgraduate admissions; working closely with the Admissions Director, the role-holder must successfully administer the review of all applications and coordinate nominations for funding. Given the broad coverage of the discipline, which almost uniquely spans arts, humanities, social and physical sciences, the administrator will need to develop detailed knowledge and understanding of a wide range of student sponsors and Funding Councils, and the different working practices of multiple Doctoral Training Centres and Programmes. The Postgraduate Administrator is supported by the full-time Teaching Administrator, approximately half of whose time is allocated to postgraduate matters. Applicants should review the further particulars attached for more details on the role and its responsibilities. Fixed-term: 12 months. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions about the vacancy and/or the application process, please email Mrs Alessandra Uomo, HR Coordinator, at hr@geog.cam.ac.uk . Interviews are anticipated to take place on Friday, 20th November 2026. Please quote reference LC51429 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Geography Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference LC51429 Category Assistant staff Date published 22 October 2026 Closing date 11 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/programme-coordinator-postgraduate-education-nq51452": {
      "title": "Programme Coordinator (Postgraduate Education)",
      "description": "Programme Coordinator (Postgraduate Education) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Programme Coordinator (Postgraduate Education) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Programme Coordinator (Postgraduate Education) Purpose of the role \nTo co-ordinate the programme support systems and processes on behalf of the Department to ensure the smooth and efficient running of the course(s). The current departmental course portfolio includes: two PhD programmes (PhD in Chemical Engineering and PhD in Biotechnology), one Centre for Doctoral Training and one associated standalone MRes programme (in Sensor Technologies and Applications, one MPhil by Dissertation (MPhil in Chemical Engineering and Biotechnology) and two taught MPhil programmes (MPhil in Advanced Chemical Engineering and MPhil in Biotechnology). Key responsibilities \nDevelop and maintain office procedures, review processes and develop systems to improve efficiency. Establish systems and processes for effective administration of student records and information, co-ordinate and update course documentation, maintain and update course website as required. Provide course coordination and administration. Co-ordinate updating of handbooks, check materials for accuracy of style and content and make recommendations for improvements where necessary. Contribute to relevant sections of brochure, proofread publicity and other materials including the course handbooks. Develop and review assignment and exam processes. Collect and collate assignments, liaise with markers, assist with the operational management of examinations, prepare course materials needed by faculty staff. Deal with enquiries from current and prospective students regarding the course such as application process, funding and course requirements. Provide advice on procedures and University regulations to students. Process student applications and gather additional information where required, liaise with the university admissions office, input information into student information system and monitor the progress. Oversee financial administration. Process finances and invoices, course fee instalments, student and tutor expenses and claims, travel and accommodation, monitor and report income and expenditure.\nSupport research, teaching and learning. Work closely with course/ programme managers to contribute towards the organisation of research methods training and course sessions in support of teaching and learning activities and events. Act as secretary to committees, arrange and attend committee meetings. Create agenda, take and circulate minutes, and ensure follow up actions are implemented. Provide input/advice on issues related to the administration of the course/programme. Provide general course/programme administration such as book venues, prepare and distribute course material, book accommodation, travel and transport arrangements, provide administration support to Course/programme Director and other academics in the department. Gather, prepare and collate course information/ data e.g. course events and attendance, course feedback, student course activities for inclusion into reports written by others for internal and external stakeholders. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference NQ51452 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Chemical Engineering and Biotechnology Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference NQ51452 Category Assistant staff Date published 20 October 2026 Closing date 4 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/project-co-ordinator-fixed-term-ry51431": {
      "title": "Project Co-ordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Project Co-ordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Project Co-ordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Project Co-ordinator (Fixed Term) The Clinical School Computing Service (CSCS) is looking for an enthusiastic and organised Project Coordinator to provide project coordination support for IT infrastructure and software development projects within the department and the Clinical School. This includes management and oversight of all activities required to achieve the project scope and objectives, coordinating with teams which will be mostly internal or University department staff, as required. The role holder will be accountable to the Senior IT Project & Programme Manager for these projects and will ensure that different phases of the projects are delivered to the required standards of quality. This role will also project manage smaller departmental projects, under management and guidance of the Senior IT Project & Programme Manager. CSCS is one of the largest IT service providers at the University of Cambridge and supports over 3,000 users, mostly at the Cambridge Biomedical Campus. Project Management and support plays a key proactive role in the continuous and efficient delivery of computing services with a focus on IT infrastructure and software development projects. We are looking for a highly motivated and responsible individual with the following skills, abilities and experience: Educated to degree level or equivalent level of practical experience preferably IT related or project management. Experience in project coordination or project management role preferably in an IT context Excellent communication & interpersonal skills Excellent planning & organisational skills Strong research, analysis and reporting skills Good presentation skills Demonstrate advanced knowledge of project coordination or management involving a critical understanding and application of relevant project management methodologies. Experience of using Microsoft 365 Suite Experience of using Jira, Miro, Confluence or other tools in a project environment. This is a hybrid role which requires onsite presence at the School of Clinical Medicine for a minimum of 3 days a week. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 12 months in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RY51431 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars (RY51431) Key information Department/location Clinical School Computing Service Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference RY51431 Category Assistant staff Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 2 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/psychology-of-unethical-decisions-and-public-trust-fixed-term-pj51443": {
      "title": "Psychology of Unethical Decisions and Public Trust (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Psychology of Unethical Decisions and Public Trust (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Psychology of Unethical Decisions and Public Trust (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Psychology of Unethical Decisions and Public Trust (Fixed Term) The Cambridge ESRC Doctoral Training Partnership [CAM-DTP] is pleased to offer an knowledge exchange studentship available for admission in October 2027. The studentship will be co-supervised by Dr Jon Roozenbeek (Department of Psychology), Dr Lee de Wit (Department of Psychology) and Moira Nicolson (UK Cabinet Office) This studentship is part of the CAM-DTP's supervisor-led topics, and we note that the successful candidate for this position is not guaranteed to receive the studentship. Rather, it is part of a DTP-wide competition, with limited studentships being available. Project description: \nUnethical decision-making is as a major societal problem. For instance, the Grenfell and Hillsborough disasters in the UK were preceded by a chain of decision failures. While there is some literature on how and why people make unethical decisions, why people tolerate or even reward those who do is understudied. This cross-cultural project will explore the psychology of unethical decision-making: what drives it, and how the public perceives it, and its connection to trust in public and private institutions. ESRC Research Priority Areas: Security, risk and resilience, Understanding behaviour.\nResearch skills you must have if applying for this topic: Understanding of survey research, randomised controlled trials, and longitudinal (cohort) studies Ability to work with longitudinal \"big datasets\", e.g., social media data, both in terms of collection and analysis Research skills it would be helpful to have if applying for this topic: Knowledge of governmental accountability frameworks (especially in the UK) Knowledge of politics in non-Western contexts (e.g., India, China, Russia) Familiarity with multiple languages About the studentship An ESRC-funded PhD studentship through CAM-DTP supports 3.5 - 4.5 years of full-time study. We particularly encourage applications from students wishing to study on a part-time basis (minimum 50% of full-time). The studentship provides: Tuition fees up to the value of the national UKRI rate for Home fees; An annual tax-free stipend at the UKRI rate (£21,805 in 2026-27 for full time students); Financial support for research training up to £900 available from the Department; A personalised training programme, to develop research, communication, employability and personal skills; Support to carry out a required three-month placement, known as an Innovation Fellowship, with an academic or non-academic partner during the course of the studentship. Successful candidates will be required to submit their PhD within the duration of the studentship award. Studentships will start on 1 October each year for both full-time and part-time applicants. International Students\nInternational students receive tuition fees up to the value of the national UKRI rate for Home fees. Students will need additional scholarships, from alternative sources, to fund the remainder of their overseas fees. If an offer of admission is made, students should be aware that they will need to cover the costs of immigration into the UK visa and health surcharge (around £3.5k) before the studentship starts. The award does not cover these costs. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 30 September 2031 in the first instance. You can find out more about the Cambridge ESRC DTP at: https://www.cam-dtp.ac.uk/ You can find out more about the Department of Psychology at https://www.psychol.cam.ac.uk/ Please address any questions about this studentship to Dr Jon Roozenbeek at jjr51@cam.ac.uk or Dr Lee de Wit lhd26@cam.ac.uk Applications for this studentship should be made to the Department of Psychology. The course code is BLPC22 (PhD in Psychology). https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/application-process/how-do-i-apply The closing date for applications will be 8 December 2026 With your application you will be required to submit (i) a draft research proposal outlining your suitability, why you are interested in pursuing a PhD in this area, your background and research interests, familiarity with the questions raised by this research. (ii) your CV (iii) copies of your academic transcripts (iv) details of two academic referees. Please quote reference PJ51443 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Psychology Salary Reference PJ51443 Category Studentships Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 8 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/receptionist-part-time-pr51423": {
      "title": "Receptionist (Part Time)",
      "description": "Receptionist (Part Time) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Receptionist (Part Time) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Receptionist (Part Time) An exciting opportunity has arisen for a friendly, organised and enthusiastic afternoon Receptionist to join our busy research institute. Alongside providing reception cover, the successful candidate will undertake a range of administrative duties, helping to ensure the smooth and efficient running of the Institute. We are looking for a proactive and flexible team player who enjoys working with people, takes pride in delivering excellent customer service and is keen to learn and develop new skills. You will join a welcoming team of highly skilled professional services colleagues in a dynamic and supportive environment, where you can gain valuable experience and make a real contribution to the Institute. As the first point of contact for the Institute, you will play an important role in creating a positive and professional first impression. You will be approachable, confident and courteous, with excellent communication and interpersonal skills and the ability to deal effectively with a wide range of people. The successful candidate will demonstrate: -Excellent customer service skills and a friendly, professional approach. -Strong organisational skills and a keen eye for detail. -The ability to remain calm, patient and tactful under pressure. -The ability to multitask, manage competing priorities and work efficiently. -Excellent written and verbal communication skills. -Confidence and professionalism when interacting with a wide range of people. -Good IT and general computing skills. The working hours are 12:45 pm to 5:00 pm, Monday to Friday (21.25 hours per week). Some flexibility to provide cover for the morning receptionist, whose hours are 8:00 am to 12:45 pm, during periods of annual leave would be welcomed. This is a permanent, part-time position, available for an immediate start. If you are enthusiastic about providing an excellent reception service, enjoy being part of a team and would welcome the opportunity to work in a friendly research environment, we would love to hear from you. For any questions about the vacancy or the application process, please contact the HR team at hr@gurdon.cam.ac.uk . Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference PR51423 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further particulars Key information Department/location Gurdon Institute Salary £25,249-£27,319 pro rata Reference PR51423 Category Assistant staff Date published 20 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistant-fixed-term-pf51406": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) The Cambridge Centre for Carbon Credits (4C) develops scientific methods to assess the climate and biodiversity benefits of conservation and direct climate finance towards effective action to protect nature. Our work combines ecological research, quantitative analysis and digital tools to improve the evidence underpinning carbon markets. We are seeking a technically capable graduate with strong analytical and programming skills and an ability to communicate clearly. This is an opportunity to contribute to applied research at the interface of conservation science, climate change and carbon markets. Working closely with the Scientific Lead, Dr Tom Swinfield, you will support the development and application of 4C's analytical methods for evaluating nature-based climate and biodiversity benefits. Your focus will be on preparing and analysing data, developing and testing code, and interpreting and communicating results. You will also support Haven - an initiative to strengthen the scientific integrity of forest carbon credits - by coordinating scientific input with our industry partners and the Haven Scientific Council to support the adoption of best practice methods. Finally, you will contribute to our external communications, by producing blog posts and other accessible accounts of our research. We are looking for someone who enjoys working with data and can explain scientific findings clearly, including their uncertainties and limitations. Key Responsibilities: Support the development, testing and application of analytical methods to evaluate the climate and biodiversity benefits of forest conservation. Prepare, manage and analyse environmental datasets, including spatial data, using R and/or Python, using well-documented and reproducible code and workflows. Support the ongoing work of the Haven Scientific Council through targeted evidence reviews, briefing materials and coordination of scientific feedback. Contribute to external communications, including blog posts and research updates, translating scientific findings into clear and accurate material for different audiences. Collaborate with researchers and partner organisations to support the delivery of 4C's research and evaluations. For more information, please refer to the Further Particulars document. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for up to 6 months. Preferred start date for the role is December 2026. The closing date for applications is midnight (GMT) on 8 November 2026. The interview for this role is expected to take place during the week commencing Monday, 23 November 2026 (subject to change) and may be conducted remotely in exceptional circumstances. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Flexible working requests (such as part-time) will be considered. The postholder will be based in the David Attenborough Building in Cambridge and will be expected to work on site at least three days per week. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact Dr Tom Swinfield [email: tws36@cam.ac.uk ]. For any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Zoology HR Office [email: hr@zoo.cam.ac.uk ] Please quote reference PF51406 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Research Assistant - Further Particulars - PF51406 Key information Department/location Department of Zoology Salary £33,002-£35,608 Reference PF51406 Category Research Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistant-fixed-term-pl51439": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a researcher position in the laboratory of Dr Catherine Lindon at the Department of Pharmacology, University of Cambridge. The successful candidate will join a team investigating the mode of action of novel tools for Targeted Protein Degradation (TPD). Small molecule PROTACs harnessing the cellular ubiquitination machinery to destroy targets of clinical importance are at the forefront of a revolution in therapeutic strategies, yet many parameters of the relevant ubiquitination pathways are not well understood. Suitably experienced candidates with an Bachelors degree in a relevant science subject area will be considered for appointment on the Research Assistant scale. All candidates should be careful and methodical workers, able to work independently or as part of a team. They should be self-motivated and able to organise, perform and analyse experiments with minimal instruction. Key duties/responsibilities will include: conducting the research project alongside other team members, updating knowledge and understanding in the research field, managing resources and activities in the lab, contributing to supervision of junior researchers in the lab. The funds to begin in this post are available from 9th December 2026. Successful candidates will be appointed as Research Assistant at Grade 5 (£33,002 - £35,608), for a fixed-term period of 5 months. The online application is to include your personal details, names and addresses of referees, as well as your CV, research publications list and cover letter. Interviews will take place in November. The School of Biological Sciences holds an Athena SWAN bronze award, demonstrating a commitment to provide equal opportunities and to advance the representation of women in science subjects. Appointment will be based on merit alone. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries about the position can be made to Dr Catherine Lindon ( acl34@cam.ac.uk ) For enquiries about the online application process, please contact the HR team by emailing hr@phar.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference PL51439 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further particulars Key information Department/location Department of Pharmacology Salary £33,002-£35,608 Reference PL51439 Category Research Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 30 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistant-fixed-term-lj51458": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a Research Assistant in the Materials Theory Group, Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy. The ERC-funded position is with Prof. Stefano Racioppi (PI) on his \"Quantum Theory of the Periodic Tables Under Extreme Conditions (QUANTEX) project. We are looking for motivated and enthusiastic applicants interested in theoretical chemistry, condensed matter physics and computational materials science. The successful candidate will join Prof. Racioppi's group to investigate how extreme pressure changes the electronic structure, chemical bonding, and properties of elements and compounds, challenging familiar chemical rules. QUANTEX aims to understand these changes and develop a theoretical framework to describe chemical periodicities under compression and guide the prediction of new materials. The appointee will also join a thriving community of computational materials scientists within the Materials Theory Group and the wider Lennard-Jones Centre at Cambridge. The role will combine first-principles calculations, largely based on density functional theory (DFT) with: crystal structure prediction and machine-learning methods; chemical bonding analysis, including the quantum theory of atoms in molecules (QTAIM); energy decomposition analysis (EDA), and analysis of electronegativities. Potential research directions include pressure-induced changes in electronic structure (e.g. core-valence boundaries),chemical periodicity (e.g. electronegativities and oxidation states), the formation of electride and superconducting states, and machine-learning accelerated materials discovery. Applicants should have (or expect to be awarded) a good UK Master's degree (or overseas equivalent) in a relevant science subject (Materials Science, Chemistry, Physics) and meet the University's academic and English-language entry requirements for their doctoral programme. A strong interest in theoretical and solid-state chemistry is essential. Previous experience in programming, machine learning, electronic-structure calculations, or atomistic modelling whilst advantageous, is not essential. Applicants should have good analytical and communication skills, intellectual curiosity, and ability to work both independently and collaboratively. They will receive training in computational methods for solid-state chemistry and physics, have access to high-performance computing facilities, and benefit from interactions with local and international theoretical and experimental collaborators.\nApplicants can be of any nationality and should be enrolled in a doctoral programme at Cambridge during the project. Informal enquiries should be directed to Prof. Stefano Racioppi, sr2200@cam.ac.uk . Interviews will be held online: 1 March 2027.\nExpected start date: 1 October 2027. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 42 months in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Additionally, PhD applications should be submitted separately via the University Application Portal: www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/apply ,  identifying Prof. Stefano Racioppi as the proposed supervisor with QUANTEX as the project of interest. Applications must include: (i) A short research statement/cover letter describing the applicant's past research, future goals, and why the applicant is interested and suitable for this position. (ii). A curriculum vitae, including publications. (iii). The contact details of two referees who can provide a letter of recommendation. Please quote reference LJ51458 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Key information Department/location Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy Salary £33,002-£35,608 Reference LJ51458 Category Research Date published 21 October 2026 Closing date 31 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistant-associate-x2-go51404": {
      "title": "Research Assistant / Associate x2",
      "description": "Research Assistant / Associate x2 | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant / Associate x2 Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant / Associate x2 The Leverhulme Centre for the Future of Intelligence (CFI), with the Institute for Technology and Humanity (ITH) at the University of Cambridge invites applications for two Research Assistant / Associates. The appointment will be full-time, for 24 months in the first instance (ideal start date 1st of December, or soon after that). The successful candidates will work on a project focused on establishing \"red lines\" for AI using cognitive capability and propensity profiles. The work will focus on AI Evaluation and will be drawing from multiple disciplines, but primarily from artificial intelligence, technical AI safety, Bayesian statistics and causal modelling, psychology and AI governance. Appointment at the Research Associate level is dependent on the award of a PhD. This is a postdoctoral-level appointment, those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at the Research Assistant -Grade 5, which will be amended to Research Associate-Grade 7 once the award of the PhD has been conferred. CFI is a highly interdisciplinary research centre addressing the challenges and opportunities posed by artificial intelligence (AI). Based at the University of Cambridge, the Centre has close links with industry, policymakers, and many academic disciplines. In order for your application to be considered, please upload the following: Curriculum Vitae (CV) A Covering Letter of no more than 1,000 words. This should detail relevant experience and interests with particular regard to this project The names and contact details of two referees who are familiar with your work. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and should be directed to Professor Jose Hernandez-Orallo at jh2135@cam.ac.uk . For questions on the application process, please contact the School's HR Team on sahhr@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is midnight (GMT) on Sunday 1st of November 2026. Interviews are planned for the second week of November, subject to change. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference GO51404 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Cambridge Institute for Technology and Humanity Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference GO51404 Category Research Date published 16 October 2026 Closing date 1 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
