    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantassociate-in-specification-testing-fixed-term-nr51437": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate in Specification Testing (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Associate in Specification Testing (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Specification Testing (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Specification Testing (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 30 September 2027. The Department of Computer Science and Technology is an academic department that encompasses computer science along with many aspects of engineering, technology and mathematics. We have a worldwide reputation for academic research with consistent top research ratings. The Department has an open and collaborative culture, supporting revolutionary fundamental computer science research, strong cross-cutting collaborations internally and externally, and ideas which transform computing outside the University. Please follow the link at: https://www.cst.cam.ac.uk to find out more about our department. We are seeking a Research Assistant/Associate in the general area of specification testing to join the team led by Professors Peter Sewell and Neel Krishnaswami. The general aims of the project are to advance the use of runtime testing of separation-logic specifications for systems C code, further developing the Fulminate tooling for CN runtime testing.  For this specific post we are seeking candidates with experience in separation-logic testing for C. The position is available for 1 year. Flexible and part-time working can be considered. Requirements: Candidates should have been awarded a PhD (or be very close to submission of one), or relevant experience and have a strong publication record in separation-logic testing.  The candidate should also have good programming skills. Good communication, presentation and management skills are also desirable given the size of the project team. Appointment at the Research Associate level is dependent on the award of a PhD. This is a postdoctoral-level appointment; those who have submitted (or expect soon to have submitted) but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at the Research Assistant -Grade 5, which will be amended to Research Associate-Grade 7 once the award of the PhD has been conferred. Applicants should contact Prof Peter Sewell for further information: https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~pes20/ In addition to the base salary stated above, the successful candidate for this post will receive an additional 2.5% supplement to their pay. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Required documents include a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, and contact information for two references. If you upload any additional documents, which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please quote reference NR51437 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary £34,610-£35,608 £37,694-£46,049 Reference NR51437 Category Research Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 28 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-jk51444": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) This is a full time, fixed term position for 18 months, 1 December 2026 to End date: 31 May 2028 Salary - £37,694 This opportunity is funded through Dr Theil's AHRC grant. Dr Stefan Theil is looking for up a postdoctoral Research Associate for his AHRC funded project The legal impact of UK court decisions, parliamentary deliberations and administrative policymaking on outcomes at the European Court of Human Rights. The project aims to identify to what extent UK court decisions, parliamentary deliberations and administrative policymaking are taken into account by the European Court of Human Rights and whether they have any discernible impact. The research involves doctrinal analysis of a considerable body of court decisions from the ECHR and the UK as well as recording findings in a comprehensive dataset. The Research Associate will be line-managed by Dr Stefan Theil. Person specification: Essential · An undergraduate degree in law or closely related subject · A PhD in law or closely related subject (either completed or nearing completion at the time of application) · A strong understanding of UK public law and familiarity with the European Convention on Human Rights · Experience with legal referencing systems and a track record of independent research commensurate with career level · Experience drafting, editing and revising research documents in MS Word · Attention to detail and ability to manage workload independently Desirable · Experience extracting doctrinal datapoints from court decisions · Experience with social science research methods · Experience recording, editing and maintaining datasets in MS Excel Your normal place of work will be the Faculty of Law, University of Cambridge. Eligibility This opportunity is open to any resident of the United Kingdom subject to a successful right to work check. The closing date for applications is midnight (GMT) on 16 November 2026, and interviews are expected to take place shortly after. Please use the subject line: Research Associate Application and explain clearly how you meet the requirements for the role. The Faculty of Law reserves to right to not make any appointment. Informal enquiries about the project may be addressed to Dr Stefan Theil ( st608@cam.ac.uk ). Enquiries on the recruitment process should be emailed to hr@law.cam.ac.uk Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. As part of your application, please submit a CV (2 pages max), a cover letter (maximum 800 words), and a recent writing sample (maximum 10,000 words) as well as the contact details of two referees. Please quote reference JK51444 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Key information Department/location Faculty of Law Salary £37,694 Reference JK51444 Category Research Date published 20 October 2026 Closing date 16 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-ps51462": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) The UK Hub for Predictive and AI-Enabled Human Disease Models is a UK national research initiative funded by the Medical Research Council (MRC), which aims to translate academic New Approach Methodologies (NAMs) into validated, industry-ready systems. The Hub brings together leading scientists, clinicians and commercial organisations to accelerate the development, validation and adoption of predictive human disease models for biomedical research, drug discovery and experimental medicine. The Hub is based at the Cambridge Stem Cell Institute (CSCI), University of Cambridge. The successful candidate will join the research group led by Professor Matthias Zilbauer at the Cambridge Stem Cell Institute. The group specialises in human intestinal stem cell biology, organoid technology and the study of intestinal health and disease, with a particular focus on epithelial biology and epigenetic regulation. We are seeking an enthusiastic and motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate to contribute to the development, optimisation and validation of human intestinal organoid models as part of this ambitious national programme. The postholder will play a key role in expanding the human intestinal organoid biobank, contributing to the Hub's target of establishing an additional 2,000 organoid lines. They will develop and optimise organoid culture systems, support the introduction of automated culture workflows and undertake molecular and epigenetic profiling, including DNA methylation profiling, spatial transcriptomics and other multi-omics approaches. The role will involve independent and collaborative research, including the development of functional assays and application of molecular and cellular techniques to characterise intestinal organoid models. The postholder will work closely with researchers, clinicians and external partners to support the development of robust, reproducible and predictive human disease models. The successful candidate will have: A PhD in Stem Cell Biology, Molecular Biology, Cell Biology or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated hands-on experience in establishing, maintaining, expanding and cryopreserving human intestinal organoid cultures. Experience with molecular biology techniques and molecular profiling approaches, including DNA methylation, transcriptomics or related multi-omics methods. Experience with CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing or related genetic manipulation techniques. Basic knowledge or experience of bioinformatic analysis. Strong organisational and communication skills, with the ability to undertake independent research and work collaboratively. Experience with advanced imaging, flow cytometry, organoid-derived monolayers or automated cell culture workflows would be advantageous. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a major national research initiative, working at the interface of human stem cell biology, disease modelling and translational research. The position offers opportunities to develop new experimental approaches, work with academic and industry partners, and contribute to research with applications in biomedical science and drug discovery. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD, an equivalent research doctorate. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at Research Assistant level (Grade 5), which will be amended to Research Associate once PhD has been awarded. Full-time: 1.0 FTE Fixed-term until 31/03/2030 Informal enquiries: Professor Matthias Zilbauer - mz304@medschl.cam.ac.uk To apply online for this vacancy and view further information about the role, please use the University of Cambridge recruitment website. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Applicants must have (or be close to obtaining) a PhD. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will initially be appointed as a Research Assistant (Grade 5, Point 38 £34,132) moving to Research Associate (Grade 7) upon confirmation of your PhD award. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference PS51462 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars (PS51462) Key information Department/location Cambridge Stem Cell Institute (SBS) Salary £34,610-£35,608 £37,694-£46,049 Reference PS51462 Category Research Date published 21 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-health-psychologist-fixed-term-sj51496": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Health Psychologist) (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Health Psychologist) (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Health Psychologist) (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Health Psychologist) (Fixed Term) We are seeking an enthusiastic and motivated postdoctoral research associate with training and experience evaluating and delivering health behaviour change interventions and an interest in the psychology of obesity and weight management. You will take a lead role in a multi-disciplinary research programme to evaluate an acceptance-based guided self-help intervention to support weight loss maintenance. It builds on a successful feasibility study of the Supporting Weight Management (SWiM) intervention (DOI:10.1002/osp4.70048)  and will further refine, adapt and evaluate this intervention to provide evidence needed for implementation within the NHS. This is a 5-year research programme led by Prof Amy Ahern and funded by NIHR Programme Grants for Applied Research (NIHR206801). We have successfully completed the first 2 years of the programme and this is a 3-year fixed term position. You will have responsibility for leading the mixed methods process evaluation of a large national randomised controlled trial (N=1840) and supporting the adaptation of the SWiM intervention for people who have received bariatric surgery or taken obesity medications. You will also train and supervise the health coaches employed to deliver the \"guide\" element of guided self-help during the trial. The successful candidate will have a PhD or equivalent qualification in psychology or behavioural science and demonstrable experience in undertaking high quality qualitative and mixed methods research. Applicants should have excellent written and verbal communication skills, and the ability to build and maintain excellent working relationships with collaborators, stakeholders and team members. This is an NIHR career capacity building position that is intended to increase capacity in interdisciplinary research and implementation science for practitioner psychologists who have an interest and experience in obesity. It would be an ideal position for a qualified (or near qualified) Health Psychologist or someone with similar training and experience. Substantial support will be provided for professional development in research and practice, with a bespoke professional development plan based on your skills, experience and career aspirations. You will become a member of the NIHR Academy and will have access to associated training and mentorship opportunities. The appointment will be made on a fixed-term basis for a period of 3 years in order to undertake a specific project of finite duration. A timely start is desired and candidates should indicate their earliest potential start date on application. Please contact Prof Amy Ahern ( Amy.Ahern@ims.cam.ac.uk ) for informal enquiries. The closing date is Tuesday 17th November 2026 and interviews will likely be held 25th ¿ 27th November 2026. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 October 2029 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Applicants must have (or be close to obtaining) a PhD. \nAppointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will initially be appointed as a Research Assistant (Grade 5, Point 38 £34,610) moving to Research Associate (Grade 7) upon confirmation of your PhD award.\nPlease ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application.\nPlease include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference SJ51496 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Clinical Biochemistry (Institute of Metabolic Science Epidemiology) Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference SJ51496 Category Research Date published 23 October 2026 Closing date 17 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-honorary-resident-doctor-fixed-term-cardiovascular-imaging-rc51491": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Fixed Term) - Cardiovascular Imaging | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Fixed Term) - Cardiovascular Imaging Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Fixed Term) - Cardiovascular Imaging Start date: 1st April 2027. A clinical research associate position is available working on a collaborative project to develop diagnostic and prognostic tools for coronary artery disease, as part of a multi-disciplinary team led by Professor Martin Bennett at the Department of Medicine, University of Cambridge, where the position will be based. Cardiovascular disease is the largest cause of death worldwide, and new diagnostics and treatments are needed, particularly in those at highest risk. In collaboration with the Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics, Centre for Mathematics Sciences, Cambridge, we have developed an AI-based machine learning algorithm to analyse intracoronary images and identify high-risk patients and assess the efficacy of drugs to prevent or treat disease. The clinical research associate will work within a growing team of clinical and non-clinical scientists to further develop this and other tools for clinical use, focusing on image co-registration, segmentation, and post-processing to generate outputs suitable for clinical decision making. We are particularly looking for candidates with experience in invasive coronary image processing for real-world problems, and experience with relevant image analysis software would be very advantageous. Further information on the project from Professor Martin Bennett (mrb24@ cam.ac.uk). Duties include collaborative research objectives and defining individual project work streams. Working with the PI, the role holder will be expected to plan and manage their own research and administration, with guidance if required, and to assist in the preparation of proposals and applications to external bodies. You must be able to communicate material of a technical nature and be able to build internal and external contacts. Fixed-term: This post has funding for up to 2 years in the first instance, which may be extendable to 3 years. There may be the opportunity for the successful candidate to pursue a PhD during their employment, subject to approval. This appointment requires an Honorary Clinical Contract. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. This is an integrated appointment between the University and Cambridge University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust (\"NHS Trust\").  You will therefore also be issued with an Honorary Clinical Contract by the NHS Trust. You should be aware that if your contract with the NHS Trust is terminated your employment with the University will be terminated, and vice versa.\nPlease ensure that you upload a covering letter, CV and a completed Additional Information for Clinical Researchers form in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application.\nPlease include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RC51491 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Additional Information Form Key information Department/location Department of Medicine Salary £40,190-£76,582 Reference RC51491 Category Research Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-honorary-resident-doctor-part-time-fixed-term-rn51515": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Part Time, Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Part Time, Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Part Time, Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Honorary Resident Doctor) (Part Time, Fixed Term) We are seeking a medically qualified Clinical Research Fellow to join the Cambridge BioMind Laboratory (Dr Mary-Ellen Lynall, Department of Psychiatry) and the Prediction and Learning Laboratory (Professor Rebecca Lawson, Department of Psychology). Funded by a major Wellcome award, the post offers hands-on clinical experience in experimental medicine alongside training in computational psychiatry and multi-omics, with a clear route to a PhD, subject to admission. 22.2 to 25.9 hours per week  (3 to 3.5 days/week), allowing time for continued clinical work. Salary will be pro rata according to the NHS scale for resident doctors (FTE will depend on pay point). The postholder will contribute to an ambitious experimental medicine programme investigating the biological mechanisms of uncertainty processing and the stress response, and the relevance of these mechanisms to anxiety and mental health. They will play a central role in delivering an ethically approved, multi-arm study examining how uncertainty processing and stress responses are affected by uncertainty awareness training and by commonly used SSRI and SNRI medications. The study combines detailed cognitive measures with physiological assessments, blood sampling and high-dimensional omics profiling. The postholder will provide medical support for the study and work closely with the wider research team to ensure that participant screening, study visits, pharmacological procedures and safety monitoring are conducted to a consistently high standard. Depending on their interests and experience, there will also be substantial scope to contribute to the analysis of cognitive, physiological and omics data and to help shape the scientific direction of the project. For further details, please read the further particulars, please provide at least 3 references, a CV and covering letter indicating how you meet the criteria for this post. Informal enquiries are encouraged and may be directed to Dr Mary-Ellen Lynall, Principal Investigator mel41@cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact Simon Gurry, HR Coordinator( hradminpsychiatry@medschl.cam.ac.uk ). Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a Disclosure and Barring Service check (enhanced with children and adults' barred lists) and a health assessment. This appointment also requires an Honorary Clinical Contract. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. This is an integrated appointment between the University and Cambridge University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust (\"NHS Trust\"). You will therefore also be issued with an Honorary Clinical Contract by the NHS Trust. You should be aware that if your contract with the NHS Trust is terminated your employment with the University will be terminated, and vice versa.\nPlease ensure that you upload a covering letter, CV and a completed Additional Information for Clinical Researchers form in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Deadline for applications is  9th November 2026, with interviews TBC. Please quote reference RN51515 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information File Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Psychiatry Salary £40,190-£76,582 pro rata Reference RN51515 Category Research Date published 27 October 2026 Closing date 9 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-digital-assets-fixed-term-ns51510": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate - Digital Assets (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate - Digital Assets (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate - Digital Assets (Fixed Term) The Cambridge Centre for Alternative Finance (CCAF), based at Cambridge Judge Business School, is a global research centre focused on addressing information gaps in alternative finance, digital financial services, and emerging financial technologies. We are now seeking a Research Associate in Digital Assets to contribute to rigorous, policy-relevant research on blockchain-based financial systems. About the role: This pivotal role will be responsible for investigating the functioning, stability and risks of digital assets and decentralised finance (DeFi), including asset tokenisation, smart contracts and interconnected protocols. You will develop and apply quantitative and computational methods, analyse blockchain data, and contribute to research tools such as the Cambridge DeFi Navigator. Working with the Principal Investigator and research team, you will produce academic publications, research reports and policy-oriented outputs. You will explain technical findings to regulators, policymakers and industry audiences, and contribute to collaborative projects, funding proposals and knowledge exchange. About you: To be successful in this role, you will have: *A PhD in Computer Science, Finance, Economics or a closely related quantitative discipline relevant to the research. *Research experience in digital assets, DeFi, blockchain-based financial systems or a closely related field, with an understanding of blockchain technology, smart contracts and DeFi protocols. *Experience using quantitative or computational research methods, supported by appropriate programming and data analysis skills. *The ability to evaluate research critically and produce clear outputs, explaining complex findings to specialist and non-specialist audiences. *The ability to work collaboratively, manage your own research activities and meet agreed deadlines. This is a fantastic opportunity to play a central role in supporting research that informs global policy and practice, while developing your career within a world-leading academic institution. Why work for Cambridge Judge Business School? We are the business school of the University of Cambridge, advancing knowledge and leadership through people who leave a mark on the world.  Cambridge Judge Business School leverages the power of academia for real world impact to transform individuals, organisations and society. Since 1990, we have forged a reputation as a centre of rigorous thinking and high-impact transformative education, situated within one of the world's most prestigious research universities, and in the heart of the Cambridge Cluster, the most successful technology entrepreneurship cluster in Europe. Benefits of working for Cambridge Judge Business School: You'll receive excellent benefits at Cambridge Judge Business School including: *Generous holiday allowance *Defined benefits pension schemes *Flexible working options *Family-friendly initiatives *Career development opportunities *Support for health and mental wellbeing *Discounts on shopping *Tax-efficient bicycle and charity-giving schemes The role is available for 12 months in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal queries may be directed to hrsupport@jbs.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference NS51510 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information FPs Key information Department/location Judge Business School Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference NS51510 Category Research Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 10 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-molecular-virology-and-viral-emergence-fixed-term-rc51503": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate - Molecular Virology and Viral Emergence (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate - Molecular Virology and Viral Emergence (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate - Molecular Virology and Viral Emergence (Fixed Term) We are seeking an enthusiastic and motivated researcher to join the laboratory of Dr Suzannah Rihn at the Cambridge Institute of Therapeutic Immunology and Infectious Disease (CITIID), University of Cambridge. Emerging and re-emerging viruses pose a continuing threat to human health. Understanding the molecular mechanisms that enable viruses to cross species barriers, adapt to new hosts and establish infections in humans is essential for improving our preparedness for future epidemics and pandemics. Our research uses a combination of molecular virology, cell biology and experimental models of virus infection to investigate the interactions between viruses and their hosts that determine the potential for viral emergence. The successful candidate will contribute to an exciting research programme investigating the molecular determinants of viral cross-species transmission. Working as part of a collaborative research team, they will use a range of experimental approaches to investigate how viral and host factors influence infection, replication and adaptation. The project will provide opportunities to develop expertise in molecular virology, including cell culture, molecular cloning, viral reverse genetics and functional assays of virus infection. The postholder will be expected to plan and conduct experiments, analyse and interpret data, maintain accurate research records and contribute to the preparation of scientific manuscripts and presentations. They will also participate in laboratory meetings, contribute to the wider scientific activities of the group and collaborate with colleagues within CITIID and beyond. Applicants should have, or be close to obtaining, a PhD in biological, biochemical or biomedical sciences, with relevant experience in molecular biology or virology. We are particularly interested in candidates with experience studying virus infection, a strong background in experimental molecular virology and an emerging track record of scientific publications or presentations. Experience with viral reverse genetics, the handling of replication-competent viruses or small animal models of infection would be advantageous but is not essential. The successful candidate will be highly motivated, scientifically curious and capable of working both independently and collaboratively. Excellent communication and organisational skills, together with a commitment to rigorous and reproducible research, are essential. This position offers an opportunity to contribute to research addressing fundamental questions in viral emergence and pandemic preparedness, within the collaborative and interdisciplinary research environment at CITIID. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Applicants must have (or be close to obtaining) a PhD. \nAppointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will initially be appointed as a Research Assistant (Grade 5, Point 38 £34,610) moving to Research Associate (Grade 7) upon confirmation of your PhD award.\nPlease ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application.\nPlease include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RC51503 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particular Key information Department/location Department of Medicine Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference RC51503 Category Research Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-contracts-specialist-ew49288": {
      "title": "Research Contracts Specialist",
      "description": "Research Contracts Specialist | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Contracts Specialist Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Contracts Specialist Multiple posts are available. We are seeking a number of enthusiastic and motivated self-starters to join the Research Office as Research Contracts Specialists.  Research Contract Specialists negotiate research contracts on behalf of the University and our researchers. As a Research Contracts Specialist, you will manage a portfolio of varied research contracts, including material transfers, data access, studentships, research collaborations, large consortia, industry collaborations. You will\nneed to take into consideration the terms of our varied funders, ranging from Research Councils, UK Government Ministries, the European Commission, charities, overseas organisations and a wide range of industry. You will have to understand, interpret, review, draft and negotiate contract terms in an often complex context and to coordinate several parties in order to finalise an agreement. You will have a pragmatic approach to drafting terms that facilitate the partnership between the University's researchers and external collaborators, while strategically assessing and managing risk to the University. To do this well, you will need to liaise and interact with researchers to ensure agreements are appropriate to the research context. The role will offer the successful applicant the opportunity to:\n1.Be a member of an experienced contracts team\n2.Work within one of the most dynamic and varied research centres in the world;\n3.Represent the University in negotiations with a wide and varied range of funders and collaborators;\n4.Work with and foster close relationships with researchers who are at the forefront of scientific advancement;\n5.Develop negotiation and drafting skills to respond effectively to complex, and often ground-breaking, research\ncollaborations;\n6.Develop a clear understanding of contractual aspects important in research, such as intellectual property and publication, within a world-renowned institution. You will have good understanding of research-related collaborations and contracts. You will ideally have knowledge of intellectual property principles, preferably gained through experience of working within research environments such as a university or a research-intensive organisation, including industry. You must be well\norganised and able both to prioritise and work to deadlines. You must be able to work well independently and as part of a team and have excellent verbal and written communication skills. Diplomacy, tact, attention to detail and a calm approach are essential. You will be proficient in using Microsoft IT packages. We aim to support hybrid working, whilst retaining the positive aspects of our unique in-person environment and culture. Elements of this role are suitable for hybrid working, where staff can split their time between in-person collaboration and remote working. This role requires onsite attendance in Cambridge for a minimum of two\ndays per week. This arrangement is subject to change and will not form a part of the contractual terms of the role. Please note this role is full-time only. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please see the Further Particulars document for more details of this vacancy. Your application should clearly set out how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification (the skills and experience required for the role), along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted based on this criteria. Please include details of your referees, including email address, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please note: If you have applied for this role within the last three months, we kindly ask that you do not reapply at this time. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: \nAlice McKay Hill at alice.mckayhill@admin.cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact:\nCambridge Research Office Recruitment at: crorecruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference EW49288 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cambridge Research Office Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference EW49288 Category Academic-related Date published 22 October 2026 Closing date 15 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-institute-administrator-fixed-term-ve51333": {
      "title": "Research Institute Administrator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Institute Administrator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Institute Administrator (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Institute Administrator (Fixed Term) Ever wanted to work in an institution that is home to one of the world's great collections of cultural treasures and research materials? Spanning thousands of years of human thought and discovery, the University of Cambridge Libraries and Archives house unique treasures and research materials made up of nearly ten million books, maps, manuscripts, photographs and priceless objects. The role The Research Institute Administrator will play a vital role in supporting and developing the Libraries and Archives' vibrant research community. They will coordinate the administration of the University Library Research Institute (ULRI), a centre for collections-led research, by contributing to its portfolio of research projects, events programme and general running of the Institute. This is a fixed-term post in the ULRI team, funded for one year in the first instance, with the intention to extend subject to funding. As Research Institute Administrator you will: Coordinate administrative processes for research grant applications, including monitoring milestones and preparing costings. Track the progress of awarded grants, including budget management and maintaining project management records. Provide support for the logistics and delivery of the ULRI's research events programme. Coordinate recruitment and onboarding processes for visiting scholars, fellows and embedded PhD students. Lead on office administration for the ULRI, including answering enquiries, organising meetings and supporting research communications. What we are looking for A keen interest in research libraries and collections-led research. Excellent attention to detail and organisational skills. Demonstrable budget management skills. Ability to work collaboratively as part of a team. Experience in an administrative role, and of supporting events and working to deadlines. What we offer Flexible and hybrid working opportunities such as part-time hours, job shares, and staggered hours. Generous annual leave allowance of 36 days. Exceptional parental leave (including maternity, paternity / co-parent, adoption and shared parental leave). Discounts on major retailers. Professional development opportunities such as apprenticeships and access to a variety of training courses. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. We particularly welcome applications from candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries please contact Eleanor Parmenter, Research Engagement Facilitator at researchdevelopment@lib.cam.ac.uk Application closing date: Sunday 8 November 2026.  Interview date: Tuesday 24 November 2026 For further details please refer to the Further Particulars. \nIn applications, we advise you to state clearly how your skills and experience meet the criteria listed in the Person Specification section of the Further Particulars. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Please quote reference VE51333 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location University Library Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference VE51333 Category Assistant staff Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-laboratory-technician-fixed-term-pd51426": {
      "title": "Research Laboratory Technician (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Laboratory Technician (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Laboratory Technician (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Laboratory Technician (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a Research Laboratory Technician to carry out experimental work within Professor Alison Smith's group to support research in chloroplast synthetic biology in microalgae. The role-holder is responsible to the Head of Group and will carry out experimental work in close collaboration with post-doctoral researchers. Overview of the role To provide technical support for research activity in order to ensure the smooth running of a laboratory or research facility. The successful candidate will be involved with generation, maintenance and characterisation of transgenic micro-algae lines. Day to day tasks include cloning of DNA constructs, growth and maintenance of algal cultures, conducting algae transformations, phenotyping of algae lines, recording data and providing results. The role will require the ability to set up experiments under minimal supervision with due care and attention. The successful candidate will be responsible for following and ensuring strict safety procedures and safety checks. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 March 2028 in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please notice that if you have not received any news from us 1 month after the closing date you should consider that on this occasion your application has not been successful. Please quote reference PD51426 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Plant Sciences Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference PD51426 Category Assistant staff Date published 19 October 2026 Closing date 11 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
