overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Urological Malignancies Administration Assistant (Part Time, Fixed Term) The Urological Malignancies Virtual Institute is a dynamic, multidisciplinary programme with around 100 members based in Cambridge. We are looking for an Administration Assistant to support the delivery of our varied study portfolio in urological cancers. This is a varied and busy role working on several activities at any given time and supporting a number of different people. There is contact with a wide range of internal and external people and the role requires an organised and methodical approach as well as tact and diplomacy. The role also includes a range of other administrative tasks including maintenance of records and distribution lists, updating webpages, communications support, providing support for committees and dealing with expense claims and enquiries. Informal enquiries may be made to Mrs Carley Batley, the Urological Malignancies Programme Manager in the first instance ( cb2147@cam.ac.uk ). Further information on our group can be found at our website: https://www.cambridge-urologicalmalignancies.org.uk . Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 March 2028 in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing date for applications is 17 November 2026. Interview dates to be confirmed. Please quote reference RE51492 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Surgery Salary £31,861-£36,320 pro rata Reference RE51492 Category Assistant staff Date published 23 October 2026 Closing date 17 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/cambridge-africa-research-fund-coordinator-fixed-term-pp51537": {
      "title": "Cambridge Africa Research Fund Coordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Cambridge Africa Research Fund Coordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Cambridge Africa Research Fund Coordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Cambridge Africa Research Fund Coordinator (Fixed Term) We are looking for a motivated individual with excellent organisational, written and oral communication skills as well as strong IT skills to join the Cambridge-Africa team to coordinate the Cambridge-Africa ALBORADA Research Fund. The person will have excellent interpersonal skills and a demonstrable ability to develop collaborative working relationships with a range of stakeholders in Cambridge and in Africa. The ability to work under pressure and to tight deadlines is also essential. The funds for this post are available until 31 December 2028 in the first instance. Cambridge-Africa, established in 2008, is a University programme that supports African researchers and promotes mutually beneficial collaborations and equitable partnerships between Africa and Cambridge. The ethos of the programme and its partnerships are based on equity, transparency and trust. Activities are driven by needs identified by African partners in order to address African (research) priorities. One of the key initiatives is the Cambridge-Africa ALBORADA Research Fund, which was established in 2012 thanks to generous donations from The ALBORADA Trust, UK. The aim of this Research Fund is to enable principal investigators at the University of Cambridge, across all disciplines, to apply jointly with a partner at a sub-Saharan African university for grants to initiate and/or strengthen equitable research collaborations. In recognition of the success of the Fund, The ALBORADA Trust awarded additional funding to Cambridge-Africa for the period 2026 to 2031, to support further engagement between Cambridge and Africa on a long-term basis. We seek to recruit a dedicated Coordinator, based in the Department of Pathology who will work closely with other members of the Team and be involved in cross cutting Cambridge-Africa activities. We particularly welcome candidates from a BME background. Key responsibilities include: Updating the call documentation, launching the online call and disseminating the competitive Cambridge-Africa ALBORADA Research Fund call for proposals Using existing databases and local knowledge / contacts to match African researchers to Cambridge colleagues Reviewing submitted applications and performing quality control checks. Preparing documents and arranging meetings for the award assessment panel Communicating effectively with: Cambridge-Africa team; Cambridge-Africa ALBORADA Research Fund Panel; Cambridge and African co-applicants about the outcome of award applications and ongoing progress of the award activities; colleagues in Research Operations Office regarding various aspects of the awards Dealing with queries from applicants and awardees Supporting researchers to make arrangements for two-way travel between Cambridge and Africa and engage with awardees throughout and beyond the duration of funding Monitoring and evaluation of awards, including writing case studies about awards/awardees Assisting with the writing of blogs based on the awards. Financial monitoring of awards Contributing to the wider activities of the Cambridge-Africa Programme, including event organisation Managing and keeping up to date the Cambridge-Africa website and social media engagement Problem solving Applicants should have a graduate degree or equivalent qualification. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Further information can be found www.vet.cam.ac.uk Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Please quote reference PP51537 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £34,630-£40,704 Reference PP51537 Category Assistant staff Date published 28 October 2026 Closing date 18 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/characterisation-pdra-fixed-term-lj51507": {
      "title": "Characterisation PDRA (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Characterisation PDRA (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Characterisation PDRA (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Characterisation PDRA (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: \nThe Research Associate position is available from 1 February 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter, for 12 months. Applicants should have experience in the solution-phase synthesis of inorganic nanoparticles with control over particle size, shape and crystalline phase, and in characterising both the colloidal state of nanoparticle dispersions (e.g. dynamic light scattering, optical microscopy, UV-Vis spectroscopy) and their crystalline phase (e.g. Raman spectroscopy, powder X-ray diffraction), with the judgement to reconcile results across techniques. Hands-on experience with in-line colloidal characterisation is desirable. Python skills for data analysis and a demonstrated interest in high-throughput experimental methods are essential. Experience in bio-templated or protein-mediated mineralisation, protein¿inorganic interfaces, or functional inorganic nanomaterials (e.g. magnetic or optical) is desirable, as is prior experience of high-throughput or automated experimentation and data-driven experimental design (e.g. Bayesian optimisation). Electron microscopy and magnetometry are carried out with project collaborators; experience in preparing samples for, and interpreting, scanning or transmission electron microscopy or SQUID magnetometry is welcome but not required. The successful candidate will contribute intellectually to research on the protein-templated assembly of inorganic nanoparticles. They will establish and run a multimodal, multi-fidelity screening workflow, including rapid colloidal screening followed by phase identification to find synthesis conditions that deliver these targets and to understand the assembly across length scales. They will work closely with the protein design, biomineralisation and advanced characterisation teams across the project; teamwork is essential to the role. They will also contribute to drafting scientific manuscripts and support the day-to-day supervision of Master's and PhD students. Applicants should hold a PhD in Materials Science, Chemistry, Chemical Engineering, or a related discipline, and demonstrate the ability to work both independently and as part of an interdisciplinary team spanning chemistry, biology, physics, mechanical engineering, and computer science. The successful candidate will work in the Autonomous Materials Group under the supervision of Dr Shijing Sun and collaborate across the ARIA project teams. This job is part of an Advanced Research + Invention Agency-funded project; as a result funding for this position is only confirmed for 12 months. \nFurther details can be discussed at interview. Successful candidates who have not yet received their PhD by 1 January 2027 will be employed on University Grade 5 SP038 as Research Assistants (£35,302). \nUpon being awarded their PhD, their salary will be upgraded to Grade 7 041 (£38,448) as Research Associates. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. We encourage early applications, as the vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. Documents in support of applications should include a CV and a research statement (max. two pages) addressing the candidate's experience in nanoparticle synthesis and characterisation and their interest in high-throughput, automated experimental methods. Should you have any queries, please reach out to Ana Talaban-Bailey: ajt69@msm.cam.ac.uk , with a copy to: hrmsm@msm.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference LJ51507 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy Salary £34,610-£35,608 £37,694-£46,049 Reference LJ51507 Category Research Date published 28 October 2026 Closing date 30 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/gaunt-group-research-laboratory-manager-ma51499": {
      "title": "Gaunt Group Research Laboratory Manager",
      "description": "Gaunt Group Research Laboratory Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Gaunt Group Research Laboratory Manager Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Gaunt Group Research Laboratory Manager Applications are invited for a senior scientific position in the research group of Professor Matthew Gaunt at the University of Cambridge. This is an opportunity for an experienced and ambitious synthetic chemist to take on a central role in the scientific development, leadership and operation of a research group working at the interface of synthetic organic chemistry, catalysis, medicinal chemistry and chemical biology. The Gaunt Group develops new concepts and methods for molecular synthesis, with particular interests in catalytic reaction discovery, the development of new synthetic strategies and the application of these advances to biologically important molecules. The successful candidate will work closely with Professor Gaunt to help shape and deliver the group's research programme, contributing to the development of new research ideas, the initiation and coordination of projects, the preparation of research proposals and the publication of scientific results. This is primarily a scientific leadership role, rather than a conventional laboratory management position. The postholder will be expected to bring substantial research experience, intellectual independence and sound scientific judgement to the position. They will play an active role in guiding ongoing research, advising on experimental design and mechanistic interpretation, troubleshooting challenging chemistry and identifying promising new directions. They will also contribute to the supervision and mentoring of postdoctoral researchers, PhD students and undergraduate researchers, with particular responsibility for supporting early-stage researchers and helping them develop the skills and confidence required for independent research. As a senior member of the group, the postholder will help maintain a productive, collaborative and supportive research environment, providing continuity of scientific expertise across multiple research programmes. They will take responsibility for aspects of laboratory organisation, research infrastructure, equipment, resources and financial oversight, ensuring the effective operation of the research laboratories. There will also be opportunities to contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching and training, depending on the interests and career aspirations of the successful candidate. The position would be particularly attractive to a researcher considering a future independent academic career, offering an opportunity to develop experience in scientific leadership, research strategy, project development, funding applications and the supervision of researchers, while remaining actively engaged in cutting-edge synthetic chemistry. We are looking for an individual with a PhD in synthetic organic chemistry or a closely related discipline, together with substantial postdoctoral or equivalent research experience and a strong record of scientific achievement. The successful candidate will have broad expertise in modern synthetic chemistry, excellent experimental and problem-solving skills, and the ability to contribute intellectually to research programmes beyond their immediate area of specialisation. Experience of mentoring researchers, coordinating collaborative projects and contributing to research proposals and publications would be particularly valuable. Equally important are excellent communication and organisational skills, initiative, sound judgement and a genuine commitment to supporting the development of others. This position offers the opportunity to combine active scientific engagement with a substantial leadership role in a dynamic research environment, contributing directly to the direction, development and success of an internationally recognised programme in synthetic chemistry. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal queries please contact Emma Graham ( ejg49@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference MA51499 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The School of Physical Sciences holds an Athena Swan Silver Award which recognises and supports progress in gender equality. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference MA51499 Category Academic-related Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 8 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/head-of-commercial-da51348": {
      "title": "Head of Commercial",
      "description": "Head of Commercial | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Head of Commercial Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Head of Commercial The Fitzwilliam Museum: Opening up the past to transform our futures. We invite applications for our new Head of Commercial role to lead on commercial strategy and income. Working closely with, and reporting to the Chief Operating Officer, the post-holder will have lead responsibility for making decisions about growing, sustaining and delivering this critical income stream as part of the Museum's business model. This is an exciting time to join the Fitzwilliam Museum, shortlisted this year for Art Fund Museum of the Year. Commercial income growth is a key priority in our business plan and the new postholder will be responsible for creating the conditions for success, establishing an entrepreneurial culture in the Museum and developing colleagues' skills and confidence. The role covers existing commercial activity including exhibition ticketing, venue hires, retail and catering contracts and their effective performance against KPI targets, as well as identifying and growing new commercial business opportunities in previously unidentified areas. The successful candidate will oversee commercial income strategy across venue hires and events, exhibitions, public programmes, partner events and membership activity, working with colleagues and teams across the organisation. We are looking for someone to join our team who has senior-level experience in a commercial, trading, or income-generation role, within a cultural, heritage, visitor attraction, or not for profit environment. In particular, we are looking for: evidence of strong commercial acumen; the ability to balance income generation with audience experience and ethical considerations; a track record of translating strategy into delivery; and the ability to build strong relationships across a range of stakeholders. In return, we offer a supportive and inclusive workplace, a generous pension scheme, substantial holiday allowance, and a range of employee benefits, including retail discounts. You will be based at the Fitzwilliam Museum, which is the principal museum of the University of Cambridge and the largest cultural venue in the region, The Fitzwilliam Museum acts as a crucial bridge between the University, the City and the rest of the world, and has an international reputation for extraordinary research, world class exhibitions, award-winning public programmes and significant collections spanning many cultures and centuries. The Hamilton Kerr Institute (HKI) is one of the world's leading centres for teaching and research in conservation and heritage science. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. We are committed to creating a diverse and inclusive workplace where everyone can thrive. As colleagues from Black, Asian and ethnically diverse backgrounds are currently under-represented within our workforce, we particularly welcome applications from candidates from these communities. We believe that a diversity of perspectives and experiences makes us a stronger and more effective organisation.\nThe University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Interviews for the role will be held: First stage 2nd December 2026 and Second stage 10th December 2026 Please quote reference DA51348 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Head Of Commercial Key information Department/location Fitzwilliam Museum Salary £64,878-£68,817 Reference DA51348 Category Academic-related Date published 28 October 2026 Closing date 22 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/pdra-on-ultralgan-lj51505": {
      "title": "PDRA on UltrAlGaN",
      "description": "PDRA on UltrAlGaN | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs PDRA on UltrAlGaN Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs PDRA on UltrAlGaN Post-doctoral Research Associate \n- MOVPE Growth of Nitride Materials for Power Electronics We are seeking a talented and creative postdoctoral researcher to work in the Cambridge Centre for Gallium Nitride, performing growth of nitride materials and devices for applications in power electronics and characterising the resulting materials. The Cambridge Centre for Gallium Nitride is part of the Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy at the University of Cambridge and also leads the nitride activity within the EPSRC National Epitaxy Facility. This post is available under the UltrAlGaN Programme Grant (EPSRC) in collaboration with the University of Bristol and other partners. The broad objective of the Programme is the development of high-performance AlGaN solid-state circuit breakers (SSCBs). In this context, the successful candidate will contribute to research on the growth of aluminium and nitrogen-polar AlGaN, the growth of graded AlGaN layers, the regrowth of AlGaN, growth on bulk AlN substrates, and the realisation of doping in AlGaN, in particular polarisation doping. The project team also includes the capability for growth by molecular beam epitaxy (MBE), and the successful candidate will exhibit enthusiasm for collaboration across the two growth technologies to achieve optimised materials. The role may also present the opportunity for basic device fabrication to facilitate epitaxy characterisation, and hence experience of device fabrication is desirable. Responsibilities:\n- Manage research activities, generate high-quality data, and develop scientific techniques.\n- Contribute intellectually to the direction of the nitride research within the Centre.\n- Present data locally at lab meetings and seminars, and more widely at international scientific conferences.\n- Draft scientific manuscripts with support from Prof Oliver and contribute to the day-to-day supervision of Masters or PhD students. Requirements:\n- Hold a Ph.D. in Materials Science, Physics, Electrical Engineering, or a related area, or be nearing completion of one.\n- Extensive experience in crystal growth, preferably using metal-organic vapour phase epitaxy (MOVPE).\n- A track record of research on nitride semiconductors or closely related materials.\n- Well-versed in a range of techniques for the characterisation of the structure and properties of materials, with experience in two or more of the following techniques: scanning probe microscopy, x-ray diffraction, scanning electron microscopy, cathodoluminescence spectroscopy, photoluminescence spectroscopy, transmission electron microscopy, Hall probe measurements, or C-V measurements. Candidates with in-depth expertise in scanning probe microscopy are particularly encouraged to apply.\n- Skills in data presentation, both verbally and in writing.\n- Committed to collaborative and interdisciplinary research and able to work both independently and as part of a team. Applications are particularly welcome from women, black, and minority ethnic candidates. The salary range if appointed as a Research Associate is Grade 7 £38,448 - £46,970 per annum (SP041-048), Successful candidates who have not yet received their PhD will be employed on University Grade 5 SP038 (£35,302 per annum) as Research Assistants. Upon being awarded their PhD, their salary will be upgraded to Grade 7 041 as Research Associates. Fixed-term: The funds for the post are available for 24 months in the first instance. For more information about the Cambridge Centre for Gallium Nitride, visit https://www.gan.msm.cam.ac.uk/ Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. We encourage early applications, as the vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. Documents in support of applications should include a CV and a research statement. Should you have any queries please reach out to ajt69@cam.ac.uk with a copy to hrmsm@msm.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference LJ51505 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy Salary £34,610-£35,608 £37,694-£46,049 Reference LJ51505 Category Research Date published 28 October 2026 Closing date 30 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-fellowship-fixed-term-gf51531": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellowship (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellowship (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Fellowship (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Fellowship (Fixed Term) The Cambridge Interfaith Programme (CIP) invites applications from outstanding early-career researchers keen to develop and independent research project contributing to CIP's research programme Peace, Conflict and Religion. Based in the Faculty of Divinity, the Cambridge Interfaith Programme is a research centre for interreligious and interdisciplinary inquiry and for dialogue between communities of faith and secular society. From 2026-2031, CIP's research programme examines how people and communities create, experience, contest and sustain peach in contexts of conflict and political violence. We are particularly interested in the cultural, religious, ethical and lived dimensions of peace and conflict: how people interpret violence, develop moral and political responses to it, mobilise religious and cultural resources, and imagine or enact alternative forms of social and political life. We invite excellent researchers to apply for the internal selection process at CIP. The successful candidate will be supported in putting forward an application for an Early Career Fellowship to the Leverhulme Trust. The Faculty of Divinity will provide the required match-funding, which will be contingent on the candidate's successful selection by the Leverhulme Trust. The successful fellow will work closely with the Academic Director of CIP, Professor Esra Özyürek, who will serve as their mentor throughout the fellowship. The fellow will also be expected to contribute to CIP's research culture through participation in seminars, workshops and other intellectual activities. We are particularly interested in projects that: use ethnographic, anthropological, or closely related interpretative approaches to examine contemporary societies; investigate how religious and cultural resources shape experiences and understandings of conflict, violence, peace, justice or resistance; examine how individuals and communities negotiate, contest or rework boundaries between religion, politics and society; explore practices of peacebuilding, nonviolent resistance, reconciliation, repair, solidarity or social transformation; investigate how memories, narratives and representations of violence shape contemporary understandings of conflict and possibilities for peace. Projects may address different geographical, cultural and religious settings, but should concern the recent or contemporary period (i.e. from the 1950s onwards). We particularly welcome projects that focus on the Middle East and its related geographies. Applicants should submit: Curriculum Vitae (CV), maximum two sides of A4 List of publications, maximum one side of A4, under the headings in preparation, Submitted and Published Statement of current and previous research, maximum 250 words Research Proposal, (maximum two sides of A4), outlining: the research question and aims; methodology and proposed research activities; the project's contribution to existing scholarship; anticipated outputs and a realistic plan for the three-year fellowship; potential contribution to and benefit from the intellectual environment of CIP The names and contact details of two referees who are familiar with your work. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. The closing date for applications is midnight (GMT) on Tuesday 15 December 2026. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and should be directed to Professor Özyürek at ego24@cam.ac.uk . For questions on the application process, please contact the School's HR Team at sahhr@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference GF51531 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Key information Department/location Faculty of Divinity Salary £38,448 - £41,885 Reference GF51531 Category Research Date published 28 October 2026 Closing date 15 December 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/ref-administrator-uoa12-nn51486": {
      "title": "REF Administrator (UoA12)",
      "description": "REF Administrator (UoA12) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs REF Administrator (UoA12) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs REF Administrator (UoA12) Would you like to spend your time working alongside academics, supporting them with a national exercise that comes round only once every seven years? The Research Excellence Framework decides how research funding is distributed across UK universities and how their research is judged nationally. Its results shape funding and reputation for years afterwards. The next exercise, REF2029, is submitted in November 2028. The School of Technology is looking for a REF Administrator to support the submission for the Unit of Assessment (UoA) 12 in Engineering, the University's second largest Unit of Assessment. This UoA covers three departments in the University: Engineering; Chemical Engineering & Biotechnology; Materials Science & Metallurgy. It comprises around 260 full time equivalent research staff and expects to submit in the region of 650 research outputs, together with a portfolio of impact case studies and a Strategy, People and Research Environment (SPRE) statement. At its heart this is a role about supporting people. REF requirements are detailed, they change as national guidance develops, and they land on academics who are already busy. You will spend much of each week in the departments rather than at a single desk, explaining what is needed in plain terms, helping researchers get their work into the system correctly, answering the questions nobody else can answer, and taking as much of the administrative load off academic colleagues as you can. Reporting to the School REF Project Manager, you will take day to day responsibility for agreed parts of the submission, which may span research outputs, impact case studies, the SPRE statement, staff eligibility and the staff volume measure. You will work closely with the UoA12 Chair and Deputy Chairs, departmental REF leads and individual researchers across the three departments, keep activity moving against the project plan, support the UoA12 Committee, and coordinate the collection and validation of the evidence the submission depends on. You will not be doing this on your own. You will work as part of a small REF team in the School Office, with the support of the School REF Project Manager, and alongside colleagues in the central University REF, Research Information and Impact teams. There is real scope to shape how the work is organised, to build a network across the University, and to take on more as the submission develops. The work is varied and the post is a visible one. At different points you might be helping a researcher with their publication records, working with an academic lead on an impact case study, preparing papers for a Committee or checking staff contract data. Few people get the chance to see a REF through from preparation to submission, and it is experience that is valued across the sector long afterwards. You do not need to have worked on the REF before, and we would rather hear from the right person than the obvious one. What matters most is that you enjoy working with academic colleagues and are good at it: approachable, patient, able to explain detailed requirements clearly to people who have little time, and trusted to handle sensitive information with discretion. Alongside that you will need to be well organised, comfortable working with detailed information, and able to use your judgement where guidance is incomplete or still evolving. You might be continuing a career in university professional services, or you might be a capable administrator or project manager from another sector looking for something with a clear purpose and a visible result at the end of it. Both are welcome, and we are happy to talk the role through informally before you decide whether to apply. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please provide a cover letter and a copy of your CV. In your cover letter, please outline how you match the criteria for the post and your motivation for applying. Further information on the role and employee benefits can be found in the job particulars. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: Ben Mason, REF Project Manager \nEmail: bm664@cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact:\nSusan Meah, Office Co-ordinator\nEmail: admin@tech.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference NN51486 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information document Key information Department/location Office of the School of Technology Salary £36,320-£46,970 Reference NN51486 Category Academic-related Date published 28 October 2026 Closing date 22 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistant-yu-group-fixed-term-pt51536": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Assistant - Yu Group (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant - Yu Group (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant - Yu Group (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a Research Assistant in the group of Dr Haopeng Yu at the Sainsbury Laboratory Cambridge University (SLCU). The Yu Group develops AI methods to understand how plants use genetic information to build different cell types, regulate gene expression and respond to their environment. We build interpretable AI and biological foundation models that learn the regulatory \"language\" of plant genomes and RNA and use them to decode the function of non-coding sequences and to generate hypotheses for experimental testing. The group also develops AI agents as virtual research partners for plant scientists. More information about the group and Dr Yu's research can be found at: https://www.slcu.cam.ac.uk/research/research-groups/yu-group and https://www.slcu.cam.ac.uk/people/haopeng-yu . The post holder will contribute to the group's ongoing research projects, including processing and analysing plant genomic and multi-omic data on high-performance computing (HPC) systems, fine-tuning and interpreting AI foundation models, and helping prepare manuscripts and funding applications. This is a fully computational role, suited to candidates with a background or strong interest in bioinformatics and computational biology. Main responsibilities will include: Processing and analysing genomic and multi-omic datasets on HPC systems, using bioinformatics tools and pipelines in Bash, Python and/or R Fine-tuning and interpreting AI foundation models, including preparing and curating training datasets Working with experimental collaborators to analyse and interpret their data, and to design predictions for experimental testing Carrying out statistical analysis and keeping clear, reproducible records of code and results Preparing figures, presenting results to the group, and contributing to manuscripts and funding applications Candidates should hold a first or Master's degree in bioinformatics, computational biology, plant biology, molecular biology or a related field. Practical experience of bioinformatics analysis and programming in Bash, Python and/or R is essential, together with a foundation in machine learning and an understanding of plant molecular biology and gene regulation. Practical wet-lab experience, which helps in understanding and interpreting experimental data, would be desirable, as would experience with statistical analysis, HPC or deep learning frameworks. Good organisational skills, a high degree of accuracy including outstanding record keeping, and the ability to work both independently and as part of a team are essential. The Laboratory provides a welcoming and collaborative environment with a wide range of family-friendly benefits and development opportunities. More about the Sainsbury Laboratory and details of what the University offers to employees can be found at: http://www.slcu.cam.ac.uk/ . Please ensure that you upload a copy of your full CV, and a cover letter highlighting your suitability for the position and contact information of three referees. The post is available with a start date as soon as possible. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 year in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Scientific enquiries apart from the formal application should be directed to Dr Haopeng Yu at hy461@cam.ac.uk ( https://www.slcu.cam.ac.uk/people/haopeng-yu ). For questions regarding the application process, please email HR@slcu.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PT51536 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information PT51536 - Research Assistant Further Information Key information Department/location Sainsbury Laboratory Salary £33,662-£36,320 Reference PT51536 Category Research Date published 28 October 2026 Closing date 25 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
