      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate - Marine Behavioural Ecology Group (Fixed-term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate - Marine Behavioural Ecology Group (Fixed-term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate - Marine Behavioural Ecology Group (Fixed-term) Research Associate - Marine Behavioural Ecology Group (Fixed-term) We are seeking a highly motivated computational researcher for a six-month project in the Marine Behavioural Ecology Group (MBEG), in the Department of Zoology at the University of Cambridge. The project will investigate how individual movement, predator-prey interactions and social interactions shape fish behaviour, using trajectory data and simulation-based approaches. Fish trajectories provide a detailed record of how individuals move and interact, but extracting the behavioural rules underlying those patterns requires careful data processing and quantitative analysis. You will develop and apply reproducible workflows for trajectory reconstruction, quality control, temporal alignment and the extraction of kinematic and spatial measures. You will use these data to analyse individual and group movement, predator-prey interactions, and develop agent-based and evolutionary simulations to test how behavioural rules and selection processes could generate the patterns observed. This is an opportunity to work at the interface of computational modelling and behavioural ecology. You will exercise technical judgement in selecting and developing analytical methods, and contribute to interpreting and presenting results through research outputs and reports. The work will involve close collaboration with the Principal Investigator and other members of MBEG, while requiring independence in managing and progressing your computational research. The postholder will join a collaborative research group studying animal movement, collective behaviour and predator-prey interactions. They may also contribute to research discussions and seminars, and support student research or skills development where appropriate. For more information, please refer to the Further Particulars document. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 6 months commencing on 1 February 2027. An extension may be possible, subject to the availability of funding. The interview for this role is expected to take place during the week commencing Monday, 16 November 2026 (subject to change) and may be conducted remotely in exceptional circumstances. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Flexible working requests will be considered. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact Professor James Herbert-Read [email: jh2223@cam.ac.uk ]. For any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Zoology HR Office [email: hr@zoo.cam.ac.uk ] Please quote reference PF51528 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - PDRA - PF51528 Key information Department/location Department of Zoology Salary £38,448-£46,970 Reference PF51528 Category Research Date published 28 October 2026 Closing date 12 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/accounts-assistant-nq51506": {
      "title": "Accounts Assistant",
      "description": "Accounts Assistant | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Accounts Assistant Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Accounts Assistant The Department of Chemical Engineering and Biotechnology wishes to appoint an Accounts Assistant to help support our busy finance team.\nThe successful candidate will undertake the administration of accounts and maintain financial records within the Department to contribute to the management of Departmental finances. \nThe role will undertake the administration of accounts and maintain financial records within the Department in order to contribute to the management of Departmental finances. The postholder will also assist in the provision of a largely autonomous accounting and purchasing service, responsible for the processing of about 4500 invoices per annum of total value £2M. Grant income is £6M per annum. The postholder will have purchasing responsibility up to £5,000, and will undertake cash management, journal transfers, and various accounting reports. The postholder will also provide cover in Stores during periods of leave of Stores personnel, due to the nature of the work manual handling training will need to be completed for this role. Other key tasks in Stores includes assisting members of the Department in the ordering via the online system and receipting goods. \nEducated to A- Level standard or equivalent, the post holder will be expected to have (or be working towards) an appropriate AAT qualification and also have previous experience working in an Accounts environment. A successful candidate would need to demonstrate knowledge of facts, principles, processes and general concepts related to Finance/Accounts Administration.\nFurther information about the Department is available from the website: https://www.ceb.cam.ac.uk/ .\nWe are looking for someone who has the ability to communicate at all levels and who can maintain a high level of accuracy whilst working to deadlines. Along with familiarity with Microsoft Office packages will also be expected along with experience of appropriate reporting tools. The ability to work within a small team is essential.\nOnce an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Depending on your individual circumstances, we may not be able to provide visa sponsorship for this role. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. . Please quote reference NQ51506 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Chemical Engineering and Biotechnology Salary £27,865-£31,861 Reference NQ51506 Category Assistant staff Date published 26 October 2026 Closing date 9 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/hr-administrator-part-time-ka51535": {
      "title": "HR Administrator (Part Time)",
      "description": "HR Administrator (Part Time) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs HR Administrator (Part Time) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs HR Administrator (Part Time) We are seeking an organised, enthusiastic and professional individual to join our friendly, supportive HR team in the Cavendish Laboratory.   We have recently moved into our new home, the state-of-the-art Ray Dolby Centre, and would like to find a highly motivated individual with a positive attitude towards change and development to join us. The successful candidate will assist on a wide range of HR administrative duties, including, but not limited to; support with recruitment and onboarding processes, including creating interview schedules and contacting candidates; undertaking right to work and basic screening checks; managing visitor administration and compliance requirements; logging and tracking sickness absence, and other essential HR administration. You will be able to demonstrate a strong admin and IT background, along with excellent communication and interpersonal skills to enable you to offer a fantastic level of customer service.    You will be highly organised and possess an ability to work reactively and flexibly as the role demands.  You will understand how to handle sensitive information with discretion, and show exceptional attention to detail. The hours are 22.5 per week, and the successful candidate will be required to work on a Friday, but we can be flexible with the other days.  Please note your preferred working days in your application.  During the probation period, the role holder will be required to work in the office.  On successful completion of their probation, we will agree up to 1 day per week working from home, dependent on the operational needs of the team. The ideal candidate will have some HR admin experience, but we will consider candidates who can demonstrate excellent administrative and organisation skills; the ability to produce work to a consistent high level of accuracy is essential. We reserve the right to close this vacancy early if we receive sufficient applications for the role.  Therefore, if you are interested, please submit your application as early as possible. No agencies please. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Susie Curtis at hr@phy.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference KA51535 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further particulars HR Administrator Key information Department/location Department of Physics Salary £27,865-£31,861 pro rata Reference KA51535 Category Assistant staff Date published 28 October 2026 Closing date 22 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-protein-self-assembly-biomineralisation-fixed-term-ma51551": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Protein Self-Assembly & Biomineralisation (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Protein Self-Assembly & Biomineralisation (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Protein Self-Assembly & Biomineralisation (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Protein Self-Assembly & Biomineralisation (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 18 months in the first instance, if project milestone targets are met at 18 months, a further 18 months of funding will be allocated. Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA) to develop approaches for the control of self-assembly of naturally occurring and synthetic proteins into 2D and 3D material phases. This project is funded by an Advanced Research + Invention Agency (ARIA) international consortium. The PDRA will be hosted in the group of Professor Tuomas Knowles in the Department of Chemistry at Cambridge University and will be involved in a multidisciplinary consortium between the University of Cambridge Department of Chemistry, Department of Engineering and Department of Chemical Engineering, along with the University of Liverpool and AMOLF, Netherlands. The PDRA will be responsible for developing techniques to combine a macro-scale printing method to guide and control the 'bottom-up' tuning of protein-templated biomineralisation. The PDRA will lead the efforts in developing protein toolkits for screening proteins enabling controlled nucleation, protection, alignment and locking. The PDRA will then apply the generated platform to allow controlled synthesis of protein-inorganic magnetic components and conversion-capable nanocrystal using droplet/flow/field processing to form anisotropic systems. Further duties include contributing to the supervision of PhD and Master's students and assisting with the maintenance of the group's custom instruments. Candidate requirements: A PhD (or be about to obtain) in Physics, Chemistry, Materials Science, biochemistry, or a related discipline.\nThe ideal candidate will possess: Experience studying the synthesis and/or characterisation of crystalline inorganic materials. A sound understanding of microfluidics-based approaches, flow/spray-based patterning, 3D printing and related templating methods. Knowledge of protein design, nucleation, self-assembly and biomineralisation. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. For informal enquiries, please contact Echo Williamson - Jw825@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference MA51551 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The School of Physical Sciences holds an Athena Swan Silver Award which recognises and supports progress in gender equality. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £33,662-£46,970 Reference MA51551 Category Research Date published 29 October 2026 Closing date 12 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-in-agentic-ai-for-cancer-care-fixed-term-rd51541": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Agentic AI for Cancer Care (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Agentic AI for Cancer Care (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate in Agentic AI for Cancer Care (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate in Agentic AI for Cancer Care (Fixed Term) Ovarian cancer is one of the most challenging malignancies to diagnose and manage, with critical treatment decisions requiring the integration of complex, multimodal clinical evidence. Transforming this evidence into reliable, interpretable, and deployable AI is one of the most exciting open problems at the frontier of machine learning and medicine. IMPACT-OC is a new, major European multi-centre project building a multi-agent AI system for ovarian cancer, spanning imaging, genomics, histopathology, and clinical records, with privacy-preserving federated learning at its core. As a Postdoctoral Research Associate, you will join the IMPACT-OC consortium and play a leading role in the project, which brings together multimodal AI pipelines, digital twin technology, and clinician-facing deployment across eight European partner institutions. This post is a joint collaboration between the Department of Radiology and the Early Cancer Institute at the University of Cambridge. You will be co-supervised by Professor Ferdia Gallagher (Department of Radiology) and Dr Mireia Crispin-Ortuzar (Early Cancer Institute), and will be embedded across both research groups, giving you access to world-class expertise in clinical imaging and cancer AI, alongside the rich, multidisciplinary environments that each brings. You will be encouraged to take part in multiple projects, contribute ideas, shape new analytical directions and participate in publications, conference presentations and collaborative funding applications. This is a genuinely translational role, sitting at the interface of artificial intelligence, clinical oncology and radiology, and human-centred design, with real potential to shape how AI enters clinical practice. This is an opportunity for an ambitious, passionate researcher to shape methods which have the potential to make management-altering changes to the way patients are treated in the future. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. This appointment requires a Research Passport application. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Dr Mireia Crispin-Ortuzar ( mc973@cam.ac.uk ) and Professor Ferdia Gallagher ( fag1000@cam.ac.uk ). Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Closing Date: 29th November 2026 Interview Date: 15th December 2026 (subject to change) Applicants must have (or be close to obtaining) a PhD. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will initially be appointed as a Research Assistant (Grade 5, Point 38 £35,302) moving to Research Associate (Grade 7) upon confirmation of your PhD award. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post, why you are applying for this role and your vision for the role (maximum 2 pages) and also include a list of publications. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Please quote reference RD51541 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Oncology Salary £35,302-£46,970 Reference RD51541 Category Research Date published 29 October 2026 Closing date 29 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-in-nucleic-acid-computing-and-molecular-programming-fixed-term-le51105": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Nucleic Acid Computing and Molecular Programming (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate in Nucleic Acid Computing and Molecular Programming (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate in Nucleic Acid Computing and Molecular Programming (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate in Nucleic Acid Computing and Molecular Programming (Fixed Term) We invite applications for a 2-year Postdoctoral Research Associate position in Nucleic Acid Computing and Molecular Programming in the laboratory of Dr Gamze Gürsoy at the University of Cambridge. This is a primarily experimental, wet-lab position. Although Dr Gürsoy's primary academic appointment is in the Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics (DAMTP), she also runs a wet laboratory in the Department of Genetics, where the successful candidate will be based and conduct their day-to-day experimental research. The position is intended for a researcher with a strong experimental background in synthetic biology, molecular biology, biochemistry, or molecular engineering, rather than someone seeking a primarily computational role. The successful candidate will join an interdisciplinary research programme at the interface of biochemistry, molecular biology, biomolecular engineering, and synthetic biology. Our goal is to develop scalable molecular and synthetic biology tools for genome engineering, applying these technologies to the exciting and emerging challenge of genetic privacy. This research brings the design and engineering of programmable molecular systems to develop fundamentally new ways of protecting genetic information. The postholder will develop programmable nucleic-acid systems that sense defined human genomic sequences and convert those molecular recognition events into engineered DNA outputs. The broader aim is to develop molecular systems in which useful genetic information can be measured while limiting direct exposure of the underlying genomic sequence. The successful candidate will help design, build, experimentally validate, and iteratively improve these systems, and extend them toward robust, multiplexed applications. The majority of the postholder's time will be spent conducting hands-on experimental research at the bench in the Department of Genetics. We are looking for a researcher who enjoys designing, constructing, and troubleshooting novel molecular systems, rather than simply applying established protocols. The postholder will also engage directly with quantitative data generated by their experiments, analysing assay performance, identifying failure modes, comparing alternative designs, and using these results to guide subsequent experimental iterations. The appointee will be expected to plan and manage their own research and administration, with guidance from the PI as required. They will take an active role in the research group, contribute to graduate student research skills, and participate in seminars, workshops, and interdisciplinary collaborations across molecular biology, synthetic biology, computational biology, applied mathematics, and genomic privacy. They will also be encouraged to develop independent research directions and pursue fellowship funding, with mentorship and support from the PI. Applicants should possess, or be close to completing, a PhD in biochemistry, molecular biology, chemical biology, bioengineering, synthetic biology, biotechnology, or a closely related experimental field. The essential qualification is demonstrated hands-on experience designing and experimentally testing nucleic-acid systems beyond routine or predominantly kit-based molecular biology. We are particularly interested in candidates who have reasoned explicitly about nucleic-acid sequence, structure, molecular recognition, enzymatic activity, and function, and translated those ideas into new experimental systems. Refer to the Further Particulars (page 4) for the full essential and desirable criteria. Experience in all of these areas is not expected. We are more interested in evidence of creative, design-driven molecular thinking and strong experimental problem-solving than in any particular technology. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please provide the contact details (including email addresses) of two academic referees on the online application form and upload a full curriculum vitae and a description of your recent research (not to exceed three pages). Please ensure that at least one of your referees is contactable at any time during the selection process and is aware that they will be contacted by the Mathematics HR Administrator to upload a reference to our Web Recruitment System. Please encourage them to do so promptly. Informal enquiries can be made by contacting Dr Gamze Gursoy at gg584@cam.ac.uk . If you have any queries about the application process, please email LE51105@maths.cam.ac.uk . Interviews will be held shortly after the closing date. Please quote reference LE51105 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information FP's Research Associate_hr7_LE51105 Key information Department/location Department of Applied Mathematics and Theoretical Physics Salary £38,448-£44,352 Reference LE51105 Category Research Date published 29 October 2026 Closing date 20 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/principal-clinical-veterinarian-small-animal-primary-care-service-pp51513": {
      "title": "Principal Clinical Veterinarian: Small Animal Primary Care Service",
      "description": "Principal Clinical Veterinarian: Small Animal Primary Care Service | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Principal Clinical Veterinarian: Small Animal Primary Care Service Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Principal Clinical Veterinarian: Small Animal Primary Care Service The QVSH is an integral part of the Department of Veterinary Medicine, providing a multidisciplinary small animal referral hospital, alongside our primary care services.  Our small animal primary care services include the QVSH General Practice and veterinary clinical services provided to the local Cambridge branch clinic of the RSPCA.  Teaching and learning are at the heart of our clinical activities, with our veterinary undergraduate students participating in the management of these cases as opportunities to develop their clinical experience, knowledge and skills, under the supervision of our clinicians. The Role As Principal Clinical Veterinarian: Small Animal Primary Care, you will lead and inspire the small animal primary care team, ensuring the sustained growth and development of the QVSH General Practice, launched in April 2025, alongside the continued provision of veterinary services to the RSPCA Cambridge clinic.  This new role presents an exciting opportunity to shape the future of the small animal primary care services at the QVSH, leading the General Practice to provide a diverse and robust primary care caseload that will support the delivery of high-quality clinical teaching to undergraduate veterinary students and contribute to the financial sustainability of the QVSH and Department of Veterinary Medicine. Key Responsibilities Working within the Hospital's operational leadership structure, you will oversee small animal primary care services, ensuring high standards of clinical care and operational efficiency. You will lead the primary care clinical team, promote professional development, and work with the Hospital's Management team to deliver clinical services, drive operational and service improvements, support high clinical standards, maintain regulatory compliance, and meet educational, financial, patient and client care targets. You will manage the day-to-day provision of small animal primary care services, leading in the delivery of all aspects of high-quality clinical patient care, optimised workflows and operational efficiency, customer service and sustainable business performance.  You will implement the Department's strategy for the growth and development of the small animal QVSH General Practice, actively contributing to the marketing, communication and promotion of primary care services.  You will oversee and participate in the clinical teaching of veterinary undergraduates at the QVSH General Practice and RSPCA clinic.  You will be expected to contribute to fostering a positive learning environment for staff and student development.  The role requires the flexibility to participate in a weekend working and out of hours on-call rota About You\nYou are an experienced small animal veterinary surgeon with strong first opinion clinical skills and an accomplished background in the operational management of a small animal general practice.  You have the commercial acumen and awareness to support an increasing caseload and to drive practice growth towards financial sustainability.  You are committed to high standards of care, customer service and collaborative working.  You have the skills, experience and values to contribute positively to an exceptional clinical education for veterinary students. Benefits 41 days' annual leave inclusive of bank holidays Generous defined benefit pension scheme Flexible working arrangements, with scope for informal hybrid working in line with service needs Onsite parking (subject to need/availability) Enhanced family friendly policies Access to a dedicated Personal and Professional Development team Wellness programme including Occupational Health team and Staff counselling Staff discount scheme including shopping vouchers Cycle to work scheme Travel to work loans Eye care voucher scheme Discounted gym membership Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: Oliver Falconbridge, Clinical\nBusiness & Operations Manager, via email: owmf2@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PP51513 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Principal Clinical Veterinarian Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £70,878 Reference PP51513 Category Academic-related Date published 29 October 2026 Closing date 26 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-locke-group-fixed-term-pt51544": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate - Locke Group (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate - Locke Group (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate - Locke Group (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a Research Associate to join Professor James Locke's group at the Sainsbury Laboratory, University of Cambridge. The post will develop and apply machine-learning approaches, including DNA foundation models, to investigate how regulatory DNA sequences influence gene expression dynamics, variability between individual cells and plants, and responses to the environment. The research The successful candidate will develop, adapt and evaluate computational models that link promoters and other regulatory sequences to gene expression behaviour. Research questions include which sequence features influence expression variability, whether models can predict the effects of regulatory sequence changes, and how these relationships depend on environmental conditions and circadian timing. A central focus will be exploring how DNA foundation models and other sequence-based approaches can help address these questions. The project offers scope to develop new methods, compare modelling approaches and identify regulatory features that generate testable biological predictions. Models will be developed and tested using quantitative gene expression measurements, including fluorescent reporters, time-resolved transcriptomics and single-nucleus RNA sequencing. The post holder will work closely with experimental researchers to connect model predictions with measurements of gene expression and guide experiments that test how regulatory sequences shape expression variability and environmental responses. The successful candidate will help shape the project's scientific and computational direction, building on their expertise and interests within these research aims. Person specification Applicants should hold, or be close to obtaining, a PhD in computational biology, bioinformatics, systems biology, quantitative plant science, computer science, statistics, or a related subject. Essential requirements are strong programming skills in Python and/or R; experience developing or applying machine-learning methods to biological sequence, gene expression or related datasets; and a good understanding of statistical analysis and model evaluation. Experience with DNA foundation models, biological sequence modelling, deep learning, regulatory genomics or interpretable predictive modelling would be particularly valuable. Familiarity with plant biology, gene expression variability, circadian biology, transcriptomics or time-series analysis would also be advantageous. Candidates should be self-motivated, able to work independently, and enthusiastic about close collaboration with experimental researchers. Research environment and career development The Locke group works across quantitative plant biology, gene regulatory dynamics and systems biology. The Sainsbury Laboratory offers an interdisciplinary research environment, mentoring and professional development opportunities. The post holder will be supported in presenting and publishing research and, where appropriate, preparing applications for independent fellowships. Flexible working arrangements may be considered subject to the requirements of the role and University and Laboratory approval. How to apply Applicants should submit a full CV, and a cover letter explaining their suitability for the post, and contact details for three referees through the University of Cambridge recruitment system when the vacancy is advertised. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 16 months in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Scientific enquiries apart from the formal application should be directed to Professor James Locke at James.Locke@slcu.cam.ac.uk For questions regarding the application process, please email HR@slcu.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference PT51544 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information PT51544 - Research Associate Further Information Key information Department/location Sainsbury Laboratory Salary £38,448-£46,970 Reference PT51544 Category Research Date published 29 October 2026 Closing date 12 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-supramolecular-chemistry-x-4-fixed-term-ma51546": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate - Supramolecular Chemistry x 4 (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate - Supramolecular Chemistry x 4 (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate - Supramolecular Chemistry x 4 (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 12 months in the first instance. Applications are invited for four post-doctoral research associates to work with Professor Christopher Hunter in the Department of Chemistry at the University of Cambridge in the general area of supramolecular chemistry. The appointee will join a multidisciplinary team of several post-docs and PhD students based in Cambridge. Applicants should have (or be about to obtain) a PhD in chemistry. Experience in some or all of organic synthesis, supramolecular chemistry, physical organic chemistry and computational chemistry is essential. The appointees will be expected to give oral presentations on their work, write internal reports and produce draft publications, and relevant communication skills are required. In addition to their own research, the appointees will be expected to take some responsibility for the supervision and training of post-graduate and undergraduate research students and for the day-to-day running of the laboratory. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. For queries regarding applying online for this post, please contact Dr M Cristina Misuraca (e-mail: herchelsmith.orgchem@ch.cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference MA51546 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The School of Physical Sciences holds an Athena Swan Silver Award which recognises and supports progress in gender equality. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £33,662-£46,970 Reference MA51546 Category Research Date published 29 October 2026 Closing date 12 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
